<b>Table </b><b>4.</b><b>3.</b> The width classes <i>c</i> of the timed noise (2001 to 2999): the pulse width is 500 per mille shifted right <i>c</i> times, and 999 per mille for <i>c</i> = 9. The last row gives the pulse at the clock of 16,000 Hz on Timer 2 (TOP 124, ticks of 0.5 µs).

Each class is half the width of the one before, as long as the pulse is wider than one tick: at the 16,000 Hz clock the classes 6, 7 and 8 all give the one-tick pulse. The class 9 is a random <b>telegraph</b>: the pin is HIGH or LOW for whole periods, apart from a LOW notch of one tick in each HIGH period, because the width is limited to TOP ticks. Codes 2001 to 2099 are therefore timed noises of 1 to 99 ms at 500 per mille; a noise at 500 per mille cannot last 100 ms (class 0 with <i>d</i> = 0 would be the refused code 2000), so a longer one is made with the continuous code 1500 and ended by a later order.
<b>How the Arduino performs an effect.</b> An effect is run by the overflow interrupt of the actuator’s own timer, once per period of the waveform: it writes the compare value of the next period (for a glide, also the period). No order ever waits for an effect; every other actuator runs as before, interrupted for a few microseconds once per period. The noise clock is limited to 16,000 Hz (<i>FX_MAX_RATE</i>), which bounds the processor time an effect can take. The random decisions come from one 16-bit linear-feedback shift register shared by all timers (taps 0xB400, the polynomial x16 + x14 + x13 + x11 + 1, starting value 0xACE1), which repeats after 65,535 decisions, 4.1 s at a 16 kHz clock [see FX_RANDOM_BIT in the Arduino firmware]. The state of an effect belongs to the timer, so one effect at a time can run on each timer; a new effect on the same timer replaces the old one.
<b>When an effect ends.</b> An effect ends when:
a level is set for any PWM M-Level on the same timer, with a plain duty or a noise code (a code 4000 to 4999 does not end it);
the actuator is halted with act_halt, its pin is freed or re-assigned, or it is destroyed;
the kill switch is thrown (Mega2560 with <i>FULL_SAFETY</i>: every M-Level is halted);
a timed noise has lasted its time.
An order for any other actuator, an M-Level on another timer, a stepper, a servo, a binary or a sensor, and any act_get, neither ends an effect nor waits for it. A noise that ends leaves its pin silent: on a 16-bit timer with the compare value 0, the pin staying on its timer; on Timer 2 disconnected and held LOW. A timed noise on Timer 2 that ends by itself leaves the pin on its timer with a constant LOW (during a noise Timer 2 drives the pin inverted); the next level for that timer (other than a code 4000 to 4999), or act_halt, disconnects it first, so that a plain level which follows has the normal polarity.
<b>The glide time and the attack step belong to the timer.</b> The glide time (16-bit timer) or the attack step (Timer 2) is stored with the timer, together with the actuator that last sent a code 4000 to 4999 or last started a noise on that timer, and the pin it used then. It applies only to that actuator, and, for a glide, only while its p0 is still that pin. It survives act_halt and every effect; it is changed only by a new code 4000 to 4999, and cleared when an actuator with the same ID is created. If two actuators use the same timer one after the other, send the code again before relying on it: a noise started by the other actuator makes the other actuator the owner, and it then uses the stored time.
<b>The clean onset (16-bit timers).</b> When an actuator that owns its timer’s effect state (it has sent a code 4000 to 4999, 4000 included, or started a noise on that timer) sets a plain level that is a pulse train while its pin is on its timer but silent (after a row of duty 0, or a noise that has ended), and no other output of that timer is connected, the period and the compare value are set at once and the counter is set one tick below the new TOP. The new compare value is loaded at the next BOTTOM of the counter, and the first pulse, centred on the BOTTOM after that, is whole: it comes about one and a half periods of the new frequency after the order reaches the Arduino. No glide is made from silence. A level set on a pin that is not connected to its timer (after the pin is assigned, after act_halt, a row of frequency 0, a constant LOW or HIGH, or the kill switch) is set at once by the firmware function analogEmit(), which writes the new TOP into the timer wherever its counter is. Whenever the counter is above the new TOP at that moment, it first runs on to 65,535, so that the first pulse can be up to 65,536 ticks late, 32.8 ms at <i>PWM_PSC_8</i>. A pin that should start its next sound promptly is therefore best silenced with a row of duty 0 rather than with act_halt.
You will be refused the pins of Timer 0 for PWM M-Level actuators (D5 and D6 on the Uno and Nano; D4 and D13 on the Mega2560), because Timer 0 runs the timing functions of the board (<i>millis()</i>, <i>micros()</i> and <i>delay()</i>) on which the stepper pulse timing depends. You will also be refused the channel-A pin of Timer 2 (D11 on the Uno and Nano; D10 on the Mega2560), because the firmware uses that channel’s register to set the frequency of Timer 2; on Timer 2 only D3 (Uno and Nano) and D9 (Mega2560) can carry a PWM output. A pin on no timer at all is refused in the same way. Each of these refusals is reported as “Pin number out of range”.
To give you the widest possible choice of frequencies you may choose the clock pre-scaler for each PWM M-Level, but only before p0 is associated with an Arduino pin. The default is <i>PWM_PSC_8</i>. If you want to change the pre-scaler of an actuator whose p0 is already assigned, you must first free the pin, then change the pre-scaler, then assign the pin again.
Pins on a 16-bit timer (Timers 1, 3, 4 and 5) run in “Phase and Frequency Correct” PWM mode (mode 8, TOP = ICR<i>n</i>). The pre-scalers of these timers have no divisor of 32 or 128, so an actuator whose pre-scaler is <i>PWM_PSC_32</i> or <i>PWM_PSC_128</i> will be refused a 16-bit pin (“Pin number out of range”) at the moment you assign p0. Pins on the 8-bit Timer 2 run in “Fast PWM” mode (mode 7, TOP = OCR2A) and accept all seven pre-scalers.
<b>IMPORTANT</b>: All the PWM pins on one timer share that timer’s pre-scaler and its period, because a timer has a single clock divider and a single “TOP” register. The firmware writes an actuator’s pre-scaler into the timer when its p0 is assigned, and writes its TOP and its pre-scaler again with every noise and every plain level it sets at once, that is, not by a glide or a clean onset, unless a servo shares the timer. Two or more PWM M-Levels on the same timer therefore always oscillate at the same frequency, and whichever of them most recently set a level has set the frequency and the pre-scaler for all of them. Each may still set its own duty cycle, but its compare value is calculated only when its own level is set: when another member of the group changes the TOP, the others keep their compare values, so that their duty cycles (compare value ÷ TOP) change as well. A glide and the clean onset write only the period, the counter and the compare value, and keep the pre-scaler already in the timer. A level of any member also ends an effect running on the shared timer. Because Timer 2 has only one usable PWM pin on each board, timer sharing can only arise on the 16-bit timers: on the Uno and Nano, D9 and D10 share Timer 1; on the Mega2560 the groups are {D11, D12}, {D2, D3, D5}, {D6, D7, D8} and {D44, D45, D46}. If a servo’s signal pin is assigned to a timer that one or more inactive PWM M-Levels already use, the servo’s pre-scaler (8 on a 16-bit timer) and frame frequency take precedence and are imposed on the M-Level(s).
<b>Order of assignment on a shared timer</b>: a PWM M-Level and a servo can share a 16-bit timer only if the M-Level’s p0 is assigned <i>first</i> and the servo’s signal pin <i>last</i>. Assigning the p0 of a PWM M-Level to a timer that already carries a servo is refused with a “Read only” error, because setting up the M-Level would disconnect the servo’s output and replace the servo’s pre-scaler, which would stretch every later servo pulse (with <i>PWM_PSC_64</i>, a 1,500 µs command would become 12,000 µs) . If you need both on one timer, assign the M-Level pins first and the servo pin last, or simply keep servos and PWM M-Levels on different timers. On a timer that carries a servo, a noise code is refused with “Read only”, and a glide or a clean onset is not made: the level is set at once, against the servo’s frame.
A pre-scaler can be chosen only while p0 is unassigned: <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> is refused whenever p0 is assigned, by the server, with the message “You can only set the prescaler for pin 0 if it is unset.”, and, should such an order ever reach the Arduino, by the firmware too, with a “Read only” error. So this command can never change the pre-scaler of a running timer. A running timer’s pre-scaler <i>can</i> be changed by another PWM M-Level on the same timer: by the assignment of its p0, or, once that is assigned, by any plain level or noise it sets, since each of these writes its own pre-scaler and TOP into the timer. Changing the pre-scaler of a timer while an M-Level on it is oscillating can have undesired consequences, namely:
<b>Mid-cycle clock skew and phase fragmentation</b>: altering the clock-select bits (CSnx) of a running timer interrupts its counter (TCNT) mid-stream, so that the active cycle is stretched or compressed into an unaligned, partial pulse.
<b>Acoustic transient shocks</b>: if a piezo buzzer is driven through a mid-cycle pre-scaler change that abruptly lowers its frequency while it holds a high duty cycle, the element receives a sudden step in drive. This is heard as a loud click or pop, and repeated shocks can fatigue the piezo diaphragm.
For this reason you will be forbidden to assign the p0 of a PWM M-Level to a timer on which another PWM M-Level is active (the attempt is reported as a “Read only” violation). De-activate the other M-Level first (for example with act_halt) and then assign the pin again; a refused attempt changes nothing, so it can simply be repeated. The firmware does not, however, prevent PWM M-Levels whose p0 were assigned earlier from being active together and changing each other’s period and pre-scaler with their levels; give the members of such a group the same pre-scaler, or keep sounds that must not disturb each other on different timers.
<b>Changing the pin of an M-Level.</b> To move an M-Level pin to another Arduino pin you may either free it first (act_set with <i>FREE_PIN</i>, 255) and then assign the new pin, or assign the new pin in one act_set. In the second case the firmware first makes every test on the new pin (range, lock, use, timer, servo, active PWM M-Level); a refused request changes nothing. It then releases the old pin: an oscillating output is stopped and any effect on its timer ended, the pin is written LOW, made a high-impedance input with its pull-up off, and freed for other actuators. Then it sets up the new pin as an output, LOW; for a PWM M-Level it puts the new pin’s timer in its PWM mode with the actuator’s pre-scaler, and the actuator becomes inactive (<i>INFO_ML_STATE</i> 0). Assigning the pin an M-Level pin already has is accepted and changes nothing. In either case the new pin stays LOW until the next level order reaches the Arduino. Because the server does not send a level equal to the last one it sent, set a different row first, or use act_halt, which clears the server’s record of the last level. For a PWM M-Level that is active (<i>INFO_ML_STATE</i> 1), a direct move to another pin of the <i>same</i> timer is refused with “Read only”, because the actuator is itself the active M-Level on that timer: halt it first. A glide time set on a 16-bit timer belongs to the old pin: after a move, send the code 4000 + <i>P</i> again.
<b>Activity, </b><b>act_halt</b><b> and the kill switch.</b> An M-Level becomes active (<i>INFO_ML_STATE</i> 1) when a level order for it is carried out: any plain duty, including a row of duty 0 or of frequency 0, or a noise code; a code 4000 to 4999 leaves the state as it was. It becomes inactive (0) when it is created, when the p0 of a PWM M-Level is assigned, when it is halted with act_halt or by the kill switch, and when its last pin is freed. A timed noise that has ended by itself leaves it active. act_halt and the kill switch disconnect a PWM pin from its timer, end any effect on that timer and hold the pin LOW; they write every pin of a parallel M-Level LOW. act_halt of an inactive M-Level does nothing. The server clears its record of the last level when it sends act_halt, but it knows nothing of the kill switch, of a timed noise that has ended by itself, or of an effect ended by another actuator on the same timer. After any of these, set another row, or send act_halt, before you set the same level again: otherwise the server does not send it, and nothing sounds.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Servo</span>
 
A servo is a multi-position actuator that uses positional feedback to travel to one of many precise positions, as may be used, for example, to drive a filter wheel or an objective changer. The feedback control loop is inside the servo itself. Some servos make the position reported by their internal sensor available on a fourth wire, which a PCS script can read; others keep it internal.
There are many kinds of servo actuator, including stepper-motor servos, analogue-motor servos (brushed, brushless or coreless) and servos powered hydraulically or by other non-motor means. Analogue-motor servos may have analogue or digital control circuits (analogue servos and digital servos).
PARDUS supports 3-wire and 4-wire (the fourth wire carrying the position feedback voltage) analogue-motor servos with analogue or digital controllers, such as the TowerPro MG995 and similar, or faster high-end models such as the Savox HV digital brushless servos. The defining feature of a servo PARDUS supports is that its position is commanded by the width, in microseconds, of a pulse repeated at a constant frame rate, in one of three frequency bands (nominally 50 Hz, 200 Hz and 333 Hz), with no other signalling. Specialist industrial servos that use proprietary digital protocols and dedicated drivers, and other categories of servo (stepper servos and so on), are not currently supported.
A servo needs a PWM-capable pin with a constant frame frequency and pre-scaler. The pins a servo may use are the same as those a PWM M-Level may use (see the table of pins in the section on act_set): any pin of a 16-bit timer, and D3 (Uno and Nano) or D9 (Mega2560) on the 8-bit Timer 2. A pin on Timer 0, a pin on no timer, and the Timer-2 channel-A pin (D11 on the Uno and Nano, D10 on the Mega2560) are refused with “Pin number out of range”. A 16-bit timer gives a servo a pulse resolution of 1 microsecond in every band; Timer 2 gives 64 microseconds in the 50 Hz band and 16 microseconds in the other two (see the section on act_set).
If a servo’s signal pin is assigned to a 16-bit timer that a PWM M-Level actuator already uses, the servo’s pre-scaler and frame frequency take absolute priority over whatever has been set for the M-Level(s). The reverse order (an M-Level’s p0 assigned to a timer that already carries a servo) is refused with a “Read only” error; see the section on M-Level actuators. A later attempt by such an M-Level to change the timer’s frequency is ignored by the firmware without error. The M-Level can still change its duty cycle, but the duty-cycle resolution is then set by the servo’s frame (see the section on act_set).
For accurate positioning and for the safety of the hardware (to avoid overheating, stripped gears and other damage), each servo used with PARDUS must be calibrated and given a suitable range of limits when its actuator is created in PCS. This is described in the act_set section of this manual.
The PARDUS servo subsystem is programmed from scratch by direct register manipulation. It therefore has several advantages over the standard Arduino Servo.h library: PARDUS servos can be given speed regulation and acceleration profiles, and any number of them can be grouped into a Bresenham set for co-ordinated motion. Because the pulses are generated by the timers’ own compare-match output hardware and not by timed CPU interrupts (the method Servo.h uses), PARDUS servo control places very little load on the AVR processor.
Servos are moved in PARDUS according to this general model:
Select a motion profile for servos using act_accel. 
Load each servo’s desired destination into a queue using act_set commands. 
Execute the motion of all servos in the queue using a single act_moveto command. 
The queue is cleared whenever the act_moveto command is issued. The same procedure applies whether you want to move one servo (a queue of 1) or many. For details see the individual command entries.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Sensor</span>
These allow access to the numerical value of a specific single input pin on the breakout board. They may be specified as being of analogue or digital type (giving access to an analogue pin or digital pin respectively). 
A sensor actuator can have its sensing pin 'hot-swapped' to a different pin of the same type (analogue or digital) within a script. However you cannot simply 'hot-swap' a pin from analogue to digital or <i>vice versa</i>. You can achieve the same effect, however, by simply destroying an analogue sensor actuator and re-creating it (with the same name and ID if that is your choice) but with the type being digital (or <i>vice versa</i> - destroy a digital sensor and re-create it as an analogue sensor). It takes a few more programming steps in the script than simply hot-swapping the pin but the overall effect is as if you had hot swapped an analogue pin to a digital one (or <i>vice versa</i>). Here is an example PCS script to illustrate this hot-swapping ability.
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Pardus pcs 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>verbosity 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>###########################################################</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Testing sensor nodes - 'hot-swapping' an analogue pin   #</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># and effectively hot-swapping analogue to digital using  #</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># undef_actuator and def_actuator.                        #</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>###########################################################</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#include stdpard.pch</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var string actname1 _</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int loopidx 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int pin_state 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>get_actuators</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Safety Layer: Clean up any previously defined actuator of the same index</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># and type (but</tt><tt> </tt><tt>possibly with a different name to the one you are about to</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># create</tt><tt>) to prevent a duplicate definition error later.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_getname ACT_SENSOR 1 actname1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp actname1 != null</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>do_if Condexp</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>undef_actuator actname1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>enddo</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Instantiate the sensor actuator and make it of the analogue type</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_actuator ACT_SENSOR 1 Sensor_general</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_set Sensor_general INFO_SEN_TYPE  SEN_ANALOGUE        </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_set Sensor_general INFO_SEN_PIN   5      # Reading analogue pin 5</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Make periodic sensings and print the value</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>print_value Sensing_on_pin_A5:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>call_func sensor_readings</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Now hot-swap the sensed pin from A5 to A3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>print_value Swapping_to_pin_A3:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Sensor_general INFO_SEN_PIN   FREE_PIN    # Release current pin</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Sensor_general INFO_SEN_PIN   3           # Reading analogue pin 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Make periodic sensings and print the value</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>print_value Sensing_on_pin_A3:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>call_func sensor_readings</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Now 'hot-swap' from an analogue sensor to a digital one:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>print_value Swapping_to_pin_D7:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># We can't just swap the pin so we destroy the analogue sensor first:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>undef_actuator Sensor_general</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Now re-define it as a digital sensor</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>def_actuator ACT_SENSOR 1 Sensor_general</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Sensor_general INFO_SEN_TYPE  SEN_DIGITAL        </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Sensor_general INFO_SEN_PIN   7      # Reading digital pin 7</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var loopidx 32</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>print_value Sensing_on_pin_D7:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>call_func sensor_readings</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func sensor_readings</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var loopidx 16</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>loop_while loopidx</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt># Read the sensor value</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_get Sensor_general INFO_SEN_VAL pin_state</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt># Print the value</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>print_value pin_state</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>sleep 1.0                 </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>sub_var loopidx 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>endloop</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Use of Arduino PWM Modes</span>
The PWM-type M-Level actuators and the Servo actuators both use PWM pins on the Arduino breakout boards. However the generated waveforms are made using bare-metal timer register manipulation and not CPU-emulation. CPU wave emulation is used in some common libs like Servo.h and has the advantage that more pins may be used to drive servos (even non-PWM pins) but this emulation mode brings with it severe limitations which would degrade the multi-actuator type precision control system of PARDUS. Thus PARDUS accepts a reduction in the maximum number of pins available for PWM mode actuators in exchange for preserving precision multi-actuator control.
The hardware-driven waveform architecture of PARDUS relies on the internal magnitude comparators of the ATmega silicon. Because the CPU writes to the control registers once and walks away, it is physically impossible for high-speed serial data packet bursts, stepper motor pulse train loops, or multi-axis matrix calculations to cause microsecond-level timing jitter. This provides the absolute positioning stability required for sub-micron microscopy imaging and smooth robotic path sweeps. This holds for every plain level of an M-Level. The special-effect codes of the PWM M-Level (noise, timed noise, burst and hiss, glide) add one interrupt per period of the waveform, the timer’s own overflow interrupt, which writes the compare value of the next period (for a glide, also the period). The edges of the waveform are still made by the comparator hardware, but each period of an effect costs the processor a few microseconds, and the noise clock is limited to 16,000 Hz for that reason.
However, because these waveforms are tied directly to physical silicon modules, their deployment is governed by two strict hardware constraints: <i>Shared Clock Prescalers</i> and <i>Fixed Frequency Ceilings</i>. Table 4.4 shows the timers that PARDUS uses for PWM, and how the firmware sets them.



  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Board      </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Timer</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Bits</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Mode                                    </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">TOP register</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">PWM pins (compare output)               </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Pre-scalers        </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Effects      </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────┼───────┼──────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┼───────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ Uno, Nano   │ 1     │ 16   │ 8: phase and frequency correct           │ ICR1         │ D9 (OC1A), D10 (OC1B)                    │ 1, 8, 64, 256, 1024 │ noise; glide  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Uno, Nano   │ 2     │ 8    │ 7: fast PWM                              │ OCR2A        │ D3 (OC2B); D11 (OC2A) refused            │ all seven           │ noise; attack │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Mega2560    │ 1     │ 16   │ 8: phase and frequency correct           │ ICR1         │ D11 (OC1A), D12 (OC1B)                   │ 1, 8, 64, 256, 1024 │ noise; glide  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Mega2560    │ 2     │ 8    │ 7: fast PWM                              │ OCR2A        │ D9 (OC2B); D10 (OC2A) refused            │ all seven           │ noise; attack │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Mega2560    │ 3     │ 16   │ 8: phase and frequency correct           │ ICR3         │ D5 (OC3A), D2 (OC3B), D3 (OC3C)          │ 1, 8, 64, 256, 1024 │ noise; glide  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Mega2560    │ 4     │ 16   │ 8: phase and frequency correct           │ ICR4         │ D6 (OC4A), D7 (OC4B), D8 (OC4C)          │ 1, 8, 64, 256, 1024 │ noise; glide  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Mega2560    │ 5     │ 16   │ 8: phase and frequency correct           │ ICR5         │ D46 (OC5A), D45 (OC5B), D44 (OC5C)       │ 1, 8, 64, 256, 1024 │ noise; glide  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ every board │ 0     │ —    │ not changed (it runs millis(), micros(), │ —            │ none: D5, D6 (Uno, Nano) and D4, D13     │ —                   │ —             │ </tt>
  <tt> │             │       │      │ delay())                                 │              │ (Mega2560) are refused                   │                     │               │ </tt>


<b>Table </b><b>4.</b><b>4.</b> The timers used for PWM actuators, as the firmware sets them. A period lasts 2 × TOP ticks in mode 8 and TOP + 1 ticks in mode 7; one tick lasts <i>N ÷ 16</i> µs.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">1. The Shared Clock Prescaler Rule</span>
Each hardware timer module on the Arduino (Timer 1 and 2 on the Nano/Uno; Timers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 on the Mega 2560) can only run a single prescaler divisor and a single counting ceiling (TOP) at any given moment.
If multiple PWM-type M-Level actuators or Servo actuators share a timer module, they are bound to the same time-base envelope. Because all Servo actuators are standardized to a stable base carrier frequency (e.g. 50Hz requiring an immutable hardware prescaler of 8 on 16-bit timers and 1024 on 8-bit timers), any coexisting PWM M-Level actuator on that same timer is forcefully adapted to that clock envelope.
While the M-Level’s individual duty cycle remains fully independent, its emitted frequency is completely overridden by the servo’s carrier base. Among PWM M-Levels that share a timer, the one that most recently set a level imposes its frequency and pre-scaler on the others, whose compare values are not recalculated (see the section on M-Level actuators). To prevent mid-cycle phase fragmentation, clock skew, and acoustic transient shock pops, the firmware refuses, with a read-only conflict (<i>PARDU_ERR_RDONLY</i>, “Read only”):
the assignment of the p0 of a PWM M-Level to a timer that carries a servo, or on which a PWM M-Level is active;
the assignment of a servo’s signal pin to a timer on which a PWM M-Level is active, or which carries servos of another frequency band;
a noise code on a timer that carries a servo;
any change of <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> while the actuator’s own p0 is assigned.
The firmware does not refuse a level of a PWM M-Level whose p0 was assigned to the shared timer earlier, even when it changes the TOP and the pre-scaler under another M-Level that is sounding. Only one special effect can run on a timer at a time, and a level of any M-Level on that timer ends it.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">2. The Fixed Frequency Ceiling Restriction (The Pin 11 / Pin 10 Block)</span>
To slow down the small, high-speed 8-bit Timer 2 module to the safe ~50Hz-60Hz envelope required by some analog servos and audio buzzers, the PARDUS firmware locks the silicon into Fast PWM Mode 7.
In this mode, Timer 2 does not count blindly to its hardware maximum of 255. Instead, it uses its Output Compare Channel A register (OCR2A) as a custom, programmable counting ceiling: the counter counts from 0 up to the number stored in OCR2A and then starts again from 0, so that a period lasts OCR2A + 1 ticks, which stabilises the time-base. Because Channel A is completely allocated to regulating the global carrier frequency, its physical hardwired pin can no longer modulate independent duty cycles.
Consequently, the physical pin tied to Channel A is entirely blocked for use by any Servo or PWM-type M-Level actuator. Attempting to allocate it inside a script will be forcefully rejected by the firmware validation gates, throwing an immediate <i>PARDU_ERR_PINRNUM</i> error packet (“Pin number out of range”). Due to differing silicon routing layouts between the microcontrollers, this blocked frequency ceiling pin moves between platforms:
On the Arduino Nano / Uno: Channel A maps to Digital Pin 11. Pin 11 is therefore completely blocked for Servo/PWM M-Level use. (It remains fully usable for non-timed tasks like standard Parallel-type M-Level actuators or SPI data communications.)
On the Arduino Mega 2560: Channel A maps to Digital Pin 10. Pin 10 is therefore completely blocked for Servo/PWM M-Level use.
Symmetrically, Channel B remains fully open on both boards as your functional, independent 8-bit output driver line (mapping to Pin 3 on the Nano/Uno and Pin 9 on the Mega 2560). It reads the common ceiling natively, allowing full, separate positioning sweeps or tone modulations without risk of system register contamination. With TOP at its smallest value, 1, a period of Timer 2 lasts two ticks, so the highest frequency it produces is <i>F ÷ (2N)</i>, with only 0, 500 or 1000 per mille.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Maximum number of simultaneous PWM actuators</span>
In practice this means that the PARDUS / PCS system using an 8-bit AVR Arduino board (Uno, Nano or Mega2560) can deploy a total simultaneous number of PWM-capable actuators (M-Level or Servo) of 3 on the Uno/Nano - on digital pins 3 (8-bit timer), 9 and 10 (16-bit timers); or, on the Mega2560, 12 when the line #define FULL_SAFETY is commented out - on digital pins 9 (8-bit timer), 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 11, 12, 44, 45, 46 (16-bit timers) - and 10 when <i>FULL_SAFETY</i> is defined, which the firmware does automatically for the Mega2560: D2 (the kill-switch input) and D12 (the motor-voltage relay output) are then locked, leaving pins 9, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 11, 44, 45 and 46. The server and the firmware also allow at most 10 M-Level actuators, of both subtypes together (IDs 0 to 9), and at most 10 servos (<i>MLEVELSMAX</i>, <i>SERVOSMAX</i> in parddefs.h).
The Uno/Nano uses 2 timers across those pins - so can vary two frequencies independently on those 3 pins. The Mega2560 uses 5 timers across those pins - so can vary 5 frequencies independently on those pins because each timer can use only one prescaler value (that determines the frequency range available), and one TOP value (that determines the specific frequency of oscillation). The pulse width and duty cycle can be varied individually for each pin (not only for each timer), with the reservation that each pin’s compare value is calculated against the TOP in force when that pin’s own level is set. Likewise, one special effect at a time can run on each timer: at most two at once on the Uno/Nano and five on the Mega2560.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="xx-large" foreground="#1a5fb4">The PCS preprocessor</span>

When a PCS script is loaded into the interpreter program (such as pardclient) it undergoes preprocessing before it is syntax checked. The preprocessor function does two things:
It includes any external files the script requests via #include directives.
It makes any string substitutions the script requests via #define directives.
For version 1 of PCS that is all the preprocessor does. 
To be a valid directive there must:
be no non-white space characters before the '#'
be no white space characters between the '#' and the rest of the directive
be at least one white-space after the end of the directive before its argument
be the required number of arguments after the directive.
Using a '#' in your PCS file without following the first 3 rules means the '#' will be interpreted as a comment (as will a typo like '#defin'). Following the first 3 but not following the last rule will result in a script check error.
The preprocessor analyses the script file that is loaded and outputs its resulting pre-processed version of that script to a file called _pppcs.pcs (or _stpcs.pcs for a 'stop script' - see the pardclient user manual for more about stop scripts) so do not try to load that file directly into the client or a file access error may result. These pre-processed script files will be saved in your OS temp directory.
You may read the _pppcs.pcs file in a separate text editor if you would like to see the results of pre-processing on your input file. The actual PCS file on disc that you load into the client program is not altered itself. After successful pre-processing, the script checker loads the _pppcs.pcs file to compile and check it and the script interpreter runs the commands in the _pppcs.pcs file.
Although superficially resembling preprocessor directives of the C programming language PCS preprocessor directives behave differently in that only file include and simple string substitutions are supported and the directives all have global scope no matter where, in the script, they occur.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">The #define directive</span>
Usage:
#define &lt;find_string&gt; &lt;replace_string&gt;
Description:
The #define directive allows the programmer to perform automatic text macro substitutions throughout their script prior to execution.
Arguments:
&lt;find_string&gt; - an alphanumeric identifier string (letters, digits, and underscores only, up to 64 characters) to be replaced.
&lt;replace_string&gt; - the string token used to replace the &lt;find_string&gt; wherever it is encountered as a distinct word entity.
<b>Note</b>: Only simple string substitution is supported in version 1 of PCS. There is no facility to use arguments in parentheses or substitute in multi-word expressions. 
<b>Note</b>: Substitutions apply strictly to isolated alphanumeric word boundaries. A word is defined as a continuous sequence of letters, digits, and underscores. Any character that is not a letter, digit, or underscore—such as spaces, brackets, parenthesis, commas, or mathematical operators—safely isolates the macro name.
For example, if you define `#define CURRIDX 5`, the word CURRIDX inside complex nested code structures like:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var myvar myarray[CURRIDX]</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var myint pcg_ar[gptr[CURRIDX]]</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var total base+CURRIDX</tt> </span>
will be isolated cleanly by the delimiters and successfully expanded into:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var myvar myarray[5]</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var myint pcg_ar[gptr[5]]</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var total base+5</tt> </span>
(Note: 'base+5' is not a valid mathematical expression in PCS but it could be the name of a variable - albeit a poor style choice for a variable name).
A macro name will not be replaced if it is directly fused to other alphanumeric characters or underscores (e.g., MY_CURRIDX or CURRIDX2 will be ignored). The final line length after all substitutions are made must not exceed 2047 bytes.
The main use is to allow the script writer to use human-meaningful words in their script where numerical values are required by the main PCS syntax. For example to use the word 'ACT_STEPPER' in place of the numerical value '1' by means of the preprocessor directive:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ACT_STEPPER  1</tt> </span>
Without such a preprocessor string substitution ability, the only other way to use human meaningful labels for numbers would be to define a variable with the human-friendly name and set its value to the number value but doing so would incur a cost in terms of RAM allocation and variable evaluation processing time every time that variable is used in the script at run-time.
It is convention to use all capitals for the &lt;replace_string&gt; to distinguish them from the names of variables or PCS commands but it is not mandatory.
Neither argument is expected to be encased in quote marks - if quote marks are used they will be interpreted as part of the literal string to be found or replaced.
The fact that preprocessor substitution is purely string based - without regards to the semantics of the string - means that you can use #define substitutions for <b><i>command aliasing</i></b>. For example if you do `#define print_value PRINT` then you can use aliased command PRINT in your script instead of print_value.
More than one #define of the same &lt;find_string&gt; is an error ("Duplicate 'find' string"), even with the same replacement.
A &lt;find_string&gt; or &lt;replace_string&gt; longer than 64 bytes is truncated when it is registered.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">The #include directive and header files</span>
Usage:
#include &lt;filename&gt;
Description:
The #include directive allows the programmer to include a PCS header file in their script.
Arguments:
&lt;filename&gt; - the name or full path and name of a file containing more pre-processor directives.
<b>Note</b>: The &lt;filename&gt; must not have any spaces in its name or in a path and the angled brackets are not part of the syntax (unlike in C). So an example is:
#include myfile.pch
<b>Note</b>: The search path for &lt;filename&gt; is resolved as follows:
The pre-processor will first try to open &lt;filename&gt; exactly as written, so if you provide a full path as well as the file name the pre-processor will look there.
If the first attempt fails then the pre-processor will look for &lt;filename&gt; in the same directory as the PCS script that is being processed.
If both attempts to read the include file fail, the pre-processor will also fail with an error message.
Note: Every file used for inclusion must have a properly formatted header signature on its very first coding line. This signature must consist of exactly three words: the case-sensitive string "Pardus", followed by the file format token "pch", and completed by an integer format version number. 
Currently, the only supported file format version number is 1, meaning the first coding line of a valid include file must be exactly:
Pardus pch 1
Any file that omits this signature, places code before it, or uses an incorrect file format token (such as "pcs") will be rejected immediately with an explicit header format error specifying the exact filename and line number. 
After this signature line can come comments, blank lines, and pre-processor directives. As with a standard PCS script, leading and trailing whitespace and blank lines are safely ignored. The header file must be explicitly terminated with an exit command; all text trailing after the exit line is ignored. Any text before the exit line which is not a preprocessor directive is also ignored, allowing for standard code comments.
By convention, header files have the extension .pch but this is not mandatory for proper reading and parsing of the files.
<b>Note</b>:  Advanced Features in Version 1:
1. Full Recursion: The #include directive is completely recursive. Header files may safely contain their own #include directives to reference deeper library files.
2. Silent Include Guards: The pre-processor contains a historical tracking registry that tracks completely processed headers. If a file has already been parsed earlier in the script run, subsequent attempts to #include it are silently ignored. This natively prevents duplicate macro definition errors and immunises your code against infinite circular inclusion loops.
3. Include Depth Limits: Scripts support nested inclusions up to a maximum depth of 16 layers deep before throwing a stack protection limit error.

Some pre-prepared include files are available on the PARDUS open source repository to make PCS programming more 'human friendly' such as the file stdpard.pch which includes all the actuator-related symbols used in the C source code for the interpreter allowing PCS programmers to use those symbols directly in their PCS scripts (such as the names of the various actuator types and the names of the various information item numbers, etc. as will be described later in this manual).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="xx-large" foreground="#1a5fb4">Variables and arrays</span>

There are two classes of variables in PCS,  user-defined variables and system variables.
All variables have global scope.
All variables are arrays (single variables are just arrays of size 1)
The names of variables are case sensitive.
The possible data types for variables are int, float, and string. These are described in more detail, together with the rules on casting, in the chapter 'Data types, the ‘varval’ and casting'.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">User-defined variables</span>
These are created with the def_var and def_array commands and, once created, they may be destroyed using the undef_var command. All user-defined variables will be destroyed automatically when the script terminates for any reason. User-defined variables are read-write capable. Because all variables are arrays, when the term 'variable' is used in this manual it automatically also refers to 'array'. 

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Dynamic memory allocation and scope</span>
The size of an array may be specified by another variable at the time the array is created with def_array and does not need to be pre-determined before the script is run. This allows for dynamic array size allocation in PCS scripts. The size of an array may also be changed during run-time using a def_array -&gt; undef_var -&gt; def_array sequence. See the detailed description of def_array for more information.
All variables have <b>global scope</b> but may only be used from the point in the script at which they are defined and they may not be used (as variables) from the point in the script at which they are undefined (if they are ever undefined). This gives the PCS programmer the ability to manually determine the <b>temporal scope</b> of a variable. See undef_var for more detail.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Limitations imposed by the syntax checker on variable use</span>
There are 3 rules imposed by the PCS syntax checker that you must be aware of to avoid syntax errors or run-time behaviour you might not otherwise have expected.
<b>1. </b><b>The use of dereferencing brackets</b>: The PCS syntax rules make a distinction between scalar variables (that is, arrays of size 1) and vector variables (that is, arrays of size &gt;1). All scalars must be used with their base name only and no de-referencing brackets. All vectors must be used with dereferencing brackets with the sole exceptions of use in the commands def_array and undef_var where only their base name must be supplied.
<b>2. </b><b>Limitations on the temporal scope of a variable</b>: The interpreter's syntax checker goes through the script <i>line-by-line sequentially from the start</i> of the file to the first use of the exit command regardless of any conditional branching or looping rules. As soon as it sees a def_var or def_array command it will attempt to create the variable. As soon as it sees an undef_var command it will attempt to destroy that variable and forbid is use as a variable from that line on unless or until the variable is re-created with an other def_var or def_array command. Any attempt to use a variable name (with or without a dereferencing bracket component) that has been destroyed will be treated as though you are supplying a string literal, not a variable, and this may not result in an error but in behaviour you might not expect during run time. This means that if you place a def_array or an undef_array inside a conditional block, like a do_if block or a function that may or may not be called, then those variable creation and deletion events will be registered by the script checker when it comes to that line in the script regardless of any conditional code that would block that line from being executed during run time.
<b>3. The checker only uses the initialisation value of a variable</b>: Because the syntax checker executes the actual creation and deletion of variables as it comes across them, and because the syntax checker does not perform any operation that may change the values of variables already created, it means that if you use a variable as an argument to some other command (including as a size argument to a def_array command) then the value you supplied as the initialising value at the the time of def_var or def_array will be the value used by the syntax checker whenever that variable is used. For this reason, make sure you supply an initial value that will not cause a syntax error wherever that variable is used. Before actually running the script, all variables created during the syntax checking phase will be totally deleted so this will not affect any calculations your script makes during run time (e.g. for dynamic allocation of array sizes). This applies to every argument that the checker validates: a variable used as a goto label, a function name or a math_var function must be initialised to a valid label, function or function name. The type of error that may occur at the syntax check phase due to improper initialisation value selection is illustrated in the following examples:
a) In this example you will get a syntax error because you initialised the sz integer to 0 and you cannot use 0 as the value of size for defining an array:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var </tt><tt>int </tt><tt>sz 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var </tt><tt>int </tt><tt>mp 10</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>add_var sz mp</tt><tt>    </tt><tt>    </tt><tt>   </tt><tt>    </tt><tt># This will not be performed by the syntax checker</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_array </tt><tt>int </tt><tt>myarray sz 0</tt><tt>  # </tt><tt>so </tt><tt>sz will remain 0 hence syntax check will fail</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> here.</tt> </span>
b) In this example you will get a syntax error because you initialised sval to '_' and '_' cannot be cast to an integer:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var </tt><tt>string </tt><tt>sval _</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_str sval 10</tt><tt>          </tt><tt>   </tt><tt># This will not be performed by the syntax checker</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_array </tt><tt>int </tt><tt>myarray sval 0</tt><tt> # so sval will remain '_' and the check</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                             </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> will fail here.</tt> </span>
The correct thing to do in example a) would be to define sz with a value &gt;= 2 such as 'def_var sz 2' and in example b) define svar to a value that can be cast to an integer such as 'def_var sval 3'.
<b>Note</b>: Although it is perfectly legal to use a size value of 1 with def_array, using 1 will create a single variable, not a multi-element array. Although the interpreter's internal C source code treats all variables as arrays - even arrays of size 1 - the interpreter itself, while interpreting PCS source code, makes a distinction between arrays of size 1 (PCS source code cannot use dereferencing brackets with such 'arrays', they are treated as single variables for interpretation purposes) and arrays of size &gt;=2 (these must use dereferencing brackets when used in PCS source code). This is why I use the '&gt;=2' value in the above commentary, because I make the assumption that the coder intends to create a multi-element array.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">System variables</span>
These are a set of variables that always exist and the script writer has read-access to. Some of these variables can also be written to by the user but only via specific commands. Other system variables are strictly read only for the PCS script writer. There follows a list of all current system variables with a description of their contents and / or purpose.



  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Name         </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Type  </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Description                                       </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├───────────────┼────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ Space         │ string │ Contains a single space character ‘ ‘. It is for   │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ adding spaces to strings during concatenation with │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ the add_var command or creation with the def_var   │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ or setting with set_var or comparison operations.  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Hash          │ string │ Contains a single hash character ‘#’. It is for    │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ adding hashes to strings during concatenation with │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ the add_var command or creation with the def_var   │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ or setting with set_var or comparison operations.  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ DateTime      │ string │ This is a string that is updated whenever it is    │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ evaluated and contains a time-date string up to    │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ the nearest second of the format:                  │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ YearMonthDay_HourMinuteSecond for example          │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ 20260303_124911                                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Condexp       │ int    │ This value of 0 or 1. It is set to have a value of │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ 0 by default at the start of execution of a        │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ script. The value of Condexp may be set or changed │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ by means of issuing the 'set_condexp' command in a │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ script (which see). There is no other way for a    │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ user to set the value of Condexp. The value of     │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ Condexp is retained throughout the running of a    │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ script until it is set or changed by a set_condexp │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ command. If you want to use the current value of   │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ Condexp even after another set_condexp command is  │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ run then you must set a user-defined variable to   │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ the current value of Condexp before calling the    │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ next set_condexp command.                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ Sysval        │ int    │ This may take on any integer value. This is set to │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ have a value of 0 by default. It is potentially    │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ changed any time the 'int system(const char        │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ *command)' function is run because it takes the    │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ return value from this function (so may change     │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ whenever the command 'execute_system' is           │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ processed, for example). The user cannot alter     │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ this variable at will but can copy its current     │ </tt>
  <tt> │               │        │ value to any varval that accepts integer values.   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TRUE          │ int    │ This holds a numerical value of 1                  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ FALSE         │ int    │ This holds a numerical value of 0.                 │ </tt>
  <tt> │ ON            │ int    │ This holds a numerical value of 1.                 │ </tt>
