<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333"></span>


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="xx-large" foreground="#1a5fb4">The PCS Library Archiver and Linker</span>


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Introduction</span>
Like many other programming languages, PCS has the ability to allow you to create (archive) and use (link) libraries.
A PCS <b>library</b> is a collection of PCS functions, together with the global variables and arrays they use, that any script can incorporate into itself by means of the add_lib command. The variables of a library may serve as arguments, results or behaviour modifiers of the functions of that library.
A library is written as an ordinary PCS script. It becomes a library when it is compiled by means of the command make_lib in its source code. When the PCS run-time engine sees this command it <b>compile</b><b>s that PCS program into</b><b> library file</b>, whose extension is always .pcl. 
A script uses a compiled library with the command add_lib. On seeing this command, the pardclient copies the library’s definitions and functions into the script’s compiled program before the script is checked, so the script runs exactly as if the library’s source had been written into it.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Writing a library</span>
A PCS file that you intend to compile into a library must follow the following pattern - which is more restrictive than for general PCS programs in that there are restrictions imposed here that do not exist for general PCS programs that are not intended to be compiled to libraries:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Pardus pcs 1</tt><tt>                             # The usual PCS magic.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>make_lib</tt><tt>                                 # Must be the first command.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var   &lt;type&gt; &lt;name&gt; &lt;value&gt;          # </tt><tt>Define any</tt><tt> global variables</tt><tt> and/or</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_array &lt;type&gt; &lt;name&gt; &lt;size&gt; &lt;value&gt;   # arrays </tt><tt>the library exposes to the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                                         </tt><tt># user. No other commands or goto</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                                         </tt><tt># labels are allowed before main.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main                            # </tt><tt>T</tt><tt>he library's own test program</tt><tt>.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>...                                  # </tt><tt>This main function will not be</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt><tt>                                   # copied into the library. This main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                                         </tt><tt># must not use any goto labels.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func &lt;first library function&gt;</tt><tt>        # Then follows as many functions as</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>...</tt><tt>                                  # is wanted. These must not depend</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt><tt>                                   # depend on anything defined in main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func &lt;second library function&gt;       </tt><tt># and there must be no code at all</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>...</tt><tt>                                  # between the last return and the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt><tt>                                   # exit line.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">The rules</span>
The pardclient enforces the following rules when the library is made. Every refusal names the rule (by its number in the specification, '<i>Rules</i><i> </i><i>for</i><i> </i><i>libs</i><i>'</i>, detailed at the end of this chapter) and the line concerned.
<b>A full </b><b>syntax </b><b>check first.</b> The library source must pass the full script check applies to all PCS programs (which is why they must include a main function even though that is stripped away when the source is archived into a library file). The pardclient checks it once more when make_lib runs, even if you are using a dove file as your library source (usually, dove files skip the script check phase because a script check would have been done when creating the dove file, but for programs that are about to undergo archiving into a lib, the check is repeated regardless of whether you load the lib source a .pcs or as a pre-compiled .dove file).
make_lib<b> comes first.</b> make_lib must be the first coding line after the magic line, and may appear only once. Anywhere else it is a syntax error, reported by the script check.
<b>Only definitions before </b>main<b>.</b> Before def_func main, only def_var and def_array may stand. Nothing else is allowed there: no other command, no goto label, no null_command, and no add_lib.
<b>No library inside a library.</b> A library cannot use another library directly. To build a library using other libraries, copy those other libraries' PCS source into the new library’s source.
main<b> and at least one other function.</b> There must be a function main, and at least one other function after it.
<b>No label in </b>main<b>.</b> The function main may contain no goto label at all.
<b>No gaps between functions.</b> Between the return of one function and the def_func of the next, nothing may stand, not even a goto label.
<b>Only </b>exit<b> after the last function.</b> After the return of the last function, nothing but exit may stand.
main’s<b> names are its own.</b> No variable or array defined in main may have the same name as one defined before main or after main.
<b>The functions must not depend on </b>main<b>.</b> main is not copied into the library, so every other function must be able to run all its code paths, including every optional path, without anything that only main defines. The pardclient refuses a library when any of the following holds:
<b>Variables.</b> A variable or array that only main defines is used outside main: as an argument, as an array subscript at any depth, or as a file name. Words that set_str and cat_str copy as text are not uses.
<b>Actuators.</b> The name of an actuator that main defines appears anywhere outside main. The name is taken as the script check takes it: as written, or as the value of the string variable that holds it. Define a library’s actuators in one of its own functions (for example an initialisation function) instead.
main<b> itself.</b> A function calls main.
<b>The program without </b>main<b>.</b> The library, with the body of main removed, fails the full script check. This catches every other dependence the check can see, for example a command whose prerequisites (such as af_setup before autofocus) only main supplies.
Goto labels need no test of their own: rule 6 allows none in main.
<b>Two things that cannot be judged before the script runs.</b> Rule 10 cannot judge them, and the author must take care of them:
<b>The current actuator </b>CurrAct<b>.</b> Every command that names an actuator changes it. A function that uses CurrAct relies on whoever called it to have selected an actuator.
<b>Names computed at run time.</b> A function or label name that is assembled into a string variable while the script runs, and then used with call_func or goto, cannot be known in advance.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Good practice</span>
The rules are the minimum that the client requires. A good library also:
gives its functions, labels and variables names that a script is unlikely to use, for example with a common prefix such as rv_;
assumes no particular hardware;
defines, in its own functions, every actuator it uses;
puts in main a short test of its own functions.
During development it helps to comment out make_lib: the library source then runs as an ordinary script.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Making a library (archiving)</span>
Load the library source .pcs file into pardclient and press <i>Run</i>. Instead of running the script, the client takes four steps:
It checks the rules above.
It asks where to save the library. The default is the script’s own folder and name, with the extension changed to .pcl, and the user may change both.
It always saves the library with the extension .pcl. A name ending .dove or .pcs has that extension replaced, and any other name has .pcl appended.
It writes the compiled library. The console then reports, for example:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Library written: /home/prof/pcs/mathlib.pcl (6 definition rows, 28 function rows in 3 functions).</tt> </span>

If the save is cancelled, the run ends with the error:
 make_lib: the save was cancelled; no library file was written.
The library is first written to a temporary file, &lt;library&gt;.tmp, in the same folder. It replaces any older library of the same name only when it is complete. The temporary file is always deleted.
A library source that was previously saved as a compiled program (.dove) can also be made into a library: load the .dove and press <i>Run</i>. The library is still checked in full (unlike with other dove programs that do not contain the make_lib command) and, if archiving is successful, the newly created library with be saved as a .pcl file as described above.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Using a library</span>
A script names its libraries immediately after the magic line like this:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Pardus pcs 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>add_lib mathlib.pcl</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>add_lib /home/prof/pcs/libraries/robovox.pcl</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>.</tt><tt>..</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
The rules are:
<b>Placement.</b> Every add_lib must come immediately after the magic line, before any other command or goto label, and all add_lib lines must be contiguous.
<b>Formal functions.</b> The script must contain at least one formal function (def_func main at least).
<b>The file name.</b> The file name is the rest of the line after add_lib, so it may contain spaces.
The library inclusion (linking) process does this: Each library’s definitions are placed after the add_lib lines, and its functions after the script’s last function. The script and its libraries share one set of names. A name defined in both, or a library named twice, is refused by the script check as a duplicate definition.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Compiled programs (.dove) that use libraries</span>
If a script that uses libraries is saved as a compiled program (.dove), the libraries are included in it. The .dove is a static build: it runs without the .pcl files.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Where the client looks for a library</span>
The file named by add_lib is looked for first exactly as written: either a full path, or a name relative to the folder from which the client was started. If it is not found there, it is looked for in <b>the folder of the last script loaded</b>. Files named by #include are looked for in the same way.
<b>Caution.</b> The main script and the stop script (also called an 'interrupt script' - see the pardclient user manual for details) share that “last script loaded”. Suppose a stop script is loaded from another folder <i>after</i> the main script. If the main script is then compiled again (the <i>Run</i> button compiles it afresh from its file whenever no compiled program is ready), its libraries and included files are looked for in the <b>stop script’s</b> folder.
To avoid this, either give full paths in add_lib and #include, or keep the main script and its stop script in the same folder.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Messages about library lines</span>
Every line of a script keeps its own source code line number in messages, and so does every line of a library. A message about a line that came from a library therefore names the library and the line in the library’s source file, for example:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>[user.pcs][13]: Array index out of bounds: 'el_tab[el_i]' (Index: 10). (line 13 of library errlib.pcl)</tt> </span>
The same holds when the program was saved as a .dove.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">The compiled library file (.pcl)</span>
The layout of a compiled PCS library.


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">offset</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">size      </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">content                                           </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├────────┼────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ 0      │ 7 bytes    │ the characters PCS LIB (no terminating zero)       │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 7      │ 32-bit     │ format version (1)                                 │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │ unsigned   │                                                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 11     │ 32-bit     │ ndefs: the number of definition rows               │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │ unsigned   │                                                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 15     │ 32-bit     │ nfuncs: the number of function rows                │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │ unsigned   │                                                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 19     │ (variable) │ ndefs rows, then nfuncs rows, each in the row      │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │            │ format of a .dove file                             │ </tt>


The rows are stored exactly as the compiler produced them. They carry no position: every position in a program, such as the target of a goto or the start of a function, is worked out by the script check (or, for a .dove, by the AOT linker) after the libraries have been linked and just prior to running the program.
The client refuses a library file that does not have this form exactly. The faults it detects are:
a wrong magic or version;
fewer or more rows than the header announces;
a damaged row;
definition rows that are not def_var or def_array;
functions that do not end with return.

