<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Rules for Libs</span>
These are the rules that the numbers in the above messages pertain to.
A library is written as an ordinary PCS source code file but it cannot be used as a library until it is pre-processed and compiled into a library as follows:

All library source files are just normal pcs files but they need to be constructed according to some rules that make them usable as a library.
A pcs file is compiled into a library by the archiver if it sees the command 'make_lib' as the first coding line after the magic line.
The command 'make_lib' appearing anywhere after the first coding line (post magic) is a syntax error and the pre-processor / syntax checker must reject a script as malformed if it finds that command anywhere else in a script or more than once in a script.
If the run-time engine sees the command 'make_lib' (op code OP_MAKE_LIB) in the compiled PCS program then, instead of running the rest of the program, it processes the script into a lib file according to the following:
The following rules are checked. If any one of them fails then the script is rejected as not compatible with a lib file and this is treated as a run-time error:
If the script contains a goto label or any code after the return of the last function and before exit it is rejected.
If the script contains no functions other than main it is rejected. (This implies that main must be present.). Thus  'main' must be the first function, and at least one other  function must follow it.
No variable or array defined in main may have the same name as one defined (def_var or def_array) before main or after main.
main may contain no goto label at all.  Then nothing outside main can jump into main.
between the return of one function and the def_func of the next nothing may stand, not even a goto label.
If any coding line (including a goto label) occurs between the return of the last function and the exit command it is rejected.
If any command or label, other than def_var or def_array, occurs before 'def_func main' it is rejected. This excludes the initial magic line and add_lib command - but nothing else.



<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="xx-large" foreground="#1a5fb4">PCS and AI Coding Agents</span>


PCS was designed at a time when AI coding assistants were becoming commonly available and widely adopted. For this reason PCS was designed to be 'AI-friendly' in that is has certain features built into the language that make it easy and efficient for AI agents to learn, to debug and to code in.
While PCS remains fully accessible to human programmers, certain design choices prioritise the way Large Language Models (LLMs) and automated planners process logic, tokenise syntax, and manage state.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">The AI-friendly design principles of PCS</span>
The following summarise the core principles of the language which are of particular value to AI coding agents.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">1. Atomic and Predictable Syntax</span>
PCS avoids nested functional complexity (e.g., x = func1(func2(y))). Instead, it uses a flat, instruction-based format where each line performs a single, verifiable operation. This reduces "token attention" drift and ensures the AI can maintain perfect syntactic accuracy over long scripts.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">2. Operator Abstraction (Dynamic Dispatch)</span>
In PCS, logical and mathematical operators (like ==, &gt;, sin, or pow) are often treated as data tokens or variables. An AI can store the argument of a goto command, a function name in a call_func command or a comparison symbol in a string variable (including an array of strings) and "decide" at runtime which operation to perform. This allows for highly compact, meta-programmable logic that bypasses the need for massive if/else trees.
The use of this feature in the goto command as well as the call_func command means the AI agent is free from the restrictions of using only run-time string constants (a restriction found in many other languages like C). This enables '<b><i>first-class control flow</i></b>'. By treating control flow elements (labels, functions, and operators) as first-class variables, PCS allows AI agents to architect flexible, non-linear workflows. This removes the brittleness of hard-coded logic and empowers the script to adapt its own structure in response to environmental data. Such <b><i>meta-programming</i></b> allows for the use of a '<b><i>Jump Table</i></b>' approach that is more efficient for LLMs to manage than complex branching logic.
Here is a skeleton template example for PCS meta-programming:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Pardus pcs 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>verbosity 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_array string CompOp 6 ==</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int CompIdx 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_array string FuncName 100 func_1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int FnIdx 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_array string GotoLabel 20 exit_label</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int GtIdx 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var float fvar1 0.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var float fvar2 0.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ivar 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ivar 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># Populate the comparison operations array</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_var CompOp[0] ==</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_var CompOp[1] !=</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_var CompOp[2] &gt;=</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_var CompOp[3] &gt;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_var CompOp[4] &lt;=</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_var CompOp[5] &lt;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># Likewise populate the function name array and goto label array</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># ... populating code here ...</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># Other code, manipulating CompIdx, fvar1, fvar2, FnIdx, GtIdx, etc.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># Note that the 'if </tt><tt>&lt;varval&gt;</tt><tt>' component of call_func and goto are optional</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># but, as an example, we could do something like this:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_condexp fvar1 CompOp[CompIdx] fvar2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>call_func FuncName[FnIdx] if Condexp</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># Other code</tt><tt> here ...</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_condexp ivar1 CompOp[CompIdx] ivar2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>goto GotoLabel[GtIdx] if Condexp</tt><tt>   # Also remember that a PCS goto is</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                                     </tt><tt># 'stack aware' and capable of non-local</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># etc.</tt><tt>                             # jumps.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Other functions defined below here.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit_label:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">3. Transparent State Management</span>
PCS uses a Global-Only variable environment. By eliminating local scope and variable "shadowing," the AI always has a clear, unambiguous view of the entire machine state. This is particularly effective for Non-Local Jumps (goto), allowing the AI to move between different processing modes without the risk of stack-frame corruption or hidden memory leaks.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">4. Vectorised Reasoning via Universal Arrays</span>
Every variable in PCS is an array. This encourages the AI to think in "batches" or "lookup tables." By storing function labels, branch targets, or mathematical operators in arrays, an AI can navigate complex behavioural states using simple index-based dispatching.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">5. Explicit Data Casting</span>
PCS employs a "Left-Hand Master" rule for automatic casting. When comparing or operating on different data types, the interpreter clearly defines how the conversion occurs. This predictability ensures that an AI-coder can accurately anticipate how the system will handle "3" (string) versus 3 (integer).
<i>Example:</i>
set_var myStr 5
results in myStr containing the string composed of ['5']['\0'] whereas, using this same myStr, if we do
set_var myInt myStr
this results in myInt containing the numerical value 5.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">6. Minimalist Delimiter Logic</span>
By dispensing with quotes and brackets for strings and complex expressions, PCS minimises "syntactic noise." This reduces the likelihood of the AI "forgetting" a closing character and ensures that the literal data provided is exactly what sits in memory.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">7. The Scratchpad zone</span>
The PCS exit command allows for "free text annotation" below it in any script file. This is a very useful feature for LLMs. The area below exit may be used for persistent memory, scratchpad logic, or storing JSON metadata that doesn't need to be parsed by the PCS interpreter. AI models appreciate having a designated "scratchpad" area.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Guide for AI prompting</span>
When using an AI LLM to generate or debug PCS scripts, providing a clear "System Prompt" is essential. Because PCS uses a flat, global-only architecture with unique string handling, you should explicitly instruct the AI to adopt the following coding style:

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">The "PCS Expert" Prompt</span>
To get the best results, start your request to the AI with a prompt similar to this:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>*"Act as an expert PCS programmer. Write a script for version 1 that follows these rules:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Provide a 'Plan' in the Scratchpad zone below the exit command before writing the main code.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Use a flat logic structure (avoid deep nesting where possible).</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Remember that strings have no delimiters; quotes are literal data.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>All variables are global; use unique prefixes for function-specific variables.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Use the set_condexp result Condexp for all branching logic</tt><tt> that requires a conditional expression to be evaluated</tt><tt>.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>All variables are arrays; use var[index] syntax for sizes &gt; 1."*</tt> </span>

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Guidelines for AI Code Generation</span>
<b>Atomic Steps</b>: Ask the AI to "break complex maths into individual steps using math_var or individual arithmetic commands like add_var." This prevents the model from trying to use C-style inline equations which will fail the PCS syntax check.
<b>String Formatting</b>: Remind the AI that "spaces between arguments are collapsed." If the AI needs to generate a formatted UI, instruct it to "use the Space system variable with add_var (or cat_char with a value 32) to build precise whitespace." and that "PCS does not use delimiters of any kind to define or declare strings; if a delimiter is used it will be a literal part of the string".
<b>Dynamic Labels</b>: Encourage the AI to "use string variables for goto labels and function call destinations to create state-machine transitions." LLMs are excellent at managing state tables when given the permission to do so.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Avoid blind trust</span>
Since PCS is a language that controls hardware, mistakes can lead to physical damage (just as mistakes in G-code can damage a 3D printer or CNC machine).
It may take a lot of specialist PCS training before an AI can generate code that approximates to 'safe' code for hardware control. 
It is highly advisable <b><i>not</i></b> to simply 'trust and run' PCS scripts written by an AI. You should examine the code to ensure it does what you intend in a safe way (for example, that all extant motors have the correct limit switch pin assignments) before running it on a live robot, CNC stage or other mechanism. Close supervision of the robot / stage is also advised, especially on the first run and be ready to hit the kill switch if necessary.



<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="xx-large" foreground="#1a5fb4">List of commands</span>

Here is an alphabetically ordered list of current prime commands and their usage notes.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_accel</span>
act_accel &lt;act_type&gt; [args based on &lt;act_type&gt;]
This sets essential common parameters for motion of an actuator of type &lt;act_type&gt; which will be used for all non-homing motions with actuators of that type till you change those parameters with another call to this command. 
&lt;act_type&gt; - for the options for this integer see def_actuator. The arguments that are expected to follow &lt;act_type&gt; will depend on &lt;act_type&gt;.
For &lt;act_type&gt; = 1 (stepper) the following format is expected: 
act_accel &lt;act_type&gt; &lt;prop_acc&gt; &lt;isd_min&gt; &lt;isd_max_min&gt; 
Their meanings, data types and value ranges are as follows: 
&lt;prop_acc&gt; - a float number between 0.0 and 1.0. This is the proportion of the motion steps that are involved in acceleration and deceleration (as opposed to the remaining steps which will be done at a constant (maximum) velocity in between the acceleration and deceleration phases. 
&lt;isd_min&gt; - For stepper motors this is the minimum inter-step delay (isd) in microseconds. This determines the maximum speed of travel once travelling at constant velocity (i.e. at the end of any initial acceleration phase from a standstill). It must be &gt; 1 (and is usually a few hundred or more. A value much lower than this probably won't be honoured in the motion because the various C source code statements that occur between motor pulse steps (like checking for limit switch status, etc.) will take about 150-200 microseconds to complete meaning any value you supply here much less lower this will just get over-ridden with whatever minimum time it takes to do those statements). 
&lt;isd_max_min&gt; - This is the longest isd you are willing to tolerate at the start and end of the motion. This number should be considerably larger than &lt;isd_min&gt; or the motion profile calculation will fail. A value in the low thousands would be typical. Both &lt;isd_min&gt; and &lt;isd_max_min&gt; are integers representing a whole number of microseconds. 
For &lt;act_type&gt; = 4 (servo) the following format is expected: 
act_accel &lt;act_type&gt; &lt;s_acc&gt; &lt;duration&gt; 
Their meanings, data types and value ranges are as follows: 
&lt;s_acc&gt; - an integer between 0 and 255. This is the proportion of the motion steps that are involved in acceleration and deceleration (as opposed to the remaining steps which will be done at a constant (maximum) velocity in between the acceleration and deceleration phases. The proportion is calculated by first converting the required servo motion range for a particular movement into number of 'steps' (a step is the smallest movement the servo can make at its current settings and bit depth of the timer its motion signal pin is connected to). This total number of steps is first divided by 2 to give the maximum number of steps that can be allotted to an acceleration or deceleration phase (called 'max_ramp_steps') and then the actual number of these steps to use for the acceleration and deceleration ramps is given by (using bit shift division):
(s_acc * max_ramp_steps)/256
To prevent initial and final acceleration steps from taking to long, a hard-coded lower limit of 0.5 seconds is imposed for the maximum inter-step delay at the start and end of any acceleration ramp. Unlike with steppers (where a sigmoid acceleration curve is used) servos use a simple linear ramp acceleration profile.
&lt;duration&gt; - This is the duration, in microseconds, that you would like the entire motion sequence (acceleration, deceleration and any intervening constant motion phase) to take. It must be &gt; 0. If the value you supply here is not longer than the estimated time it will take the servo to make the entire motion at its fastest intrinsic speed then the servo will undergo its motion at its native speed and will not be slowed down using this act_accel motion profile. This 'native speed' is calculated using the value you set for the servo minimum inter-step-delay using act_set with INFO_SRV_ISD (called isd_min in the source code) according to:
isd_min * total_steps
This is why it is important to set an acceleration profile before you add a servo to the motion list - because the decision on whether to move the servo with the acceleration and speed profile you set here is made at the time you list a servo on the motion queue with act_set and INFO_SRV_QPOS. Thus, if you ever change the acceleration profile after listing a servo on the queue you should unlist that servo and re-list it prior to issuing the act_moveto command in order to achieve a predictable motion profile for it. 
<b>Note</b>: This command must not be called for the Binary actuator type because these do not use motion characteristics and so to call act_accel for this type of actuator will actually cause an error that will terminate the program.
<b>Note</b>: This command needs to be called at least once per login session in order for other motion commands (apart from stepper homing) to be allowed. A call to act_moveto or act_moveby will result in a script-terminating error if the interpreter sees that act_accel has not been called for the the actuator type you are trying to move. Likewise a call to act_set with INFO_SRV_QPOS (but not INFO_SRV_SPOS) for a servo will be rejected with an error as a safeguard to avoid erroneous motion queue parameter calculations (for the reasons discussed above).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_get</span>
act_get &lt;act_id&gt; &lt;info_ID&gt; &lt;var&gt; [&lt;var_y&gt; &lt;var_z&gt;]
This reads the value of the &lt;info_ID&gt; struct member of the actuator defined by &lt;act_id&gt; and sets it as the value of the variable &lt;var&gt;.
&lt;act_id&gt; - See undef_actuator for a full description of this.
&lt;info_ID&gt; - must be an integer corresponding to a readable item from the actuator's data structure. The currently valid readable info item integers are shown below (taken from parddefs.h). Note that only the integer value of the information item must be supplied, not the 'INFO_' token string, <i>unless</i> you include a pch header file containing these #defines (excluding the C comments) in your script, then you may use these #defined 'INFO_' token strings in your PCS script to make it more readable. The PCS include file, stdpard.pch, available on the PARDUS open source repository, was created to allow you to use all the following symbols in your PCS scripts:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// These are the values for a Stepper actuator:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define INFO_ENA_STATE 1  // Ardu only    // Ask Ardu</tt> </span>
