The items labelled 'Server only' are data only stored on the server for this actuator (they are not stored on the Arduino) so accessing these items does not require the server to communicate with the Arduino. Conversely the items labelled 'Ardu only' are only held on the Arduino so can only be accessed by means of the server communicating with the Arduino and that could cause a stutter or short delay in otherwise continuous actions (like motion) being performed by the Arduino.
All the other items are maintained in sync on both the Arduino and the server. To save unnecessary comms with the Arduino, only those items that may be set or changed as a result of Arduino actions are read from the Arduino ('Ask Ardu') by the server instead of being looked up on the server's local copy of the structure (local get or 'LG') in response to a client's act_get command. Obviously, an act_set command will require communication with the Arduino to set the data on the Arduino's version of the actuator structure with the exception of 'Server only' data items.
For the command to succeed &lt;act_id&gt; must represent an actuator that is currently defined on the server, &lt;info_ID&gt; must correspond to a readable member of that actuator’s structure and &lt;var&gt; must be a user-defined variable (not a literal constant value or an internal system variable) and must be of a data type that can accept the requested info item (i.e. a string, float or int).
The optional two arguments &lt;var_y&gt; &lt;var_z&gt; are used in the cases where &lt;info_ID&gt; specifies three values. For stepper actuators these are:
 INFO_NHS
 INFO_XYZCOORDS
In the latter case the supplied &lt;act_id&gt; is overridden by the stepper actuator IDs of 0, 1 and 2 (the default XYZ motors of the stage) and this command will only be allowed if those three steppers are defined.
<b>Note</b>: Table AG.1 gives every info item of an M-Level actuator, with what act_get and act_set may do with it.



  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">No.            </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Info item </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">act_get                  </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">act_set                    </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Held on    </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Values                                  </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Meaning                                 </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼─────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ 1               │ INFO_ML_ST │ yes, from the Arduino     │ no (read only)              │ Arduino     │ 0 or 1                                   │ 1 once a level order has been carried    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ ATE        │                           │                             │             │                                          │ out; 0 after creation, assignment of p0  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                           │                             │             │                                          │ (PWM), act_halt, the kill switch, and    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                           │                             │             │                                          │ the freeing of the last pin.             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 3               │ INFO_ML_TY │ yes                       │ once only, first            │ both        │ MLT_PARALLEL 0, MLT_PWM 1 (MLT_UNSET 3   │ The subtype; nothing else can be set     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ PE         │                           │                             │             │ when created)                            │ before it.                               │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 4               │ INFO_ML_PS │ yes                       │ only while p0 is unassigned │ both        │ 0, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10 (default 3,         │ The pre-scaler code that the next        │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ C          │                           │                             │             │ PWM_PSC_8)                               │ assignment of p0, and every level, use.  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 6               │ INFO_ML_TI │ yes, from the Arduino     │ no (read only)              │ Arduino     │ 0 (none), 1 to 5                         │ The timer of p0; 0 while p0 is           │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ MER        │                           │                             │             │                                          │ unassigned and always for a parallel     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                           │                             │             │                                          │ M-Level.                                 │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 8               │ INFO_ML_TT │ yes, with three variables │ yes, with three values      │ server only │ row; frequency 0 to 8,000,000 Hz; duty 0 │ One row of the translation table.        │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ ABLE       │                           │                             │             │ to 4999 except 2000 and 3000             │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 19 to 24, 47,   │ INFO_PIN_P │ yes                       │ yes                         │ both        │ an Arduino digital pin; 255 (FREE_PIN) = │ The pins; a PWM M-Level uses INFO_PIN_P0 │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 48              │ 0 to INFO_ │                           │                             │             │ unassigned                               │ only.                                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ PIN_P7     │                           │                             │             │                                          │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 30              │ INFO_MATUR │ yes                       │ no (read only)              │ server only │ 0 to 3                                   │ 1 for the type, 1 more for p0, 1 more    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ ITY        │                           │                             │             │                                          │ for a PWM M-Level with a table. A        │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                           │                             │             │                                          │ parallel M-Level accepts levels from 2,  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                           │                             │             │                                          │ a PWM M-Level from 3.                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 33              │ INFO_ML_FR │ yes                       │ no (read only)              │ Arduino     │ —                                        │ The frequency ceiling the firmware       │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ EQ         │                           │                             │             │                                          │ computed when p0 was assigned (see the   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                           │                             │             │                                          │ note below).                             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ 49              │ INFO_ML_LE │ yes, the server’s record  │ yes                         │ both        │ PWM: a row 0 to n − 1; parallel: 0 to 2n │ The level last sent to the Arduino.      │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ VEL        │                           │                             │             │ − 1; act_get returns 2147483647 when no  │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                           │                             │             │ level has been sent since creation or    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                           │                             │             │ the last act_halt                        │                                          │ </tt>


Table AG.1. The info items of an M-Level actuator.

<b>Note</b>: For M-Level actuators the ‘&lt;var_y&gt; &lt;var_z&gt;’ arguments are used when setting or getting rows in the translation table using <i>INFO_ML_TTABLE</i>, and the format of the act_get command is then:
act_get &lt;act_id&gt; INFO_ML_TTABLE &lt;row&gt; &lt;freq&gt; &lt;duty&gt;
Where:
&lt;row&gt; is a user-defined integer variable that holds the row in the table (starting at 0) whose data you want to retrieve. It must be a variable, not a literal number, because the client requires every receptor of act_get to be a user-defined variable, and it must not be negative.
<i>&lt;</i>freq<i>&gt;</i> is the variable to hold the returned frequency integer (0 to 8,000,000).
<i>&lt;</i>duty<i>&gt;</i> is the variable to hold the returned duty integer (0 to 4999: a duty cycle in per mille from 0 to 1000, or a special-effect code from 1001 to 4999).
The values returned are the values stored in the table, which are the values you asked for. They are not necessarily the values the hardware produces: the frequency produced is the nearest achievable frequency at or above the stored one, limited to the range of the pre-scaler, and the duty cycle produced is the nearest achievable at that frequency (see the section on act_set). A stored frequency of 0 means silence (pin held LOW), unless the duty is 1000 (pin held HIGH).
If the actuator has no translation table, an attempt to act_get a row of this non-existent table is an error. Such an error is not fatal to the script but is signified by the &lt;row&gt; value being incremented by 1 when the command executes.
If the actuator has a translation table but the row you supplied does not exist in it (index out of bounds), this error is signified by the &lt;row&gt; value being incremented by 2 when the command executes.
You can therefore check for these errors by keeping a copy of the value you supply for &lt;row&gt; and comparing it with the value in &lt;row&gt; after the command executes. If the two differ, the values returned in &lt;freq&gt; and &lt;duty&gt; are garbage and must not be used.
<b>Note</b>: For M-Level actuators, <i>INFO_ML_STATE</i> reports whether the actuator has been set to its current activation level (which can be read with <i>INFO_ML_LEVEL</i>) or whether it has been de-activated (for example by an act_halt command or by the physical kill switch). It is read from the Arduino. It is 1 after any level order that the Arduino has carried out, including a row of duty 0 or of frequency 0 (the pin is then silent but the actuator is active) and a noise code; a code 4000 to 4999 leaves it unchanged, and a timed noise that has ended by itself leaves it at 1. It is 0 after the actuator is created, after the p0 of a PWM M-Level is assigned, after act_halt or the kill switch, and after its last pin is freed. A PWM M-Level whose state is 1 prevents the assignment of the p0 of another PWM M-Level, or of a servo’s pin, to its timer.
<b>Note</b>: <i>INFO_ML_LEVEL</i> is read from the server’s own record, without asking the Arduino. It is the level (the row number, for a PWM M-Level) last sent with success. It reads 2147483647 (<i>MLEVEL_RESET</i>, <i>INT32_MAX</i>) after the actuator is created and after every act_halt sent for it. The server is not told of the kill switch, of the end of a timed noise, or of an effect ended by a level of another actuator on the same timer, so the value read is then the last level sent, even though the pin may be silent.
<b>Note</b>: <i>INFO_ML_TIMER</i> is read from the Arduino: 0 while p0 is unassigned and for a parallel M-Level; otherwise the number of the timer that drives p0 (1 or 2 on the Uno and Nano; 1 to 5 on the Mega2560).
<b>Note</b>: <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> returns the actuator’s own pre-scaler code, as last set with act_set. It is the pre-scaler in force on the timer only while this actuator was the last on that timer to set a level (see the note on shared timers below), and on a timer shared with a servo it is not in force at all.
<b>Note</b>: For PWM M-Level actuators, <i>INFO_ML_FREQ</i> (33) is the frequency ceiling the firmware computes for the actuator’s pre-scaler when p0 is assigned; any translation-table frequency above it is reduced to it. Its value can also be calculated instead of read: for a pin on a 16-bit timer the ceiling is <i>F ÷ (2N)</i> by integer division (8,000,000, 1,000,000, 125,000, 31,250 and 7,812 Hz for the pre-scalers 1, 8, 64, 256 and 1024); at that ceiling TOP is 1, the duty can only be 0 or 1000 per mille, and the highest frequency at which the pin makes a pulse train is half of the ceiling, <i>F ÷ (4N)</i>, with 500 per mille only. For the pin on Timer 2 the ceiling is <i>F ÷ N</i> Hz, but the highest frequency Timer 2 can actually produce is half of that, <i>F ÷ (2N)</i> Hz, because a fast-PWM period cannot be shorter than two timer ticks. Before p0 is first assigned the firmware holds the value 11 (<i>PWM_PSC_UNDEF</i>) in its place.
<b>Note</b>: When a PWM M-Level actuator is on a pin that shares a 16-bit timer with a servo actuator, the servo’s frame frequency (50 Hz, 200 Hz or 333 Hz) and pre-scaler (8) are used for the M-Level, overriding whatever you may read with <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> and whatever frequency is stored in the relevant row of the translation table. (Such sharing arises only when the servo’s pin was assigned after the M-Level’s p0; the firmware refuses the opposite order.)
<b>Note</b>: As discussed in more detail in the section on act_set, a group of PWM M-Level actuators that share a hardware timer also share one pre-scaler and one frequency (the timer’s “TOP” value), the last M-Level of the group to set a level having set them for all. The other members of the group may therefore return act_get values of pre-scaler that are not actually in force, and their duty cycles are those of their own compare values against the TOP now in force. It is the responsibility of the PCS programmer to keep track of the frequency and pre-scaler most recently set on each timer.
<b>Note</b>: Every M-Level info item conveys an integer, so the receptor &lt;var&gt; (and, for <i>INFO_ML_TTABLE</i>, the three receptors) must be user-defined variables capable of taking an integer value.
<b>Example</b> (PCS). Read the state, the timer, the last level and one table row of a PWM M-Level called Voice, and check the row for errors:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ml_state 0        # INFO_ML_STATE: 0 or 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ml_timer 0        # INFO_ML_TIMER: 0 to 5</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ml_level 0        # INFO_ML_LEVEL: the last level sent</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ml_row 2          # the row to read</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ml_row_copy 2     # a copy, to detect an error</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ml_freq 0         # receives the stored frequency</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int ml_duty 0         # receives the stored duty</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_get Voice INFO_ML_STATE ml_state</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_get Voice INFO_ML_TIMER ml_timer      # 1 for D9 on a Nano</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_get Voice INFO_ML_LEVEL ml_level      # 2147483647 if no level since creation or act_halt</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_get Voice INFO_ML_TTABLE ml_row ml_freq ml_duty</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp ml_row != ml_row_copy</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>do_if Condexp</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>print_value ml_row                    # copy + 1: no table; copy + 2: no such row</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>enddo</tt> </span>

<b>Note</b>: For Servo actuators the '&lt;var_y&gt; &lt;var_z&gt;' arguments are never used.
<b>Note</b>: The &lt;info_ID&gt; of INFO_UNIT_TYPE is labelled as 'read only' because it can only be used with act_get but not act_set. However you can set this value on the server using the separate command act_unit (which see).
<b>Note</b>: Here is an extract from the parddefs.h file that describes the actual struct members and various actuator types. This includes struct members the above info items relate to (as well as struct members the PCS script writer does not have direct access to):
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Actuator Definition Structure for a Stepper Motor</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifdef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the Arduino:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  driver_type; // Type of driver used </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>TMC2209Stepper *tmc_driver; // Pointer to the TMC driver object </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  ena_state;   // The enabled status (one of the ENA_ #defines)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  motion_mode; // MM_STEALTHY or MM_PRECISE</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  turbo_boost; // 0 = off, 1 = transient on, &gt;1 = latched on</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  homed_to;    // Which limit switch the motor is currently homes to</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// (one of the LIM_ #defines except SOFT1 and SOFT2 )</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  dirn1;       // The direction value (1 or 0) that causes the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// motor to spin in the direction that causes motion</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// towards limit switch 1.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  dirn2;       // The direction value (1 or 0) that causes the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// motor to spin in the direction that causes motion</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// towards limit switch 2.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  currdirn;    // The current direction (one of the DIRN_ #defines)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  prevdirn;    // The last direction the motor moved in (DIRN_ #defs)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t msdenom;     // The microstep denominator (2,4,8,16,32,64,etc)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t fastmsd;     // The microstep denom. for the  fast phase of homing</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pulwidth;    // The number of microseconds for a pulse (high part)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t blsteps;     // The number of backlash steps to take when needed</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t tensteps1;   // The number of (de)tensioning steps to take in dirn1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t tensteps2;   // The number of (de)tensioning steps to take in dirn2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t onestep;     // The number of maxmsdenom steps that a single step in</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// the current msdenom is worth. This will be &gt;= 1.0 .</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t stepcount;   // The number of maxmsdenom steps from the homed pos.n</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t stallcount;  // The number of stall signals (for StallGuard use).</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t duralam;     // The duration (in microseconds) of the last motion.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t steplam;     // The number of steps taken in the last motion.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t sw_lim1;     // Stop if stepcount is &gt;= this software limit.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t sw_lim2;     // Stop if stepcount is &lt;= this software limit.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t  nhs0;       // (n)ull (h)ome (s)teps - initial dirn2 steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t  nhs1;       // (n)ull (h)ome (s)teps - dirn1 steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t  nhs2;       // (n)ull (h)ome (s)teps - final dirn2 steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t isd_hobl;    // The inter-step delay to use for homing and backlash</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  limstate;    // The current state of the limit switches (LIM_ #defs)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  sg_buffpc;   // Stall isd percentage buffer (&lt;100 deactivates SG)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  sg_thresh;   // The SGTHRS value for StallGuard4 (0 deactivates SG)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_ms1;     // Arduino pin to use for ms1 for non-TMC drivers</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_ms2;     // Arduino pin to use for ms2 for non-TMC drivers</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_ms3;     // Arduino pin to use for ms3 for non-TMC drivers</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_pul;     // Arduino pin to use for the PUL signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_dir;     // Arduino pin to use for the DIR signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_lim1;    // Arduino pin to use for the LIM_SWTC1 signal (analogue)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_lim2;    // Arduino pin to use for the LIM_SWTC2 signal (analogue)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_diag;    // Arduino pin to use for the LIM_STALL signal (analogue)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_ena;     // Arduino pin to use for the ENA signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_StepperNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifndef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the server PC:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  driver_type; // One of the SD_ #defines </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  homed_to;    // Which limit switch the motor is currently homes to</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// (one of the LIM_ #defines except SOFT1 and SOFT2 )</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  dirn1;       // The direction value (1 or 0) that causes the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// motor to spin in the direction that causes motion</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// towards limit switch 1.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  dirn2;       // The direction value (1 or 0) that causes the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// motor to spin in the direction that causes motion</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// towards limit switch 2.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  currdirn;    // The current direction (one of the DIRN_ #defines)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pulwidth;    // The number of microseconds for a pulse (high part)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t blsteps;     // The number of backlash steps to take when needed</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t tensteps1;   // The number of (de)tensioning steps to take in dirn1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t tensteps2;   // The number of (de)tensioning steps to take in dirn2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t maxblsteps;  // The backlash steps at maximum microstep denom</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t msdenom;     // The microstep denominator (2,4,8,16,32,64,etc)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t fastmsd;     // The microstep denom. for the  fast phase of homing</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t maxmsdenom;  // The maximum microstep denom</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t onestep;     // The number of maxmsdenom steps that a single step in</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// the current msdenom is worth. This will be &gt;= 1.0 .</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t stepcount;   // The number of maxmsdenom steps from the homed pos.n</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// the current msdenom is worth. This will be &gt;= 1.0 .</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>double   maxcalibri;  // The number of maxmsdenom steps in a calibrated unit.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t sw_lim1;     // Stop if stepcount is &gt;= this software limit.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t sw_lim2;     // Stop if stepcount is &lt;= this software limit.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t  nhs0;       // (n)ull (h)ome (s)teps - initial dirn2 steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t  nhs1;       // (n)ull (h)ome (s)teps - dirn1 steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t  nhs2;       // (n)ull (h)ome (s)teps - final dirn2 steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t isd_hobl;    // The inter-step delay to use for homing and backlash</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  limstate;    // The current state of the limit switches (LIM_ #defs)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_pul;     // Arduino pin to use for the PUL signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_dir;     // Arduino pin to use for the DIR signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_lim1;    // Arduino pin to use for the LIM_SWTC1 signal (analogue)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_lim2;    // Arduino pin to use for the LIM_SWTC2 signal (analogue)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_diag;    // Arduino pin to use for the LIM_STALL signal (analogue)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_ena;     // Arduino pin to use for the ENA signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_ms1;     // Arduino pin to use for ms1 for non-TMC drivers</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_ms2;     // Arduino pin to use for ms2 for non-TMC drivers</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_ms3;     // Arduino pin to use for ms3 for non-TMC drivers</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>char    *lsname[5];   // List of limit switch names ([0] is always "Unhomed")</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>char    *unitstr;     // Name of units motor is calibrated to.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>char     Name[32];    // Name of this actuator as supplied by the user.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  maturity;    // Must reach a certain value for the actuator to be</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// usable (starts off as 0 when the actuator is first</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// created.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_StepperNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Actuator Definition Structure for a Binary actuator</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifdef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the Arduino:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  bin_state;   // The binary status (one of the BIN_ #defines)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  on_val;      // The value (1 or 0) that, when written to the pin, </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// causes the actuator to be in the BIN_ON state.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  off_val;     // The value (1 or 0) that, when written to the pin, </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// causes the actuator to be in the BIN_OFF state.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_bin;     // Arduino pin to use for the bin signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_BinaryNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifndef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the server PC:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  on_val;      // The value (1 or 0) that, when written to the pin, </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// causes the actuator to be in the BIN_ON state.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  off_val;     // The value (1 or 0) that, when written to the pin, </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// causes the actuator to be in the BIN_OFF state.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_bin;     // Arduino pin to use for the bin signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>char     Name[32];    // Name of this actuator as supplied by the user.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  maturity;    // Must reach a certain value for the actuator to be</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// usable (starts off as 0 when the actuator is first</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// created.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_BinaryNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Actuator Definition Structure for an M-Level actuator</tt><tt>:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define MLEVEL_RESET INT32_MAX // Use INT not UINT because PCS user variables</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                               </tt><tt>// are always int32_t.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifdef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the Arduino:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  ml_type;    // The type of MLevel (one of the MLT_ #defines)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  ml_pin[8];  // Arduino pin(s) to use for the signal(s) (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  is_active;  // Safety interlock flag for emergency stop routines</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  ml_psc;     // Prescaler power 2 exponent for pin 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t ml_max;     // Maximum frequency allowed for pin 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  npins;      // Number of pins used (1 for PWM, up to 8 for Parallel)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  timer;      // What Timer the ml_pin[0] is using</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_MlevelNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifndef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the server PC:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint16_t duty;    // The duty cycle </tt><tt>mille</tt><tt> (0 to 100</tt><tt>0</tt><tt>), or a</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                     </tt><tt>// special-effect code (1001 to ML_DUTY_CODE_MAX)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t freq;    // Frequency (from lower to upper limit as per timer)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} MLevel_TTable;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  ml_type;     // The type of MLevel (one of the MLT_ #defines)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>MLevel_TTable *ml_tt; // To hold a dynamically created translation table</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t ntt;         // Number of elements in the translation table</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>size_t   sztt;        // Current byte size of the translation table</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  ml_pin[8];   // Arduino pin(s) to use for the signal(s) (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  ml_psc;      // Prescaler exponent to use with each pin in PWM mode.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>char     Name[32];    // Name of this actuator as supplied by the user.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t last_level;  // Caches the last level sent to avoid duplicate calls</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  npins;       // Tracks active pin count on the server</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pingaps;     // Are there gaps in the pin assignments? (0/1)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t max_level;   // Pre-calculated absolute maximum level ceiling</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  maturity;    // Must reach a certain value for the actuator to be</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// usable (starts off as 0 when the actuator is first</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>// created.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_MlevelNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Actuator Definition Structure for a Servo actuator</tt><tt>:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifdef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the Arduino:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_srv;     // Arduino pin to use for the servo signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_pos;     // Arduino analogue pin to use for reading the servo position</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  is_active;   // Safety interlock flag for emergency stop/halt routines</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  freq;        // Carrier frequency band</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint16_t current_us;  // Active pulse duration in microseconds </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint16_t srv_min;     // Position lower limit (microseconds, default 544)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint16_t srv_max;     // Position upper limit (microseconds, default 2400)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint16_t isd_min;     // Time to complete an atomic step move in microseconds</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint16_t isd_minp;    // isd_min rounded up to whole numbers of carrier periods</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint16_t tick_size;   // Minimum step change in position (depends on frequency band)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  timer;       // What Timer pin_srv is using (default = 0 i.e. unset)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_ServoNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifndef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the server PC:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_srv;     // Arduino pin to use for the servo signal (digital)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_pos;     // Arduino analogue pin to use for reading the servo position</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>char     Name[32];    // Name of this actuator as supplied by the user.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  maturity;    // High-level maturity factor metric</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  freq;        // Carrier frequency band</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t destn_us;    // Queued position (</tt><tt>shadow of the Arduino value</tt><tt>)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t srv_min;     // Position lower limit (microseconds, default 544)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t srv_max;     // Position upper limit (microseconds, default 2400)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint32_t isd_min;     // Time to complete an atomic step move in microseconds</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>double   theta_min;   // Minimum angle of servo (default 0)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>double   theta_max;   // Maximum angle of servo (default 180)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>double   calibri;     // Used to convert </tt><tt>between</tt><tt> angle </tt><tt>and</tt><tt> microseconds.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_ServoNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Actuator Definition Structure for a Sensor actuator</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifdef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the Arduino:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_sense;   // Arduino pin (analogue or digital) to use for reading</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  type;        // Whether it is analogue or digital</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_SensorNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#ifndef ARDUINO</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// This is the structure as used on the server PC:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>typedef struct {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  pin_sense;   // Arduino pin (analogue or digital) to use for reading</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  type;        // Whether it is analogue or digital</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>char     Name[32];    // Name of this actuator as supplied by the user.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>uint8_t  maturity;    // High-level maturity factor metric</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>} Act_SensorNode;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#endif</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
and here are some of the relevant #defined constants with their explanations (all of these are also included in stdpard.pch so you may use these constants in your PCS scripts if you include that file):
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// For Stepper actuators:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// The ENA values</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ENA_DISABLED 0  // Disabled, non-energised, coils</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ENA_ENABLED  1  // Enabled, energised, coils but not currently spinning</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ENA_SPINNING 2  // Motor is currently undergoing mot</tt><tt>i</tt><tt>on steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// The limit or limit switch (LIM_) identifier values. These are used to</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// identify which limit switch a motor is homed_to and also which limit or limit</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// switch is currently triggered in the limstate member:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_UNSET   0  // Not homed (or no limit switch triggered)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_SWTC1   1  // Physical switch 1 (triggered by moving in dirn1)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_SWTC2   2  // Physical switch 2 (triggered by moving in dirn2)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_NULL1   3  // Null-homed 1 (step count goes to 0 by moving in dirn1)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_NULL2   4  // Null-homed 2 (step count goes to 0 by moving in dirn2)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_SOFT1   5  // Software limit 1 activated (cannot be homed to)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_SOFT2   6  // Software limit 2 activated (cannot be homed to)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_ZERO    7  // Motor attempted to move to a negative step count. This</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// can happen if a limit switch is a bit noisy or has</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// some error or if there is some error in the axes such</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// that returning to a step count of zero does not</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// trigger the limit switch. Alternatively this can</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// happen in cases of null homing where there is no</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// physical end stop switch to trigger and step count is</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// all we have.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_STALL   8  // The StallGuard4 threshold was exceeded - likely stall.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// If a stall is detected then the Arduino sends back the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// the error code  to the user but that does not tell you</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// which motor triggered the stall event. However the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// Arduino also sets the limstate member of the motor</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// that triggered the stall to this LIM_STALL value. So</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// the user can do a 'cold read' of the limit state of</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// each motor in turn (using act_get with INFO_LIMSTATE)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                       </tt><tt>// to find out which motor triggered the stall.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Stepper Driver_type:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SD_UNDEF   0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SD_A4988   1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SD_DRV8825 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SD_TMC2209 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// For Binary actuators:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// The BIN values</tt><tt> for binary actuators</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define BIN_OFF 0    // Disabled, Off, state 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define BIN_ON  1    // Enabled, On, state 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define BIN_UNSET 3  // Unset / unknown</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// For M-Level actuators:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// The INFO_ML_TYPE values</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define MLT_PARALLEL 0    // Parallel digital pin actuator</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define MLT_PWM      1    // PWM actuator</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define MLT_UNSET    3    // Type unset/unknown</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// PWM Prescaler options (options for INFO_ML_PSC)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PWM_PSC_1     0  // Translates to:   1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PWM_PSC_8     3  // Translates to:   8</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PWM_PSC_32    5  // Translates to:  32</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PWM_PSC_64    6  // Translates to:  64</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PWM_PSC_128   7  // Translates to: 128</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PWM_PSC_256   8  // Translates to: 256</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PWM_PSC_1024  10 // Translates to:1024</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PWM_PSC_UNDEF 11</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Duty-cycle related codes</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ML_DUTY_MAX       1000  // plain PWM duty: 0 .. 1000 per mille</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ML_FX_BASE        1000  // duty = ML_FX_BASE * E + P</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ML_FX_NOISE       1     // E = 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ML_FX_TIMEDNOISE  2     // E = 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ML_FX_BURSTHISS   3     // E = 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ML_FX_GLIDE       4     // E = 4</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define ML_DUTY_CODE_MAX  4999  // largest valid duty value</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define FX_MAX_RATE                     16000UL</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define FX_BURST_MS                     10</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define FX_GLIDE_MIN_TOP                256</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define FX_ATTACK_MIN_MS                30</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define FX_PSC8_MIN_HZ                  7813UL</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>For Servo actuators:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Servo carrier frequency range bands</tt><tt> (options for  INFO_SRV_FREQ)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SRV_FREQ_MID50   1  // Target: ~50Hz  (Safe Range: 40Hz to 70Hz)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SRV_FREQ_MID200  2  // Target: ~200Hz (Safe Range: 150Hz to 250Hz)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SRV_FREQ_MID333  3  // Target: ~333Hz (Safe Range: 250Hz to 400Hz)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SERVO_RESET INT32_MAX  // </tt><tt>Initial position for server structs</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>For Senor actuators:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// The INFO_SEN_TYPE values</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SEN_DIGITAL  0    // Digital pin sensor</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SEN_ANALOGUE 1    // Analogue pin sensor</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define SEN_UNSET    3    // Type unset/unknown</tt> </span>

<b>Note</b>: You can supply all three receptor variables in cases where only one is needed. This will not generate an error, the other two variables will just be ignored.  However, you must supply the additional two variables in cases where the requested information item requires them or a syntax error will result.
<b>Note</b>: act_get is a <i>mandatum interruptivum</i>, a command that is allowed to interrupt other commands. Only some 'other commands' will look for and so act on <i>mandata interruptiva</i>. These include motion commands for steppers.
<b>Note</b>: For guidance on whether to use float or int type for any of the 'var' arguments for actuators of type Stepper or Servo, see the entry on the command act_unit. For reading the value of a Sensor actuator an integer will be retrieved so you should use an integer (or float) type for the receptor 'var' argument.
<b>Note</b>: You will be forbidden from using act_get to read info items specific to TMC2209 stepper drivers for stepper actuators that are not marked as using that driver. Those data items are as follows:
INFO_PIN_DIAG
INFO_TMCSGVAL
INFO_TMCSTATUS
INFO_TMCCHCONF
INFO_SG_BUFFPC
INFO_SG_THRESH
INFO_TURBO_BST
INFO_TMCMSCNT
<b>Note</b>: You will be forbidden for getting non-TMC driver specific info items from stepper actuators that are marked as using the TMC2209 driver. The data items for the non-TMC drivers (A4988 and DRV8825) are common to both of them and are as follows:
INFO_PIN_MS1
INFO_PIN_MS2
INFO_PIN_MS3
<b>Note</b>: When getting coordinates or distances using INFO_XYZCOORDS, INFO_STEPCOUNT, INFO_SW_LIM1 and  INFO_SW_LIM2, the value you will receive from the server using act_get will be in atomic units regardless of what you have set act_unit to be.
<b>Note</b>: For servos the difference between INFO_SRV_QPOS and INFO_SRV_SPOS is as follows:
INFO_SRV_QPOS - an act_get operation retrieves the destn_us position from the server side shadow struct (this is a shadow of the servo queue) and does not communicate with the Arduino. An act_set communicates the value to the Arduino servo motion queue. The value will also be synced to the local server destn_us struct member after act_set. This value is initialised to SERVO_RESET (= INT32_MAX) and is also set to that value when not used (i.e. the servo is not on the queue to be moved) or not set.
INFO_SRV_SPOS - an act_get operation retrieves the current_us position from the Arduino servo struct. An act_set communicates the value to the Arduino servo struct destn_us member directly (without moving the servo) and does not affect the motion queue.
<b>Note</b>: The INFO_SRV_SPOS option is provided so that you can alter the default servo position value or correct or update the position value of a servo. See act_set for more details.
<b>Note</b>: When getting a servo position (using INFO_SRV_QPOS or INFO_SRV_SPOS), the value you will receive from the server using act_get will be in microseconds regardless of what you have set act_unit to be. You may convert this to angular units using the servo's current calibri factor by means of the following formula:

    theta = theta_min + ((musec - srv_min) / calibri)

Where:
    * theta     = The target computed position in degrees.
    * musec     = The value returned by act_get using INFO_SRV_QPOS or SPOS.
    * srv_min   = The servo's srv_min value (INFO_SRV_MIN).
    * theta_min = The servo's theta_min value (INFO_SRV_THMIN).
    * calibri   = The servo's calibration factor (INFO_SRV_CALIB).
And here is an example implementation in PCS assuming 'LabServo' is an extant servo actuator on the system:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># =========================================================================</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># CONVERSION EXAMPLE: FETCHING SERVO ANGLES</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># =========================================================================</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var float current_raw_us 0.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var float s_min 544.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var float t_min 0.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var float s_calib 10.311111</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var float calculated_degrees 0.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># 1. Fetch the microsecond count straight from the </tt><tt>Arduino struct</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_get LabServo INFO_SRV_</tt><tt>S</tt><tt>POS current_raw_us</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># 2. Fetch the active structural limits and calibration slope</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_get LabServo INFO_SRV_MIN   s_min</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_get LabServo INFO_SRV_THMIN t_min</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_get LabServo INFO_SRV_CALIB s_calib</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># 3. Execute the Inverse Formula</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var calculated_degrees current_raw_us</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>sub_var calculated_degrees s_min            # (musec - srv_min)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>div_var calculated_degrees s_calib          # ((musec - srv_min) / calibri)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>add_var calculated_degrees t_min            # theta_min + ...</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Output the resolved angle</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>print_value calculated_degrees 2</tt> </span>
<b>Note</b>: For the INFO_TMCSTATUS option, which only applies to steppers, your supplied &lt;var&gt; will be assigned an int32_t cast of the original uint32_t status register but the interpreter's private internal global TMC_Status_Reg variable will also be updated to the unaltered uint32_t register value. You cannot access this TMC_Status_Reg variable directly via the script but you can access the status information items it contains by means of the tmc_status_parse command, which see. However, the sign of the signed value you receive in your &lt;var&gt; here provides you with one piece of status information - if it is positive it means the driver's standstill status flag is unset and if it is negative it means that the flag is set.
<b>Note</b>: For the INFO_TMCCHCONF option, which only applies to steppers, your supplied &lt;var&gt; will be assigned an int32_t cast of the original uint32_t chop conf register but the interpreter's internal private global TMC_ChopConf_Reg will also be updated to the unaltered uint32_t register value. You cannot access this TMC_ChopConf_Reg variable directly via the script but you can access the status information items it contains by means of the tmc_chconf_parse command, which see. However, the sign of the resulting value will reflect the state of the 'Short protection low side' feature in the register, with a positive value indicating this protection is ON and a negative value indicating this protection is OFF.
<b>Note</b>: Any call to get a data item that requires communication with the Arduino (marked as 'Ask Ardu' in the list above) can cause a delay or jitter in motion due to extending the inter-step delay (while the Arduino processes your request) and possibly even more of a time disruption will occur with those information requests that involve UART comms with the stepper driver (i.e. anything that begins with INFO_TMC). So avoid making such requests during sensitive motion actions.
<b>Note</b>: Setting TurboBoost mode with INFO_TURBO_BST to some non-zero number will mark that motor for using extra RMS ampage for its next movement command - whatever that command may be. The TurboBoost setting will take place immediately (the motor must be stationary and an enforced 100 ms delay without any steps will be imposed at the Arduino when making the change) so setting TurboBoost without motion will increase the holding current. After a motion command is executed for that motor the TurboBoost status will automatically be reset to 0 and current will return to normal if and only if the TurboBoost status was previously set to 1 (as opposed to any other non-zero number). If TurboBoost was set to 2 or more then that signals TurboBoost to latch on and so it will not reset to 0 until the PCS programmer deliberately sets it to 0 or until they set it to 1 thereby allowing it to automatically clear back down to 0 after the next motion command. The actual current values for TurboBoost and 'normal' (non-Turbo) current are set in #defines near the start of the Arduino source code. Typical values may be (for non-Turbo) 600 mA RMS during motion  and 300 mA at rest and for TurboBoost that will increase to 670 mA RMS during motion and 335 mA at rest but clearly you can change that via the Arduino sketch source code (parduino.ino). Leaving motors on TurboBoost at rest should only be done when necessary and you should manually set TurboBoost to 0 when you no longer need the stronger holding rest current. This is to avoid unnecessary heating of the motors and the drivers.
<b>Note</b>: For M-Level actuators, in the Arduino structure, is_active is the value of INFO_ML_STATE, timer that of INFO_ML_TIMER and ml_max that of INFO_ML_FREQ. In the server structure, last_level is the value of INFO_ML_LEVEL, and last holds the frequency and the duty last sent, which the server compares with the row selected by a new level (see the section on act_set).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_getid</span>
act_getid &lt;name_string&gt;
This will update the internal system variables CurrActIdx and CurrActType with the index and actuator type (respectively) of the actuator of name &lt;name_string&gt;. The CurrActType integer value will correspond to the actuator's type thus: 
1 = Stepper
2 = Binary
3 = Mlevel
4 = Servo
5 = Sensor
If there is no extant actuator with the supplied name, the values of CurrActIdx and CurrActType will both be set to -1. In this way you can test if an actuator of a certain name exists. See also act_getname.
&lt;name_string&gt; - A varval of type 'string' containing the actuator name you want to assess. 
<b>Note</b>: This works by searching the local (client side) database of currently extant actuators and does not communicate with the server. You should ensure, therefore, that the command get_actuators has been called at least once before using this command.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_getname</span>
act_getname &lt;act_type&gt; &lt;act_index&gt; &lt;string_variable&gt;
This gets the name of the actuator of type &lt;act_type&gt; and index number &lt;act_index&gt; and sets the string variable &lt;string_variable&gt; to that name.
&lt;act_type&gt; must be one of these integers or the command will give a run-time error:
1 = Stepper
2 = Binary
3 = Mlevel
4 = Servo
5 = Sensor
If there is no extant actuator with that type and index, the value of &lt;string_variable&gt; will be set to "null" (without the quotes). So, in this way you can test if an actuator exists. See also act_getid.
<b>Note</b>: This works by searching the local (client side) database of currently extant actuators and does not communicate with the server. You should ensure, therefore, that the command get_actuators has been called at least once before using this command.
<b>Note</b>: def_actuator and act_getname are the only two commands that require you to input two separate numbers to identify an actuator instead of the single &lt;act_id&gt; argument (described, for example, under undef_actuator)


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_halt</span>
act_halt &lt;act_id&gt;
Causes an actuator to stop what it is doing as soon as the act_halt instruction is received, even if the actuator has not finished its task. This has no effect for binary actuators.
&lt;act_id&gt; - See undef_actuator for a full description of this.
<b>Note</b>: act_halt is a <i>mandatum interruptivum</i>, a command that is allowed to interrupt other commands.  Only some 'other commands' will look for and so act on <i>mandata</i><i> </i><i>interruptiva</i>. These include motion commands for steppers.
<b>Note</b>: act_halt is not applicable to Sensor actuators.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_home</span>
act_home &lt;act_id&gt; &lt;home_type&gt;
Home the actuator defined by &lt;act_id&gt; in a way defined by &lt;home_type&gt;
&lt;act_id&gt; - See undef_actuator for a full description of this.
&lt;home_type&gt; - an integer which corresponds to one of the following:
If the actuator is of type stepper these integers are potentially valid:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_SWTC1  1 // Home to limit switch 1 (triggered by moving in dirn1)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_SWTC2  2 // Home to limit switch 2 (triggered by moving in dirn2)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_NULL1 </tt><tt> </tt><tt>3 // Null-homed 1 (step count goes to 0 by moving in dirn1)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define LIM_NULL2 </tt><tt> </tt><tt>4 // Null-homed 2 (step count goes to 0 by moving in dirn2)</tt> </span>
I say 'potentially' valid because any of them may be invalid if, for example, the stepper structure has not been set up for null homing or with a limit switch pin set to an Arduino pin.
You cannot home to a software limit.
An actuator of type stepper cannot be moved with any other command until it is homed.
When you select one of the two null homing types, your choice of LIM_NULL1 or LIM_NULL2 will not necessarily be what ends up as the value of the homed_to member of the motor structure. All these options do at this stage is tell the server to initiate a null homing procedure. What ultimately determines whether the motor is homed_to LIM_NULL1 or LIM_NULL2 is your choice of the nhs0, nhs1 and nhs2 step counts as set using the act_set command (if the homing procedure continues to completion) because the last direction the motor moves in when homing ends, the homed_to value will be the opposite of that direction (if the last direction was dirn1 then the motor will be homed_to=LIM_NULL2 and <i>vice versa</i>). 
If the homing procedure is halted before completion homed_to will be LIM_UNSET (= un-homed).
The reason the null homing switch number is opposite to the last moved-in direction is to make the terminology of null homing 'limits' analogous to the terminology used for physical end stops - LIM_SWTC1 and LIM_SWTC2. So, for example, when homed to physical end-stop LIM_SWTC2 it means that this is the switch that will get triggered when the motor moves enough steps in dirn2. So moving <i>away from</i> LIM_SWTC2 causes step count to rise. Likewise, in null homing where the last moved-in direction is dirn1, it will be like homing to a virtual end stop switch 2 where moving away from this switch (i.e. in dirn1) will cause the step count to rise and moving towards the switch (in dirn2) will cause the step count to decrease. 
Only actuators of a type that can be homed will be accepted by this command. If the &lt;act_id&gt; specifies any other type then the command will return an immediate error.
The command will return OK if the server finds no problem with the arguments and the Arduino does not have a problem with the arguments. This means the Arduino has 'agreed' to attempt the homing procedure and will get on with it but the Arduino returns immediately after the command passes its tests and does not wait for the homing procedure to start or finish. If an error occurs during homing the 'PARD move report' (PMR) that you can check with the command act_movereport (which see) may contained information it its error code that may help you understand the reason for failure. 
You, the PCS script writer, can check for success or failure of this command by doing an act_get command on the same actuator's &lt;homed_to&gt; member. If you make this request before homing completes you could get a 'busy' response. Otherwise if the &lt;homed_to&gt; member equals the value you set as &lt;home_type&gt; it means the homing completed OK. Any other value suggests an error occurred.
<b>Note</b>: Because the Arduino returns control back to the user immediately this avoids lengthy delays in comms (that might cause a timeout error) and also allows the user to do other things - like generating a live preview - while the homing action is taking place thereby allowing for visual feedback assessment of the homing procedure which can give another means to know if something went wrong and when the procedure has ended.
<b>Note</b>: For null-homing no physical limit switch will be checked during the homing procedure and no physical limit switch will be checked for any subsequent motion unless you have also set a pin for a physical end stop. If you have not set any physical end stop pin then it is entirely the user's responsibility to take measures to avoid those physical limits being hit and potentially damaging the hardware.
<b>Note</b>: Homing will set the currdirn value on the breakout board. Until that is done the currdirn may show as UNSET when attempts are made to act_get it.
<b>Note</b>: Immediately after successful homing of any type, the step count will be reset to 0 and you can only move in the direction away from the limit switch you homed to (you can't move to a negative step count). For null homing this is also true. for example, if you have null homed to LIM_NULL1 this means that motion in dirn2 increases your step count from 0 and motion in dirn1 decreases your step count but if you try to move below a step count of 0 this will be treated as if a physical limit switch was triggered (and limstate will take the value LIM_ZERO to indicate this). However, in the case of a null homed motor where the is no physical limit stopping you, you may want to move in this 'negative' direction (e.g. to operate a filter wheel that may rotate in any direction without a physical stop or to operate a pump with similar lack of restriction). To enable this, just issue an act_set command after homing to set the current step count to some (usually large) positive number. Then you will be able to move in any direction until the step count reaches zero (or until a physical limit switch is triggered in cases where you have one setup for that direction (set up both in terms of physical hardware and stepper actuator configuration with act_set commands).
<b>Note</b>: Even with physical limit switches you may get a halt triggered by step count reaching zero (the homed position) even if the physical limit switch does not trigger at this point due to some error in motion (stalls or other error) or simply due to some error/variation in the triggering position of some limit switches. In that case you can tell whether this has occurred simply because you reached a step count of zero without triggering the switch (limstate will show LIM_ZERO) compared to whether the switch was actually triggered (limstate will show LIM_SWTC1 or LIM_SWTC2) by checking the limstate value with act_get. In the case of a LIM_ZERO halt you should be able to continue to move the motor in the forbidden direction by setting the step count to some positive number and carry on moving in that direction but this will only work till the actual physical limit switch triggers - a triggered physical limit switch will override all attempts at motion regardless of step count.
<b>Note</b>: For stepper motors you can check when the motion is complete in three main ways. The first is the preferred way because it does not interrupt the Arduino while the motor is moving and so cause potential slight disruptions to the regularity of the inter-step delay and this is by using the act_movereport command (which see). If that method is not available for some specific reason then another way is by doing an act_get for the stepper for &lt;info_ID&gt; = 1 (INFO_ENA_STATE). The ena_state will be 2 when the motor is spinning and 1 when at rest. You could alternatively do an act_get for &lt;info_ID&gt; = 2 (INFO_HOMED_TO). The difference between the latter two methods is that the homed_to state will only change from un-homed to homed (to the appropriate limit) if the motion completes successfully but ENA state will change from 2 to 1 when the motion ends even if, for some reason, it ended prematurely such that the homing could not be complete. This may occur if an act_halt command stopped it before it could finish (for example).
<b>Note</b>: When homing starts, the homed_to member of the motor structure is set to LIM_UNSET (to indicate that it is un-homed) so that if homing is interrupted or fails this will be the resulting state of the motor.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_moveby</span>
act_moveby &lt;act_id&gt; &lt;duration&gt; &lt;distance&gt; [&lt;dist_y&gt; [dist_z]]
This moves the actuator identified by &lt;act_id&gt; by a certain distance from its current position in its current direction (current direction can be set using the act_set command, which see) and attempts to do this motion over a time of &lt;duration&gt; microseconds.
&lt;act_id&gt; - See undef_actuator for a full description of this. In addition to the options described there, this command may also accept an &lt;act_id&gt; of StageXY or StageXYZ (case sensitive) which will attempt motion of 2 (X and Y) or 3 (X, Y and Z) axes of the stage. This means stepper actuators of ID 0 for X, 1 for Y and 2 for Z. In the case of &lt;act_id&gt; = StageXY you must supply one more argument, &lt;dist_y&gt;, which will apply to the Y axis motion (the X axis motion being governed by the &lt;distance&gt; argument). If you supply &lt;act_id&gt; = StageXYZ then you must supply the [dist_z] argument in addition to &lt;distance&gt; and &lt;dist_y&gt;. The motion will be done using the multidimensional Bressenham line algorithm to effect simultaneous coordinated motion for 2 or 3 motors. If you only want to move the X and Z motors then use &lt;act_id&gt; = StageXYZ and set the &lt;dist_y&gt; value as 0. You can use these StageXY and StageXYZ options for single motor activation (if all but 1 of the distance arguments are 0). In that case the PCS script interpreter will simply convert your multi-motor move command into a single motor move command in the program (because there are less overheads in doing single motor movements because they do not need Bressenham calculations). Also you can use these StageXY and StageXYZ options for no motion at all (all the distance arguments are 0) - the script will just do no motion in that case.
&lt;duration&gt; - an integer which may be 0 or positive. If 0 it means you want the duration calculated automatically based on your choice of &lt;isd_min&gt; (from the act_accel command) and the total number of steps the movement will require (which will be calculated over on the server from your choice of microstep denominator and &lt;distance&gt; and &lt;unit&gt; (from the act_unit command). If it is positive then it represents the number of microseconds you want the whole motion to take. Your choice here may cause an acceleration curve to be impossible to satisfy resulting in failed calculation falling back to the default uniform motion - so use this manual option for &lt;duration&gt; with care.
&lt;distance&gt; - a float-capable value &gt;= 0.0 specifying the number of distance units to move.
&lt;dist_y&gt; - a float-capable value &gt;= 0.0 specifying the number of distance units to move for the stage Y axis motor when &lt;act_id&gt; is StageXY or StageXYZ.
[dist_z] - a float-capable value &gt;= 0.0 specifying the number of distance units to move for the stage Z axis motor when &lt;act_id&gt; is StageXYZ.
<b>Note</b>: All distances are expected to be supplied in either calibrated units (if you set act_unit to &gt; 0 for steppers) or in atomic steps (i.e. 1/maxmsdenom microsteps). No negative values are allowed. Because a stepper motor can only travel in steps of a minimum of 1/msdenom microsteps, this means that whatever values you supply here for distances and positions will be rounded to the nearest <b><i>onestep</i></b> value (which is calculated internally in the server program using the formula onestep =  maxmsdenom / msdenom). For example, if you set one of the distance arguments to 15 when maxmsdenom in 256 and msdenom is 8 (giving a onestep value of 256/8 = 32) then this will result in an effective distance of 0 and the motor will not move at all because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (15.0 / 32.0)) = 0. However if you set a distance argument = 16 then the effective value will be 1 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (16.0 / 32.0)) = 1 and your motor will travel 1 msdenom step meaning the step count will increment or decrement by 32 atomic units or less. I say 'or less' for the potential cases where you have been moving around with different msdenom settings such that, for example, if your current atomic step count is 10 and your act_moveby command results in a request to move back by 32 atomic steps, no movement will actually occur but the step count will be set to 0. Such 'drift' should not be possible if you are careful to observe msdenom and distance discipline when operating at different msdenom settings in one login session.
<b>Note</b>: In order for this command to be processed you must have, at least once during the current log in session, made a call to act_accel for the type of actuator you want to move with this command. Failure to do this will result in the command being denied with an error (the script will terminate or fail to check out OK).
<b>Note</b>: The movement you requested may not be fulfilled exactly as you requested. For example a limit might be reached (either a software limit or a physical end-stop) that terminates the motion before the full &lt;distance&gt; is achieved or the &lt;duration&gt; you specified might not be possible for some reason and the movement may take longer (or shorter if a limit is triggered). Thus you might want to get information on the actual motion, for example so you can make corrections in future motions (like decrease &lt;duration&gt; in any future motion requests to try to make up for motions that took longer than expected). You can do this as follows:
To find out the actual steps taken you can use act_get to get the step count before and after the motion.
To get the duration you can use act_get to read the 'INFO_DURALAM' member of the actuator structure which stores the last know duration for the last movement it made (apart from homing).
Just as with homing, the command will return just prior to actually beginning the motion so you can tell when the motion has ended, e.g., by using the act_movereport command or querying the ena_state with act_get (it will be 2 while in motion and 1 when at rest).
<b>Note</b>: If act_moveby is called with a &lt;distance&gt; (or one of the other distance arguments) value of 0 then a 'dummy' PMR is set up at the server side to respond to any call to act_movereport. This dummy PMR will just send back all the PMRep variable values as they were last (without changing their values) and also the actuator's duralam and steplam members will not have changed from when the actuator last moved so will not be reliable in those circumstances. In the case of &lt;act_id&gt; = StageXY, this dummy PMR situation will only occur when both the &lt;distance&gt; and &lt;dist_y&gt; values are 0. In the case of &lt;act_id&gt; = StageXYZ, this dummy PMR situation will only occur when all three of &lt;distance&gt;, &lt;dist_y&gt; and [dist_z] values are 0.
<b>Note</b>: When doing multi-stage motor movements (&lt;act_id&gt; = StageXY or StageXYZ) and 1 or more of the distance values are 0 - and especially if all but 1 of the distance values are 0 - this may result in different times for motion to complete if you are running speeds that are near the limit of capacity of the breakout board. This is because the breakout board might be able to honour your choice of minimum ISD if only 1 motor is being activated but might not be able to do so and so will effect a longer minimum ISD than you chose or than was possible with only 1 motor activated.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_movereport</span>
act_movereport &lt;int_result&gt; &lt;actype&gt; &lt;actid&gt;
This listens on the incoming serial port of the server for a PARDUS move report (PMR) but it does not wait for one. If a PMR is present when it looks it will do the this:
set the value of &lt;int_result&gt; to 1
set the value of &lt;act_type&gt; to the type of actuator the PMR pertains to
set the value of &lt;act_index&gt; to specific actuator the PMR pertains to
set the system variables PMRep1, PMRep2, PMRep3 and PMRep4 to values relevant to a report for that actuator type and the specific actuator.
terminate the script with an error message if the PMR conveys that an error has occurred. The error message conveyed to the client via the server will contain information about the nature of the error as provided in the PMR.
In the case that the last movement was that of a stepper motor, these values will contain the current step counts of the X, Y, Z motors (in PMRep1, PMRep2 and PMRep3 respectively) if any of those motors are defined, plus the step count of the most recently activated motor (in PMRep4) if that motor was defined and if it was the motor whose motion ending triggered the PMR. If any of those motors are not defined, the respective PMR value will be 0.
All step counts conveyed by the PMRep variables will be in either calibrated units or atomic (1/maxmsdenom) microsteps depending on your selection for act_unit.
If the last motion (the motion whose ending triggered the PMR) was by a different type of actuator, the values will have meanings relevant to that actuator.
If a PMR is not present when it looks it will set the value of &lt;int_result&gt; to 0 and will not alter the values of &lt;actype&gt;, &lt;actid&gt; or the PMR system variables.
&lt;int_result&gt; - a user-defined variable capable of accepting an integer result.
&lt;actype&gt; - a user-defined variable capable of accepting an integer result.
&lt;actid&gt;  - a user-defined variable capable of accepting an integer result.
<b>Note</b>: Once a PMR is read by act_movereport, it is effectively 'used-up' and so it no longer exists. Subsequent calls to act_movereport will return 0 (unless a new PMR is pushed onto the serial port from the Arduino by another actuator completing its motion).
<b>Note</b>: Any command that sends an order to the Arduino will clear the incoming buffer as part of the order send communications system. So, if there was an unread PMR waiting there, it will be cleared (i.e. flushed and lost) and not stored anywhere. You will have lost the opportunity to read it with act_movereport. You may still use other methods to check for end of motion like sending an act_get order to look for the current ena_state of a stepper motor, for example.
<b>Note</b>: Because several motion orders, like act_moveby, do not wait for the motion to complete but return as soon as the motion order is accepted by the Arduino, there needs to be some way for the script writer to know when a motion has completed so they do not send non-<i>interruptivum</i> commands to the Arduino during the motion and so trigger a 'Busy' error that will terminate the script. One way to find out this information is to periodically do an act_get command to look for the ena_state of a stepper motor (for example) but this necessitates a to-and-fro communication with the Arduino while the motion is potentially still being carried out and this can briefly interrupt the motion making it un-smooth. It is for this reason that this alternative act_movereport method is available because it does not require the Arduino to interrupt its current operation to receive its instruction as an order, interpret it, process it and send a report before getting back to whatever it was doing - as it must do for mandata interruptiva like act_get. Instead, act_movereport passively checks the incoming serial buffer on the server PC. The Arduino is programmed to send a PMR at the end of any motion command so this does not interfere with the motion while it is happening. By periodically calling act_movereport therefore you do not interrupt the Arduino but you still get to find out when a motion command completes - just as if you had periodically called act_get for ena_state but without the downsides of that.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_moveto</span>
act_moveto &lt;act_id&gt; [arguments as per actuator type]
This moves a motion actuator (that is, a stepper or a servo) actuator to a specific position.

For use with steppers the command structure is this:
act_moveto &lt;act_id&gt; &lt;duration&gt; &lt;position&gt; [&lt;posn_y&gt; [posn_z]]
&lt;act_id&gt;   - see act_moveby for a full description of this.
&lt;duration&gt; - an integer which may be 0 or positive. See act_moveby for more.
&lt;position&gt; - a float-capable value that represents some point along the stroke of the actuator. The units are whatever you set the units to be using the act_unit command (which see). 
&lt;posn_y&gt; - a float-capable value &gt;= 0.0, as for &lt;position&gt; but pertaining to the Y axis when &lt;act_id&gt; is StageXY or StageXYZ.
[posn_z] - a float-capable value &gt;= 0.0, as for &lt;position&gt; but pertaining to the Z axis when &lt;act_id&gt; is StageXYZ.

For use with servos the command structure is this:
act_moveto &lt;act_id&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
&lt;act_id&gt;   - see act_moveby for a full description of this. For this to be valid you must supply the id of an extant individual servo but only actuator type (not the specific actuator id) will be used.
&lt;varval&gt; - a binary choice varval. If the value of &lt;varval&gt; evaluates to a non-zero numerical value or to TRUE or ON then all the servos on the current queue will be moved to their destinations according to the current motion profile supplied by your last call to act_accel for servos. Otherwise the current servo queue will be completely wiped and no motion will be done.

<b><i>Notes for Servos</i></b>
<b>Note</b>: This is an 'all actuator' trigger - either all servo actuators on the queue are moved or all servo actuators on the queue are removed from the queue. This is why only the actuator type is important for the &lt;act_id&gt; argument.
<b>Note</b>: The entire movement will be done in approximately the duration and acceleration profile you set with act_accel if the range of motion and native speed of the servos will allow it. If even one of the servos on the list will permit that motion profile then that motion profile will be attempted. Any servos on the queue that cannot be moved with that profile (because, for example, the duration is too short for their natural speed) then they will be moved at their natural speed while other servos are moved with the motion profile.
<b>Note</b>: If none of the servos on the list can be moved with the motion profile set (or if the duration was set to 0 with act_accel) then they will all move with their natural speed and the servos will be triggered in a tight loop only a few microseconds apart so they will appear to all start their motion simultaneously. They may not end simultaneously, however, due to the fact they all run at their own speed and may have different distances to travel.
<b>Note</b>: If more than one servo can be paced at the slower speed dictated by the act_accel duration parameter, they will all move in a coordinated fashion using the Bresenham algorithm so that they will all start together and finish together even if they each move through different angles.
<b>Note</b>: If the queue contains some servos that can travel at the slower speed dictated by the act_accel duration parameter and some that cannot, those servos that cannot be speed-limited will trigger their single intermediate small step right at the fractional mid-point tick of the Bresenham sequence. At the end of the Bresenham sequence they will then be sent directly to their destination positions at their native speed. Consequently, you will observe a mixture of Bresenham coordinated motions and, at the end, fast native destination snaps.

<b><i>Notes for Steppers</i></b>
<b>Note</b>: All position units are expected to be supplied in either calibrated units (if you set act_unit to &gt;0 for steppers) or in atomic steps - that is, in (1/maxmsdenom microsteps). Positions are measured from 0 (= the homed position) and no negative values are allowed. Because a stepper motor can only travel in steps of a minimum of 1/msdenom microsteps, this means that whatever values you supply will be rounded to the nearest <i>onestep</i> value (see page 97 for the meaning of onestep). For example, if you set &lt;position&gt; to 15 (assuming we are using atomic steps and not calibrated units) and the motor is currently at position 30, then the difference between current position (30) and desired destination (15) will be calculated as 15 atomic steps. However, when maxmsdenom is 256 and msdenom is 8 (giving a onestep value of 256/8 = 32) this 15 atomic steps will result in an effective motion of 0 (no move at all) because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (15.0 / 32.0)) = 0.
However if you set &lt;position&gt; = 14 making the difference between current position (30) and destination (14) = 16 then the effective value will be 1 step in the current 1/8 step size (= 32 atomic steps) because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (16.0 / 32.0)) = 1. The motor will therefore try to move 32 atomic steps back towards home but it will not actually move because this would take it past 0. This is because the Arduino code has this check in it prior to taking any step:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>if(Stepperlist[id]-&gt;stepcount &lt; Stepperlist[id]-&gt;onestep){</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>       </tt><tt>Stepperlist[id]-&gt;stepcount = 0.0;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>       </tt><tt>Stepperlist[id]-&gt;limstate=LIM_ZERO;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>       </tt><tt>return 1;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>} else Stepperlist[id]-&gt;stepcount-=Stepperlist[id]-&gt;onestep;   </tt><tt> </tt> </span>
So the step counter will be set to 0 (even though it was 30 and even though no motion will be done) and the step function will return a 1 (usually triggering an 'end of motion' signal). This scenario is unusual and should only occur if you switch microstep size to a different value during some motion session. This is because you could never get a stepper position of 15 if you only ever moved in 1/8 steps (in the case of 1/8 steps your motor position will be incremented in units of 'onestep' = 32). 
<b>Note</b>: In order for this command to be processed you must have, at least once during the current log-in session, made a call to act_accel for the type of actuator you want to move with this command. Failure to do this will result in the command being denied with an error (the script will terminate or fail to check out OK).
<b>Note</b>: This command works by making a calculation on the server of the difference between the actuator's current position and the &lt;position&gt; you supply here. From this difference it works out the absolute distance the actuator has to travel and in what direction. It sends a command to the Arduino to set the direction and then sends another command to do the motion - that command literally being an act_moveby command. Thus act_moveto ultimately boils down to an act_moveby command so see act_moveby for further details.
<b>Note</b>: Whether actuator succeeds at moving to the position you specify will depend on whether it meets any limits (like software limits or limit switches) during the motion and whether the motion is halted by an act_halt command. So you should not assume the actuator has gone all the way and you should check the actuator's position at the end of the motion using an act_get command.
<b>Note</b>: The Arduino will not wait for the motion to complete before returning control to the server (and hence to the PCS script). It will return control as soon as a valid move command has been verified and before the motion begins. So as for how to know when the motion is complete, use the act_movereport command (preferably) or do a act_get for things like step count or ena_status (although performing act_get commands while the actuator is in motion might cause slight interruption or jitter to the motion which is why act_movereport is preferred). 
<b>Note</b>: If the act_moveto is repeated to the same position (i.e. there are no steps to take because the actuator is already there) then a 'dummy' PMR is set up at the server side to respond to any call to act_movereport. This dummy PMR will just send back all the PMRep variable values as they were last (without changing their values) and also the actuator's duralam and steplam members will not have changed from when the actuator last moved so will not be reliable in those circumstances. In the case of &lt;act_id&gt; = StageXY, this dummy PMR situation will only occur when both the &lt;position&gt; and &lt;posn_y&gt; values result in 0 steps of movement. In the case of &lt;act_id&gt; = StageXYZ, this dummy PMR situation will only occur when all three &lt;position&gt;, &lt;posn_y&gt; and [posn_z] result in no movement.
<b>Note</b>: When doing multi-stage motor movements (&lt;act_id&gt; = StageXY or StageXYZ) and 1 or more of the position values result in no movement - and especially if all but 1 of the distance values result in no movement - this may result in different times for motion to complete if you are running speeds that are near the limit of capacity of the breakout board. This is because the breakout board (Arduino) might be able to honour your choice of minimum ISD if only 1 motor is being activated but might not be able to do so and so will effect a longer minimum ISD than you chose or than was possible with only 1 motor activated.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_set</span>
act_set &lt;act_id&gt; &lt;info_ID&gt; &lt;varval&gt; [&lt;varval_y&gt; &lt;varval_z&gt;]
This sets the value of the &lt;info_ID&gt; struct member of the actuator defined by &lt;act_id&gt;  to be equal to the value of the variable or constant value &lt;varval&gt;.
&lt;act_id&gt; - See undef_actuator for a full description of this.
&lt;info_ID&gt; - an integer corresponding to a writeable member of that actuator’s struct and the value of that member must be compatible with the type of value conveyed by &lt;varval&gt; (string, float or int). Integer values for &lt;info_ID&gt; that can be used for a stepper actuator are all those listed in the section on act_get (which see) with the exception of those labelled 'Read only'.

<b><i>Notes for using act_set with Stepper actuators</i></b>
<b>Note:</b> Once a stepper is created with def_actuator, its default driver type is SD_UNDEF. You will not be allowed to set any property of a stepper actuator until this is changed to one of the defined driver types using act_set with INFO_SD_TYPE. Once the driver type is set, it cannot be changed (you will have to undefine the actuator and create it anew if you want a stepper actuator with the same name to have a different driver type).
<b>Note</b>: The driver type will determine what features of the stepper actuator you can read (with act_get) or write (with act_set). Any time in this manual where driver-specific features are mentioned, it must be understood that those features will only be in effect if your stepper actuator is set to use that driver..
<b>Note</b>: Physical limit switches may only be associated with analogue pins on the  Arduino. You cannot specify 'A' here in any of the varval arguments, like A0 or A4. Instead just use the integer value without the 'A' - it will automatically be treated as if it has an 'A' in front of it. The fact that you are setting  the value for a limit switch is what tells the program to interpret this as an analogue pin number. When setting pins for any other purpose the program will interpret the number you give to be be for a digital Arduino pin. Again, do not use 'D', just the integer value and note that values 0 and 1 will be forbidden for digital pins (and so result in an error) because D0 and D1 are reserved for the USB communications that allow the pardserver to communicate with the Arduino. For the limit switch analogue pins you may use 0 and 1 without problem.
<b>Note</b>: When setting the names of limit switches (i.e. using info items like INFO_NAME_LS1) the name you set is limited to 31 printable bytes. Longer names will just have the first 31 printable bytes copied over and the rest discarded. Remember that these are bytes - not logical characters (Unicode UTF-8 characters count as 3 bytes each if you use UTF-8). 
<b>Note</b>: If you want to change the identity of the Arduino pin that an actuator pin member is currently associated with, (e.g. a limit switch pin on a stepper) then you must do this in two act_set commands. In the first one you must pass the value 255 (this causes the pin to be 'unset'). Then you will be allowed to set the new pin by making another call to act_set to assign your new Arduino pin to that actuator pin. You can't just change from one Arduino pin to another in a single act_set command. 
<b>Note</b>: Be aware that this command may change more than just the member you requested it to change. For example, if you use act_set to set a limit switch pin to 'unset' this command will reassess the homed_to member value and, if it was set to be homed to that limit switch, it will cause the homed_to member to be unset also so you will need to re-home the actuator before you can move it again. Likewise if you use act_set to alter a software limit or the current step count of one or more steppers then the limitstate member will be reassessed and compared to the current step count to see if it needs to change the limstate member and so you might not be able to move the actuator in the direction of the new software limit (these software limit checks only occur if limstate is not currently set to one of the hardware limit switches because they take priority over software limits).  Also the  backlash steps are recalculated automatically whenever you change the microstep denominator (msdenom), or the maximum values pertaining to those things (maxmsdenom, maxblsteps). This is why the current backlash steps is read-only to the PCS script writer and should not be set manually by altering the C source code of the interpreter.
<b>Note</b>: You can supply the three varval arguments in cases where only one is needed. This will not generate an error, the other two variables will just be ignored. However, you must supply the additional two varval arguments in cases where the requested information item requires them or a syntax error will result.
<b>Note</b>: For guidance on whether to use float or int varval arguments, see the entry on the command act_unit.
<b>Note</b>: The following will be forbidden:
setting maxmsdenom to &lt; 1 (i.e. 0),
setting msdenom to anything if maxmsdenom is &lt;= 1
setting msdenom to be greater than maxmsdenom.
setting maxcalibri to &lt;1.0e-3
unless specifically stated to be admissible, setting any value negative.
<b>Note</b>: The following only pertains to using TMC2209 drivers. Setting TurboBoost mode with INFO_TURBO_BST to some non-zero number will mark that motor for using extra RMS ampage for its next movement command - whatever that command may be. The TurboBoost setting will take place immediately (the motor must be stationary and an enforced 100 ms delay without any steps will be imposed at the Arduino when making the change) so setting TurboBoost without motion will increase the holding current. After a motion command is executed for that motor the TurboBoost status will automatically be reset to 0 and current will return to normal if and only if the TurboBoost status was previously set to 1 (as opposed to any other non-zero number). If TurboBoost was set to 2 or more then that signals TurboBoost to latch on and so it will not reset to 0 until the PCS programmer deliberately sets it to 0 or until they set it to 1 thereby allowing it to automatically clear back down to 0 after the next motion command. The actual current values for TurboBoost and 'normal' (non-Turbo) current are set in #defines near the start of the Arduino source code. Typical values may be (for non-Turbo) 600 mA RMS during motion  and 300 mA at rest and for TurboBoost that will increase to 670 mA RMS during motion and 335 mA at rest but clearly you can change that via the Arduino sketch source code (parduino.ino). Leaving motors on TurboBoost at rest should only be done when necessary and you should manually set TurboBoost to 0 when you no longer need the stronger holding rest current. This is to avoid unnecessary heating of the motors and the drivers.

<b><i>Notes for using act_set with M-Level actuators</i></b>
<b>Note</b>: When you create a new M-Level actuator the only item you will be allowed to set first is its type, with the <i>INFO_ML_TYPE</i> info item. Once the type is set it cannot be changed; to use a different type you must undefine the actuator, define it again and set the other type.
<b>Note</b>: The bare minimum for maturity of any type of M-Level actuator is that pin 0 (<i>INFO_PIN_P0</i>) is defined. For a parallel-type actuator this is sufficient. A PWM-type actuator also needs a translation table with at least one row. Freeing p0 makes the actuator immature again; its translation table is kept.
<b>Note</b>: A PWM M-Level actuator operates on one pin only, p0, and you will be forbidden to set any other pin for it. The server refuses such a request (“It is forbidden to set any pin other than P0 for a PWM M-Level actuator”); the firmware refuses it as well, with “Pin number out of range”, should it ever reach the Arduino.
<b>Note</b>: To change the Arduino pin of an M-Level pin, you may use the two-step approach (first free the pin (act_set with <i>FREE_PIN</i>) and then assign the new pin) or you may change it in one act_set instead - in which case the change is carried out and the old pin is released first (its output stops and it becomes a free, high-impedance input). In either case the new pin is LOW until the next level order that reaches the Arduino; because the server does not resend a level equal to the last one sent, send a different level first, or use act_halt. For a PWM M-Level that is active, a direct change to another pin on the same timer is refused with “Read only”: halt it first. (See also “Changing the pin of an M-Level” in the section on actuator types.)
<b>Note</b>: Freeing a pin of a parallel M-Level (act_set with <i>FREE_PIN</i>) reduces the number of its pins, and therefore its highest level, at once: with p0 to p3 assigned the levels are 0 to 15, and after p3 is freed they are 0 to 7. The freed pin is released (written LOW, then made a high-impedance input) and becomes available to other actuators; the remaining pins keep their states, and act_halt still clamps them all LOW. Free pins from the highest downwards: freeing a pin below the highest assigned one leaves a gap in the sequence, and the server then refuses every level order for that actuator (with the message “… because there are gaps in its pin definition sequence.”) until the gap is filled again.
<b>Note</b>: Freeing p0 of a PWM M-Level (act_set with <i>FREE_PIN</i>) stops its output and any effect on its timer, releases the pin (written LOW, then made a high-impedance input with its pull-up off), and sets <i>INFO_ML_TIMER</i> to 0. The server then refuses its levels as immature until p0 is assigned again; the translation table and the pre-scaler are kept.
<b>Note</b>: For a PWM actuator, the range of frequencies available and how finely frequency and duty cycle can be chosen depend on the timer that drives p0 and on the pre-scaler (Table AS.1, and AS.3 to AS.6 below). The pre-scaler is set in the timer when p0 is assigned, and again with every noise and every plain level that the actuator sets at once (not by a glide or a clean onset), unless a servo shares the timer. So if you want a pre-scaler other than the default (<i>PWM_PSC_8</i>), you must set it with <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> before you assign p0 (or after explicitly freeing p0). An <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> order for an actuator whose p0 is assigned is refused, by the server (“You can only set the prescaler for pin 0 if it is unset.”) and, should it ever reach the Arduino, by the firmware (“Read only”). A value that is not one of the seven <i>PWM_PSC_</i> codes is refused by the server with “You are attempting to set a pre-scaler to a forbidden value (you chose <i>n</i>).”. The server accepts <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> for a parallel M-Level too, where it has no effect. On Timer 2 a noise whose clock is 7813 Hz or more runs on the pre-scaler 8, whatever <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> says; the next plain level sets the actuator’s own pre-scaler again.



