<b>Table </b><b>AS.1</b><b>.</b> The pre-scaler codes (<i>INFO_ML_PSC</i>), the clock-select bits the firmware writes, and the frequency ceilings [fw: Mlevel_set_pin(), analogEmit()].

The ceiling is the value of <i>INFO_ML_FREQ</i>: <i>F ÷ (2N)</i> on a 16-bit timer and <i>F ÷ N</i> on Timer 2, by integer division (hence 7,812 and not 7,812.5). The code 11, <i>PWM_PSC_UNDEF</i>, is not accepted.
<b>Note</b>: The pins that may carry a PWM M-Level (and, equally, a servo) are given in table AS.2. On the Mega2560 the firmware defines <i>FULL_SAFETY</i> automatically (unless you modify the firmware to prevent this), which locks D2 (the kill-switch input) and D12 (the output that drives the motor-driver Vcc voltage relay) for the safety system; a configuration that comments out the line #define FULL_SAFETY frees those two pins. A pin reported as “Refused” or “LOCKED” is rejected with the error “Pin number out of range”. D0 and D1 (the USB link to the server) are refused on every board, and so are the pins above D13 on the Uno and Nano and above D53 on the Mega2560, for parallel M-Levels as well.



