<b>Table </b><b>AS.2</b><b>. </b>The pins of the PWM M-Level (and of the servo).

<b>Note</b>: Creating and editing a translation table is done with the <i>INFO_ML_TTABLE</i> info item, with the following act_set usage pattern:
act_set &lt;act_id&gt; INFO_ML_TTABLE &lt;row&gt; &lt;freq&gt; &lt;duty&gt;
Where:
&lt;row&gt; is the integer row you wish to create or edit (starting at 0).
&lt;freq&gt; is the integer frequency in hertz, from 0 to 8000000. A frequency of 0 means silence: the pin is disconnected from the timer and held LOW, unless the duty is 1000. For a noise code it is the noise clock; for a code 4000 to 4999 it is ignored.
&lt;duty&gt; is an integer from 0 to 4999. From 0 to 1000 it is the duty cycle in per mille (tenths of a per cent): 0 holds the pin LOW and 1000 holds it HIGH, whatever the frequency. From 1001 to 4999 it is a special-effect code (Table 2 in the section on actuator types, and the formulae below). The values 2000 and 3000 (a noise or burst with <i>P</i> = 0) are refused.
All three must be whole numbers of 0 or more, and all three must be given. The table is held on the server only, so this command does not communicate with the Arduino, and a new or edited row changes nothing that sounds until a level selects it. The server refuses a frequency above 8000000 (“The frequency value must be &lt;= 8000000.”) and a duty above 4999, or of 2000 or 3000 (“The duty cycle value must be &lt;= 4999 and exclude 2000 and 3000.”). It accepts a noise code with a frequency of 0, which the Arduino refuses when a level selects the row.
If a table does not already exist, your first call must be for row 0. This is an example of the general rule that rows can only be created by strict appending: if your table already has <i>n</i> rows (rows 0 to <i>n</i> − 1), you can extend it by setting row <i>n</i>; an attempt to set a row index greater than <i>n</i> is intercepted by the server and rejected with the message “For a translation table you may only append a new entry or modify an existing entry.”. Rows cannot be deleted; the table is freed only when the actuator is destroyed.
You can edit any existing row with an act_set call to that row; the call overwrites its contents. The server also accepts a table for a parallel M-Level, but never uses it.
<b>Note</b>: A level is set with
act_set &lt;act_id&gt; INFO_ML_LEVEL &lt;level&gt;
where &lt;level&gt; is a whole number of 0 or more. For a PWM M-Level it is a row of the translation table. The server checks, in this order:
that the type is set (“You must set the type of an M-Level actuator before you set anything else.”);
that the actuator is mature (“… set the level of this M-Level actuator because it is immature.”);
for a parallel M-Level, that its pins have no gap (“… set the level of this M-Level actuator because there are gaps in its pin definition sequence.”);
for a PWM M-Level, that the row exists (“Attempting to access a translation table index that does not exist.”); for a parallel M-Level, that the level is not above 2n − 1 (“… set the level of this actuator to more than <i>m</i> because it only has <i>n</i> pins set.”);
whether the level is the one last sent: for a PWM M-Level, the same row holding the same frequency and duty as when it was last sent; for a parallel M-Level, the same level. Such a level is not sent, and the command reports success.
It then sends the row’s frequency and duty (for a parallel M-Level, the level) to the Arduino, and records the level as the last one sent when the Arduino reports success. Note the consequence of step 5: a timed noise or a burst selected twice in a row sounds once; select another row in between, or send act_halt. Since the comparison includes the row’s values, a row that you have edited is sent again and a separate row that has the same frequency and duty values as the previous row will be sent.
The firmware then acts as follows. It refuses the level if p0 is unassigned (“Attempt to alter the state of an unset pin.”). A duty above 4999, which the server never sends, is refused with “Invalid/unknown info item.”. A code 4000 to 4999 records the glide time or the attack step and returns, changing nothing audible. A noise code with <i>P</i> = 0 is refused with “Invalid/unknown info item.”; any other noise code first ends the effect running on the timer, then starts the noise, which is refused with “Invalid/unknown info item.” if the frequency is 0, and with “Attempt to alter a read only value.” (“Read only”) if a servo is on the timer. A plain duty first ends the effect running on the timer, then either starts a glide or a clean onset or sets the frequency and the duty at once. Every refusal of the firmware reaches the script as the server’s message, which names the Arduino as its source, for example '[PARDUINO] Pin number out of range.'
<b>Note</b>: How a requested frequency becomes a produced frequency. A timer can only divide its clock by a whole number, so the frequencies it can produce form a discrete set. The firmware chooses the timer’s “TOP” value by integer division (that is, rounding down), which means that the frequency produced is the nearest achievable frequency <b>at or above</b> the one requested, with two exceptions: a request above the highest frequency of the pre-scaler produces that highest frequency, and a request below the lowest frequency of the pre-scaler produces that lowest frequency. The tables AS.3 to AS.5 give the limits; the formulae after them give any particular value exactly.
Table AS.3 is for the 8-bit Timer 2 (Fast PWM mode; all frequencies in hertz; in brackets, the smallest frequency step available at that point: for “Lowest” and “Highest with 10 per-mille duty steps” the gap up to the next higher achievable frequency, and for “Highest” the gap down to the next lower one, since nothing lies above it):



