<b>Table </b><b>AS.4</b><b>.</b> The 16-bit timers: the limits of frequency.

In both tables AS.3 and AS.4 the “Tick” is the duration of one count of the timer, <i>N ÷ 16</i> µs; “Lowest” is the frequency at the largest TOP value (255 on Timer 2, 65,535 on a 16-bit timer); “Highest with 10 per-mille duty steps” is the highest frequency at which the duty cycle can still be set in steps of 10 per mille, that is 1 per cent (TOP + 1 = 100 on Timer 2, TOP = 100 on a 16-bit timer); “Highest with 1 per-mille duty steps” is the same for steps of 1 per mille (TOP = 1,000 on a 16-bit timer; Timer 2 has no such steps); and “Highest” is the frequency at the smallest TOP value, 1. A table frequency is a whole number of hertz, and 0 means silence, so the lowest frequency of a 16-bit timer with the pre-scaler 256 or 1024 is that of a row of 1 Hz: the timer could count more slowly (0.477 Hz and 0.119 Hz at TOP 65,535), but no table row can ask for it. Likewise a limit that is not a whole number is reached from the whole number below it: at <i>PWM_PSC_256</i>, 31 Hz gives TOP 1,008 (31.002 Hz), and at <i>PWM_PSC_1024</i>, 7 Hz gives TOP 1,116 (7.0004 Hz). Where the step in brackets is below 1 Hz, the step you can obtain is set by the whole hertz of the table. Pre-scalers 32 and 128 do not exist on the 16-bit timers, and an actuator with either of them is refused a 16-bit pin.
<b>Note</b>: How finely the duty cycle can be set also depends on the frequency, because at a higher frequency there are fewer timer ticks in each period. Tables AS.5 and AS.6 give the timer’s smallest step in duty cycle (in per mille, in brackets) at the frequency (in hertz) shown.



