<b>Table </b><b>AS.6</b><b>.</b> The 16-bit timers: the smallest duty step (per mille, in brackets).

The duty cycle value is stored as a whole number of per mille, so no step smaller than 1 per mille can be asked for. Where the timer’s step is finer than 1 per mille (TOP above 1,000), every whole per mille is realised to within half a timer tick, and the steps obtained are close to 1 per mille (between 0.99 and 1.01 per mille at TOP 65,535); where it is coarser, the duty produced is the nearest the timer can make. At the highest frequency of a 16-bit pre-scaler (TOP = 1) only 0 and 1000 per mille exist: a request of 1 to 499 per mille gives a constant LOW and 500 to 999 a constant HIGH (the compare value then equals TOP, which keeps the output HIGH), so the pin does not oscillate. At TOP = 2 a 16-bit timer makes 0, 500 and 1000 per mille: 1 to 249 gives LOW, 250 to 749 gives 500, and 750 to 999 gives HIGH. At the highest frequency of a Timer-2 pre-scaler, 0, 500 and 1000 per mille exist: 1 to 249 gives LOW, 250 to 749 gives 500, and 750 to 999 gives HIGH (the compare register then equals TOP).
<b>Note</b>: To calculate any particular value, use these formulae. In them <i>F</i> = 16,000,000 Hz is the Arduino’s crystal frequency, <i>N</i> is the pre-scaler divisor named by the <i>PWM_PSC_</i> symbol (1, 8, 32, 64, 128, 256 or 1024), <i>f</i> is the frequency stored in the translation table, <i>d</i> is the duty stored in the translation table (in per mille), and floor( ) means rounding down to a whole number. These are the calculations the firmware itself performs, in integer arithmetic only.
1. FOR THE 16-BIT TIMERS 1, 3, 4 AND 5 (Phase and Frequency Correct PWM mode)
Frequency ceiling: <i>f</i> is first reduced, if necessary, to <i>F ÷ (2 × N)</i> (integer division).
TOP value: <i>TOP = floor(F ÷ (2 × N × f))</i>, then limited to the range 1 to 65,535.
Frequency produced: <i>f_out = F ÷ (2 × N × TOP)</i>.
Frequency step at that point: <i>F ÷ (2 × N × (TOP − 1)) − F ÷ (2 × N × TOP)</i>.
Compare value: <i>OCR = floor((TOP × d + 500) ÷ 1000)</i>, that is, <i>TOP × d ÷ 1000</i> rounded to the nearest whole number. If OCR = 0 the pin is held LOW; if OCR <i>≥</i> TOP (and TOP &gt; 1) the pin is held HIGH.
Duty cycle produced: <i>OCR ÷ TOP × 1000</i> per mille.
Pulse width and period: the counter counts up to TOP and down again, and the pin is HIGH while the counter is below OCR, so a period lasts <i>2 × TOP</i> ticks and a pulse <i>2 × OCR</i> ticks, centred on the BOTTOM of the counter: pulse width = <i>OCR × N ÷ 8</i> µs, period = <i>TOP × N ÷ 8</i> µs.
Number of distinct duty cycles: TOP + 1 (from 0 to TOP), so the timer’s smallest duty step is <i>1000 ÷ TOP</i> per mille.
On a timer shared with a servo, TOP is the servo’s frame, 20,000, 5,000 or 3,003 for its bands of 50, 200 and 333 Hz, and the pre-scaler is 8; the frequency stored in the row is not used, and only OCR is calculated as above.

2. FOR THE 8-BIT TIMER 2 (Fast PWM mode)
Frequency ceiling: <i>f</i> is first reduced, if necessary, to <i>F ÷ N</i>.
TOP value: <i>TOP = floor(F ÷ (N × f)) − 1</i>, then limited to the range 1 to 255.
Frequency produced: <i>f_out = F ÷ (N × (TOP + 1))</i>.
Frequency step at that point: <i>F ÷ (N × TOP) − F ÷ (N × (TOP + 1))</i>.
High time in ticks: <i>H = floor(((TOP + 1) × d + 500) ÷ 1000)</i>, that is, <i>(TOP + 1) × d ÷ 1000</i> rounded to the nearest whole number. If H = 0 the pin is held LOW; otherwise the compare register OCR2B receives <i>H − 1</i>, and the pin is high for H of every TOP + 1 ticks (H = TOP + 1, that is OCR2B = TOP, is a constant HIGH).
Duty cycle produced: <i>H ÷ (TOP + 1) × 1000</i> per mille.
Pulse width and period: pulse width = <i>H × N ÷ 16</i> µs, period = <i>(TOP + 1) × N ÷ 16</i> µs.
Number of distinct duty cycles: TOP + 2 (from 0 to TOP + 1 ticks high), so the smallest duty step is <i>1000 ÷ (TOP + 1)</i> per mille.

3. FOR THE SPECIAL EFFECTS
Noise clock: the row’s frequency is first limited to 16,000 Hz. On Timer 2 the pre-scaler 8 is used if the clock is then 7,813 Hz or more, and the actuator’s own pre-scaler otherwise; on a 16-bit timer the actuator’s own pre-scaler is always used. TOP is then calculated as for a plain level at that clock, with the actuator’s own frequency ceiling, and the clock produced is <i>f_out</i> as above.
Pulse width of a noise on Timer 2: <i>h = floor(((TOP + 1) × w + 500) ÷ 1000)</i> ticks, limited to 1 to TOP, where <i>w</i> is the width in per mille (<i>P</i> for 1001 to 1999 and the hiss of 3001 to 3999; the width class for 2001 to 2999; 500 for the burst). During a noise Timer 2 drives the pin inverted, with OCR2B = TOP − h for a period that carries a pulse (a pulse of h ticks at the end of the period) and OCR2B = TOP for an empty one.
Pulse width of a noise on a 16-bit timer: <i>w_t = floor((TOP × w + 500) ÷ 1000)</i>, limited to 1 to TOP − 1. The compare value <i>w_t</i> makes the pin HIGH for the first and the last <i>w_t</i> ticks of a period, and 0 makes it LOW throughout; a pulse is therefore centred on a BOTTOM of the counter, and is 0, <i>w_t</i> or <i>2 × w_t</i> ticks wide, depending on the decisions of the two periods it straddles.
Duration of a timed noise (and of the burst of 3001 to 3999, with 10 ms): <i>n = floor(t × 16000 ÷ (N × L))</i> periods, at least 1 and at most 65,535, where <i>t</i> is the time in milliseconds, <i>N</i> the pre-scaler in force during the noise and <i>L</i> the length of a period in ticks (TOP + 1 on Timer 2, <i>2 × TOP</i> on a 16-bit timer); the noise lasts <i>n × L × N ÷ 16</i> µs.
The attack (Timer 2): with <i>h</i> the target width in ticks, the pulse starts at <i>i = ⌈ h ÷ 8 ⌉</i> ticks and grows by <i>i</i> every <i>a = floor(P × 16000 ÷ (N × (TOP + 1)))</i> periods (<i>a</i> limited to 1 to 255), where <i>P</i> is the attack step in ms; the step that would reach or pass <i>h</i> lands exactly on <i>h</i>. The widths are <i>i, 2i, …, h</i>: at most eight, so the attack lasts at most seven steps. A noise of one tick has no attack.
The glide (16-bit timers): from the present <i>(TOP_0, OCR_0)</i> to the target <i>(TOP_t, OCR_t)</i>, calculated as for a plain level, in <i>s = floor(P × 16000 ÷ (N × (TOP_0 + TOP_t)))</i> steps, one per period (at most 65,535). Each step adds to TOP and to OCR the whole-number quotient of their total change by <i>s</i>, and distributes the remainders evenly, so that both reach their targets exactly after <i>s</i> steps. The walk is linear in the period, not in the frequency, and lasts about <i>P</i> ms. From a clean silence (a present compare value of 0) the clean onset is made instead of a glide. The level is set at once, without a glide, when either TOP is below 256, when <i>s</i> is below 2, when no glide time is set, when the actuator does not own the timer’s effect state or p0 has moved, when the timer carries a servo, when the pin is not on its timer or another output of the timer is connected, when the new row is a silence, a constant HIGH or frequency 0, or when its compare value is 0 or reaches TOP. An order for the timer during a walk leaves the period and the compare value of the step reached.

<b>Worked example (8-bit)</b>: pre-scaler <i>PWM_PSC_128</i> and a stored frequency of 880 Hz:
TOP = floor(16,000,000 ÷ (128 × 880)) − 1 = floor(142.05) − 1 = 141.
The frequency produced is 16,000,000 ÷ (128 × 142) = 880.28 Hz.
For a stored duty of 500 per mille, H = floor((142 × 500 + 500) ÷ 1000) = floor(71.5) = 71, so OCR2B = 70 and the duty produced is 71 ÷ 142 = 500.0 per mille: a pulse of 71 × 128 ÷ 16 = 568 µs in a period of 142 × 128 ÷ 16 = 1,136 µs. The smallest duty step at this frequency is 1000 ÷ 142 = 7.04 per mille.
<b>Worked example (16-bit)</b>: pre-scaler <i>PWM_PSC_8</i> and a stored frequency of 440 Hz.
TOP = floor(16,000,000 ÷ (2 × 8 × 440)) = floor(2272.7) = 2272.
The frequency produced is 16,000,000 ÷ (2 × 8 × 2272) = 440.14 Hz. For a stored duty of 500 per mille, OCR = floor((2272 × 500 + 500) ÷ 1000) = floor(1136.5) = 1136: a pulse of 1136 × 8 ÷ 8 = 1,136 µs in a period of 2,272 µs. The smallest duty step is 1000 ÷ 2272 = 0.44 per mille. With 65 Hz and 60 per mille instead, TOP = 15,384, OCR = 923, the frequency produced is 65.0026 Hz and the duty 59.997 per mille, a pulse of 923 µs.
<b>Worked example (noise on Timer 2)</b>: a row of 16,000 Hz with the code 2410 (class 4, 10 ms) on D3 of a Nano whose actuator has <i>PWM_PSC_32</i>. The clock is 7,813 Hz or more, so the pre-scaler 8 is used: TOP = floor(16,000,000 ÷ (8 × 16,000)) − 1 = 124, a clock of exactly 16,000 Hz. Class 4 is 31 per mille: h = floor((125 × 31 + 500) ÷ 1000) = 4 ticks, a pulse of 2 µs. The noise lasts n = floor(10 × 16000 ÷ (8 × 125)) = 160 periods, 160 × 125 × 8 ÷ 16 = 10,000 µs. With the code 1062 and an attack step of 3 ms set by a row of code 4003, the target is 8 ticks: i = 1, a = floor(3 × 16000 ÷ (8 × 125)) = 48 periods per step, so the pulse grows 1, 2, …, 8 ticks over 7 × 48 periods = 21 ms. With 12,000 Hz and the code 3150: TOP = 165 (12,048.19 Hz), a burst of floor(10 × 16000 ÷ (8 × 166)) = 120 periods (9.960 ms) of 83 ticks, then a hiss of 25 ticks (12.5 µs).
<b>Worked example (glide)</b>: with a glide time of 80 ms (code 4080) and <i>PWM_PSC_8</i>, a plain level of 55 Hz and 51 per mille (TOP 18,181, OCR 927) that follows one of 65 Hz and 60 per mille (TOP 15,384, OCR 923) takes s = floor(80 × 16000 ÷ (8 × (15,384 + 18,181))) = 4 steps: (16,083, 924), (16,782, 925), (17,481, 926), (18,181, 927), which last 68.53 ms. With a glide time of 4 ms, s would be 0, and the level is set at once.
<b>Note</b>: When a PWM M-Level actuator and a servo actuator share a hardware timer (which can happen only on a 16-bit timer, and only by assigning the servo’s pin last: the firmware refuses an M-Level’s p0 on a timer that already carries a servo), the timer runs at the servo’s pre-scaler (8) and frame whatever the M-Level’s <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> says. <i>INFO_ML_PSC</i> cannot be changed meanwhile, because it can only be set while p0 is unassigned.
<b>Note</b>: When a PWM M-Level actuator and a servo actuator share a hardware timer, the frequency of the M-Level is the servo’s frame frequency (50 Hz, 200 Hz or 333 Hz, according to the servo’s <i>INFO_SRV_FREQ</i> band) and cannot be changed by the M-Level. The translation-table rows may contain whatever frequencies you wish, but those frequencies are not used when you set the level. This is not an error, but you should be aware of it, because the duty cycle is then set against the servo’s frame: TOP = 20,000, 5,000 or 3,003 for the three bands, so the timer’s duty step is 0.05, 0.2 or 0.333 per mille, finer than the whole per mille of the stored duty, and every whole per mille is realised to within half a tick (for example, 75 per mille at 50 Hz is OCR = 1,500, a pulse of 1,500 µs). The duty cycle can still be changed by the M-Level, and duty 0, duty 1000 and frequency 0 keep their fixed meanings. A noise code is refused on that timer (“Read only”), and a glide or a clean onset is not made there.
<b>Note</b>: When two or more PWM M-Level actuators share a hardware timer (again, this can happen only on a 16-bit timer), assigning the p0 of one of them is refused with a “Read only” violation error while another member of the group is active; de-activate it first (<i>act_halt</i>) and repeat the request; a refused request changes nothing. Each member’s pre-scaler is written into the timer when its p0 is assigned, and each member’s TOP and pre-scaler with every plain level and every noise it sets, so the member that last set a level decides the frequency and the pre-scaler of the whole group.
<b>Note</b>: When two or more PWM M-Level actuators share a hardware timer, they cannot oscillate at different frequencies at the same time, nor use different pre-scalers. The last frequency set is the one used by every member of the group, and likewise the last pre-scaler. Each member may still set its own duty cycle, but only its own level calculates its compare value: when another member then changes the TOP, this member’s compare value stays as it was, and its duty cycle becomes that compare value divided by the new TOP. The duty-cycle resolution is the same for all members, being fixed by the common frequency and pre-scaler in force on that timer. A level of any member also ends an effect running on the timer, and a glide or a clean onset is not made while another output of the timer is connected.
<b>Note</b>: Table AS.7 gathers what the client, the server and the firmware refusal conditions for each M-Level item of <i>act_set</i>. The client’s own checks come first: the item must be a whole number from 1 to 54; <i>INFO_ML_STATE</i>, <i>INFO_ML_TIMER</i> and <i>INFO_MATURITY</i> are refused as read only (“… is a read only data item.”); an item the client does not know for an M-Level, such as <i>INFO_ML_FREQ</i>, is refused as invalid (“… is an invalid hardware info item.”); a value of one byte must lie from 0 to 255, every value must be 0 or more, and <i>INFO_ML_TTABLE</i> needs all three values (“Not enough variables supplied to set data item.”).



