<b>Table </b><b>AS.7</b><b>. </b>The refusals of act_set for an M-Level actuator. Firmware errors reach the script with the prefix [PARDUINO].

<b>Example</b> (PCS). A complete script for a Nano with a speaker driver on D9 (Timer 1) and a noise channel on D3 (Timer 2). The comments give the values the firmware produces, as calculated by the formulae above.
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Pardus pcs 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>verbosity 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#include stdpard.pch</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt># ---- the voice: a PWM M-Level on D9, Timer 1 (16-bit) --------------</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>def_actuator ACT_MLEVEL 1 Voice</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_TYPE MLT_PWM          # the type first</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_PSC PWM_PSC_8         # the default; only before P0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_PIN_P0 9                 # the timer is set up; pin LOW</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_TTABLE 0 0 0          # row 0: silence (clean on a 16-bit pin)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_TTABLE 1 440 500      # row 1: TOP 2272, OCR 1136, 440.14 Hz</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_TTABLE 2 65 60        # row 2: TOP 15384, OCR 923, 65.0026 Hz</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_TTABLE 3 55 51        # row 3: TOP 18181, OCR 927, 55.0025 Hz</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_TTABLE 4 0 4080       # row 4: glide time 80 ms (a setting)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_LEVEL 1               # 440 Hz at once</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>sleep 1.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_LEVEL 4               # glide time set; nothing audible changes</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_LEVEL 2               # glides to 65 Hz</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>sleep 1.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_LEVEL 3               # glides to 55 Hz in 4 steps (68.53 ms)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>sleep 1.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_LEVEL 0               # clean silence: the pin stays on Timer 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_LEVEL 2               # clean onset: first pulse after 1.5 periods</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>sleep 1.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_ML_LEVEL 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt># ---- the noise: a PWM M-Level on D3, Timer 2 (8-bit) ---------------</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>def_actuator ACT_MLEVEL 2 Noise</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_TYPE MLT_PWM</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_PSC PWM_PSC_32        # noise clocks of 7813 Hz or more use 8</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_PIN_P0 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_TTABLE 0 0 0          # row 0: silence</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_TTABLE 1 0 4003       # row 1: attack step 3 ms</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_TTABLE 2 16000 1062   # row 2: noise, 8 ticks (4 us), until ended</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_TTABLE 3 16000 2410   # row 3: class 4, 10 ms (160 periods)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_TTABLE 4 12000 3150   # row 4: burst 9.960 ms, then hiss 12.5 us</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_LEVEL 1               # the attack step; nothing audible</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_LEVEL 2               # soft start: 1 to 8 ticks over 21 ms</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>sleep 0.5</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_LEVEL 3               # ends the noise; 10 ms burst, then silence</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_LEVEL 0               # (row 3 again would need another row first)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_LEVEL 4               # burst, then hiss until the next order</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>sleep 0.5</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_ML_LEVEL 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt># ---- release both ----------------------------------------------------</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_halt Voice</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_halt Noise</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Voice INFO_PIN_P0 FREE_PIN</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>act_set Noise INFO_PIN_P0 FREE_PIN</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
In this script an act_set<i> Voice INFO_ML_PSC PWM_PSC_64</i> after the line that assigns p0 would be refused with “You can only set the prescaler for pin 0 if it is unset.”, and a second PWM M-Level given p0 = 10 while Voice is active would be refused with “[PARDUINO] Attempt to alter a read only value.”, because D10 shares Timer 1 with D9.

<b><i>Notes for using act_set with </i></b><b><i>Servo</i></b><b><i> actuators</i></b>
Note: When you create a new servo actuator you cannot set its position until you have assigned its signal pin (pin_srv) to a hardware pin with INFO_SRV_PINS. The pins a servo may use are given in the table of pins in the notes for M-Level actuators above. If you want a frequency band other than the default (SRV_FREQ_MID50), you must set it with INFO_SRV_FREQ before you assign the pin (or after explicitly freeing the pin).
Note: You have a choice of three frequency bands. Together with the kind of timer that drives the servo’s pin, the band fixes the timer’s pre-scaler, the frame (the interval at which the pulse repeats) and the resolution with which the pulse width, and so the servo’s position, can be set, according to the following table. The smaller the tick, the finer the positioning.


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Frequency band </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Timer     </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Pre-scaler</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Tick (µs)               </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Frame period (µs)</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Frame frequency (Hz)</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Longest usable pulse (µs)</span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┼────────────┼──────────────────────────┼───────────────────┼──────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ SRV_FREQ_MID50  │ 16-bit     │ 8          │ 0.5 (pulse resolution 1) │ 20,000            │ 50                   │ 19,999                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │ SRV_FREQ_MID200 │ 16-bit     │ 8          │ 0.5 (pulse resolution 1) │ 5,000             │ 200                  │ 4,999                     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ SRV_FREQ_MID333 │ 16-bit     │ 8          │ 0.5 (pulse resolution 1) │ 3,003             │ 333                  │ 3,000                     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ SRV_FREQ_MID50  │ 8-bit      │ 1,024      │ 64                       │ 16,384            │ 61.04                │ 16,320                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ (Timer 2)  │            │                          │                   │                      │                           │ </tt>
  <tt> │ SRV_FREQ_MID200 │ 8-bit      │ 256        │ 16                       │ 4,000             │ 250                  │ 3,984                     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ (Timer 2)  │            │                          │                   │                      │                           │ </tt>
  <tt> │ SRV_FREQ_MID333 │ 8-bit      │ 256        │ 16                       │ 2,992             │ 334.22               │ 2,976                     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ (Timer 2)  │            │                          │                   │                      │                           │ </tt>



On a 16-bit timer the timer counts in ticks of 0.5 µs but counts up and then down in each frame, so the pulse width is set in steps of exactly 1 µs, and the frame frequencies are the nominal 50 Hz, 200 Hz and 333 Hz. On Timer 2 the 50 Hz band actually runs at 61.04 Hz: the longest frame Timer 2 can produce is 256 ticks of 64 µs, that is 16,384 µs, and 50 Hz would need 20,000 µs. The firmware’s own definition of this band (parddefs.h) gives its safe range as 40 Hz to 70 Hz, so 61.04 Hz lies within it. The 200 Hz and 333 Hz bands run on Timer 2 at 250 Hz and 334.22 Hz.
“Longest usable pulse” is the longest pulse the firmware will deliver, which is always shorter than the frame: a pulse as long as the frame would hold the pin permanently HIGH, which a servo must never receive. A longer command is reduced to it: on a 16-bit timer to the frame less 1 µs (19,999 µs and 4,999 µs for the 50 Hz and 200 Hz bands; for the 333 Hz band the server’s own limit of 3,000 µs is reached first), and on Timer 2 to the frame less one tick. The server accepts INFO_SRV_MAX values up to 20,000 µs, 5,000 µs and 3,000 µs for the three bands whatever the timer; in practice you should keep INFO_SRV_MAX within your servo’s own range, typically 500 µs to 2,500 µs.
Note: To obtain accurate motion in response to PCS commands, and accurate angular or microsecond resolution in all the calculations below and in the PCS / PARDUS calibrations and conversions, you must supply accurate values of the following parameters for your servo:
INFO_SRV_MIN - the smallest number of microseconds to which your servo responds linearly. Below this value the servo does not move any further in that direction in a linear manner, for whatever reason (signal processing in the servo, or a physical obstruction). The default is 544 µs.
INFO_SRV_THMIN - the angular position of the servo at a pulse width of INFO_SRV_MIN.
INFO_SRV_MAX - the largest number of microseconds to which your servo responds linearly. Above this value the servo does not move any further in that direction in a linear manner, for whatever reason. The default is 2,400 µs.
INFO_SRV_THMAX - the angular position of the servo at a pulse width of INFO_SRV_MAX.
Failure to supply physically accurate values for these parameters will result in erroneous positioning of servos and possible damage to them or to other hardware. The values may be found by careful experiment and/or from the servo’s data sheet.
When attempting to find the linear range of your servos by experiment, the following signs may help:
If you hear humming or buzzing at the frame frequency with your servo at an extreme of its motion, you have probably pushed past its linear range, and the servo is in a constant correction loop fighting the reading of its own potentiometer. Ease your limit back, reducing the range of motion, until the buzz or hum stops.
Set a slow motion with the &lt;duration&gt; parameter of act_accel and make sure the &lt;s_accel&gt; parameter is 0 (slow constant motion without acceleration). If, when the servo starts to move away from one of its limits, it first makes a fast movement before settling into its slow steady motion, the limit it started from is probably beyond its linear range. Ease that limit in until the initial fast phase no longer occurs.
Note: With accurate values for the parameters above, you can work out the resolution of your servo with the following equations. First calculate the resolution in steps (the number of distinct steps the servo arm can make across its range; the number of distinct positions is one more than the number of steps):
1. On any 16-bit timer
The firmware writes the commanded pulse width, a whole number of microseconds, straight into the timer’s compare register, and on these timers one unit of that register is exactly 1 µs of pulse. The smallest step is therefore exactly 1 µs, and the number of steps is simply the microsecond span:
Total 16-bit steps = srv_max − srv_min
Example: if a specialised servo accepts pulses from 500 µs to 2,500 µs (a span of 2,000 µs), a 16-bit timer gives exactly 2,000 steps (2,001 distinct positions) across that arc, at 1 µs per step.
2. On the 8-bit Timer 2
The firmware rounds the commanded pulse width to the nearest whole number of ticks, using the tick size of the band (64 µs for SRV_FREQ_MID50, 16 µs for SRV_FREQ_MID200 and SRV_FREQ_MID333). The number of steps is therefore:
Total 8-bit steps = floor( (srv_max + Offset) ÷ Tick_Size ) − floor( (srv_min + Offset) ÷ Tick_Size )
where Tick_Size is given in the table above, and Offset = Tick_Size ÷ 2 is the half tick that turns the firmware’s rounding-down integer division into rounding to the nearest tick.
Example using srv_min = 544 µs, srv_max = 2,400 µs and the SRV_FREQ_MID333 band (Tick_Size 16 µs, Offset 8 µs):
Upper bound ticks = floor((2,400 + 8) ÷ 16) = floor(150.5) = 150 ticks
Lower bound ticks = floor((544 + 8) ÷ 16) = floor(34.5) = 34 ticks
Total 8-bit steps = 150 − 34 = 116 steps, which over a 180° arc is 1.55° per step.
Example using srv_min = 544 µs, srv_max = 2,400 µs and the SRV_FREQ_MID50 band (Tick_Size 64 µs, Offset 32 µs):
Upper bound ticks = floor((2,400 + 32) ÷ 64) = floor(38) = 38 ticks
Lower bound ticks = floor((544 + 32) ÷ 64) = floor(9) = 9 ticks
Total 8-bit steps = 38 − 9 = 29 steps, which over a 180° arc is 6.21° per step.
For comparison, the same servo limits on a 16-bit timer give 2,400 − 544 = 1,856 steps, which over a 180° arc is 0.097° per step.
You can then convert this number of steps into a time or an angular resolution with these formulae:
Time-domain resolution (microseconds per step): this is the pulse resolution of the table above, 1 µs on a 16-bit timer and 64 µs or 16 µs on Timer 2. It is a fixed property of the timer and band, independent of srv_min and srv_max (which only fix the range of motion over which this resolution applies).
Angular resolution (degrees per step): divide the servo’s angular travel between its limits by the number of steps found above:
Angular resolution = (theta_max − theta_min) ÷ Total steps
Note: On Timer 2 the pulse the servo receives is the commanded width rounded to the nearest tick, and never shorter than one tick. So, for example, on the 50 Hz band a command of 1,500 µs is delivered as 23 ticks of 64 µs, which is 1,472 µs. Commands that the 16-bit timers would deliver exactly can therefore be up to half a tick (32 µs or 8 µs) different on Timer 2, and your calibration (INFO_SRV_MIN, INFO_SRV_MAX, INFO_SRV_THMIN and INFO_SRV_THMAX) should be made on the timer the servo will actually use.
Note: Any attempt to assign the servo’s signal pin to a hardware pin is rejected with a “Read only” error if a PWM M-Level actuator that shares that pin’s timer is currently active. You must first de-activate any such M-Level (with act_halt) before assigning the pin with INFO_SRV_PINS.
Note: Assigning the signal pin with INFO_SRV_PINS configures the timer (pre-scaler and frame) but does not yet send pulses: the pin is held LOW, and the servo does not move, until the first motion command (act_moveto) for it. From then on the servo is energised and holds its position; you can de-energise it with a separate act_halt call.
Note: Several servos may be driven by pins of the same hardware timer, but every servo on that timer must have the same INFO_SRV_FREQ band, because the band fixes the timer’s frame. An attempt to assign a servo pin on a timer that already carries servos of a different band is refused with a “Read only” error. On Timer 2 the question does not arise, because each board has only one usable Timer-2 pin.
Note: Assigning a servo to a timer that a PWM M-Level actuator already uses is allowed only while that M-Level actuator is inactive; if it is active, the request to set the servo pin is refused with a “Read only” error. If you do assign the servo pin, the servo’s pre-scaler and frame replace whatever the M-Level actuator was using, so the next time you activate that M-Level it will oscillate at the servo’s frame frequency and not at the frequency in its translation table. The other way round (an M-Level’s p0 assigned to a timer that already carries a servo) is refused with a “Read only” error. See the notes for using act_set with M-Level actuators for the other consequences of a servo sharing a timer with PWM M-Level actuators.
Note: The INFO_SRV_SPOS option allows you to change or correct the recorded position of a servo without moving it. When a new servo actuator is created (with def_actuator) its Arduino structure records a position of 1,500 µs, and the server’s shadow structure records SERVO_RESET (= INT32_MAX). The 1,500 µs default is an arbitrary choice, a typical mid-point between the default srv_min and srv_max, and it may not be the true position of your servo when its actuator is created. If it is not, the first motion command to the servo may produce a sharp, fast, unpredictable movement, because the range and direction of a motion are calculated like this:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Calculate direction and range</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>if (target_us &gt; s-&gt;current_us) {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>     </tt><tt>target_range = target_us - s-&gt;current_us;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>     </tt><tt>dirn = 1; // Vector Direction 1: Adding atomic microsecond steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>}</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>else if (target_us &lt; s-&gt;current_us) {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>     </tt><tt>target_range = s-&gt;current_us - target_us;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>     </tt><tt>dirn = 2; // Vector Direction 2: Subtracting atomic microsecond steps</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>}</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>else {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>     </tt><tt>// We are already precisely at the destination.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>     </tt><tt>// Exit instantly to prevent target_range from evaluating to 0, which</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>     </tt><tt>// would crash downstream integer division arithmetic!</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>     </tt><tt>return PARDU_ERR_NONE;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>}</tt> </span>
where current_us is 1,500 for a newly created servo. The range and direction of the first motion may therefore be quite wrong if the servo is not actually at the position that 1,500 µs represents. With act_set and the INFO_SRV_SPOS option you can silently change this recorded position to one closer to where your servo arm actually is. The value to use can be found by experience with 3-wire servos; with 4-wire servos you can read the reported position with act_get and the INFO_SRV_AFB option and, after converting it into microseconds or degrees according to your current units mode, write it into the Arduino structure with act_set and INFO_SRV_SPOS. If you set a value that does not reflect the true position of the servo arm, you can cause erratic behaviour, so use this option only when it is needed and with physically accurate information.
Note: The units for setting a servo position with INFO_SRV_QPOS or INFO_SRV_SPOS are those set with act_unit (which see). A units value of 1 means the server expects the position in degrees (which may be a float with decimal places, not restricted to whole degrees). A units value of 0 means the server expects the position in microseconds (an integer). When the server starts (or restarts) the units value is 0 (microseconds). It is good practice, therefore, always to set the units explicitly in your scripts with act_unit. As discussed in the section on act_get, a value retrieved with INFO_SRV_QPOS or INFO_SRV_SPOS is always in microseconds, whatever the current units value.
Note: Managing the servo queue. An act_set with INFO_SRV_QPOS does not move the servo; it lists the servo in the queue for motion at the next act_moveto command. A second act_set with INFO_SRV_QPOS for the same servo, before the act_moveto, simply overwrites its entry with the newer position. To remove a servo from the queue, issue an act_set with INFO_SRV_QPOS and the value SERVO_RESET; the same SERVO_RESET value is used whatever the units (degrees or microseconds). To wipe the whole queue without moving any of the servos in it, issue an act_moveto for servos with the argument OFF (or FALSE, or 0); see act_moveto. As discussed in the entry for act_accel, the &lt;duration&gt; value supplied with act_accel decides whether the motion to the position you queue here is made with or without motion control, and that decision is recorded in the queue entry. You must therefore set the motion-control parameters for servos with act_accel before adding any servo to the queue, and if you change the act_accel &lt;duration&gt; parameter for servos you must refresh any entries already in the queue so that their motion profiles are recalculated. The server keeps a shadow copy of the queue, so act_get calls with INFO_SRV_QPOS do not travel to the Arduino.
Note: For 4-wire servos you may assign a pin to sense the position feedback with act_set and INFO_SRV_PINP. This must be an analogue pin; give its number as labelled on the Arduino board but without the “A” prefix. The value on this pin is read with act_get and INFO_SRV_AFB.
When using act_get with INFO_SRV_AFB, bear in mind that the value returned is an integer from 0 to 1023, the 10-bit analogue-to-digital conversion of the voltage of the servo’s internal potentiometer. Because manufacturers scale their feedback voltages differently, you must map this 0 to 1023 range onto servo position in microseconds before using it for positioning (for example, before writing it into the servo’s current_us value with act_set and INFO_SRV_SPOS).

<b><i>Notes for using act_set with </i></b><b><i>Sensor</i></b><b><i> actuators</i></b>
<b>Note</b>: When you create a new Sensor actuator the only item you will be allowed to set (before anything else) is the type of Sensor using the INFO_SEN_TYPE info item. Once the type is set you will not be allowed to change it. You, of course, have the option to undefine the actuator and re-define it then set it to a different type.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">act_unit</span>
act_unit &lt;act_type&gt; &lt;unit&gt;
This sets the way distance and position values (as supplied in motion command arguments) are interpreted for actuators of type &lt;act_type&gt;.
&lt;act_type&gt; - for the options for this integer see def_actuator
&lt;unit&gt; - this is an integer varval the meaning of which depends on the actuator type specified previously. For &lt;act_type&gt; = 1 (stepper motors) the &lt;unit&gt; options are:
   0 - For steppers this means the distance or position values (including the specification of software limit positions) supplied in any subsequent stepper motor command are to be interpreted in terms of number of steps at the maxmsdenom microstep size (these are called 'atomic units' or 'atomic steps'). For servos it means units in literal microseconds.
   1 - For steppers this means the distance or position values supplied in any subsequent stepper motion command are to be interpreted in terms  of calibrated units and so the same distance or position value will result in a different number of steps being done by the motor depending on the       current microstep denominator setting and values for maxmsdenom and maxcalibri. For servos it means units are supplied in angular degrees.
<b>Note</b>: To read the current value of &lt;unit&gt; from the server you use act_get with the info item INFO_UNIT_TYPE and submit an extant actuator of the &lt;act_type&gt; of interest (the specific actuator index does not matter for this act_get because only the actuator type is used for the look up on the server but the actuator must exists in order to pass the pre-command checks in the interpreter).
<b>Note</b>: The units you choose shall also apply to values you send to the server for setting using the act_set command but they do not apply to values you receive back from the server (for example in motor coordinate or 'stepcount' act_get requests). This is why coordinates (e.g. step 'counts' for steppers or the position value of a servo) and distances are supplied via act_get in float variables instead of integers - because these may need to convey integer atomic steps or float-capable calibrated units (for example, a stepper calibrated 'stepcount' may be something like 20156.832 and will be interpreted in the units you have calibrated the stage to e.g. microns). However, when you <i>receive</i> such distances and positions with an act_get you will always receive the value as a raw atomic integer so you should supply integer-capable receptacle variables to receive the result regardless of the act_unit status.
<b>Note</b>: There are a few specific exceptions to these general rules for steppers:
All nhs steps (used for both for null homing and limit switch homing) are always interpreted (supplied and received) as integer number of steps in the current microstep (msdenom) step size, regardless of maxmsdenom or calibration factor.
maxcalibri will always be a float capable number. It does not have to be a whole number of atomic steps. However it must not be &lt;1.0e-3.
blsteps will always be interpreted as literal steps in the motor's current microstep (msdenom) size. This is read-only for client. It is calculated by the server as:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Stepperlist[ui82]-&gt;blsteps = Stepperlist[ui82]-&gt;maxblsteps /</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                                             </tt><tt>      </tt><tt>Stepperlist[ui82]-&gt;onestep;</tt> </span>
maxblsteps will always be interpreted as atomic steps.
tensteps1 and tensteps2 will always be interpreted as atomic steps. The values held in the Arduino will be automatically converted to steps in the current microstep (msdenom) step size as was done for blsteps.
<b>Note</b>: The default unit type will be 0 for each actuator type until you call act_unit for that actuator type at least once during a log in session and this choice will remain at what you set it till you deliberately change it or log out (so it is not set back to default at the end or start of any script).
<b>Note</b>: For the other actuator types (Binary, M-Level and Sensor) the unit type is not used.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">add_lib</span>
add_lib &lt;file_path&gt;
This instructs the linker to include a library into a program. 
&lt;file_path&gt; - a string varval. This must evaluate the name of the file (optionally including its full directory path) to be loaded. No white space is allowed. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: See the chapter on libs (page 57) for detailed information on usage.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">add_var</span>
add_var &lt;varname&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
This adds a value to a user-defined variable. 
&lt;varname&gt; - The name of the variable whose value you want to change
&lt;varval&gt; - The value (or variable containing the value) you want to add to &lt;varname&gt;
<b>Note</b>: This is symbolically equivalent to &lt;varname&gt; += &lt;varval&gt; in C.
<b>Note</b>: When the data type of &lt;varname&gt; is 'string' then the &lt;varval&gt; argument will be evaluated and that value cast as a string (if it is not already a string) and printed onto the end of whatever string is in &lt;varname&gt; .  This feature can be used to print numerical values into strings.
Note: The cast follows the rule given under def_var: a constant written in the script is appended exactly as written (`add_var s 007` appends “007”), a numeric variable is appended as a number, and a string variable is appended as it is.
<b>Note</b>: Addition can also be done inline using math_var (which see).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">af_ccrit</span>
af_ccrit &lt;max_iterations&gt; &lt;max_difference&gt; &lt;min_true_peak&gt; &lt;timeout&gt;
This sets convergence criteria parameters for the autofocus command search algorithm. Use this command in conjunction with the af_setup command to fully set up the auto focus (AF) system which may then be invoked via the autofocus command.
&lt;max_iterations&gt; - a varval of type int and must be positive (&gt;= 1). This is the maximum number of iterations (without finding an acceptable peak focus) the search algorithm will tolerate before giving up the search as a failure. 
&lt;max_difference&gt; is a varval of type float and must be &gt;= 0.0 . This is the maximum distance in Z (in either direction) from the existing best focus Z position that can be accepted as a new best focus after a search (see 'Note' below). If the AF search results in a position further away than this then AF fails and the pre-existing best Z position is preserved. This helps to limit the acceptable search area and prevent the algorithm converging to a local peak that is far off true focus (e.g. for microscopy this would mean focus on debris or pen marks on a coverslip in an image with little tissue contrast). If you do not want to use this limit, set the value to 0. 
&lt;min_true_peak&gt; is a varval of type 'float' and must be &gt;= 0.0. This is the minimum peak value (returned from the goodness-of-focus function) acceptable as a success. If, after a search, a maximum in the goodness-of-focus function is found which is less than this value then the procedure is considered a failure even though a peak was found. This is so false peaks / local maxima can be avoided. The optimum value for this criterion will be found by empirical methods because it may vary by the kind of specimen / object being imaged as well as magnification and imaging modality (i.e. how much contrast can be expected in a focussed area) and the goodness-of-focus function used. 
&lt;timeout&gt;  This is as an integer in milliseconds. If the AF search takes  longer than this to complete it will fail. Use 0 if you do not want a timeout to be used.
<b>Note</b>: All positions / coordinates / distances are expected to be supplied in either calibrated units (if you set act_unit to &gt;0 for steppers) or in atomic steps (1/maxmsdenom microsteps). Positions are measured from 0 (= the homed position) and no negative values are allowed. Because a stepper motor can only travel in steps of a minimum of 1/msdenom microsteps, this means that whatever values you supply here for distances and positions will be rounded to the nearest <i>onestep</i> value (see p.97). For example, when using atomic steps, if you set &lt;max_difference&gt; to 15 when maxmsdenom in 256 and msdenom is 8 (giving a onestep value of 256/8 = 32) then this will result in an effective &lt;max_difference&gt; of 0 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (15.0 / 32.0)) = 0. However if you set &lt;max_difference&gt; = 16 then the effective value will be 1 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (16.0 / 32.0)) = 1.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">af_setup</span>
af_setup &lt;initial_focus_z&gt; &lt;initial_step_size&gt; &lt;max_retries&gt; &lt;dirn_up&gt; &lt;col_channel&gt; &lt;failure_action&gt;
This sets all but the convergence criteria parameters and focus function for the autofocus (AF) procedure. Use this command in conjunction with the af_ccrit and prv_toggle commands to fully set up an AF search system which can then be called via the autofocus command. Autofocus is a Z motor-specific facility provided as part of the PARDUS standard. See the autofocus command for more details about the actual procedure.
&lt;initial_focus_z&gt; - a varval of type float and is the initial Z axis position (coordinate) of current best focus (must be &gt;= 0.0). After this initial setting, this value is automatically updated every time the autofocus action succeeds so the value supplied here is just the initial value used prior to the first autofocus success. If the autofocus fails the Z axis moves to the last AF-successful value of this position (or to the value provided here if AF has not had any success since its initial attempt) as the fall-back default focus position. 
&lt;initial_step_size&gt; - a varval of type float and must be positive (&gt; 0.0). It is the search step distance for Z motion in the hill-climbing search (not to be confused with motor step size).
&lt;max_retries&gt; - a varval of type int that must be a &gt;= 0. If the AF search fails first time round it may succeed if repeated so this specifies how many times to repeat before finally giving up.
&lt;dirn_up&gt;  - An integer representing the direction that moves the Z axis up (must be 1 or 0)
&lt;col_channel&gt; - An integer representing which colour channel to use for the focus statistic. These are #defined in pardcapdefs.h as the 'CCHAN_' values. Currently only 2 (= Red), 3 (= Green) and 4 (= blue) are allowed. 
&lt;failure_action&gt; - a string keyword that determines how to proceed if the iterative AF ultimately fails. This is one of: ignore, exit.
ignore - this just carries on regardless - the last best value for focus will be used as best focal position and the program will continue. This can be perfectly legitimate if, for example, the auto-focus attempt took place over an area with no relevant object in the field of view of the camera (e.g. blank area of slide for microscopy).
exit - this will terminate the program immediately. 
<b>Note</b>: All positions / coordinates / distances are expected to be supplied in either calibrated units (if you set act_unit to &gt;0 for steppers) or in atomic steps (1/maxmsdenom microsteps). Positions are measured from 0 (= the homed position) and no negative values are allowed. Because a stepper motor can only travel in steps of a minimum of 1/msdenom microsteps, this means that whatever values you supply here for distances and positions will be rounded to the nearest <i>onestep</i> value (see p.97). For example, when using atomic steps, if you set &lt;initial_step_size&gt; to 15 when maxmsdenom in 256 and msdenom is 8 (giving a onestep value of 256/8 = 32) then this will result in an effective &lt;initial_step_size&gt; of 0 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (15.0 / 32.0)) = 0. However if you set &lt;initial_step_size&gt; = 16 then the effective value will be 1 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (16.0 / 32.0)) = 1.
<b>Note</b>: The focus function used for AF is selected using the frame grabber settings commands such as prv_toggle with the 'fc_...' (focusser statistic) options. See prv_toggle for details.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">autofocus</span>
autofocus
This causes the Z axis to perform an autofocus iterative hill-climbing focus-function maximisation search that involves taking a preview image, measuring its goodness-of-focus value with a focus-function then moving the Z motor (which is assumed to control focussing motion for this to work) and iterating to find the Z position with the best (maximum) value of the focus function.
This command expects no arguments because all the autofocus settings are set using the af_setup, af_ccrit and prv_toggle commands (which see). These should be called prior to first use of autofocus.
<b>Note</b>: If tissue content thresholding is enabled, an autofocus Z-search will only be performed if the tissue content threshold is passed (i.e. if there is tissue detected in the field) - otherwise the autofocus function will exit reporting AF_SUCCESS but without actually doing anything (it is not an error).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">average_scale_means</span>
average_scale_means &lt;ON|OFF&gt;
This determines whether (ON) or not (OFF) to scale the mean of any image (captured as part of a multiframe average series) to the mean of the first image captured in the series.
&lt;ON|OFF&gt; - a binary choice varval.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">beep</span>
beep &lt;duration_ms&gt; &lt;freq_Hz&gt; [amplitude_percent]
This emits a non-blocking sine wave audio tone using the sound system of the PC that pardclient (or pcs-cli) is running on (no request to the pardserver is made).
&lt;duration_ms&gt; - this is an integer varval &gt;= 10 determining the duration of the beep.
&lt;freq_Hz&gt; - this is a float varval representing the frequency in Hz of the beep. The value is not checked for range  but see Note below.
[amplitude_percent] - this is an integer varval &gt;= 0  and &lt;=100 determining the amplitude of the sound.
<b>Note</b>: If you do not supply an [amplitude_percent] value then 100 (percent) will be used by default. A value of 0 will result in silence. If you supply a value &gt;100 then this will be silently clipped to 100 (no error will be emitted and the script will not terminate).
<b>Note</b>: The effector function automatically clips your input values into the following ranges like so:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>if (duration_ms &lt; 10) duration_ms = 10;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>if (duration_ms &gt; 5000) duration_ms = 5000;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>if (frequency_hz &lt; 50.0) frequency_hz = 50.0;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>if (frequency_hz &gt; 5000.0) frequency_hz = 5000.0;</tt> </span>
<b>Note</b>: This is a non-blocking command. Up to 8 note polyphony is supported. If you want the command to behave in a blocking-like way then just issue a sleep command right after the beep command for the same duration (bear in mind that the sleep argument is in seconds so a beep at 50 (ms) should be followed by a sleep for 0.05 (seconds)).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">call_func</span>
call_func &lt;function_label&gt; [if &lt;varval&gt;]
This calls a function. This command can be simple (i.e. just go to the function) or extended as a conditional call (i.e. only go to the function if some condition is satisfied).
&lt;function_label&gt; - this is a string varval. The string must be a label that is part of a def_func command.
if - If an extended (conditional) function call is required then the second argument must be the word if and must be followed by a varval of a type described below.
&lt;varval&gt; - a binary choice varval. If the value of &lt;varval&gt; evaluates to a non-zero numerical value or to TRUE or ON then the script interpreter will immediately go to the line in the script where the function is defined. Otherwise the script will continue execution from the next coding line immediately following this call_func line.
<b>Note</b>: The argument for call_func can be a string variable meaning that the same call_func command in a script can be used to call different functions if the script sets the string variable argument to different values (different function labels) according to some algorithm. That is, &lt;function_label&gt; does not have to be a hard-coded string. For def_func the &lt;function_label&gt; argument must be a hard-coded string, not a variable.
<b>Note</b>: Inside the interpreter program this does two things:
It creates a new element on the RetPoint[] stack pointing to just after the call_func line in the script.
It sends control of flow to the function defined as &lt;function_label&gt;.
<b>AI Tip</b>: For high-performance state machines, store your destination function labels in a string array. This allows you to dispatch control flow using an index (e.g., call_func state_labels[idx]). This 'Jump Table' approach is more efficient for LLMs to manage than complex branching logic.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">camera_get</span>
camera_get &lt;Control_ID&gt; &lt;varval&gt; [info_item]
This requests a the value of a camera setting (called a 'control' in v4l2 terminology) on a v4l2 imaging device connected to the server (such as brightness, contrast, etc. - whatever controls the imaging device may present).
&lt;Control_ID&gt; - a varval of type int that must be &gt;= 0 and be the v4l2 ID number of a video device setting (the setting you want to read the value of). Control ID numbers are those retrieved by the get_camsettings command (which see).
&lt;varval&gt; - a varval of type int that is to receive the value you want to get. .
[info_item] - an optional varval of type int that defines what aspect of that control's value you want to get and must be one of these values as shown in parddefs.h and as included in stdpard.pch:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define INFO_CAM_MIN   50                 // LG (on client)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define INFO_CAM_MAX   51                 // LG (on client)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define INFO_CAM_CURR  52                 // Ask PARDcap using PS_CIID_CAMG</tt> </span>
Submitting a value of 50 or 51 gets the reported minimum or maximum permissible value for the control. These values are reported to the client at login and, because they cannot be altered, the values will be retrieved for you from the local client without and network traffic. Submitting a value of 52 will request the current value of the control and so will result in a request being passed to th pardserver and thence to the pardcap server of the current camera to fetch that value.
<b>Note</b>: If you do not supply the optional [info_item] argument then the default behaviour is to request the current value of the control.
<b>Note</b>: If the value of a control's minimum and maximum entries are identical then that control's value will be read only. Even if the minimum and maximum entries are not identical, a control's current value may be functionally read-only in certain circumstances (e.g. you can't change the exposure while auto-exposure is enabled).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">camera_set</span>
camera_set &lt;Control_ID&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
This requests a change to a setting (called a 'control' in v4l2 terminology) on a v4l2 imaging device connected to the server (such as brightness, contrast, etc. - whatever user-alterable controls the imaging device may have).
&lt;Control_ID&gt; - a varval of type int that must be &gt;= 0 and be the v4l2 ID number of a writeable video device setting (the setting you want to change). Control ID numbers are those retrieved by the get_camsettings command (which see).
&lt;varval&gt; - a varval of type int representing the new value you want the setting to change to. This value must be in the permitted range for the setting as defined by maximum and minimum values shown in the camera settings information (retrieved by the get_camsettings command).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">cat_char</span>
cat_char &lt;STR_VAR&gt; &lt;char_as_int&gt;
This allows you to add a single UTF-8 Unicode compatible character to the end of a string variable by specifying the character's integer reference number.
&lt;char_as_int&gt; - must be an integer in the range 1 to 1114111 inclusive but excluding the surrogate code point range (U+D800 to U+DFFF, that is 55296 to 57343 in decimal). This may be supplied in an integer variable or as an integer constant or a value that may be cast as such.
<b>Note</b>: For this command (only) you may supply the &lt;char_as_int&gt; integer constant in a UTF-friendly format that begins with 'U+' in addition to the other formats more generally accepted in PCS (like hexadecimal, octal or decimal). That is, you may specify constants like U+1F600.
<b>Note</b>: If you want to add a space or a hash character with this command you cannot use the system variables Space or Hash because those are strings, but you may use this command to the same effect by supplying &lt;char_as_int&gt; as 32 (for space) or 35 (for the hash character: #).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">cat_str</span>
cat_str &lt;STR_VAR&gt; [[str1] [[str2] ...]]
This allows you to add a list of white-space separated words to the end of a user-defined variable of 'string' type.
&lt;STR_VAR&gt; - the string variable to be modified
[[str1] [[str2] ...]] - a list of between 0 and 24 string constants separated by white space. These will be added to the end of (concatenated to) the existing contents of &lt;STR_VAR&gt; 
<b>Note</b>: Up to 24 &lt;STR_VAR&gt; string arguments may be used in one command. If no [str] arguments are provided the command has no effect. The limitations on what arguments are permitted are the same as for the command set_str  (which see).
<b>Note</b>: Each [str] argument will be appended to the &lt;STR_VAR&gt; separated by a single space (ASCII character 32) regardless of whatever type or number of white space character(s) was used to separate arguments in the command. No space will be added between the initial value of &lt;STR_VAR&gt; and the first [str] argument. No space will be printed to the end of the list of argument strings. 
<b>Note</b>: Each [str] argument will be treated as a string constant and not a variable (of any data type). If a variable name is included in these arguments it will be treated just as a string and will not be evaluated (so the variable's name will be concatenated 'as is'). If you want to add the value contained in some variable to the end of a string then use the add_var command instead (which see).
<b>Note</b>: The interpreter has a limit on the maximum size of any argument. This is set by the MAX_ARGLEN value in the interpreter source code which, at the time of writing is 64 bytes. It is important to note that bytes do not necessarily equate to characters. While standard ASCII characters are 1 byte each, UTF-8 Unicode characters (such as box-drawing symbols or emojis) typically consume 3 or 4 bytes each. For example, the ═ symbol uses 3 bytes; therefore, a single argument can contain a maximum of 21 of these characters before exceeding the 64-byte limit.
<b>Caution</b>: Do not exceed MAX_ARGLEN bytes per argument. If a string exceeds this limit, the interpreter will truncate it at the byte level. This may result in <b>malformed UTF-8 sequences</b> for Unicode characters, likely causing the string to fail to render. For ASCII text, the string will simply be truncated.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">cli_path</span>
cli_path &lt;full_path_image_name_root&gt;
This allows you to input the string used to construct the common part of file names generated for saving the latest preview display image. It is mostly of use when running programs in CLI mode.
&lt;full_path_image_name_root&gt; is a string varval specifying a string without white space which will be used to construct the file name of the saved preview p6 ppm image. This string argument may be up to PCS_PATH_MAX bytes long.
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than PCS_PATH_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: This may include a path component if you want to store the image in a particular location but the path must not contain any white space.
<b>Note</b>: The full preview image name will be constructed as &lt;full_path_image_name_root&gt;_preview.ppm .
<b>Note</b>: Preview images will only start to be saved after you first call this command in a script. Images will be saved every time the preview update function is called in the run-engine (via the C function `update_img_preview_picture`). If you do not call this command in a script then no preview images will be automatically saved at all. The default position at the start of any script is to set this as 'un-called'.
<b>Note</b>: The image saved will be the final displayed preview image. This means the preview buffer plus any graphics overlay that may be imprinted onto it. If you want to save only the pure preview image or the pure graphics overlay buffer then see the separate command that allow for those to be saved ( and ).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">cmath_array</span>
cmath_array &lt;rec_re_array&gt; &lt;rec_im_array&gt; &lt;cx_op&gt; [&lt;mod_re_array&gt; &lt;mod_im_array&gt;]
This allows you to do complex maths with arrays (of type float). The index values you supply with the array arguments is respected according to the rules described below.
&lt;rec_re_array&gt; - the receptor real array to be modified
&lt;rec_im_array&gt; - the receptor imaginary array to be modified
&lt;cx_op&gt; - the operation to perform (must be a string varval)
&lt;mod_re_array&gt; - the modifier real array (only required for some operations)
&lt;mod_re_array&gt; - the modifier imaginary array (only required for some operations)
<b>Note</b>: Each array argument carries an index, e.g. my_array[idx]. The operation starts at the supplied index of each array and continues for as many cells as ALL the arrays involved (both receptors and, if used, both modifiers) have left from their respective indices to the end of the array, i.e. for the smallest of the residual cell counts. Cells before the supplied indices, and any cells beyond the processed range, are left untouched. For the cyclic shifts, the shift is applied to the processed range only.
<b>Note</b>: The options for &lt;cx_op&gt; are as shown below. Any operation that requires a modifier must have the modifier arrays (both of them) present or this will result in an error. In cases where no modifier arrays are required you may still supply them if you wish - they will just not get used.:
=	-  Assignment equals. Requires the modifiers.
add	- Complex addition. Requires the modifiers.
sub	- Complex subtraction. Requires the modifiers.
mul	- Complex multiplication.  Requires the modifiers.
div	- Complex division.  Requires the modifiers.
conj	- Complex conjugate. No modifiers used.
pow	- Spectral power. No modifiers used.
pol	- Convert to arg and mag.  No modifiers used.
cycf	- Cyclic re-arrangement (shift) "forwards".  No modifiers used.
cycb	- Cyclic re-arrangement (shift) "backwards".  No modifiers used.

<b>Note</b>: The general pattern implemented with this command is:
receptor = operation(receptor) 
or
receptor = receptor (operation) modifier
<b>Note</b>: As with math_var, the &lt;cx_op&gt; string may be supplied as a compile-time constant or as a variable / array member.
<b>Note</b>: Unlike the single variable version of this command (cmath_var) where the modifier arguments may be constants and not user variables, this array version will only allow modifier arguments to be user-defined arrays - not constants.
<b>Note</b>: The complex division done here is not naive. It uses Smith's algorithm inside Stewart-Baudin scaling thresholds and branch paths.
<b>Note</b>: For the polar coordinate transform, undefined phases are returned as 0.0 and phases may return -0.0 due to the use of atan2 in the implementation code.
<b>Note</b>: Aliasing is permitted (you can use the same variable for more than one argument with or without the same index in the various arguments).
<b>Note</b>: The designation "forwards" and "backwards" for the cyclic shifts is not some universal terminology so I will explain what I mean. I use "forward" and "backward" from the Fourier perspective (even if the shifts are applied to spatial domain signals). Forward takes the left-most pixel - the pixel at the lowest index (or top left pixel in the 2D case), which represents the DC offset after an FT - and puts it in the middle (so the spectrum is now in 'diffraction pattern order'). "Backward" does the opposite.
<b>Note</b>: These operations do not have any multi-dimensional-specific support. They may be used on arrays you are using for 2D data but anything requiring actual multi-dimensional specific features will not be respected. For example, the cyclic shift operations consider the array span in use to be 1-dimensional. See the fourier command for the 2D case.
<b>Note</b>: Only arrays of type float can be used with this command.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">cmath_var</span>
cmath_var &lt;rec_re&gt; &lt;rec_im&gt; &lt;cx_op&gt; [&lt;mod_re&gt; &lt;mod_im&gt;]
This allows you to do complex maths with single variables (of type float). See cmath_array for similar operations using array variables.
&lt;rec_re&gt; - the receptor real variable to be modified
&lt;rec_im&gt; - the receptor imaginary variable to be modified
&lt;cx_op&gt; - the operation to perform (must be a string varval)
&lt;mod_re&gt; - the modifier real varval (only required for some operations)
&lt;mod_re&gt; - the modifier imaginary varval (only required for some operations)
<b>Note</b>: The modifier arguments must be present when using an operation that requires them. They may also be present for other operations when they will be silently ignored. Modifier arguments may be constants (they do not have to be variables).
<b>Note</b>: The general pattern implemented with this command is:
receptor = operation(receptor) 
or
receptor = receptor (operation) modifier
<b>Note</b>: The &lt;cx_op&gt; options and meanings are the same as for cmath_array so see that section for more details.
<b>Note</b>: For this single variable command, the cyclic shift operators may be invoked but they will do nothing (they act as an identity operation).
<b>Note</b>: Only varvals of type float can be used as receptors or modifiers with this command.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">corrections_mask</span>
corrections_mask &lt;OFF|ON|image_file&gt; 
This determines whether to load and use a corrections mask for flat field and dark field correction during full frame image capture (separate commands exist for preview images).
&lt;OFF|ON|image_file&gt; - a string varval. This must evaluate to either the (case sensitive) strings ON or OFF or the file name (optionally to include the full path) of an image to be used as the mask image. File names and paths must not contain spaces. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: If you select to supply an image file for the mask it only loads the file and passes it to the server. It does not set the 'Use mask' flag to ON. Whether to use that file or not depends on you issuing this command again with a specific OFF or ON argument (it defaults to OFF before you first make a definitive choice).
<b>Note</b>: Permitted file formats for a corrections mask are pgm (P5), ppm (P6) or bmp (8bpp or 24bpp). For a file name to be acceptable it must have a ipgm, .ppm or .bmp extension (case is not important here).
<b>Note</b>: More detail about the use of a corrections mask can be found in the pardcap user manual.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">def_actuator</span>
def_actuator &lt;act_type&gt; &lt;act_index&gt; &lt;name_string&gt;
This defines a new actuator on the pardserver. The actuator structure will be formed in the nascent state with most of its members set to 0 or 'unset' or NULL but it will be given the name you supply here.
&lt;act_type&gt; - an integer defining the type of actuator you are attempting to create. It must have a value of one of these:
1 = Stepper
2 = Binary
3 = Mlevel
4 = Servo
5 = Sensor
&lt;act_index&gt; - an integer representing the actuator index of the actuator you are attempting to create. This which must have a value from 0 to the maximum number of allowed actuators of the type specified in &lt;act_type&gt;, minus 1. That maximum number is specified in the source file parddefs.h and is 10 for all actuator types at the time of writing this manual so the permitted range is 0 to 9 inclusive.
&lt;name_string&gt; - a string varval that will uniquely identify the newly created actuator. This must not have spaces and must be less than or equal to 31 printable bytes. if you supply a name with more characters then only the first 31 printable bytes will be used. Note that these are bytes which are not always the same as 'characters'. For example if you use UTF-8 Unicode then each character is 3 bytes long so counts as 3 bytes to your 31 byte limit.
<b>Note</b>: Upon successful completion the system variable CurrActIdx will be set equal to the index number of the newly created actuator and CurrActType will be set equal to the value you supplied as &lt;act_type&gt;.
<b>Note</b>: You will not be allowed to create an actuator with a name that is identical to the name of an existing actuator of any type (only the first 31 chars will be checked for identity). An actuator name must be at least one byte long. Otherwise there is no restriction on what you can name it (a number, a name that is identical to a system or user variable name, a name that is identical to a function name or a goto label or a command, etc. - all these are acceptable, even if not good programming practice, and will be distinguished at run-time by context). 
<b>Note</b>: You will not be allowed to define an actuator with an index number that is identical to the index number of an existing actuator of the same type as the type you are attempting to create.
<b>Note</b>: The local list of all defined actuators is automatically undefined at the start of each script check and script run session. This does not undefine any actuators that may have been set up on the pardserver and Arduino by previous script commands during any previous login as well as the current login (only when the Arduino is reset or reboots are those cleared). Thus it is good practice to call the command get_actuators at the start of any script to populate the locally held lists of actuators with whatever happens to be defined on the Arduino or, if you want to start from scratch, you can call the undef_actuator command in wildcard mode for each actuator type to ensure you have a clean sheet to work from.
<b>Note</b>: def_actuator and act_getname are the only two actuator commands that require you to input two separate numbers to identify an actuator instead of the single &lt;act_id&gt; argument (the latter is described under undef_actuator).
<b>Note</b>: A nascent actuator, such as is created by this command, will not be usable until it is 'matured' by the application of a series of commands to set a range of mandatory properties (using act_set). The rules as to what constitutes the 'range of mandatory properties' will differ for each type of actuator. Any attempt to use activation commands on an immature actuator will result in a script terminating with an 'immature activator' error message.
<b>Note</b>: For actuators of type 'Stepper', the range of mandatory motor struct members that must be set before it will be marked as 'mature' (and so be responsive to any kind of motion commands) are:
In all cases:
driver_type
pin_pul
pin_dir
pin_ena 
dirn1
dirn2
and at least 1 homing maturity condition:
If you want to do homing to a limit switch:
pin_lim1 and/or pin_lim2
If you want to do null homing you must set these members to a non-zero value:
nhs1
nhs0 and/or nhs2
In addition, all steppers using driver type A4988 or DRV8825 must have these set:
pin_ms1
pin_ms2
pin_ms3 
In the pardserver, each one of these essential settings contributes a maturity point of 1 to the motor struct's maturity member. Since there are 7 essential settings for steppers that use TMC drivers and 10 for those that use the non-TMC driver, a stepper actuator will only be considered mature by the pardserver if the total maturity score is &gt;= 7 (for TMC drivers) or &gt;= 10 (for non-TMC drivers). This maturity score is updated with every call to act_set so you can 'un-mature' a previously mature stepper (for example by un-setting a required pin).
<b>Note</b>: For a newly created stepper actuator, once mature, the only action that it can be commanded to perform is some form of homing - either homing to a physical limit switch (in which case you must have defined pin_lim1 and/or pin_lim2 depending of which limit switch you want to home it to) or null homing (in which case you must have set at least 2 of the nhs members to be &gt;0 as described above). Only after it is successfully homed can other motion commands be enacted.
<b>Note</b>: For actuators of type 'Binary', the mandatory struct members that must be set before it will be marked as 'mature' (and so be responsive to the act_set command for its state) are:
In all cases:
pin_bin 
on_val
off_val
The reason you must define the on and off values is that, when a binary actuator is has its state set to 1 (or some positive non-zero value) using the act_set command, the Arduino does not just set its pin 'HIGH'. Rather it sets its pin to the 'on_val' (which you must first have set to be either 1 or 0). Likewise for when you set its state to 0 the Arduino will actually set the pin to off_val and so you must first have defined off_val as being 1 or 0.
The reason for this extra layer of abstraction in binary actuators is that setting an Arduino digital pin to HIGH does not necessarily produce an effect that is intuitively associated with 'ON' or 'ACTIVATE'. For example you may have the pin connected to an NPN transistor switch circuit that turns current flow to some device on only when the Arduino pin signal is LOW. In that case it will make your PCS programs more intuitive to write (and read) and make programming errors less likely if you set the on_val to 0 and the off_val to 1. Usually on_val and off_val will be the complement of each other - but that choice is up to you. At some point in your program you might want to fix a binary actuator in a particular state regardless of whether the rest of the code tries to set its state to ON or OFF. In that case you can achieve this by simply setting the on_val == off_val == to whatever state to want to keep it in.
<b>Note</b>: For actuators of type 'M-level', the mandatory struct members that must be set before it will be marked as 'mature' (and so be responsive to the act_set command for its state) are:
In all cases:
ml_type (This must be set before any other member)
ml_pin[0] 
For PWM-type M-level actuators 
ml_tt (must have at least one entry set in its translation table)
<b>Note</b>: For actuators of type 'Servo', the mandatory struct members that must be set before it will be marked as 'mature' (and so be responsive to the act_set command for its state) are:
In all cases:
pin_srv 
<b>Note</b>: For actuators of type 'Sensor', the mandatory struct members that must be set before it will be marked as 'mature' (and so be responsive to the act_set command for its state) are:
In all cases:
type (This must be set before pin_sense)
pin_sense


<b>Example</b>:  Here is an example PCS script that defines a stepper motor actuator called 'X_Axis' and matures it up ready for homing:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Pardus pcs 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>verbosity 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#include stdpard.pch   </tt><tt>  </tt><tt># This contains the integer values mapped to the</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> capitalised tokens used in commands below</tt><tt>. Without</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt># such a header you would not be able to use (e.g.)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt># act_set CurrAct INFO_PIN_PUL  PUL_X</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt># but would have to write something like this instead</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                         </tt><tt># act_set CurrAct 19 22</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main</tt><tt> </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>get_actuators  # Update the </tt><tt>local list of </tt><tt>extant actuators </tt><tt>f</tt><tt>rom the server</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>def_actuator 1 0 X_Axis  # If this succeeds it will automatically set</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                          </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> CurrAct</tt><tt> to the ID of the newly formed actuator</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                          </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> but if it fails the script will terminate with an</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                          </tt><tt># error automatically.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt># All these are mandatory for maturity</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_PIN_PUL  PUL_X</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_PIN_DIR  DIR_X</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_PIN_ENA  ENA_X</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_DIRN1 DIRNLFU </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>a</tt><tt>ct_set CurrAct INFO_DIRN2 DIRNRBD</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt># Only one of these is essential for maturity (assuming we will be homing</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt># to a physical limit switch) but I chose to define them both:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_PIN_LIM1 OPTO_XL</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_PIN_LIM2 OPTO_XR</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># The motor is now ready to be homed but I will define the diag pin so we</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt># can make use of the TMC2209 StallGuard4 feature - this is optional</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_PIN_DIAG DIAG_X</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
By the end of this script, the X_Axis motor may legally be homed to one of its two physical limit switches. Only after successful homing can other motion commands be accepted. Note that you do not need to do the homing itself here - the changes you make with this script will be enacted by the pardserver and parduino to populate the motor struct. These changes will persist even after the script exits and even after you log off the server. This is one of the reasons why you should always perform a get_actuators command at the start of each script - so you know what actuators have already been defined. You can also use the act_get command with the INFO_MATURITY integer value argument to read how mature the motor is.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">def_array</span>
def_array &lt;vartype&gt; &lt;varname&gt; &lt;size&gt; &lt;initial_varval&gt;
This creates and initialises a user-defined 1-dimensional array of variables.
&lt;vartype&gt; - a string varval specifying the data type of the elements in the array. It must be one of these: int, float, string.
&lt;varname&gt; - a string constant defining the name of the array to be created. This is case sensitive and must not be already taken by an extant variable or array, whether a system variable or user-defined variable or array (no two arrays can have the same name as each other or as any single variable), it must not contain spaces or square brackets and must not be a protected name (see p.46).
&lt;size&gt; - a varval that evaluates to the number of elements in the array. It must evaluate to an integer &gt;= 1.
&lt;initial_varval&gt; - the value to initialise every element in the new array to. This must be a value that is compatible with the data type provided as the &lt;vartype&gt; argument.
Note: When the new variable is a string, &lt;initial_varval&gt; is cast to a string as follows. A string variable or string constant is copied as it is. An int or float variable (or system value) is written out as a number, an int in whole digits and a float with up to 9 significant figures (so a float holding 1.5 gives “1.5”). A constant that you write in the script is copied exactly as you wrote it, even if it looks like a number: `def_var string s 007` gives “007” (not "7"), `def_var string s 1.50` gives “1.50” (not "1.5"). The same rule applies to def_array with a string &lt;vartype&gt;.
<b>Note</b>: All arrays are global but can only be used from the point in the script at which they are  defined. They can no longer be used <b><i>as variables</i></b> after they are destroyed by a call to undef_var (which see). Thus arrays can have manually managed temporal scope. See p.39 for more details on variable scope.
<b>Note</b>: The qualifier '<b><i>as variables</i></b>' in the note above is important because you may still use the name of an undefined (non-existent) variable in a script but if you do this name (and its dereferencing bracket expression) will simply be treated as a string constant argument rather than a variable that can be evaluated. This might be perfectly legal depending on the context and so might not raise any syntax error during the checking phase or runtime error. However, you may get undesired behaviour during run-time if you do this inadvertently. For example the following code will just print 'myarr[3]' as a string literal and will not print the value '20' - there will be no errors or warning because this is perfectly legal PCS code:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_</tt><tt>array</tt><tt> </tt><tt>int </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr</tt><tt> </tt><tt>5 </tt><tt>20</tt><tt> # </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr is brought into existence (all values = 20)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>undef_var </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr         </tt><tt> </tt><tt># </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr ceases to exist as an array variable.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>print_value </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr[3]</tt><tt> 2</tt><tt>  </tt><tt> </tt><tt># '</tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr[3]' is treated as a string literal.</tt> </span>
If you comment out the undef_var line, then the output would be '20'. This works without error because print_value does not require its argument to be a variable (although it may be). Other commands have more stringent criteria for their arguments such as set_var which expects its first argument to be a variable that can be written to. So this script would not pass even the syntax checker:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_</tt><tt>array</tt><tt> </tt><tt>int </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr</tt><tt> </tt><tt>5 </tt><tt>20</tt><tt> # </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr is brought into existence (all values = 20)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>undef_var </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr      </tt><tt>    </tt><tt># </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr ceases to exist as an array variable.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var</tt><tt> </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr[3]</tt><tt> 2</tt><tt>   </tt><tt>    </tt><tt># ERROR: </tt><tt>my</tt><tt>arr[3] is not a writeable variable.</tt> </span>

<b>Note</b>: PCS supports runtime dynamic allocation for arrays. The &lt;size&gt; argument in def_array may be a variable that is evaluated at the moment of execution of the def_array command. This allows you to create arrays whose size is determined by previous calculations or data inputs. The maximum size of an array is limited only by the available system RAM. However, for high-reliability robotics, it is recommended to keep array sizes within predictable bounds to avoid RAM allocation failure errors at runtime.
<b>Note</b>: The size of an array may also be changed at run time by undefining the array (with the command undef_var) and then re-defining the array with the new size. Undefining the array variable will delete all its current contents so if you want to re-size an array without loosing its contents you must first make a backup copy (e.g. in another array variable) before calling undef_var and then restore the contents to the newly (re)created array from your backup.
<b>Note</b>: All variables are treated as arrays in PCS so variables created using def_var are just arrays of size 1.
<b>Note</b>: Array members are accessed using a square bracket syntax like this:
my_array[&lt;index_varval&gt;]
These are the rules for indexing (accessing) members of an array with this system:
The value of &lt;index_varval&gt; must be reducible to a non-negative integer that is within the bounds of the array.
Array indices always start from 0 (as in the C programming language)
As per the above rules the legal, 'in-bounds' range for an index value is from 0 to &lt;size&gt;-1 inclusive. Anything more than this will results in an error that will terminate the script.
The &lt;index_varval&gt; may be a constant number or a variable, including another array variable with an index of its own.
The value of &lt;index_varval&gt;, if not natively of type int, will be automatically attempted to be cast to an integer using the PCS rules of (casting described on p.14). This means that even string variables may be used to index an array provided that the string value can be cast to an integer with a value in the bounds of the array.
It is forbidden to use expressions as an index value. For example, the following are illegal and will result in an error that will terminate the script or fail the initial script-checking phase:
my_array[3 + 1]
my_array[ind1[idx] != idx]
It is forbidden to use any indexing brackets at all in an array that is of &lt;size&gt; 1. This includes all variables created with the def_var command. In all such cases the following will be illegal:
def_array int my_array 1 0
set_var my_array[0] 3    # This is illegal
# The correct way is simply to use it like a
# a scalar variable, thus:
set_var my_array 3       # This is correct.
It is forbidden to use the name of an array of &lt;size&gt; greater than 1 on its own, without square brackets. So this would be illegal:
def_array int my_array 5 0
set_var my_array 3    # This is illegal
<b>Note</b>: While only 1-dimensional arrays are permitted (i.e. you cannot do things like my_array[idx][idy]) you can always use your own raster indexing system to use a 1-dimensional array as if it were a multi-dimensional array. Just bear in mind that any calculations of the index value must be done separately and stored in a variable before using that index value in the array brackets. For example, to implement an equivalent of a 2D array of size2d=height*width, you can create a 1-D array of &lt;size&gt; = size2d. Then, where idy is the y coordinate and idx is the x coordinate, you may access the value at position (x,y) in the array by doing something like this:
set_var posxy idy
mul_var posxy width
add_var posxy idx
set_var my_array[posxy]  # Here, posxy = y*width + x
<b>Note</b>: In general, problems may occur if you use def_var, def_array or undef_var inside loops or blocks of code that are conditionally accessed (by do_if, else or any block that is the target of a conditional jump etc.). Such usage is not forbidden and may well pass the syntax checking phase (where conditions are not evaluated and jumps or loops are not enacted) but may results in errors or unforeseen behaviour during run time. Sometimes such placement may be desirable but it is left to the programmer to determine when this is appropriate according to their intended logic. For example, it is almost never a good idea to use these commands inside a loop_while loop but it might be acceptable if you need to read large amounts of data into an array from a file and the size of that array might need to change on each iteration of the loop. In such cases using def_array for dynamic allocation of the array to the appropriate size followed, after reading and using the data, by undef_var before the loop repeats might be a resource efficient way to code this and would be perfectly safe provided the loop can never be exited by a non-local jump that comes between def_array and undef_var.
<b>Note</b>: Because all variables, even single variables, can be created with def_array, this makes def_var superfluous. While def_var is technically superfluous, it is kept as an option because it makes the PCS script coder's intention clear and makes it clear that any variable defined with it cannot be indexed with square brackets.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">def_func</span>
def_func &lt;function_label&gt;
This defines the entry point line to a function. 
&lt;function_label&gt; - a string constant (not a variable), which must be unique to any function in the script and must not contain white space.
<b>Note</b>: There is no equivalent of 'declaring' a function separately to defining it. The def_func line
must be the first line of the actual function itself.
<b>Note</b>: Every def_func command starts a code block that must be terminated with one - and only one - return command. In between the def_func line and its return line you may have any legitimate script text except that you cannot have another def_func command (this implies that functions cannot be nested inside other functions).
<b>Note</b>: If a script contains any functions at all it must define a function called main before any other function is defined. When main returns, the script ends.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">def_tracker</span>
def_tracker &lt;tracker_type&gt; &lt;tracker_name&gt; &lt;template_dim&gt; &lt;search_dim&gt; &lt;cell_dim&gt;
This creates a tracker to track objects on the VGA canvas of the current preview buffer. Trackers follow the position of a target defined by a square template window of &lt;template_dim&gt; x &lt;template_dim&gt; pixels. The zmncc, hog, com and ght trackers look for the target in a square search area of &lt;search_dim&gt; x &lt;search_dim&gt; pixels centred on the most recently identified template position, while the klt tracker uses the whole VGA canvas (and ignores &lt;search_dim&gt;).
&lt;tracker_type&gt; - a string varval specifying the type of tracker (its algorithm). It must be exactly one of these (lower case): com, ght, hog, klt, zmncc.
&lt;tracker_name&gt; - a string varval defining the name of the tracker to be created. This is case sensitive and must not be already taken by an extant tracker. It must be at least 1 printable character long and must not be a proper number (integer or float).
&lt;template_dim&gt; - an integer varval that is the length of one side of the square target template window. It must evaluate to an integer &gt;= 16.
&lt;search_dim&gt; - an integer varval that is the length of one side of the square search area. It must evaluate to an integer &gt; &lt;template_dim&gt; and &lt;= 480. The klt tracker ignores the value but it must still be supplied and obey these rules. For more specific information about the allowed ranges, see the notes below.
&lt;cell_dim&gt; - an integer varval that is the length of one side of a histogram of oriented gradients (HOG) cell. For the hog tracker it must be one of 8, 16 or 32. For all other tracker types it is not used but it must still be supplied (to keep the argument count correct) and it must be an integer &gt;= 8.
<b>Note</b>: The name of a tracker has more liberal rules than for a variable. No rules are imposed other than those described above. This means you may use the names of existing variables, system variables, punctuation marks (like '.') or even PCS commands - the run-engine will not get confused because it interprets by context. However, using any of those former examples may well confuse a human code reader so are best avoided as a point of good programming practice.
<b>Note</b>: The name and type of a tracker may be supplied in string variables (similar to how the names of actuators may be supplied). This differs from the rules for defining the names of variables and arrays where only string constants are allowed (a variable's type may be supplied as a variable, but not its name).
<b>Note</b>: The &lt;tracker_type&gt; argument string refers to the following algorithms:
com - Centre-of-Mass. Follows the centroid (the intensity-weighted centre) of the bright pixels (or the dark pixels, see TKSET_COM_POLARITY) in the search area.
ght - Generalised Hough Transform (translation only). Matches the pattern of strong edges of the template. It does not allow for rotation or size change.
hog - Histogram of Oriented Gradients using 9 bins for orientation. Matches the pattern of edge directions of the template.
klt - Sparse pyramidal Kanade-Lucas-Tomasi with background compensation. Follows corner features and cancels background motion.
zmncc - Zero-Mean Normalised Cross correlation. Matches the brightness pattern of the template (which adapts slowly to changes).
<b>Note</b>: Argument range rules applied for specific algorithms are as follows:
hog:
&lt;template_dim&gt; must be an exact multiple of &lt;cell_dim&gt; and it must be at least twice the size of &lt;cell_dim&gt;.
&lt;search_dim&gt; must be &gt;= &lt;template_dim&gt; + &lt;cell_dim&gt;.
zmncc, com and ght:
&lt;search_dim&gt; must be at least &lt;template_dim&gt; + 8 pixels so that there is a little room for the template window to move.
klt:
No additional rule: &lt;search_dim&gt; is not used.
<b>Note</b>: A new tracker is not yet able to track: it has no target until you use init_tracker. Its status flag is TRKS_UNSEEDED until then.
<b>Note</b>: Trackers have certain parameters that may be adjusted for optimum use with various target types. Upon creation, a new tracker is given a set of default values for those parameters but you can change them using the set_tracker command (which see). A tracker's type cannot be changed.
<b>Note</b>: A tracker holds memory until you destroy it with undef_tracker. A klt tracker uses several megabytes.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">def_var</span>
def_var &lt;data_type&gt; &lt;varname&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
This creates and initialises a user-defined variable.
&lt;data_type&gt; - a string varval specifying the data type of the variable and must be one of these: int, float, string.
&lt;varname&gt; - a string constant defining the name of the variable to be created. This is case sensitive and must not be already taken by an extant variable or array, whether a system variable or user-defined variable (no two variables can have the same name), it must not contain spaces or square brackets and must not be a protected name (see p.46).
&lt;varval&gt; - the value to initialise the new variable to. This must be a value that is compatible with the data type of the new variable.
Note: When the new variable is a string, &lt;varval&gt; is cast to a string as follows. A string variable or string constant is copied as it is. An int or float variable (or system value) is written out as a number, an int in whole digits and a float with up to 9 significant figures (so a float holding 1.5 gives “1.5”). A constant that you write in the script is copied exactly as you wrote it, even if it looks like a number: `def_var string s 007` gives “007” (not "7"), `def_var string s 1.50` gives “1.50” (not "1.5"). The same rule applies to def_array with a string &lt;vartype&gt;.
<b>Note</b>: All variables are global but can only be used from the point in the script at which they are  defined. They can no longer be used <b><i>as variables</i></b> after they are destroyed by a call to undef_var (which see). Thus arrays can have manually managed temporal scope. See p.39 for more details on variable scope.
<b>Note</b>: The qualifier '<b><i>as variables</i></b>' in the note above is important because you may still use the name of an undefined (non-existent) variable in a script but if you do this name will simply be treated as a string constant argument rather than a variable that can be evaluated. This might be perfectly legal depending on the context and so might not raise any syntax error during the checking phase or runtime error. However, you may get undesired behaviour during run-time if you do this inadvertently. For example the following code will just print 'myvar' as a string literal and will not print the value '20' - there will be no errors or warning because this is perfectly legal PCS code:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var </tt><tt>int </tt><tt>myvar 20</tt><tt>   # myvar is brought into existence with a value 20</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>undef_var myvar</tt><tt>      </tt><tt>  </tt><tt># myvar ceases to exist as a variable.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>print_value myvar 2</tt><tt>  </tt><tt>  </tt><tt># myvar is treated as a string literal.</tt> </span>
If you comment out the undef_var line, then the output would be '20'. This works without error because print_value does not require its argument to be a variable (although it may be). Other commands have more stringent criteria for their arguments such as set_var which expects its first argument to be a variable that can be written to. So this script would not pass even the syntax checker:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var </tt><tt>int </tt><tt>myvar 20</tt><tt>   # myvar is brought into existence with a value 20</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>undef_var myvar</tt><tt>    </tt><tt>    </tt><tt># myvar ceases to exist as a variable.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var</tt><tt> myvar 2</tt><tt>    </tt><tt>    </tt><tt># ERROR: myvar is not a writeable variable.</tt> </span>

<b>Note</b>: All variables are treated as arrays in PCS so variables created using def_var are just arrays of size 1. Arrays of size 1 (whether created by def_var or def_array) cannot use square brackets to 'dereference' or 'index' them - i.e. you cannot do this: my_var[0]. See the section on def_array for more about indexing rules.
<b>Note</b>: In general, problems may occur if you use def_var, def_array or undef_var inside loops or blocks of code that are conditionally accessed (by do_if, else or any block that is the target of a conditional jump etc.). Such usage is not forbidden and may well pass the syntax checking phase (where conditions are not evaluated and jumps or loops are not enacted) but may results in errors or unforeseen behaviour during run time. Sometimes such placement may be desirable but it is left to the programmer to determine when this is appropriate according to their intended logic. For example, it is almost never a good idea to use these commands inside a loop_while loop but it might be acceptable if you need to read large amounts of data into an array from a file and the size of that array might need to change on each iteration of the loop. In such cases using def_array for dynamic allocation of the array to the appropriate size followed, after reading and using the data, by undef_var before the loop repeats might be a resource efficient way to code this and would be perfectly safe provided the loop can never be exited by a non-local jump that comes between def_array and undef_var.
<b>Note</b>: While def_var is technically superfluous, it is kept as an option because it makes the PCS script coder's intention clear and makes it clear that any variable defined with it cannot be indexed with square brackets.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">df_correction</span>
df_correction &lt;OFF|ON|image_file&gt; 
This determines whether to load and use a dark field correction image for full frame image capture (separate commands exist for preview images).
&lt;OFF|ON|image_file&gt; - a string varval. This must evaluate to either the (case sensitive) strings ON or OFF or the file name (optionally to include the full path) of an image to be used as the dark field correction image. File names and paths must not contain spaces. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: If you select to supply an image file this command only loads the file and passes it to the server. It does not set the 'Use df correction' flag to ON. Whether to use that file or not depends on you issuing this command again with a specific OFF or ON argument (it defaults to OFF before you first make a definitive choice).
<b>Note</b>: A dark field correction image can only be of raw doubles format but may be mono (specify the a file name ending in _y.dou or _i.dou) or colour (specify one file name of a triplet that end in _r.dou, _g.dou, _b.dou. For example if you have these files on disc: mydfimg_r.dou, mydfimg_g.dou, mydfimg_b.dou then you could use mydfimg_g.dou as your file name argument here.). Ensure each file has its associated .qih external header file also on disc. 
<b>Note</b>: More detail about the use of a dark field image can be found in the pardcap user manual. More information about raw doubles format images (the .dou and .qih conventions) can be found in the Biaram user manual.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">div_var</span>
div_var &lt;varname&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
This divides the value of a user-defined variable by a value. 
&lt;varname&gt; - The name of the variable whose value you want to change
&lt;varval&gt; - The value (or variable containing the value) you want to divide &lt;varname&gt; by.
<b>Note</b>: This is symbolically equivalent to &lt;varname&gt; /= &lt;varval&gt; in C.
<b>Note</b>: Divide by zero will result in script termination with an error.
<b>Note</b>: Division can also be done inline using math_var (which see).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">do_if</span>
do_if &lt;varval&gt;
When the script encounters do_if command it evaluates the &lt;varval&gt; argument. If that has a value of 0 the program jumps to the associated else line if one exists otherwise it jumps directly to the associated enddo line.
&lt;varval&gt; - a binary choice varval. If it evaluates to 0 (or OFF or FALSE) then the do_if block is skipped. If it evaluates to non-zero (or ON or TRUE) then the do_if block is entered.
<b>Note</b>: It is often useful (but not mandatory) to use the do_if functionality in combination with conditional expressions. See set_condexp for more information on how to use conditional expressions.
<b>Note</b>:
Every do_if must have one, and only one, associated enddo command.
Every do_if may (optionally) have one, and only one, associated else command.
An else must always come before the line containing the associated enddo
You can have nested do_if block up to a maximum of INT_MAX deep if your available computer resources allow it.
<b>Note: </b>The main difference between a do_if and a loop_while is that do_if specifies a one-off execution of its block of code but a loop_while must always re-assess its condition before moving on (unless the user breaks out of the loop with a goto which is one way how a loop_while can be used as a kind of do_if - but without the added convenience of an optional associated else). So, while all the logical branching functionality of the if/else system can be achieved with loop_while plus other lines of code, the if/else system does the same thing but with fewer lines of code and makes the control of flow easier to follow by a human reader.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">else</span>
else
This is an optional component of the PCS do_if ... enddo code block. See do_if for details.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">enddo</span>
enddo
This is a mandatory component of the PCS do_if ... enddo code block. See do_if for details.
Note: A do_if command does not have to be in the same loop block or even the same function block as its matching enddo. This code is perfectly legal:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int idx 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>loop_while TRUE</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>do_if idx</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>print_value idx 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>sub_var idx 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>endloop</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func close_if</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>enddo</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>
and will output:
3
2
1


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">endloop</span>
endloop
This is a mandatory component of the PCS loop_while ... endloop code block. See loop_while for details.
<b>Note</b>: A loop_while command does not have to be in the same function as its terminating endloop. This code is perfectly legal:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int idx 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>loop_while idx</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>print_value idx 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt><tt>call_func close_loop</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func close_loop</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>sub_var idx 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>endloop</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>
and will output:
3
2
1


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">eval_char</span>
eval_char &lt;string&gt; &lt;position&gt; &lt;receptacle&gt;
This allows the user to extract the integer byte value of any character in a character string and put it into the receptacle variable. That value will be in the range 0 to 255.
&lt;string&gt; - a string that may be a string constant or a string variable.
&lt;position&gt; - a varval that must evaluate to a non-negative integer that is not greater than the number of printable characters in &lt;string&gt; minus 1 (so if &lt;string&gt; contains Hello then &lt;position&gt; must be no greater than 4). The position is based on a zero being the first character in a string.
&lt;receptacle&gt; - a writeable (user) variable that may hold an integer value.
<b>Note</b>: This is for byte-level exploration of strings held in memory. For byte-level parsing of file IO data see fset_var and fput_var.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">execute_daemon</span>
execute_daemon &lt;progname&gt; [&lt;arg1&gt; ...]
This allows the user to execute a custom command or external program on the client computer asynchronously. This method allows the command to execute without the script stalling or waiting for the command to complete execution, facilitating rapid parallel processing (the client computer processes the background task while the script carries on running).
It is managed natively by an internal, asynchronous process queue within the PARDUS interpreter engine. It limits the number of simultaneous parallel background tasks to the current value set by the max_daemons command (which see) to safeguard the CPU and memory from being overloaded. If the execution pool is full, extra commands are placed securely in a lightweight memory queue and execute automatically the when the GUI loop next runs and an active background task finishes.
&lt;programe&gt; - a string which is the name of the executable file of the command to pass to the operating system's process manager.
[&lt;arg1&gt; ...] - an optional list of space-separated arguments for the program to be executed. If any of the arguments to be passed is the name of the most recently saved image, this must be specified within the argument list as [img] (case sensitive and including the square brackets). The PARDUS script interpreter will automatically replace [img] with the absolute, clean path name of the last saved image. If tissue content thresholding is active and no image was captured due to lack of tissue content, the [img] symbol will be replaced with the literal text NoTissue. Your external program should check for this specific NoTissue string to handle tissue-less iterations gracefully. If no image is saved yet at the time of reaching this command then [img] will be replaced with an empty string.
<b>Note</b>: For example if the name of the last saved image was myframe_0342_rgb.bmp in folder C:\msys64\home\parduser and you specify the execute_daemon command as this:
       execute_daemon myprog.exe  -i [img] -o outfile.txt
then the Pardus script processor will convert that into:
      myprog.exe -i C:\msys64\home\parduser\myframe_0342_rgb.bmp -o outfile.txt
and pass that to the Pardus daemon. If tissue thresholding prevents an image being captured then it will be: 
      myprog.exe -i NoTissue -o outfile.txt
<b>Note</b>: On MS Windows (even when running from an MSYS2 MINGW shell) do not use "./" i.e. NOT "./prog.exe", or any other *nix-style paths but use Windows style paths or just "prog.exe" if the program to execute is in the same directory as the PARDUS client program. Full paths to the executable must be specified in the MS Windows style i.e. C:\msys64\home\parduser\prog.exe
<b>Note</b>: Apart from [img] all the other arguments are used 'as is' - they are not interpreted or resolved or cast to anything. So, for example, if you use the name of a variable like myvar1 this will be used as the literal string 'myvar1' and it will not be evaluated.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">execute_system</span>
execute_system &lt;progname&gt; [&lt;arg1&gt; ...]
The execute_system command attempts to execute a custom command directly via the host operating system. Unlike execute_daemon, this command runs synchronously: script execution will pause while the target program executes, continuing only after the program terminates. No checks are made as to whether the custom command executes correctly or exits with an error. However, upon completion, the PCS system variable Sysval will be assigned the integer exit code returned by the executed program. Script authors can read Sysval to handle program success, failure, or custom termination states. If the program fails to start or crashes abruptly, Sysval will be assigned a value of -1.
&lt;programe&gt; - a string which is the name of the executable file of the command to pass to the command processor
[&lt;arg1&gt; ...] - an optional list of arguments for the program to be executed. The details are the same as for the command execute_daemon (which see).
<b>Note</b>: On MS Windows (even when running from MSYS2 MINGW shell) do not use "./" i.e. NOT "./prog.exe", or any other *nix-style paths but use Windows style paths or just "prog.exe" if the program to execute is in the same directory as the PARDUS client program. Full paths to the executable must be specified in MS Windows style i.e. C:\msys64\home\parduser\prog.exe 
<b>Note</b>: Unlike older legacy engines, this command executes binaries directly without spawning an intermediary shell processor (like /bin/sh or cmd.exe). This significantly increases execution speed and completely eliminates shell-injection vulnerabilities.
<b>Note</b>: Apart from [img] all the other arguments are used 'as is' - they are not interpreted or resolved or cast to anything. So, for example, if you use the name of a variable like myvar1 this will be used as the literal string 'myvar1' and it will not be evaluated.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">exit</span>
exit
This terminates the script. All scripts and header files must have an exit command to mark the end of coding lines. The script checker will reject the script or header file if it does not find one. An exit command must be present even in scripts that use formal functions where the return of the function main will effectively act as an exit point at run time.
<b>Note</b>: The interpreter does not check for arguments for the exit command. So if you supply arguments they will simply be silently ignored, not flagged as a syntax error.
<b>Note</b>: The interpreter stops parsing and interpreting as soon as it comes across exit. So if you put an exit command in a loop or function, all the code after it will be considered free text comments, not code. This means that an exit inside a loop results in an unclosed loop error at syntax check time. Likewise an exit inside a function results in an unclosed function error at syntax check time (e.g. "Syntax error. Function main (opened on line 11) has no 'return'.") because, as far as the interpreter is concerned, any command you type after that exit line (like endloop or return) simply does not exist.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">fbyte_mode</span>
fbyte_mode &lt;varval&gt;
This is a latching command. If this is set to ON/TRUE/non-zero then, when integer variables are being dealt with via fset_var (or fput_var), the file will be read (or written) 1 byte at a time and into (or from) the integer variable - each integer variable value will be treated as an unsigned char. In the case of fput_var the lowest 8 bits of the integer variable will be written to the file as an unsigned char. The default value for fbyte_mode at the start of any script run is 0 (OFF).
&lt;varval&gt; - a binary choice varval. 


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">ff_correction</span>
ff_correction &lt;OFF|ON|image_file&gt; 
This determines whether to load and use a flat field correction image in full frame image capture (separate commands exist for preview images).
&lt;OFF|ON|image_file&gt; - a string varval. This must evaluate to either the (case sensitive) strings ON or OFF or the file name (optionally to include the full path) of an image to be used as the flat field correction image. File names and paths must not contain spaces. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: If you select to supply an image file this command only loads the file and passes it to the server. It does not set the 'Use ff correction' flag to ON. Whether to use that file or not depends on you issuing this command again with a specific OFF or ON argument (it defaults to OFF before you first make a definitive choice).
<b>Note</b>: A flat field correction image can only be pgm (P5), ppm (P6), bmp, or raw doubles (mono or colour). For a file name to be acceptable it must have an extension or name ending appropriate to its format (case is not important here) and must be one of: .pgm, .ppm, .bmp, _y.dou, _i.dou, _r.dou, _g.dou, _b.dou . For more information about the use of raw doubles images see the section on df_correction. 
<b>Note</b>: More detail about the use of a flat field image can be found in the pardcap user manual. More information about raw doubles format images (the .dou and .qih conventions) can be found in the Biaram user manual.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">fput_var</span>
fput_var &lt;varname&gt; &lt;append?&gt; &lt;file&gt;
This reads data from the variable &lt;varname&gt; and writes it to a file. The PCS system variable NWrites is set to the number of data items written into the file from the variable or to a negative number if there was an error. Note: The NBytes system variable reports the total number of raw bytes written to the file during the execution of fput_var. It is calculated as the difference between the file position before and after the writing phase.
&lt;varname&gt; - the name of the variable to be read. 
&lt;append?&gt; - an binary choice varval that specifies whether the file to be written to should be created (and that will overwrite and pre-existing file with that name) or appended to. If the append option is selected and the file does not exist then the file will be created.
&lt;file&gt; - a string varval that evaluates to the name (optionally to include the full path) of the file to be written to. File names and paths must not contain spaces. Purely numerical file names will only be accepted if they are presented inside string variables (see note under fset_var for details). This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the &lt;file&gt; argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to access them.
<b>Note</b>: If &lt;varname&gt; is an array of size &gt; 1 then ensure you specify the start point with the usual PCS square brackets dereferencing syntax. Data will be read from the array from that point onwards till then end of the array. If &lt;varname&gt; is a single variable then only one data item will be read from it into the file (you should not use dereferencing brackets for single variables and that include variables that were defined as an array but with a size of 1).
<b>Note</b>: The file writing process will stop when one of the following conditions is encountered:
The end of the variable or array is reached
There is an error accessing or writing to the file
In the case of an file write error, the script will not terminate but the system variable NWrites will be set to a negative value. So the programmer should check the value of NWrites after each call to fput_var for good defensive programming. Here are the meanings of the value of NWrites after a call to the fput_var command:
-1 = access denied (fopen failed).
-2 = This should never occur.
-3 = file IO error (ferror was set).
-4 = the default value at the start of any script. Thus this will be the value before any call to fput_var is made or, after any call to fput_var, it means 'Error, not otherwise specified'.
&gt;= 0  = The number of data items written into &lt;file&gt;.
<b>Note</b>: The data type and content of the file will be assumed to be the same as the data type of &lt;varname&gt; according to the following rules:
&lt;varname&gt; is of type string: A 'data item' will be defined as a character string. Thus the file will be written as a text file of strings. Only the data in each string variable is output. If you wish your output to be separated by white space or have a new line character at the end then it is your responsibility to add that separator to the end of your string variable prior to calling the fput_var command or consider using the print_file and print_value commands for more managed (formatted) output of text to a file.
&lt;varname&gt; is of type float: A 'data item' will be defined as double (usually 64 bits on most PCs). The file will then be written as a raw binary file.
&lt;varname&gt; is of type int: A 'data item' will be defined as an int32_t and the file will then be written as a raw binary file. The foregoing is the default behaviour that occurs at the start of any script run. However, the size of a 'data item' may be changed to a single byte by means of the fbyte_mode command. If byte mode is enabled, only the lowest 8 bits (the least significant byte) of each int are written to the file as an unsigned char. This allows for the creation of binary files with byte-level precision.
<b>Note</b>: See also fset_var and  fbyte_mode.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">frame_number</span>
frame_number &lt;number&gt;
This sets the value of the frame counter number for the file name of the next captured image. Each image captured by the frame grabber will have a number as part of its file name - this is the frame number.
&lt;number&gt; - an integer which sets the frame number. The frame number in the name of a captured image file will start from this value and increment by one with each subsequent image captured (until and unless this value is changed by the using this command again). See the grab_image and xyzs_scan commands for more information on the names of captured image files.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">frames_to_average</span>
frames_to_average &lt;number&gt;
This sets the number of frames to average when a grab_image command is executed.
&lt;number&gt; - an integer, &gt;= 1, that specifies the number of frames to average.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">frames_to_skip</span>
frames_to_skip &lt;skip&gt;
This sets the number of frames for the frame grabber to grab and discard prior to saving a frame when the grab_image command is issued. This allows any frames stored in the grabber's frame buffer cache to be expunged to ensure that only the most recently 'seen' frames are grabbed.
&lt;number&gt; - an integer, &gt;= 0, that specifies the number of frames to skip.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">fset_var</span>
fset_var &lt;varname&gt; &lt;skip&gt; &lt;file&gt;
This reads data from a file and puts the values it reads into the variable &lt;varname&gt;. The PCS system variable NReads is set to the number of data items read into the variable from the file or to a negative number if there was an error. The NBytes system variable is set to the number of bytes consumed from the file after the &lt;skip&gt; process. The NewLines system variable reports the number of logical line endings encountered on the input stream immediately following a string-based fset_var operation. See notes below for more details.
&lt;varname&gt; - the name of the variable to be set. 
&lt;skip&gt; - an integer, &gt;= 0, that specifies the number of <b><i>data items</i></b> to skip.
&lt;file&gt; - a string varval that evaluates to the name (optionally to include the full path) of the file to be read. File names and paths must not contain spaces. Purely numerical file names will only be accepted if they are presented inside string variables (see note below). This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: Due to the PCS autocasting rules, string literal file names cannot be numbers because string literals that can resolve to a numerical value will always be treated as a number (with a few exceptions detailed elsewhere in this manual). So literal names like 123 or 123.4 will be rejected but a file called 123.d will be accepted if the name is supplied as a literal constant because that will not resolve to a numerical value by the PCS autocast engine. However, files with purely numerical names can be read if those names are contained inside string variables and you pass the variable's name to the command. So this is perfectly legal and a file called "123" will be read and processed (if it exists) as shown in the following example:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_array string sa 2 _</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var string fname 123</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>fset_var sa[0] 0 fname</tt> </span>
but this will throw an error at script check time:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_array string sa 2 _</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>fset_var sa[0] 0 </tt><tt>123</tt> </span>
of the type "fset_var: 123 is not a string variable."
<b>Note</b>: If the &lt;file&gt; argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: If &lt;varname&gt; is an array of size &gt; 1 then ensure you specify the entry point with the usual PCS square brackets dereferencing syntax. Data will be read into the array from that point onwards. If &lt;varname&gt; is a single variable then only one data item will be read into it (you should not use dereferencing brackets for single variables and that includes variables that were defined as an array but with a size of 1).
<b>Note</b>: The file reading process will stop when one of the following conditions is encountered:
The file runs out of data (EOF)
There is an error accessing or reading from the file
The variable runs out of space to accept new data
In the case of a file read error, the script will not terminate but the system variable NReads will be set to a negative number. So the programmer should check the value of NReads after each call to fset_var for good defensive programming. Here are the meanings of the value of NReads after a call to the fset_var command:
-1 = access denied (fopen failed).
-2 = file is shorter than the &lt;skip&gt; number of data items.
-3 = file read error (ferror was set).
-4 = the default value at the start of any script. Thus this will be the value before any call to fset_var is made or, after any call to fset_var, it means 'Error, not otherwise specified'.
&gt;= 0  = The number of data items read into &lt;varname&gt;.
<b>Note</b>: The data type and content of the file will be assumed to be the same as the data type of &lt;varname&gt; according to the following rules:
&lt;varname&gt; is of type string: A 'data item' will be defined as a character string separated from any other data items by white space. Thus the file will be scanned looking for strings separated by white space (not by any other delimiters) and each such string will count as 1 data item for the purposes of reading (and also for the purposes of skipping &lt;skip&gt; data items before assignments begin). During the &lt;skip&gt; phase, there is no limit on the size of strings skipped. During the reading phase there is a limit put on the size of each string read into each element of &lt;varname&gt; of 1023 non-white-space characters. If a string is encountered that is longer than this, then the first 1023 characters will be read into the current location in &lt;varname&gt; (i.e. it will read a truncated version of that string) and an error warning will be printed to the script console but the program will not terminate and NReads will not go negative. The rest of the truncated string will be ignored and the next data item in the file will be read to the next position in &lt;varname&gt; (if it is an array of of size &gt; 1). For &lt;skip&gt; purposes (but not reading purposes) the definition of a 'data item' can be modified by the fskip_size command. See below for details.
&lt;varname&gt; is of type float: A 'data item' will be defined as double (usually 64 bits on most PCs). The file will then be treated as a raw binary file and read as such - each 64 bits of the file being read as a double. For &lt;skip&gt; purposes (but not reading purposes) the definition of a 'data item' can be modified by the fskip_size command. See below for details.
&lt;varname&gt; is of type int: A 'data item' will be defined an int32_t and the file will then be treated as a raw binary file and read as such - each 32 bits of the file being read as an int32_t.  For &lt;skip&gt; purposes (but not reading purposes) the definition of a 'data item' can be modified by the fskip_size command. See below for details. For reading purposes the size of a 'data item' may be changed to a single byte by means of the fbyte_mode command. If byte mode is enabled, the file is read one byte at a time. Each byte is stored in an individual int variable slot as an unsigned char; the lower 8 bits represent the byte value, while the upper 24 bits are set to 0. This effectively treats each int as a single unsigned 8-bit value. This allows for the precise reading of binary files where the file data is not an exact multiple of 4 bytes.
<b>Note</b>: The value of a 'data item' as described above will only pertain when the fskip_size is 0. If the value set by the command fskip_size is &gt; 0 then the 'data item' will be measured in units of fskip_size bytes, regardless of the data type of the variables to be written to.
<b>Note</b>: The NewLines system variable reports the number of logical line endings encountered on the input stream immediately following a string-based fset_var operation. It has the following behaviour:
<i>Trigger</i>: NewLines is reset to 0 at the start of any fset_var call targeting a string variable or array. It is updated only after the reading phase is complete and at least one item was successfully read (NReads &gt; 0).
<i>Normalization</i>: The interpreter recognizes Unix (\n), Windows (\r\n), and legacy Mac (\r) line endings. Each represents a single count.
<i>Scope</i>: The count includes all line endings between the last string read and the very next non-whitespace character (the start of the next potential data item).
<i>Horizontal Whitespace</i>: Spaces and tabs encountered during this look-ahead are consumed but do not affect the NewLines count.
<i>Binary Safety</i>: The counting process stops immediately if a null character (\0) or any non-whitespace data is encountered, ensuring that subsequent binary data is not accidentally consumed.
<b>Note</b>: Use NewLines to detect structural breaks in files where whitespace is otherwise ignored. For example, in PNM (P2/P3) files, a NewLines value &gt;= 1 after reading the header can indicate the end of a comment line or the transition to the pixel data block.
<i>Caution (Binary Data)</i>: If a binary data block immediately follows a string block, ensure there are no trailing spaces or tabs before the binary data. The NewLines look-ahead logic consumes horizontal whitespace (Space/Tab) while searching for line endings. If the first byte of your binary data is value 32 or 9, it will be consumed and included in the NBytes count.
<b>Note</b>: The NBytes system variable reports the total number of raw bytes consumed from the file during the "reading phase" of the last fset_var command. The behaviour is as follows:
<i>What it counts:</i>
For Numeric Types (int/float): The total bytes read into the variable(s). For example, if fbyte_mode is ON, NBytes will equal NReads. If OFF, NBytes will be NReads * 4 (for ints).
For String Types: The total number of bytes consumed starting from the first character of the first string read, through to the last byte consumed by the NewLines look-ahead logic. The count includes all data characters, whitespace before the first string, internal whitespace between tokens, and all trailing whitespace (spaces, tabs, \r, \n) consumed while the interpreter was updating the NewLines system variable.
<i>What it ignores:</i> 
NBytes does not include any bytes skipped during the &lt;skip&gt; phase of the fset_var command.
<b>Note</b>: NBytes is the bridge between ASCII and Binary data. By adding NBytes to a tracking variable after reading a text header, you can determine the exact byte-offset where the binary data begins. This offset can then be used with fskip_size 1 to perform a byte-precise binary &lt;skip&gt; for the next fset_var call.
<b>Note</b>: See also fput_var, fskip_size and fbyte_mode.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">fskip_size</span>
fskip_size &lt;nbytes&gt;
This is a latching command. If &lt;nbytes&gt; is &gt; 0 then, whenever a fset_var command is issued, the &lt;skip&gt; value is measured in that number of bytes regardless of the data type of the variable to be set (in other words, the total bytes that will be skipped in &lt;skip&gt;*&lt;nbytes&gt;). Otherwise (if &lt;nbytes&gt; is 0 ) the &lt;skip&gt; value is measured in 'data items' as described in the description for fset_var. The default fskip_size value at the start of any script will be 0 meaning that 'data items' with be the unit of &lt;skip&gt;.
&lt;nbytes&gt; - an integer varval that must be &gt;= 0. 


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">fourier</span>
fourier &lt;action&gt; &lt;forward?&gt; &lt;height&gt; &lt;width&gt; &lt;in_re&gt; &lt;in_im&gt; [&lt;out_re&gt; &lt;out_im&gt;]
This performs one of a selection of processes (called 'actions') related to Fourier analysis on 1D or 2D data - not just the Fourier transform itself. The indices of the supplied arrays is respected according to the rules described below. 
&lt;action&gt; - a string varval defining what process to do.
&lt;forward?&gt; - a binary choice varval dictating the direction of a process
&lt;height&gt; - an integer capable varval defining the height in pixels of a 2D array
&lt;width&gt; - an integer capable varval defining the width in pixels of an array
&lt;in_re&gt; - an array of real values used as input
&lt;in_im&gt; - an array of imaginary values used as input
&lt;out_re&gt; - an array of real values used to receive the results (for actions that need it)
&lt;out_im&gt; - an array of imaginary value to receive the results (for actions that need it)
<b>Note</b>: The dimension arguments specify not only the dimensions and dimensionality of the action but also determine the minimum number of cells that must be available in all used arrays to avoid an error (with subsequent script termination). Each array argument carries an index, e.g. my_array[idx]. The operation acts on the &lt;height&gt; x &lt;width&gt; cells that start at the supplied index of each array. Every array involved must therefore have at least &lt;height&gt; x &lt;width&gt; cells from its supplied index to its end (the arrays need not be the same size as one another). Cells before the supplied indices, and any cells beyond the &lt;height&gt; x &lt;width&gt; block, are left untouched.
<b>Note</b>: If you want to perform an action on a 1D dataset then you must set the &lt;height&gt; to 1 (not the &lt;width&gt; ). Fourier transforms (in either direction) can never use fewer than 4 elements so, if the action is 'transform' then &lt;height&gt; must be either 1 or a value &gt;=4 and &lt;width&gt; must always be &gt;= 4.
<b>Note</b>: The string data type cannot be used for input or output arrays. Only floats and ints are allowed.
<b>Note</b>: The  string must be one of the following (and may be supplied either as a compile-time constant or as a string variable):
transform - an in-situ operation, no output arrays are used.
fvpow - an in-situ operation, no output arrays are used.
cyclise - the output arrays take the result so must be present.
power - the output arrays take the result so must be present.
vpower - the output arrays take the result so must be present.
They have the following meanings:
transform - perform a Fourier transform, direction dictated by the &lt;forward?&gt; argument.
cyclise - perform a quadrant re-arrangement (Fourier shift), in the direction dictated by the &lt;forward?&gt; argument. The meaning of 'forwards' is that the top left pixel is shifted such as to be placed in the centre of the output and 'backwards' reverses that shift.
power - produce a power spectrum.
vpower - produce a visual power spectrum. Similar to 'power' but it uses a fast approximation to a log2 operation and scaling to make the output spectrum easier for visual inspection as a bitmap image with pixels in the range 0-255. The scaling assumes that the input real and complex arrays (from which the spectrum is calculated) was the result of a previous forward Fourier transform on a real image of 8-bit depth using the Fourier transform functions of PCS.
fvpow - This takes in a real 8-bit image in &lt;in_re&gt; and it expects &lt;in_im&gt; to be all 0s. It then performs a forwards Fourier transform on them (if you supply &lt;forward?&gt; as 0 this will produce a script-halting error). It then produces visual power spectrum with the same processing a for vpower . It then performs a forward cyclise operation to put that power spectrum in diffraction pattern order and writes the result back into the input &lt;in_re&gt;. You can therefore use this to get a rapid visual Fourier spectrum on an input real image such as may be useful for fast real-time video frame processing because it saves you having to do all the separate steps in separate PCS commands. Because this is an in-situ process it can be performed directly on an 8bpp raw bitmap inside an integer graphics array (see preview_draw_array). Note that you must use separate array variables for &lt;in_re&gt; and &lt;in_im&gt; arguments for this action. The output in the &lt;in_re&gt; argument will be in diffraction pattern order and the output in the &lt;in_im&gt; argument will be the same data but in native Fourier order.
<b>Note</b>: Aliasing is permitted in all but one case. So even those operations that require output array arguments can be converted to in situ commands by supplying the same input array variables for the outputs. Generally it makes no sense to use the same variable for real and imaginary inputs but you are not forbidden from doing so except in the case of &lt;action&gt; = fvpow. In that case the input real and imaginary arrays must be different variables.





<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">get_actuators</span>
get_actuators
This returns information about what actuators currently exist (are defined) on the pardserver. This information comprises:
4 integers which will update the value of the 4 system variables: NSteppers, NBinaries, NMlevels, NServos. This allows the script programmer to know how many of each type of server exist.
A name string and an index number for each type of actuator that exists. These identify what specific actuators exist in each type category. With this information you can query the server with an act_get command to retrieve any other information you may need to know for each extend actuator.
This information also lets you know what names and index numbers to avoid when calling def_actuator so as to avoid duplication errors.
Example: The following script calls get_actuators then proceeds to check if specific actuators exist with the names X_Axis, Y_Axis and Z_Axis. It only goes on to issue the act_halt command to those actuators that actually exist - thereby avoiding a fatal error by attempting to address an non-existent actuator:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Pardus pcs 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#include stdpard.pch</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>verbosity 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>get_actuators</tt><tt>    # Get info on all extant actuators on the server</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_getid X_Axis</tt><tt>  # act_getid will set  CurrActIdx to -1 if the specified</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                  </tt><tt># actuator does not exist - a 'safe' way to check.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp CurrActIdx &gt;= 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>do_if Condexp</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>act_halt CurrAct</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_ENA_STATE 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>enddo</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_getid Y_Axis</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp CurrActIdx &gt;= 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>do_if Condexp</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>act_halt CurrAct</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_ENA_STATE 0 </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>enddo</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>act_getid Z_Axis</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp CurrActIdx &gt;= 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>do_if Condexp</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>act_halt CurrAct</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>act_set CurrAct INFO_ENA_STATE 0 </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>enddo</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">get_camsettings</span>
get_camsettings
This is a pardclient GUI update command. The very first time this is called will be automatically by the pardclient program upon any new login and, at that time, this will gather information from the server on each camera in turn in order to populate the client's internal 'CPlist' of all cameras and their parameters. The script writer will not be able to use this command in that way. Instead, when this command is issued in a script, because all the settings and resolutions data have already been gathered from the server, all it will do is ensure the GUI pages and lists of the client program have their displays and combo lists updated with the information relating to the current camera.
Thus the user should call this command after any call to select_camera if they want to ensure the GUI is updated to reflect the values of the camera they select. 
Alternatively call this command right at the end of your script to ensure that the GUI is appropriately set if that is important to you. You don't need to call this command at all if all you want to do is run scripts from the script tab and have no intention of using the other GUI tabs for any camera functions. 
<b>Note</b>: This command works in conjunction with the get_resolutions command. Not having these GUI update commands run automatically with a script-originating call to select_camera was a deliberate design decision because users may want to do rapid switching / multiplexing between cameras in a script and wasting time automatically setting GUI controls if the user doesn't need that will just reduce the maximum frequency of multiplexing. Hence these GUI update commands are separate commands to be run if and when the user needs them (when doing a select_camera from the Manual GUI drop-down combo list in pardclient, these GUI update commands <i>are</i> called automatically each time).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">get_preview</span>
get_preview &lt;ON|OFF&gt; &lt;centre_x&gt; &lt;centre_y&gt; [Display?]
This causes the frame grabber to capture a preview image and send it to the client.
&lt;ON|OFF&gt; - a binary choice varval. It determines whether the preview is zoomed (ON) or not (OFF). A zoomed preview is a crop of the full-size image, cropped to the dimensions of the preview image, and so will appear zoomed if the dimensions of the full-size image are larger than the dimensions of the preview image. An un-zoomed preview showing a scaled version of the full-size image, scaled to fit in the dimensions of the preview image whilst maintaining its original aspect ratio. 
&lt;centre_x&gt; - a varval of type int. This is the centre X coordinate of the crop in the full resolution image for a zoomed preview. 
&lt;centre_y&gt; - a varval of type int. This is the centre Y coordinate of the crop in the full resolution image for a zoomed preview.
[Display?] - a binary choice varval. If this evaluates to non-zero then the obtained preview image is immediately displayed. Otherwise it is acquired but not displayed. The default is for this to be treated as true so a loaded image is displayed unless you provide this argument and set it to something that evaluates to 0.
<b>Note</b>: If you want to display the preview at any time after calling this command with the [Display?] option set to false/0 then you can do that with the command preview_show. The reason you may want to acquire the image but not show it is that this allows you to do processing on the loaded preview before displaying it (like tracking and adding an overlay). If you display the preview as soon as loaded, the processing would commence, the overlay drawn and then the preview will be re-displayed with overlay. If this all happens before the preview is immediately refreshed with a new preview image, then the that new image will be immediately drawn replacing your previous annotated image which will therefore not stay on display for long enough to be visually appreciated - most of the time the user will see either the a naked preview image with only a brief flash of the overlaid version. This can cause flickering of the preview overly and also unnecessary GUI ops. Thus, having manual control over when a newly acquired preview image gets put out to the GUI display allows you to prevent this.
<b>Note</b>: <b><i>Special features of a preview frame grab</i></b>. Taking a preview image activates the frame grabber in a modified way (as opposed to extracting a preview image from a capture that is initiated by the grab_image command, but that only occurs in pardcapture running in stand-alone mode so never pertains to preview requests initiated by PCS commands). In this case, the 'From_preview_timeout' global variable is set in pardcapture which does this in the pardcapture grab_image() function:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>
 if(From_preview_timeout){ </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>skipframe=skiplim; // This ignores any 'frames_to_skip' value and</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                                 </tt><tt>//</tt><tt> just process the very first frame grabbed. </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>frame_timeout_sec  = 0;    // These overrule any grabber_timeout</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>frame_timeout_usec = 100;  // value currently in effect</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>goto just_the_one;  </tt><tt>      </tt><tt> // Skip any averaging - just get one frame.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>} </tt> </span>
Thus any value you may have set for grabber_timout, frames_to_average and frames_to_skip will be totally ignored when capturing a preview image for any purpose (this includes for the PCS commands get_preview_focus, get_preview_stats and get_preview_tc). The consequences of this are as follows:
1. Overruling frames_to_skip means your preview image (or stats, focus value or tissue content value derived from it for the get_preview_focus, get_preview_stats and get_preview_tc commands) will be based on the next image in the v4l2 image buffer queue. Thus, to be certain you image or stats are based on what is currently under the camera lens, you should perform a skip_frames command before calling any of the get_preview commands. Set the argument of the skip_frames command to at least the value of the number of buffers used in the pardcapture v4l2 buffer (at the time of writing this number is 2).
2. Overruling the grabber_timeout means there is a higher chance that the get_preview command (or any of the related get_preview commands that are set to grab a fresh preview image) may fail with a timeout error. This is particularly likely if the save_resolution is set higher than VGA or if you have just used the frame grabber to grab a full image immediately before attempting the preview grab. To deal with this you may impose a delay prior to issuing any of the get_preview commands using the sleep command (about 200 ms or higher should be sufficient).
Because the frame grab may fail, and because failure to grab a preview image need not mean that your script, as a whole, needs to terminate, failure of any of these commands: get_preview, get_preview_focus, get_preview_stats and get_preview_tc will not be always fatal to your script. It <i>might</i> be fatal, for example failure to activate the camera, failure to properly allocate memory and some other errors will always be fatal and your script will terminate - you have no say in that. However, for those errors that will not corrupt the memory states and that might be recoverable (for example, timeout errors that may be fixed by simply waiting or ignoring) the initial internal 'failure' flag will get transformed into a 'non-fatal' flag and your program will continue. You will still see an error report in the logs and script and/or server consoles (with a sufficient Verbosity level) but the program will not terminate.
<b><i>The PreviewReport system variable</i></b>
Because error messages to logs, etc. are only detectable interactively by you reading them, there is also a system variable whose value you can access in your script to read and see if a particular preview capture event succeeded completely or experienced a non-fatal error. This is the PreviewReport variable. This variable pertains to all preview image capture events regardless of which command requested the preview image (get_preview, get_preview_focus, etc.). At the start of any preview capture process the PreviewReport variable is assigned a value of GRAB_ERR_NONE indicating complete success. If a non-fatal error occurred its value will change to indicate the type of error. The complete set of possible values this reporter variable may take are these (these values are correct at the time of writing but the very latest values can be found in the pardcapdefs.h header file):
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Values that the image grabber function returns</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define GRAB_ERR_NONE       1 // Success!</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define GRAB_ERR_SELECT     2 // Error selecting a frame from the stream</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define GRAB_ERR_TIMEOUT    3 // Too long get</tt><tt>t</tt><tt>ing a frame from the stream</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define GRAB_ERR_NOSTREAM   9 // The camera is not streaming images</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define GRAB_ERR_BUSY      10 // The grabber is being used by another</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                              </tt><tt>// program or function - try again later.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PS_CB_E_PFC       </tt><tt>1</tt><tt>38 // Preview failure - colour convert failed</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define PS_CB_E_PFZ      </tt><tt> </tt><tt>139 // Preview failure - zoom settings failed</tt> </span>
This gives you informed control for any preview capture command. Simply read the value of PreviewReport after the get_preview (or related) command and code what do do according to the value. For example you may decide to exit the script, re-try the grab immediately or after a delay or some other intervention, or just ignore it (which may be reasonable in a high speed 'live' preview loop where dropping the odd frame is not important).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">get_preview_focus</span>
get_preview_focus
This captures a new preview image and retrieves the RGB focus stats (only) using whatever focus measure was previously set using prv_toggle.
The preview image itself is not sent over to the client. This is a more streamlined and quicker function than a call to get_preview_stats (which see) because the latter will update all the preview stats values, not just focus.  On successful completion of this command, the user-accessible system  variables Ps_Focus_R, Ps_Focus_G, and Ps_Focus_B will be updated but the other stats variables will not be updated.
This command is designed for fast access to the latest focus stats for those wishing to implement their own custom autofocus algorithm at script level (instead of using the standard PCS autofocus command which operates on the pardserver). See the autofocus command for more detail about that.
<b>Note</b>: See 'Special features of a preview frame grab' on p.Error: Reference source not found.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">get_preview_stats</span>
get_preview_stats &lt;ON|OFF&gt;
This retrieves stats for a preview image. 
&lt;ON|OFF&gt; - a binary choice varval. If the argument evaluates to ON then it takes a new preview image (using the settings last used to take a preview e.g. zoomed or not and, if zoomed, the last XY coordinates used will be used again this time) and sends over the stats calculated on that new image. If it evaluates to OFF then it sends over the stats calculated for the last preview image that was taken (so it assumes that at least one prior call to get_preview was done).
In both cases (ON or OFF) the preview image itself is not sent over to the client. Thus a call to get_preview_stats OFF after a call to get_preview should get the stats of the preview image received from that get_preview command.
<b>Note</b>: If all you want are the current preview focus stats then use the command get_preview_focus. That is a leaner version of this command that will only receive and parse the focus values. See that command for more details.
<b>Note</b>: See 'Special features of a preview frame grab' on p.Error: Reference source not found.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">get_preview_tc</span>
get_preview_tc &lt;ON|OFF&gt;
This does one of two things in regards to tissue content (tc) measurements depending on the argument. Regardless of the argument value it takes a new preview image without applying your chosen LUT, Laplacian or median filtering options. It does, however, use other settings that were last used to take a preview  e.g. zoomed or not and, if zoomed, the last XY coordinates used will be used again this time and it does carry out your choice of whether to invert intensities. However, regardless of your colour channel choices for the previous it uses the Y channel data. Using this Y preview image data it then performs one of the following two operations (only using pixels under any preview mask you supplied and only using pixels within the upper and lower histogram limits you previously set if you selected to only calculate variance within limits).
&lt;ON|OFF&gt; - a binary choice varval. If it evaluates to ON it calculates the tissue content value using the NDL   (Normalised Differential Laplacian) on the 3x3 median filtered Y pixel data as:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>tissue_content_ndl = (MALcurr / MALref) * log10(VARcurr / VARref)</tt> </span>
where MAL = mean absolute Laplacian and VAR is the variance.
It then updates the local system variable, CurrTC, with the result and, if the tc_threshold &lt;lower&gt; value is &gt;= 0.0 (see the tc_threshold command for details), also sets the local system variables NoTissue and HasTissue according to whether CurrTC falls within or on the boundaries set by tc_threshold. The &lt;upper&gt; value set by tc_threshold will only be applied to the thresholding if it is non-negative.
If the argument evaluates to OFF then it performs the 3x3 median filter on the Y pixel data and calculates the MAL and VAR but, instead of using these to calculate the NDL, it simply stores them in the preview stats struct members as the NDL reference values for future tissue content calculations - the values MALref and VARref in the above formula. The local system variables mentioned above   will not be altered after this call.
<b>Note</b>: You should call get_preview_tc OFF on a reference field (usually a blank field of illuminated background) before you call get_preview_tc ON in any given login session to a pardcap server after the pardcap server first starts up. Unless the background illumination changes or you move to a new objective or camera there should be no need to call get_preview_tc OFF again.
<b>Note</b>: Unless you call  get_preview_tc OFF on, at least once in any given login session to a pardcap server after the pardcap server first starts up, the reference values  MALref and VARref will not be set. Their default values when a pardcap server first starts are -1.0 (for each of them). If VARref is negative it means the tc reference values have not yet been set and so tc measurements (let alone tc thresholding) will not be possible.
<b>Note</b>: Any currently visible preview image is not altered, no preview image is sent back to the client and the general preview stats are not updated.
<b>Note</b>: See 'Special features of a preview frame grab' on p.Error: Reference source not found.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">get_resolutions</span>
get_resolutions
This is a pardclient GUI update command. The very first time this is called will be automatically by the pardclient program upon any new login and, at that time, this will gather information from the server on each camera in turn in order to populate the client's internal 'CPlist' of all cameras and their parameters. The script writer will not be able to use this command in that way. Instead, when this command is issued in a script, because all the settings and resolutions data have already been gathered from the server, all it will do is ensure the GUI pages and lists of the client program have their displays and combo lists updated with the information relating to the current camera.
Thus the user should call this command after any call to select_camera if they want to ensure the GUI is updated to reflect the values of the camera they select. 
Alternatively call this command right at the end of your script to ensure that the GUI is appropriately set if that is important to you. You don't need to call this command at all if all you want to do is run scripts from the script tab and have no intention of using the other GUI tabs for any camera functions. 
<b>Note</b>: This command works in conjunction with the get_camsettings command. Not having these GUI update commands run automatically with a script-originating call to select_camera was a deliberate design decision because users may want to do rapid switching / multiplexing between cameras in a script and wasting time automatically setting GUI controls if the user doesn't need that will just reduce the maximum frequency of multiplexing. Hence these GUI update commands are separate commands to be run if and when the user needs them (when doing a select_camera from the Manual GUI drop-down combo list in pardclient, these GUI update commands <i>are</i> called automatically each time).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">get_tracker</span>
get_tracker &lt;tracker_name&gt; &lt;tracker_item&gt; &lt;receptacle&gt;
This allows you to read the current state of a tracker: its status, whether it is initialised, its last known position and some diagnostic counts.
&lt;tracker_name&gt; - a string varval defining the name of the tracker to query. This is case sensitive and must pertain to an extant user-defined tracker.
&lt;tracker_item&gt; - an integer varval whose value specifies the item to read. This is one of the constants #defined in stdpard.pch as shown below. A number that is not in the range 1 to 11 is a script error and terminates the script.
&lt;receptacle&gt; - a writeable user-defined variable that will receive the information. Every item is a whole number, so an integer variable is ideal but any data type may be used and the PCS casting rules will apply.
<b>Note</b>: The current list of acceptable &lt;tracker_item&gt; values is this. Items that belong to one tracker type give 0 for the other types:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Constant       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Value</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Meaning                                           </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼───────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_OCCLCOUNT │ 1     │ Occlusion counter: the number of tracks judged     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ occluded since the tracker was last initialised    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ (or since you set it with TKSET_OCCLCOUNT).        │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_ERRSTATUS │ 2     │ The status flag: one of the TRKS_ codes (see       │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ init_tracker).                                     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_SEEDED    │ 3     │ Is the tracker initialised and able to track? 1 =  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ yes, 0 = no.                                       │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_ROW       │ 4     │ Last known top row of the template window (the     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ position last returned by track, or set with       │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ TKSET_ROW).                                        │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_COL       │ 5     │ Last known left column of the template window.     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_NPOINTS   │ 6     │ klt: the number of feature points currently being  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ followed (inside the box and in the background     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ band).                                             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_NINLIERS  │ 7     │ klt: the number of box points that agreed on the   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ target's motion in the last track (0 if the track  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ was lost).                                         │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_LOSTFRAME │ 8     │ klt: the number of consecutive tracks in which the │ </tt>
  <tt> │ S               │       │ target has been lost (0 while tracking normally).  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_NREFEDGES │ 9     │ ght: the number of edge pixels recorded from the   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ template at initialisation.                        │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_VOTES     │ 10    │ ght: the number of votes (whole number, after      │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ smoothing) for the winning position in the last    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ track.                                             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKGET_IMGEDGES  │ 11    │ ght: the number of edge pixels found in the search │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ area in the last track.                            │ </tt>




<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">goto</span>
goto &lt;label&gt; [if &lt;varval&gt;]
This causes the script to jump to the specific line in the script that is marked by a goto label. This command can be simple (i.e. just go to the labelled line) or extended as a conditional goto (i.e. only go to the labelled line if some condition is satisfied).
&lt;label&gt; - a text string (or a variable containing a text string) where the string is without white space and must be identical to a goto label that is defined somewhere in the script on its own separate line (excluding the terminal colon character - the latter is part of the syntax that identifies the label as a goto label but is not part of the label's name).
if - If an extended (conditional) goto is required then the second argument must be the word if and must be followed by a varval of a type described below.
&lt;varval&gt; - a binary choice varval. If the value of &lt;varval&gt; evaluates to a non-zero numerical value or to TRUE or ON then the script interpreter will immediately go to the line in the script marked by &lt;label&gt;. Otherwise the script will continue execution from the next coding line immediately following this goto line.
<b>Note</b>: A goto label itself (i.e. the token that marks the destination position in the script of a goto jump) is a string without white space followed immediately by a colon with no space between the label string and the colon - similar to goto labels in the C programming language - and that goto label must be on its own separate line. This goto label must be a string constant and not a string variable and must be unique amongst all other goto labels in the entire script. The 'string' used for a goto label may be a number (so '1234:' is a valid goto label). There is nothing in the PCS language interpreter rules that prevents you from using a protected name (see p.46) or the name of an extant user variable or actuator for a label but it can make a program potentially confusing to read if you do that.
<b>Note</b>: Using the Condexp system variable as the &lt;varval&gt; argument in conjunction with the set_condexp command allows conditional expressions to dictate the control of flow (see set_condexp for detail).
<b>Note</b>: This goto command is context-aware. You may jump to any label in the script, even those outside the current function or inside/outside nested blocks. The interpreter automatically manages the following:
<i>Function Stack Management</i>:
If you jump to a label in a calling function (a parent), the interpreter will "unwind" the call stack, automatically closing all recursive or nested function calls between your current position and the target.
If you jump to a "foreign" function (one not currently in your active call path), the interpreter will reset the stack to the main function level. Upon reaching a return in that new function, that return will be treated as if it were the return of main (i.e. it will exit the script).
<i>Loop and Block Synchronization</i>:
If you jump out of nested loop_while loops or do_if blocks, the interpreter automatically resets the nesting counters. You do not need to manually "close" loops you have jumped out of.
If you jump into a loop or block, the interpreter will correctly identify the new nesting level. When an endloop or enddo is reached, it will behave as if you had entered that block normally.
<i>Intra-Function Jumps</i>:
Jumps within the same function block are "stack-neutral" and do not affect your function return path, though they will still synchronize your loop_while/do_if nesting depth.
<i>Note on Jumping into </i><i>do_if</i><i>-</i><i>else</i><i> Blocks</i>:
Jumping into the 'If' portion: If a goto lands between a do_if and an else (or enddo), the block is marked as entered. When the else or enddo is reached, the interpreter will treat the block as completed and skip to the end of the structure.
Jumping into the 'Else' portion: If a goto lands between an else and an enddo, the block is marked as not entered. The code will execute until the enddo is reached. This is functionally equivalent to the original do_if condition having been FALSE.
Safety Warning: Avoid jumping directly into the middle of a conditional block if that block relies on variables that were supposed to be set at the start of the do_if. The interpreter will manage the control flow, but it is the programmer's responsibility to ensure the global variable state is valid for the landing point.
<b>Note</b>:  If a script contains no formal functions (that is, functions defined by def_func) or only the main function then goto labels may be be placed anywhere. If you have more than just the main function defined in a script (even if that / those other functions are never called) then all goto labels must be placed either within a function block (def_func ... return) or between the very last return command and the exit command of the script. That is to say, labels cannot exist in "No-Man's Land" (the space between function definitions). The script checker will flag these as syntax errors before the script runs, but only when the script contains at least one goto; otherwise they are accepted, and the code there never runs.
<b>Note</b>: The fact that the goto command can accept a &lt;label&gt; argument that is a variable of the string data type and not just a string constant allows for the implementation of informal 'functions' (subroutines) that can be 'called' from anywhere in the code and that can be made to return either to whence they were called or to anywhere else. This is achieved by altering the string value of the 'return' goto's &lt;label&gt; argument just prior to calling the subroutine.
For example, let's say I want a subroutine to do 100 frames of live preview. I can set it up and call it like this:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># pre-branching code ....</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Create a string variable to determine where the subroutine returns to</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># when done:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var string  return_point _</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Set the value of that return point to some valid script goto label</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var return_point return_point_1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># 'Call' the subroutine by simply going to a label just before it:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>goto live_preview</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Set the return point label</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return_point_1:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># post-return code ....</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># [rest of main program here</tt><tt>]</tt><tt>  - and </tt><tt>we</tt><tt> can also call live_preview</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#</tt><tt> after </tt><tt>we </tt><tt>set_var the return_point variable to a label (like</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#</tt><tt> return_point_2, return_point_3,</tt><tt> </tt><tt>return_point_4, etc.) where each</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#</tt><tt> return point label is placed just after the call, as we did above]</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>goto end_</tt><tt>of</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>script</tt><tt>  # </tt><tt>This is effectively the e</tt><tt>nd of the main program</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># All goto-based subroutines start after here and must end with a goto</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># </tt><tt>that acts as a 'return' command to return control of flow to some</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># </tt><tt>return point</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># The live preview subroutine entry point goto label</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>live_preview:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># ... [live preview code]</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Now we return to wherever the string in the variable return_point points</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># to (assuming that string is a valid goto label somewhere in this script).</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># This subroutine itself might alter the string value contained in the string</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># variable 'return_point' so this subroutine does not necessarily have to</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># return to the place that called it.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>goto return_point</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Any other </tt><tt>subroutines</tt><tt> go here, then finally we have:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># The exit goto label</tt><tt> (note that it would be illegal to place a goto label</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># here if the script had more than 1 formal function defined because this</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># would be an instance of a goto label being placed outside a</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># def_func ... return block).</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>end_</tt><tt>of</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>script</tt><tt>:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit   </tt> </span>
<b>AI Tip</b>: For high-performance state machines, store your destination labels in a string array. This allows you to dispatch control flow using an index (e.g., goto state_labels[idx]). This 'Jump Table' approach is more efficient for LLMs to manage than complex branching logic.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">grabber_predelay</span>
grabber_predelay &lt;seconds&gt;
This sets the number of seconds to wait before activating the frame grabber after the command to grab_image is given
&lt;seconds&gt; - an integer between 0 to 172800 inclusive 
<b>Note</b>: A frame capture operation may be a single image capture or a multi-frame averaging series.    In the latter case, this delay is applied only before the very first image in the average sequence.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">grabber_retries</span>
grabber_retries &lt;number&gt;
When commanded to capture an image the frame grabber may fail to deliver an image in the allotted time before declaring a timeout failure. This command sets the number of such failed attempts to ignore (and so just trying again) before finally giving up and reporting an error.
&lt;number&gt; - an integer between 0 to 4096 inclusive
<b>Note</b>: The grabber will wait a certain amount of time for each frame in this set before giving up and going onto the next try (see grabber_timeout).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">grabber_timeout</span>
grabber_timeout &lt;seconds&gt;
This sets the number of seconds for the frame grabber to wait for a frame to be delivered from the   camera driver after requesting one before it gives up and either retries (see grabber_retries) or reports an error.
&lt;number&gt; - an integer between 0 to 4096 inclusive


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">grab_image</span>
grab_image
This activates the camera frame grabber with the intent of capturing a full-size image.
This command takes no arguments but the user must have previously prepared the parameters of image capture using these commands: save_format, save_resolution, save_root, frames_to_skip, frame_number and frames_to_average. While the former settings are mandatory before calling grab_image there are many other optional settings as well as described elsewhere in this manual. For example, the use of correction images (dark field, flat field, corrections mask) and your choice for tissue content thresholding (tc_threshold) and whether or not to also save a coordinates text file with the image (save_coords).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">gui_buffer</span>
gui_buffer &lt;varval&gt;
This allows drawing to the script console to be performed in an 'off-screen' fast text buffer and then drawn all-at-once in a fast loop buffer flush. This allows the drawing of complex layouts to the script view console with minimal flicker or lag compared to not using a buffer. You can choose between two types of buffering: 1. General XY aware, 2. Continuous stream.
&lt;varval&gt; - an integer from 0 to 2. See notes below.
<b>Note</b>: Selecting 1 diverts all text and graphics emissions intended for the script console to a block buffer that also records any cursor re-positioning. This is the 'General XY aware' buffer.
<b>Note</b>: Selecting 2 diverts all text and graphics emissions intended for the script console to a block buffer that expects all emissions to be strictly contiguous without cursor re-positioning. This is the 'Continuous stream' buffer.
<b>Note</b>: Selecting 0 causes any stored data in a buffer to be instantly flushed to the script console window in a fast loop.
<b>Note</b>: Once you have initiated a type 1 buffer you cannot initiate a type 2 buffer until the type 1 buffer has been flushed - and <i>vice versa</i>.
<b>Note</b>: Issuing a repeat command has no effect and will not cause an error (for example if a 'gui_buffer 1' command follows a previous 'gui_buffer 1' command without an intervening 'gui_buffer 0' command, this will have no effect).
<b>Note</b>: The continuous stream buffer (type 2) is faster to render than the general XY aware buffer (type 1) but either is a lot faster than no buffering at all.
<b>Note</b>: The general XY aware buffer (type 1) may be used in any printing and drawing use case but the type 2 buffer will only give correct output if your input did not use any cursor re-positioning commands. The increased rendering speed of this type 2 buffer can be particularly useful if you are outputting a continuous block of printing such as a bitmap image made out of block characters.
<b>Note</b>: Any data in the buffer at the time of script termination will just be discarded - it will not be drawn to the script console.
<b>Note</b>: This will also buffer output to console when used in CLI mode although many native consoles are so fast that you probably won't need buffering (won't notice the difference) unless using a slow console emulator or network terminal.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">gui_update</span>
gui_update
This causes an immediate 'one-off' top level GUI refresh loop to execute. This can be uses in cases where the automated GUI updates are turned off or kept at a low frequency using the gui_update_period command (which see) but where you need to perform a manual GUI update for any reason.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">gui_update_period</span>
gui_update_period &lt;varval&gt;
This sets the frequency with which a top-level GUI refresh is carried out during the running of a script. The period is the number of PCS command lines that are executed before a GUI update is performed.
&lt;varval&gt; - an integer &gt;= 0. The values 1 to 16 represent the power of 2 exponent of the period. The value 0 means no periodic updates at all. Any value &gt;= 17 means trigger a GUI update with every single line. See notes below.
<b>Note</b>: The default setting at the start of each script run is effectively 17 - meaning that a GUI update will be performed with every line of PCS code. This gives maximum GUI responsiveness but at a cost of about 150x slower execution of local commands.
<b>Note</b>: Using a value of 0 turns off all automatic top-level GUI updates. This can make the script completely unresponsive to mouse clicks and other GUI actions while it is running.
<b>Note</b>: The PCS programmer is free to issue this command and change the frequency of GUI updates at any point in their script.
<b>Note</b>: If you need to terminate a script that is unresponsive to GUI input (such that you cannot click the 'Interrupt' button) then you may interrupt the script at the OS level by switching focus to the OS command prompt window from which pardclient was run and issue a Ctrl+c interrupt. This will terminate a running script the same way clicking the 'Interrupt' button would have done and you may continue with your pardclient session as normal. However, performing this Ctrl+c action when a PCS script is not running will abruptly terminate the whole pardclient program without warning.
<b>Note</b>: Even when the gui_update_period is set to 0 (and the script avoids all automatic GUI updates), the poll_user PCS command will safely inject its own lightweight 'input only' event loop flush to capture keyboard input. However, because complex graphical actions (such as focus switching, text selections, and clearing pending text view printouts) require full layout recalculations, you should issue a manual 'gui_update' command right before polling, or maintain a low positive gui_update_period, to ensure the input widget states draw correctly.
<b>Note</b>: In practice you will find that setting the gui_update_period value to a particular number will work just fine but setting it to one higher will make the program too unresponsive to manipulate with the GUI. In that case choose the value that just allowed good responsiveness (choosing a lower value to get even more frequent polling will not materially improve GUI responsiveness but will slow down execution of the script).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">init_tracker</span>
init_tracker &lt;tracker_name&gt; &lt;temp_r&gt; &lt;temp_c&gt; &lt;colchan&gt;
This initialises (or re-initialises) a tracker so it can begin tracking. Here 'initialise' refers to training the tracker on the pixels in the current preview image within the template window region defined by top left coordinates &lt;temp_r&gt; &lt;temp_c&gt;. The dimensions of the square template window were supplied upon tracker creation with def_tracker (or later changed with set_tracker). Use it again at any time to point the tracker at a new target or to recover a lost one.
&lt;tracker_name&gt; - a string varval defining the name of the tracker to initialise. This is case sensitive and must pertain to an extant user-defined tracker.
&lt;temp_r&gt; - an integer varval defining the top of the template window area. The allowed range is between 0 and 480-&lt;template_dim&gt; (inclusive).
&lt;temp_c&gt; - an integer varval defining the left extreme of the template window area. The allowed range is between 0 and 640-&lt;template_dim&gt; (inclusive).
&lt;colchan&gt; - an integer varval defining the colour component of the underlying RGB preview image pixels to use for calculating the tracking measure and must be in the range 1 to 4 (inclusive). These numbers refer to the following constants #defined in stdpard.pch:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define CCHAN_Y 1</tt><tt> </tt><tt> # The mean of all three colour channels.</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define CCHAN_R 2</tt><tt> </tt><tt> # The red channel value</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define CCHAN_G 3</tt><tt> </tt><tt> # The green channel value</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define CCHAN_B 4</tt><tt> </tt><tt> # The blue channel value</tt> </span>
The tracker remembers this colour channel and uses it for all later track and match_tracker commands. Use the channel in which the target stands out best from its surroundings.
<b>Note</b>: What initialisation does depends on the tracker type:
zmncc - records the template window pixels as the target pattern.
hog - records the pattern of edge directions in the template window.
com - records only the starting position (the template window just has to contain some variation).
ght - records the strong edges of the template and their directions. It fails with TRKS_FLAT_TEMPLATE if it finds fewer edge pixels than TKSET_GHT_MINEDGES (default 20).
klt - finds corner features inside the template window (preferring the centre) and in a band around it (to measure the background motion). It fails with TRKS_FLAT_TEMPLATE if it finds fewer than TKSET_KLT_MINPTS (default 3) features inside the window.
<b>Note</b>: For every tracker type, a template window that is nearly uniform (essentially no grey level variation) fails with TRKS_FLAT_TEMPLATE. A successful initialisation sets the status to TRKS_OK, resets the occlusion counter to 0 and leaves all your settings unchanged. A failed initialisation leaves the tracker un-initialised (even if it was previously initialised), so track will not work until you initialise it successfully.
<b>Note</b>: The tracking sub-system is designed to fail without terminating the script (except for catastrophic memory failures or invalid argument errors). In the event that your initialisation attempt is unsuccessful, the command will set the status flag of your tracker to one of the tracker error codes contained in stdpard.pch. Invalid arguments (for example &lt;temp_r&gt;, &lt;temp_c&gt; or &lt;colchan&gt; out of range) are different: they are script errors and terminate the script. The status codes are:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Constant       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Value</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Meaning                                           </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Set by                                  </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼───────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ TRKS_OK         │ 0     │ The last init or track succeeded.                  │ init_tracker, track                      │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TRKS_UNSEEDED   │ 1     │ No target: never initialised, or the               │ init_tracker, set_tracker, track,        │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ initialisation failed, or a setting change has     │ match_tracker                            │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ discarded it (see set_tracker).                    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TRKS_BAD_WINDOW │ 2     │ The tracker could not make a search at all because │ init_tracker, track, match_tracker       │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ the template window or the search area could not   │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ be placed inside the preview image (for klt:       │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ because the preview image size is not the one it   │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ was initialised with). This has nothing to do with │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ whether the target can be found - compare          │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ TRKS_OCCLUDED, where a search was made. Because    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ every coordinate is range-checked and the preview  │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ image is always 640 x 480, this should never occur │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ in practice: it is a safeguard.                    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TRKS_BAD_CHANNE │ 3     │ Invalid colour channel at initialisation. Not      │ init_tracker                             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ L               │       │ normally seen because an out-of-range &lt;colchan&gt;    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ terminates the script first.                       │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TRKS_FLAT_TEMPL │ 4     │ The template region is featureless (klt: too few   │ init_tracker, match_tracker              │ </tt>
  <tt> │ ATE             │       │ corners found; ght: too few edges found).          │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ match_tracker also uses it when a klt window has   │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ no usable feature points or a ght tracker has no   │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ stored edges.                                      │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TRKS_OCCLUDED   │ 5     │ A search was made but the best match was not good  │ track                                    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ enough: its confidence was below the occlusion     │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ threshold (klt: or the track was lost). The target │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ may be hidden, may have moved out of the search    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │       │ area or may have changed appearance.               │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TRKS_BAD_SETTIN │ 6     │ A set_tracker value was rejected; nothing was      │ set_tracker                              │ </tt>
  <tt> │ G               │       │ changed.                                           │                                          │ </tt>


You may read the current value of the status flag with the get_tracker command (which see).
<b>Note</b>: The status flag is not reset automatically by every successful command, so an old error can still be there after a command that worked. The commands which change it are these:
init_tracker and track always replace it with the outcome of that command (TRKS_OK, TRKS_OCCLUDED or an error code), so after them the flag always describes that command.
match_tracker sets it only when it fails. A successful match_tracker leaves it exactly as it was.
set_tracker sets TRKS_BAD_SETTING when it rejects a value. When it accepts a value it leaves the flag alone, except that it clears TRKS_BAD_SETTING and that a few settings un-initialise the tracker (TRKS_UNSEEDED). TKSET_STATUS sets the flag to the value you give.
get_tracker never changes it.
<b>Note</b>: To be certain whether a command failed, reset the flag first and read it afterwards, in the same way that a C programmer clears errno before a call:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_tracker t1 TKSET_STATUS TRKS_OK</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>match_tracker t1 row col score</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>get_tracker t1 TKGET_ERRSTATUS status</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># status is TRKS_OK only if the match_tracker succeeded; otherwise it says why it failed</tt> </span>
<b>Note</b>: All tracking activity is based on the VGA preview buffer - even if that buffer is displaying multiple images or images that are not of VGA dimensions (i.e. non 4:3 aspect ratio). For non-4:3 aspect ratio preview images, the grey zones of padding (used to pad the preview image to fit in the 4:3 display zone) will be used as if they were image pixels for all tracking purposes.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">jpeg_quality</span>
jpeg_quality &lt;number&gt;
This sets the compression level of files saved in the jpeg format on the client. The lower the number the more the compression (and so the lower the image quality).
&lt;quality_integer&gt; - an integer from 1 to 100 (100 = best image quality).
<b>Note</b>: This is a local command (it does not get communicated to the pardserver) and only affects files saved by the client to their chosen location. It does not affect the quality of any JPEG stream coming from the camera at the server end.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">loop_while</span>
loop_while &lt;varval&gt;
This allows for while-loop functionality. This marks the beginning of a block of code which must be terminated by the command endloop (which see). The contents of this block will be executed only if the &lt;varval&gt; evaluates to a non-zero value and, if the endloop command is reached, the control of flow will return back to its associated loop_while test line. 
&lt;varval&gt; - a binary choice varval. This will be evaluated to test whether the block of code is entered for execution or skipped. If it evaluates to 0 (or OFF or FALSE) then the block is skipped, otherwise it is entered. 
<b>Note</b>: While loops may be nested up to a maximum of INT_MAX deep.
<b>Note</b>: The maximum number of while loops allowed in a script is UINT_MAX.
<b>Note</b>: You may use the goto command to break out of a while loop block at any stage.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">make_lib</span>
make_lib
This instructs the archiver to turn your program into a library. 
<b>Note</b>: See the chapter on libs (page 57) for detailed information on usage.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">match_tracker</span>
match_tracker &lt;tracker_name&gt; &lt;row&gt; &lt;col&gt; &lt;receptacle&gt;
This uses a tracker's own algorithm to score a template-sized window of the current preview image at a position you choose, against what the tracker is following (its reference). It gives you the same kind of measure that track gives for the position it finds, but for a window that you specify. Its main purpose is to let you write your own search when tracking has failed: step a window over the likely region, score each position and, when you find a good one, use set_tracker (TKSET_ROW and TKSET_COL) or init_tracker to redirect the tracker. It is equally valid while tracking is going well, for example to gather information about other positions.
&lt;tracker_name&gt; - a string varval defining the name of the tracker whose algorithm and reference are to be used. This is case sensitive and must pertain to an extant user-defined tracker. The tracker must have been initialised for this command to succeed.
&lt;row&gt; - an integer varval defining the top of the template window area you want to test. The allowed range is between 0 and 480-&lt;template_dim&gt; (inclusive).
&lt;col&gt; - an integer varval defining the left extreme of the template window area you want to test. The allowed range is between 0 and 640-&lt;template_dim&gt; (inclusive).
&lt;receptacle&gt; - a writeable user-defined variable of float type (not int or string) that will receive the score.
<b>Note</b>: The tracking sub-system is designed to fail without terminating the script (except for catastrophic memory failures or invalid arguments). If no score can be produced, the &lt;receptacle&gt; is set to -1.0 and the tracker's status flag is set to say why. A successful match_tracker leaves the status flag unchanged. The causes of failure are: the tracker is not initialised (TRKS_UNSEEDED); the preview image size has changed since the tracker was initialised (TRKS_BAD_WINDOW, klt); no feature point of a klt tracker can be compared at this position (TRKS_FLAT_TEMPLATE).
<b>Note</b>: This command does not interfere with the tracker's tracking: it never un-initialises a tracker and it leaves the tracker's position, template, occlusion counter and everything else unchanged. You may use it while a tracker is tracking.
<b>Note</b>: The score is a value between 0 and 1 (1 = a perfect match), calculated as follows for each type:
zmncc: correlation of the window with the tracker's current (adapted) template. This is the same quantity as the confidence returned by track.
hog: how much the window's edge-direction histograms overlap those of the template. The same as the score returned by track.
ght: the number of the template's edge pixels that find an edge of a similar direction at the corresponding place in the window (smoothed, see TKSET_GHT_SMOOTH) divided by the number of template edge pixels, limited to 1. The same as the confidence returned by track. Because the score is limited to 1, positions within a pixel or two of the true position can all score 1.0: when scanning, take the middle of the positions that share the highest score.
com: the amount of bright (or dark) "mass" inside the window divided by the amount in the whole search area centred on the window. This is close to (but not exactly) the confidence track would return. It rewards windows that capture the most of the blob rather than windows centred on it.
klt: a klt tracker does not search for a stored picture of the template; it follows corner features that were chosen inside the template window when it was initialised. The score is the average correlation (0 to 1) between the small patches around the target points as they were in the reference image and the same patches shifted to this window position in the current image. It is not on the same scale as the confidence returned by track, so compare it with other match_tracker scores, not with the occlusion threshold.
<b>Note</b>: match_tracker works on the current preview image, so load the image you want to examine first.
<b>Note</b>: Because a successful match_tracker leaves the status flag unchanged, an error from an earlier command may still be showing. To know whether this command failed, set the flag to TRKS_OK with set_tracker (TKSET_STATUS) before it and read it afterwards.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">math_array</span>
math_array &lt;array_receptor&gt; &lt;math_func&gt; [array_modifier]
This is the array version of math_var (which see for more detail). Array indices are respected according to the rules below.
&lt;array_receptor&gt; - a user-defined array variable capable of receiving the numerical result and, for non-string data types, initially containing a numerical value used as an argument for the mathematical function to be performed.
[array_modifier] - a varval that may be a constant, a system variable, a user-defined single variable or a user-defined array variable containing a numerical value if it is to be used with non-string receptors. If this is to be used with a string receptor and it is also a string then it does not have to be numerical.
&lt;math_func&gt; a varval of type string that evaluates to one of the values described in the section for math_var - so see that section for a detailed description.
<b>Note</b>: Unlike the single value version of this function, the modifier (if present) must be a user-defined array and not a constant.
<b>Note</b>: The operation will take place from the indexed receptor array member to the end of the receptor array if no modifier is used. If a modifier is required then the number of elements from the index of the receptor to the end of the receptor will be compared to the number of elements from the index of the modifier to the end of the modifier. If either one of those numbers is smaller than the other then the smaller number of elements will dictate the range over which the operation will be performed and the operation will start at the index of each array and continue for that many elements. Any elements not in the operated range will be unchanged.
<b>Note</b>: If a mathematical function requires a modifier then the modifier argument must be supplied. Otherwise it may be supplied as an option without generating errors - it will just be silently ignored.
<b>Note</b>: All data types may be used but, if the receptor is of string type then only an assignment equals ('=') operation may be performed. That is why string arrays need not contain numerical values for the case of '='.
<b>Note</b>: Aliasing is permitted - you may use the same variable as receptor and modifier with the same or different index in each instance.



<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">math_var</span>
math_var &lt;var_receptor&gt; &lt;math_func&gt; [varval]
This performs a mathematical function, &lt;math_func&gt;, on the argument &lt;var_receptor&gt;. If the mathematical function requires a second argument it uses [varval]. It puts the result into &lt;var_receptor&gt; replacing its original value.
&lt;var_receptor&gt; - a variable capable of receiving the numerical result and initially containing a numerical value used as an argument for the mathematical function to be performed.
[varval] - a numerical constant or a variable containing a numerical value.
&lt;math_func&gt; a varval of type string that evaluates to one of the following string values:
  add
 sub
 mul
 div
 =
 sin
 cos
 tan
 asin
 acos
 atan
 atan2
 sinh
 cosh
 tanh
 exp
 log
 log10
 pow
 sqrt
 ceil
 floor
 abs
 imod
 fmod
norls
norl1
norl2
<b>Note:</b> The option of using a string variable for &lt;math_func&gt; gives the PCS programmer great flexibility because the same math_var line in a script can be made to perform different mathematical operations by varying the contents of the variable used as &lt;math_func&gt; according to some logic.
<b>Note</b>: This command is symbolically the equivalent of doing this: &lt;var_receptor&gt; = math_func(&lt;var_receptor&gt;,[varval]) in C.
<b>Note</b>: The meaning of the functions are as described in the C programming language with the exceptions / modifications described below. In cases where the mathematical function requires two arguments names 'x' and 'y' in the book 'The C Programming Language' by Kernighan and Ritchie, the 'y' argument shall be the [varval] argument specified here. Otherwise the sole argument of the function shall be the &lt;var_receptor&gt; argument supplied here.
Exceptions / Modifications:
The abs operator will return the absolute value of either a float or an int value input.
The imod operator is the integer modulus of the input values equivalent to the '%' operation of the C programming language.
The operators beginning 'norl' are normalisation operators. This means that for an array of numbers, they first make a calculation as to the total accumulated value (TAV) of those numbers on one pass, then, on the second pass they modify each element in the array by dividing it by that TAV. They differ in the exact details of how TAV is calculated as follows:
norls - This is the signed L1 normalisation operator. TAV is the sum of all values in the array, respecting the sign of each value in the sum. For an array of size 1 (single value operation) the result will always be +1.0.
norl1 - This is the strict mathematical L1 normalisation operator. TAV is the sum of the absolute value of each value in the array. For an array of size 1 (single value operation) the result will be either +1.0 (if the value is positive) or -1.0 (if the value is negative).
norl2 - This is the L2 normalisation operator. TAV is the square root of the sum of the squares of each value in the array. For an array of size 1 (single value operation) the result will be either +1.0 (if the value is positive) or -1.0 (if the value is negative).
<b>Note</b>: For all operators except for the basic arithmetic operators (add, sub, mul, div and =), the value of &lt;var_receptor&gt; and [varval] shall be cast to a float (double) in the script interpreter if they are not already of float type for all but the imod function (when a cast to an int will be performed if the input arguments are not already int). If &lt;var_receptor&gt; is an int for any of the functions that return floating point values then the result will be attempted to be cast into an int with a 0.5 added for rounding positive values i.e.:  int_result = (int)(0.5 + float_result) or subtracted for rounding negative values i.e.:  int_result = (int)(float_result - 0.5). For add, sub, mul, div and = on an int receptor, the [varval] is first cast to int, and the operation is exactly that of the separate commands add_var, sub_var, mul_var, div_var and set_var: division truncates, and integer over#ow wraps. However, add_var, when used with a string variable as receptor will treat the RHS-value as a string and concatenate it to the receptor's string instead of performing numerical arithmetic.
<b>Note</b>: An error (such as a range error or failure to cast float to int) will result in script termination with an error message. A run-time error will be called if you specify a &lt;math_func&gt; that requires two arguments but fail to supply [varval] - this is not checked for at syntax check time because the &lt;math_func&gt; may vary as the program progresses because it may be held in a string variable. However, no error will result if you supply the optional [varval] for functions that only require 1 argument - in that case the 'extra' [varval] argument will just be ignored and not used. This allows the use of a string variable for &lt;math_func&gt; where the value of the string in that variable may take on the value specifying any of the allowed mathematical functions that require only one argument on some occasions and two arguments on others.
<b>Note</b>: You can make extensive multi-step mathematical calculations by successive application of these maths primitives, possibly contained in a function (see def_func) but if you need significantly more complex or extensive mathematical operations then it might be more convenient to do these in an external program and call it using the PCS commands execute_system or execute_daemon, which see.
<b>Note</b>: For simple arithmetic that does not require the metaprogramming flexibility of math_var, see the commands add_var, sub_var, mul_var, div_var and set_var.
<b>Note</b>: When using operands that are numerical types (float and/or int) then the math_var operation with the assignment equals operator (`=`) will have the same effect as using set_var with numerical types. However using set_var with a string variable as the receptacle is not the same as using math_var with string variables as operands because math_var always will attempt to cast string operands to numerical values as described below (and failure of such casting will terminate the script). However set_var may be used with strings and if the receptor is a string variable then the RHS-value will be attempted to be cast to a string.
<b>Note</b>: If using string variables an automatic cast to a numerical value will be done for you provided the string variable contains a string that is consistent with a numerical value. String variables used here must initially contain a numerical string at the time of definition otherwise the script checker will reject it because the script checker will not perform any set_str commands in the script.
For example, if you define a string like this: 'def_var string str1 _' and then go on later to do this 'set_str str1 -1.3' before using it in a math_var, the script checker will only have the original defined string value to work with (that is '_') and so this is not a valid number and so it will raise an error. Even though you did a set_str to make it a number, that set_str will only be executed during run-time, not check-time, so the checker will only see '_'. To avoid this, define any string variable that may, at any time, be used as a numerical argument as a number at definition - for example like this: 'def_var string str1 0.0' - then the script checker will not complain.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">max_daemons</span>
max_daemons &lt;number&gt;
This sets the maximum number of asynchronous background commands (invoked via execute_daemon or xyzs_process - which see) that are allowed to execute simultaneously on the client host computer. This command serves as a dynamic throttle to protect system memory and CPU cores from thrashing during high-density operations like automated XY scans or tight loops in scripts. 
&lt;number&gt; - a varval of type int that evaluates to between 1 and 100 (inclusive) specifying the parallel process ceiling. 
<b>Note</b>: If a script does not explicitly invoke this command, the interpreter defaults to a value of 1. This forces background commands to run in an orderly, sequential, one-by-one queue.
<b>Note</b>: This parameter can be modified dynamically at any point within a script. If max_daemons is increased, the internal queue manager will instantly pump and fire extra pending background tasks to fill the newly opened processing slots. If max_daemons is decreased below the number of currently executing processes, running tasks are permitted to finish safely without corruption, but the queue will halt spawning new tasks until the active count drops below the newly defined threshold.
<b>Note</b>: It is advisable to keep the max_daemons number to just below your total number of CPU cores so you don't over-work the CPU and end up slowing down the whole system.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">milliduration</span>
milliduration &lt;start_millis&gt; &lt;receptor&gt;
This calculates the monotonic time in milliseconds from the value provided in &lt;start_millis&gt; to the current time (the time the command is issued).
&lt;start_millis&gt; - a varval of type int &gt;= 0 representing a monotonic millisecond count 
&lt;receptor&gt; - a variable capable of receiving an int that will receive the duration time (in milliseconds) between &lt;start_millis&gt; and the time the command is executed.
<b>Example</b>:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>Pardus pcs 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>verbosity 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var string msg _</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int idx 0   # Loop index</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int millidur 0 # Testing the milliduration command</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int startmillis 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_func main</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>print_clear</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_str msg --- Starting milliduration test ---</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>print_value msg 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_var startmillis Millis</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_str msg Starting the stop watch at:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>add_var msg Space</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>add_var msg startmillis</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>print_value msg 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>set_condexp idx == 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>loop_while Condexp</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>sleep 0.25</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>milliduration startmillis millidur</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>set_str msg milliseconds elapsed:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>add_var msg Space</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>add_var msg millidur</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>print_value msg 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>add_var idx 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>      </tt><tt>set_condexp idx &lt; 5</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>endloop</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>return</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>exit</tt> </span>
The result of running this will look something like this:
--- Starting milliduration test ---
Starting the stop watch at: 763205
milliseconds elapsed: 272
milliseconds elapsed: 527
milliseconds elapsed: 782
milliseconds elapsed: 1036
milliseconds elapsed: 1290



<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">mul_var</span>
mul_var &lt;varname&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
This multiplies the value of a user-defined variable by some value. 
&lt;varname&gt; - The name of the variable whose value you want to change
&lt;varval&gt; - The value (or variable containing the value) you want to multiply &lt;varname&gt; by.
<b>Note</b>: This is symbolically equivalent to &lt;varname&gt; *= &lt;varval&gt; in C.
<b>Note</b>: Multiplication can also be done inline using math_var (which see).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">null_command</span>
null_command
This just skips to the next command. Although no arguments are shown, this may have any number of arguments as long as you don't overshoot the line length or argument count limits. Any supplied arguments will not be validated or used. Thus, you may replace any command with null_command without altering the remaining arguments on that line if you want the interpreter to skip that line. Likewise you can put null_command in front of any command and that line will be skipped. In this capacity it acts as a comment marker that survives pre-processing. However, its main intended use is not for the PCS programmer but for the linker. It is designed so that the linker can 'blot out' any add_lib commands after they have been implemented (after the lib code has been added to the main program) to avoid libs being linked multiple times with each run of the program (which is what would happen if the add_lib command(s) were left 'as is' after linking - and that would always result in duplicate code errors).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">poll_user</span>
poll_user &lt;wait&gt; &lt;return&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
This gets a value from the user input edit box in pardclient and puts it into &lt;varval&gt;.
&lt;wait&gt; - a binary choice varval. If it evaluates to ON then the interpreter will wait for at least one character to be entered into the ext box before allowing the PCS script execution to continue. If it evaluates to OFF then the edit box will be checked immediately and any character or characters in it will be read and processed and the PCS script execution will continue regardless of whether there was anything in the edit box or not.
&lt;return&gt; - a binary choice varval. If it evaluates to OFF then the polling function will not wait for the user to hit the return key on the keyboard before reading the contents of the edit box. If it evaluates to ON then it will only read the contents of the box once return has been hit. However, even if &lt;return&gt; is ON, the script will not wait for the user to enter anything, let alone hit return unless &lt;wait&gt; is also ON.  If &lt;wait&gt; is OFF when &lt;return&gt; is ON then the interpreter will simply register that it must not read the contents of the entry box until the user hits return and it will carry on with the script. If, during script execution, the user enters something and hits return then it will set a flag to say return has been hit and if (and only if) the script polls the user again at some point after this will the interpreter read the contents of the entry box. If &lt;wait&gt; is ON and &lt;return&gt; is OFF then the interpreter will wait for the user to enter something in the entry box and, as soon as something has been entered, the interpreter will read it and carry on executing the script - without waiting for the user to hit return. This means that if the user is typing with the keyboard they will only have time to enter one character before the interpreter reads it and carries on. If the user wants to enter multiple characters in this 'automatic' mode then they need to copy the characters into the general clipboard and paste them all in one go to the entry box. This might be inconvenient, in which case the following configuration will help. If both &lt;wait&gt;  and &lt;return&gt; are ON then the interpreter will wait for a user to enter something and, furthermore, it won't carry on till the user hits return. This configuration allows a user to manually type however many characters they like (up to a maximum of PCS_PATH_MAX) at their leisure.
<b>Note</b>: While an earlier call is still waiting for Return, a new call reads into the earlier call's variable
<b>Note</b>: Non-numeric input for a numeric variable, or an empty Return, ends the wait and leaves the variable unchanged without any indication.
<b>Note</b>: The behaviour described above can be briefly summarised in this table:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">&lt;wait&gt;</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">&lt;return&gt;</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Behaviour                                         </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├────────┼──────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ OFF    │ OFF      │ Immediate check; reads input box contents          │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │          │ instantly regardless of content.                   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OFF    │ ON       │ Continues execution immediately; reads data only   │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │          │ if a future poll_user call occurs after the user   │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │          │ hits Return.                                       │ </tt>
  <tt> │ ON     │ OFF      │ Pauses execution until at least one character is   │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │          │ entered, reading instantly without waiting for     │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │          │ Return.                                            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ ON     │ ON       │ Pauses execution until user enters characters and  │ </tt>
  <tt> │        │          │ hits Return.                                       │ </tt>



<b>N</b><b>ote</b>: The user can use the GUI direction and focus buttons to provide the input to the entry box if they select the 'Divert to script' check box option on the 'Manual' tab. In this mode the buttons will emit a single character to the entry box when they are clicked, that character being their main label excluding any '[' or ']'. So if you want your script to react to these GUI button click you must set (in your PCS script) the inputs to respond to the single characters '&lt;', '&gt;', '^', 'v', '+' and/or '-'. This provides a convenient ready-made way to allow the user to use a GUI to interact with a script instead of having to type in or copy-paste in text manually. One advantage of using this method for 'manual' control of the microscope stage motors (for example) is that with an interactive script you can enact a real-time preview while responding to motion button clicks but you can't do that using the direction buttons natively in the 'Manual' tab to move the stage. While the 'Divert to script' option is ticked the outline around the button groups will turn red and the buttons will have no other effect so can't be used to effect motor motion from the Manual tab in the usual way. 
<b>Note</b>: After the script interpreter reads the entry box it will always clear its contents.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_clear</span>
preview_clear
Clears the full VGA preview display area.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_copy</span>
preview_copy &lt;height&gt; &lt;width&gt; &lt;receptacle&gt; &lt;colchan&gt;
Copies a rectangular portion of the current preview image into an integer array. The copy begins at a top left corner of the preview image defined by the current value of the system variable PreviewPos. The index of the receptacle array is respected - any writing will occur from that point onwards.
&lt;height&gt; - an integer varval with value between 1 and 480 inclusive.
&lt;width&gt; - an integer varval with value between 1 and 640 inclusive.
&lt;receptacle&gt; - an array variable of any data type to receive the copied byte values
&lt;colchan&gt; - a varval with value between 0 and 4 inclusive. 
<b>Note</b>: The supplied dimensions are the size of the area to be copied in pixels. The &lt;receptacle&gt; must have at least that many cells available from the index you supply to its end. Any &lt;receptacle&gt;  values before this index or after the last read of pixel data will not be altered.
<b>Note</b>: If the dimensions you supply overshoot the VGA limit of the preview image an error will occur (the script will terminate) but lateral wrapping will be allowed (the right boundary of the preview image array is not checked prior to copy).
<b>Note</b>: The acceptable values for &lt;colchan&gt; and their meaning are as follows:
0	- All three colour channel bytes per preview pixel will be copied, each colour channel byte will be copied to a separate array cell (the values will not be packed). This means that the number of available array cells from your supplied index must be at least &lt;height&gt;*&lt;width&gt;*3 .
1	- The calculated intensity values only will be read, each pixel's value will be read into a separate int cell in the array. This means that the number of available array cells from your supplied index must be at least &lt;height&gt;*&lt;width&gt; . In stdpard.pch this value is defined as the macro CCHAN_Y. The calculation is Y=(R+G+B)/3.
2	- The red channel values only will be read, each pixel's value will be read into a separate int cell in the array. This means that the number of available array cells from your supplied index must be at least &lt;height&gt;*&lt;width&gt; . In stdpard.pch this value is defined as the macro CCHAN_R.
3	- The green channel values only will be read, each pixel's value will be read into a separate int cell in the array. This means that the number of available array cells from your supplied index must be at least &lt;height&gt;*&lt;width&gt; . In stdpard.pch this value is defined as the macro CCHAN_G.
4	- The blue channel values only will be read, each pixel's value will be read into a separate int cell in the array. This means that the number of available array cells from your supplied index must be at least &lt;height&gt;*&lt;width&gt; . In stdpard.pch this value is defined as the macro CCHAN_B.
<b>Note</b>: Using a string receptor variable is allowed but will incur more overhead than using an integer or a float and so will take longer.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_angles</span>
preview_draw_angles &lt;angle1&gt; &lt;angle2&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This sets the angle parameters for any ellipse that is subsequently drawn using the preview_draw_shape command (which see). 
&lt;angle1&gt; - an integer between 0 and 360 inclusive. This specifies the starting angle boundary in degrees.
&lt;angle2&gt; - an integer between 0 and 360 inclusive. This specifies the ending angle boundary in degrees.
<b>Note</b>: Angles are interpreted thus: 0 degrees points directly to the right (3 o'clock position) along the horizontal X-axis, and angles increase sequentially in a clockwise direction. To render a full, complete 360-degree circle or closed ring shape, set &lt;angle1&gt; to 0 and &lt;angle2&gt; to 360 (this is the default condition at the start of any script).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_array</span>
preview_draw_array &lt;int_array&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This executes a batch of drawing commands stored sequentially within an in-memory integer array. This allows a script to pre-compile complex graphical scenes, custom fonts, animation states, or UI layouts into a single variable array and emit them to the preview overlay canvas in a single high-speed processing loop.
&lt;int_array&gt; - an integer array variable with at least 2 members. At run-time, the contents of the array must conform to the correct format for a drawing interpreted by the Graphics Sub-Interpreter (GSI) otherwise an error will terminate the script. The format rules are defined below.
<b>Note</b>: The rules for interpreting graphics commands stored in an array variable are:
The first element of the array will be a value representing the <i>type of command</i> to execute and will dictate what values must follow it.
After this will follow one or more parameter values as specified by the initial 'type of command' value
This pattern can be repeated indefinitely till the array is properly terminated with the GSI_TYPE_END termination code value (see below).
<b>Note</b>:  This command does respect the index you provide in your integer array argument. So if you provide an array as mydrawing[idx], the mydrawing array will always be read from index 0 regardless of the values of idx.
<b>Note</b>: The permitted 'Type of command' constants are those shown in the table below. In your PCS code you may use either the raw numerical value or the #defined constant if you have a header file included that includes those constants such as stdpard.pch:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">#defined       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Literal   </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Meaning                                           </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">constant       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">value     </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">                                                  </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ GSI_TYPE_ENV    │ -1         │ Set global behaviour modifiers like colour, cursor │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │ position, etc.                                     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ GSI_TYPE_PLOT   │ -2         │ Enact drawing of a shape (pixel, line, rect,       │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │ ellipse)                                           │ </tt>
  <tt> │ GSI_TYPE_STRING │ -3         │ Enact drawing of a text string                     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ GSI_TYPE_MGLYPH │ -4         │ Paint a pixel map in two colours (packed bits)     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ GSI_TYPE_CGLYPH │ -5         │ Paint a general colour pixel map (packed bits)     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ GSI_TYPE_CBITMP │ -6         │ Paint a general colour 24 bpp pixel map (raw)      │ </tt>
  <tt> │ GSI_TYPE_GBITMP │ -7         │ Paint a general monochrome 8 bpp pixel map (raw)   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ GSI_TYPE_END    │ -8         │ No more graphics sub-interpreter tasks follow      │ </tt>


Thus the very first element of a graphics array must be negative.

<b>Note</b>: After a GST_TYPE_ENV value, the GSI expects to see one of the following values:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">#defined       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Literal   </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">constant       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">value     </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ 3          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _LWIDTH         │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ 4          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _CHAMFER        │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ 5          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _ANGLES         │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ 6          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _BLEND          │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ 9          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _COLOUR         │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ 10         │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _CURSOR         │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ 17         │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _DESTTYPE       │            │ </tt>




Any other value here will result in an error. After each of the above values, the GSI expects to see exactly the following integer arguments:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">#defined       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Arguments </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">constant       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">that must </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">               </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">follow it </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ &lt;pixels&gt;   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _LWIDTH         │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ &lt;pixels&gt;   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _CHAMFER        │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ &lt;angle1&gt;   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _ANGLES         │ &lt;angle2&gt;   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ &lt;truefalse │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _BLEND          │ &gt;          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ &lt;R&gt; &lt;G&gt;    │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _COLOUR         │ &lt;B&gt;        │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ &lt;X&gt; &lt;Y&gt;    │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _CURSOR         │            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ OP_PREVIEW_DRAW │ &lt;truefalse │ </tt>
  <tt> │ _DESTTYPE       │ &gt;          │ </tt>



For a detailed description of what those arguments mean, see the entry for the corresponding command in this manual.

<b>Note</b>: After a GSI_TYPE_PLOT value, the GSI expects to see one of the following values:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">#defined       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Literal   </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">constant       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">value     </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ PDS_PIXEL       │ 0          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ PDS_LINE        │ 1          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ PDS_RECT        │ 2          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ PDS_ELLIPSE     │ 3          │ </tt>



Any other value here will result in an error. After each of the above values, the GSI expects to see exactly the following integer arguments:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">#defined       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Arguments </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">constant       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">that must </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">               </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">follow it </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ PDS_PIXEL       │ No         │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ arguments  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ at all     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ PDS_LINE        │ &lt;filled?&gt;  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ &lt;X_dest&gt;   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ &lt;Y_dest&gt;   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ PDS_RECT        │ &lt;filled?&gt;  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ &lt;X_dest&gt;   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ &lt;Y_dest&gt;   │ </tt>
  <tt> │ PDS_ELLIPSE     │ &lt;filled?&gt;  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ &lt;X_radius&gt; │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ &lt;Y_radius&gt; │ </tt>



For a detailed description of what those arguments mean, see the entry for the corresponding command in this manual.

<b>Note</b>: After a GSI_TYPE_STRING value, the GSI expects to see all of the following argument values:
&lt;size&gt; &lt;bg_R&gt; &lt;bg_G&gt; &lt;bg_B&gt; &lt;invert?&gt;
For a detailed description of what those arguments mean, see the entry for the preview_draw_string command in this manual but note that, unlike with the stand-alone command, all these parameters must be included in the array, none of them are optional. For this reason you are allowed to set any of the background colour channel values to a number &gt; 255 and, if you do that, then all the supplied background colour values supplied here and the invert value will be ignored - it is the equivalent of making them 'optional' in terms of actioning them, even though you must supply them. All supplied values must be &gt;= 0.
After the &lt;invert?&gt; the array may contain 0 or more integer values representing the ASCII value of the characters of the string to be printed. There is no need or expectation to supply a null termination character. In fact, the only thing that terminates the string will be to pass a negative number that is one of the GSI_TYPE_ constants.
<b>Note</b>: After a GSI_TYPE_MGLYPH value, the GSI expects to see all of the following argument values:
&lt;R0&gt; &lt;G0&gt; &lt;B0&gt; &lt;R1&gt; &lt;G1&gt; &lt;B1&gt; &lt;height&gt; &lt;width&gt;
Where the first 3 specify the colour of 0-value pixels in the glyph, the next three specify the colour of 1-value pixels in the glyph and the &lt;height&gt; and &lt;width&gt; are the dimensions of the glyph in pixels. After this must come one or more integer values specifying the bits in the glyph. Each integer represents a packed horizontal sequence of up to 32 pixels, read sequentially from left to right (from the most significant bit, bit 31, down to the least significant bit).
Each horizontal row of the glyph is processed independently and must be padded out to a full 32-bit boundary. This means a single row will consume exactly (width + 31) / 32 integers before the next row begins. Pixels are rendered from the top row down to the bottom row.
Pixels assigned a colour code where R, G, and B are all 0 are treated as completely transparent and will not overwrite the background buffer canvas. The entire glyph sequence block is concluded immediately after the final packed row integer is read; unlike text strings, it does not require a trailing negative termination token, as its exact array footprint is predetermined by its height and width properties. However,  a negative GST_TYPE_ value must follow the last pixel for the array to be considered correctly formatted.
<b>Note</b>: After a GSI_TYPE_CGLYPH value, the GSI expects to see all of the following argument values:
&lt;height&gt; &lt;width&gt;
No colour arguments are expected because each pixel value carries its own colour information. Following the &lt;width&gt; value must come one or more integers containing the glyph pixel information. 
Each subsequent integer represents a single individual pixel, organized sequentially in rows from the top-left to the bottom-right of the glyph boundary. The total number of colour integers expected is exactly equal to the height multiplied by the width (i.e. &lt;height&gt; * &lt;width&gt;). 
The colour data within each integer must be encoded as a packed 24-bit RGB value (which, if using the hexadecimal format would be 0x00RRGGBB). The individual colour channels are extracted by the engine using bitwise shifts: bits 16–23 specify the Red channel, bits 8–15 specify the Green channel, and bits 0–7 specify the Blue channel.
Any pixel encoded as a literal 0 (0x00000000, representing pure black) is treated as completely transparent and will not overwrite the background canvas buffer. The entire glyph sequence block concludes automatically once the pre-calculated number of pixel elements has been fully parsed; it does not depend on a trailing negative command to terminate it (although a negative GST_TYPE_ value must follow the last pixel for the array to be considered correctly formatted).
Note: When no more graphics commands are to follow you must supply the value GSI_TYPE_END. If the end of the array is reached before encountering the GSI_TYPE_END token this is an error - malformed graphics array - and will be reported as such.
<b>Note</b>: After a GSI_TYPE_CBITMP value, the GSI expects to see all of the following argument values:
&lt;height&gt; &lt;width&gt; &lt;use_alpha?&gt;
&lt;use_alpha?&gt; is either 0 or non-0 if 0 then it will not treat black (0,0,0) as transparent when rendering over other drawings and it will also change any in-coming pixel from (0,0,0) to (0,0,1) (very dark blue) so that it will not show up as transparent when the graphics buffer is finally overlain onto the preview image for display. If this argument is non-0 then the usual transparency rules will be followed.
Each 3 subsequent integers represents a single individual pixel's red, green and blue values and each will range from 0 to 255. The pixels are ordered sequentially from top left to bottom right in raster fashion. The total number of pixel integers expected is exactly equal to the height multiplied by the width multiplied by 3 (i.e. &lt;height&gt;*&lt;width&gt;*3). This is the same format as is captured by the command preview_copy (with &lt;colchan&gt; = 0) so you can copy a rectangle of the current preview image directly into this part of the graphics array using that command (which see).
<b>Note</b>: After a GSI_TYPE_GBITMP value, the GSI expects to see all of the following argument values:
&lt;height&gt; &lt;width&gt; &lt;R&gt; &lt;G&gt; &lt;B&gt; &lt;use_alpha?&gt;
&lt;use_alpha?&gt; is either 0 or non-0 if 0 then it will not treat black (0) as transparent when rendering over other drawings and it will also change any in-coming pixel from (0) to (1) (very dark grey) so that it will not show up as transparent when the graphics buffer is finally overlain onto the preview image for display. If this argument is non-0 then the usual transparency rules will be followed.
Each subsequent integer represents a single individual pixel value and each will range from 0 to 255. The pixels are ordered sequentially from top left to bottom right in raster fashion. The total number of pixel integers expected is exactly equal to the height multiplied by the width (i.e. &lt;height&gt;*&lt;width&gt;). This is the same format as is captured by the command preview_copy (with &lt;colchan&gt; &gt; 0) so you can copy a rectangle of the current preview image directly into this part of the graphics array using that command (which see).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_blend</span>
preview_draw_blend &lt;truefalse&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This determines whether any subsequent drawing are simply drawn over existing pixel colours, completely over-writing them, or whether newly drawn pixels merge with the colours of any pre-existing drawings to give a kind of semi-transparent effect. 
&lt;truefalse&gt; - a binary choice varval. If this evaluates to non-zero the semi-transparent blending effect will be activated, otherwise drawing will simply overwrite any pre-existing pixels they write to.
<b>Note</b>: When blending is active (ON), the graphics engine activates a highly optimised, cross-platform SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) blending routine. This routine averages the colour channels of the incoming drawing pixel and the destination background buffer pixel via bitwise transformations. This creates a semi-transparent, translucent vector overlay effect, allowing details on the primary application viewport underneath to remain visible through drawn blocks, text fields, or glyph segments.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_buffer</span>
preview_draw_buffer &lt;mode&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This command determines how all other preview_draw_### graphics commands behave.
&lt;mode&gt; - an integer from 0 to 4
<b>Note</b>: The effect of each value is as follows:
0 - ignore all preview_draw_### commands except preview_draw_buffer
1 - enable commands but push them to the display as soon as each command is carried out (no buffering)
2 - store all preview_draw_### commands in the buffer and only push to display on receipt of a preview_draw_show command or after a get_preview command and do not clear the drawing buffer after showing. 'Push to display' means drawing over whatever is currently in the preview bitmap image and displaying the preview bitmap image in the preview display area.
3 and 4 - as for 1 and 2 but clear the preview drawing buffer after showing.  
<b>Note</b>: In CLI mode, 'pushing to display' entails burning the current preview draw buffer data into the actual preview buffer itself, so will change the pixel values of the current preview buffer. This does not happen when a PCS program is run via the pardclient GUI - in that case the preview buffer overlay is burnt into a transient copy of the preview buffer image that is only used to update the display and the preview buffer pixel values themselves are unaltered.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_chamfer</span>
preview_draw_chamfer &lt;pixels&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This sets the corner rounding (chamfer) radius for all subsequent drawings of rectangles.
&lt;pixels&gt; - an integer from 0 to 479
<b>Note</b>: Selecting 0 turns off chamfering so all rectangles will have sharp, 90-degree, corners. This is the default setting at the start of any script run.
<b>Note</b>: If a requested chamfer dimension exceeds half the width or height of a drawn shape, the graphics engine safely caps the corner blending threshold internally to prevent layout corruption or inverse vector crossing artefacts.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_clear</span>
preview_draw_clear
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This clears the preview drawing overlay buffer of all drawings. There are no arguments.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_colour</span>
preview_draw_colour &lt;R&gt; &lt;G&gt; &lt;B&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This sets the foreground colour for any subsequent drawings.
&lt;R&gt; - an integer from 0 to 255 representing the red channel content.
&lt;G&gt; - an integer from 0 to 255 representing the green channel content.
&lt;B&gt; - an integer from 0 to 255 representing the blue channel content.
Note: Full black is not possible because 0,0,0 will be treated as 'transparent'. So 1,1,1 is the darkest paintable colour.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_cursor</span>
preview_draw_cursor &lt;X&gt; &lt;Y&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This sets the starting point for drawing any shape or text on the preview overlay buffer.
&lt;X&gt; - an integer from 0 to 639 representing the X position from the left of the VGA drawing area.
&lt;Y&gt; - an integer from 0 to 479 representing the Y position from the top of the VGA drawing area.
<b>Note</b>: For drawing single pixels this is where the pixel will be drawn. For lines this is the starting point of the line. For rectangles this it eh starting corner position of the rectangle. For ellipses this is the centre of the ellipse. For text strings, characters are drawn from the cursor point downwards and to the right. This means the specified cursor XY coordinate forms the absolute top-left boundary of the first character in the drawn string. As each character row is parsed, row increments add positive offsets to the Y coordinate, driving the font blitter down towards the bottom of the screen.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_desttype</span>
preview_draw_desttype &lt;truefalse&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This determines how destination values for drawing rectangles or lines are interpreted. 
&lt;truefalse&gt; - a binary choice varval. If this evaluates to non-zero then the &lt;X_dest&gt; and &lt;Y_dest&gt; values are interpreted as dimensions (in pixels) from the current cursor position so they may be negative. Otherwise they are interpreted as absolute coordinates and so must be 0 or positive.
<b>Note</b>: This setting only affects the drawing on lines and rectangles. Single pixels are plotted at the current cursor position always, regardless of this setting, and ellipses use the equivalent arguments as the length of radii always, regardless of this setting.
<b>Note</b>: The default setting at the start of every script run is false (use absolute coordinates).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_file</span>
preview_draw_file &lt;file_path&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This reads a P6 ppm image from disc and paints it to the preview overlay graphics buffer.
&lt;file_path&gt; - a string varval. This must evaluate the name of the file (optionally including its full directory path) to be loaded. No white space is allowed. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: The image is rendered at native 1:1 pixel scale, with its top-left corner anchored precisely at the active cursor coordinates.
<b>Note</b>: Because the GSI engine interprets a colour value of 0,0,0 strictly as a "transparent" alpha-mask layer, any absolute black pixels embedded within your source PPM image will render as completely transparent, exposing the background buffer or camera feed underneath. To ensure black image regions paint as opaque, solid shapes, pre-process your source PPM file assets so that their darkest pixels use a colour signature of 1,1,1 instead of 0,0,0.
<b>Note</b>: Memory boundary safeguards natively clip image data overflowing past the 640x480 VGA display limits without raising an error.
<b>Note</b>: See preview_draw_save for the inverse procedure (saving the graphics buffer to disc). See also preview_load and preview_save.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_lwidth</span>
preview_draw_lwidth &lt;pixels&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This effectively sets the 'brush size' or pixel size for all subsequent drawings except text and bitmap blitting.
&lt;pixels&gt; - an integer from 1 to 16 specifying the total width of the drawing line in pixels.
<b>Note</b>: A &lt;pixels&gt; value of 1 routes graphics drawings through a separate fast route. For the fastest performance therefore - such as for tracking moving objects at speed - you may wish to set this to 1 if larger values are too slow.
<b>Note</b>: The specified &lt;pixels&gt; value represents the total thickness (diameter) of the drawing brush, meaning a line will expand symmetrically outward from its true mathematical coordinate path.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_pcg</span>
preview_draw_pcg &lt;char_number&gt; &lt;int_array&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This allows you to program a custom character shape into one of the 256 graphics text font positions (i.e. it imparts the functionality of a programmable character generator - pcg).
&lt;char_number&gt; - an integer from 0 to 255 specifying which of the font characters you want to program.
&lt;int_array&gt; - an integer array variable with at least 8 members from the index you provide.
<b>Note</b>: The graphics text font characters are 8x8 meaning 8 rows of one byte (8 bits) per row. You program a character row-by-row from top to bottom in successive integer values from the input integer array. The 8 bits for each row are provided in the lowest 8 bits of an integer from the &lt;int_array&gt;.
<b>Note</b>: Unlike the command preview_draw_array, which uses the integer positions in the input array starting at array index 0, regardless of what index you specify in the command argument, this preview_draw_pcg command <i>does</i> respect the index you provide. So if you provide an array as mychars[idx] then your array mychars must have at least 8 members free from the member idx, not from index 0 (unless idx == 0). 


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_save</span>
preview_draw_save &lt;file_path&gt;
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This saves the current graphics overlay buffer as a P6 ppm image to disc.
&lt;file_path&gt; - a string varval. This must evaluate the name of the file (optionally including its full directory path) to be loaded. No white space is allowed. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: See preview_draw_file for the inverse procedure (loading an image from file into the graphics buffer). See also preview_load and preview_save.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_shape</span>
preview_draw_shape &lt;type&gt; [&lt;filled?&gt; &lt;X_dest/radius&gt; &lt;Y_dest/radius&gt;]
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This allows you to plot a pixel or draw a shape at the current cursor position using the current values of the graphics environent variables set by other commands (such as print_draw_colour and print_draw_lwidth, etc.)
&lt;type&gt; - an integer from 0 to 3 specifying what shape to draw.
&lt;filled?&gt; - a binary choice varval. If this evaluates to non-zero then the shape is drawn solid, otherwise it is drawn in outline.
&lt;X_dest/radius&gt; - an integer from -639 to 639 specifying the width radius of an ellipse or the X position of the destination of a rectangle or line (the start position is determined by the current X value of the graphics cursor set by the last call to preview_draw_cursor or its default value, 0).
&lt;Y_dest/radius&gt; - an integer from -479 to 479 specifying the height radius of an ellipse or the Y position of the destination of a rectangle or line (the start position is determined by the current Y value of the graphics cursor set by the last call to preview_draw_cursor or its default value, 0).
<b>Note</b>: The options for &lt;type&gt; are those PDS_ constants shown in the table under the entry for preview_draw_array. If PDS_PIXEL is chosen (value 0) then the remaining arguments are optional and they will not be used if supplied. For all other shapes you must supply all arguments.
<b>Note</b>: Ellipses are drawn from the current cursor position being their centre point.
<b>Note</b>: When the value for preview_draw_lwidth is set to 1, all shapes (and individual pixels) are drawn via a separate fast-path algorithm, otherwise a slower general algorithm is used.
<b>Note</b>: The negative options for &lt;X_dest/radius&gt; and &lt;Y_dest/radius&gt; are only valid for rectangles and lines and, even then, only if the destination type is set to true (e.g. via the preview_draw_desttype command). Otherwise only 0 or positive numbers will be valid at run-time.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_show</span>
preview_draw_show
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This pushes the overlay graphics buffer to the display. It updates the GUI to show the current preview image and the overlay graphics buffer. It also updates any other pending GUI events. See also the command preview_show.
<b>Note</b>: In CLI mode, the updated preview image will be saved to disc if you have set the CLI save root and path string with the command cli_path.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_draw_string</span>
preview_draw_string &lt;size&gt; &lt;varval&gt; [&lt;bg_R&gt; &lt;bg_G&gt; &lt;bg_B&gt; &lt;invert?&gt;]
This is one of the preview overlay graphics commands. This draws a text string onto the preview overlay graphics buffer.
&lt;size&gt; - an integer from 0 to 63. It represents a scale factor - not a "point size".
&lt;varval&gt; - The text to be rendered. This does not have to be a string variable - any data type will do (it will be cast to a string by the PCS autocasting rules).
&lt;bg_R&gt; - an integer from 0 to 255 representing the red channel content for background colour.
&lt;bg_G&gt; - an integer from 0 to 255 representing the green channel content for background colour.
&lt;bg_B&gt; - an integer from 0 to 255 representing the blue channel content for background colour.
&lt;invert?&gt; - a binary choice varval. If this evaluates to non-zero then the string is rendered with background and foreground colours reversed.
<b>Note</b>: A colour of 0,0,0 means 'transparent' and that is the default font rendering background colour.
<b>Note</b>: The newline character (ASCII 10) will be respected when rendering (text will break to a new line when this is encountered) but not carriage return.
<b>Note</b>: The safeguards to ensure text can overflow the VGA boundaries without causing a problem mean that text rendering (and string reading) cease when the render encounters a boundary. This therefore means that any character, including newline characters, will not be read or acted on if they occur outside the boundaries of the 640x480 drawing area).
<b>Note</b>: The &lt;size&gt; value is a multiplicative scale factor. The font will be rendered that many times its natural pixel size. Setting a value of 0 for this will effectively prevent the text render function from running so the text will not be printed.
 

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_file</span>
preview_file &lt;file_path&gt;
Sets the file path and file name which will be used to save preview images with the command preview_save (which see).
&lt;file_path&gt; - a string varval. This must evaluate the name of the file (optionally with full path) to be saved. No white space is allowed. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: You must call this command at least once for any camera from which you wish to save a preview image or the command preview_save will have no effect when you are connected to at least one camera server.
<b>Note</b>: This command may be called when you are not connected to any camera servers. In that case it will set the file name (+/- full path) for saving the current preview image to your disc. The default name for such an image is "OfflinePreview.ppm" so that is what any saved preview image will be called if you do not call this command to change it at least once after start of the pardclient program. The offline name reverts to "OfflinePreview.ppm" at the start of every script run.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_load</span>
preview_load [Display?]
If you are connected to at least one camera server then this reads a p6 ppm image from disc and loads it into the preview display buffer replacing whatever was in it. It will only accept an image of a resolution that matches the preview resolution of the currently selected camera. If the current camera is currently set for full VGA previews then only a full VGA image will be allowed. If the current camera is set to QGVA  then either QVGA or QQVGA images will be allowed and if the current camera is set for QQVGA resolution previews then, again, either QVGA or QQVGA images will be allowed. The file name / path used to locate the image on disc will be the one set using the command preview_file (which see). If you are not connected to any camera server then only full VGA images can be read. Issuing this command triggers a refresh of the displayed preview image.
[Display?] - a binary choice varval. If this evaluates to non-zero then the loaded image is immediately displayed. Otherwise it is loaded but not displayed. The default is for this to be treated as true so a loaded image is displayed unless you provide this argument and set it to something that evaluates to 0.
<b>Note</b>: If you want to display the preview at any time after calling this command with the [Display?] option set to false/0 then you can do that with the command preview_show.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_save</span>
preview_save
If you are connected to at least one camera server then this saves the last retrieved preview image to disc as a p6 ppm image. This uses the file name and path set using the command preview_file (which see). This saves either the full 640x480 VGA PreviewImg buffer or it saves the PreviewQ buffer that stores the QGVA and QQVGA previews. Which one is saves depends on the preview resolution setting of the camera that is currently selected  at the time of issuing this command. If you are not connected to any camera server then this saves the full VGA preview image to disc. This image may contain the last displayed image from any task (like viewing a scan from disc or an image retrieved from a camera that you might have previously been connected to) and/or any drawings you may have made onto the preview using the preview_draw_### commands.
<b>Note</b>: This command will have no effect if the preview save path has not been set for the current camera using the preview_file command.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_show</span>
preview_show
This causes the current preview image buffer (together with any preview graphics overlay buffer waiting to be drawn) to be display in the GUI preview display area. It also updates any other pending GUI events.
<b>Note</b>: The command preview_draw_show performs the same actions but only if the preview overlay buffer is active. The preview_show command will do them regardless.
<b>Note</b>: In CLI mode, the updated preview image will be saved to disc if you have set the CLI save root and path string with the command cli_path.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">preview_tile</span>
preview_tile &lt;number&gt;
This determines (more precisely, 'suggests') whereabouts in the 640x480 main preview area to begin drawing a preview image whenever the get_preview command succeeds. The &lt;number&gt; you supply here will be valid for previews obtained from the currently selected camera. Thus each camera can have its own preview_tile number.
&lt;number&gt; - An integer from 0 to 15 inclusive which determines where the top left pixel of the preview image is placed within the standard VGA preview area of the client program. The positions 0 - 15 map to jumps of QQVGA dimensions in X and Y with 0 being the top left corner of the main VGA display area, 1 being (row,col) = (0,160), 2 being (0,320), 3 being (0,480), 5 being (120,0), 6 being (120,160) and so on, in raster fashion, till we get to 15 being position (360,480).
<b>Note</b>: This is called a 'suggestion' because the interpreter will not allow preview images to spill-over the boundaries of the VGA main preview area or to overlap each other. So if the preview image retrieved from the server is of size VGA then the preview_tile value will be ignored and the preview image will be displayed starting at (0,0) and filling the whole main preview area. If the preview image from the server is of size QVGA then only preview_tile values of 0, 2, 8 and 10 will be honoured - anything else will be overridden with 0 and 0 will be used. If the preview image coming from the server is of size QQVGA then all possible &lt;number&gt; values will be honoured.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">print_clear</span>
print_clear
This clears the text buffer in the script console, including anything out of sight (i.e. ant text that would have needed scrolling to be seen). 


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">print_colour</span>
print_colour &lt;R/P&gt; [&lt;G&gt; &lt;B&gt;]
This sets the foreground colour of any subsequent print_value prints to the script console. This is a latching command - the colour you set here remains active until either you change it with another print_colour command or the script terminates. At the start of each script run, the default foreground colour for print_value output will always be white.
&lt;R/P&gt; - an integer varval &gt;= 0 and &lt;= 255 and is the red component of a 24 bit colour or a 256 colour palette entry.
&lt;G&gt; - an integer varval &gt;= 0 and &lt;= 255 and is the green component of a 24 bit colour
&lt;B&gt; - an integer varval &gt;= 0 and &lt;= 255 and is the blue component of a 24 bit colour
<b>N</b><b>ote:</b> 8-bit Palette Mode (1 argument): This selects a colour from a fixed RGB332 mapping:
Red: Bits 7-5 (8 levels)
Green: Bits 4-2 (8 levels)
Blue: Bits 1-0 (4 levels)
<b>Note</b>: In 8-bit palette mode a &lt;R/P&gt; value of 0 is black and 255 is white.
<b>Note</b>: 24-bit True Colour Mode (3 arguments): The three input values are the red, green and blue components of a 24 bit colour. This allows for precise control to select over 16.7 million colours.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">print_cursor</span>
print_cursor &lt;X&gt; &lt;Y&gt;
This sets the position of the cursor in the script console such that the next print_value command will commence an overwrite print from that position.
&lt;X&gt; - an integer varval &gt;= 0 which marks the number of character positions from the left
&lt;Y&gt; - an integer varval &gt;= 0 which marks the number of lines up from he bottom
<b>Note</b>: Take care to avoid new lines in your print_value command formatting when using this command (unless the effect of a newline is a deliberate design choice on your part).
<b>Note</b>: The print_cursor positioning is non-latching - it will default back to 0,0 (bottom left) after any text is printed at the set coordinates.
 

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">print_file</span>
print_file &lt;exclusive?&gt; &lt;file_path&gt;
This allows the print_value output to be diverted to a file. This acts as a behaviour modifier for the print_value command (which see) - it does not actually print anything itself.
&lt;exclusive&gt; - a binary choice varval. If this evaluates to non-zero (ON) it will cause any print to file output to be printed to the chosen file in &lt;file_path&gt; only and will block any printing to the console or log file. If this evaluates to zero (OFF) then the print_value output will be printed to both the log file +/ script console as well as to the chosen file.
&lt;file_path&gt; - a string varval that evaluates to the file name (or full path including file name) of the file to be written to (without white space), or the word NULL (all in capitals). If this is not NULL then the interpreter will assume it is a file name and will attempt to print the print_value output to that file. If this is NULL then no attempt will be made to print the print_value output to a file.
<b>Note</b>: Any printing to a file will be done with the 'a' (append) option. This means that if the file does not already exist a new file will be created otherwise any existing file will have the new output appended to it.
<b>Note</b>: The printed string will not automatically have a newline character added to the end of it. It will be the user's responsibility to add any required formatting as per the print_value formatting options.
<b>Note</b>: This command acts as a latching behaviour switch for the print_value command. All print_value commands that follow a print_file command will show the behaviour dictated by that print_file command until another print_file command is issued, at which point the behaviour of all subsequent print_value commands will follow the new behaviour. Thus you can switch on and off printing to a file by issuing a print_file command with a valid file name at the start (to turn on file printing) and, when you have finished printing to file, issue another print_file command with NULL as your file path to switch off printing to file.
<b>Note</b>: Each time you call print_file, any currently open file for printing (from a previous call to print_file) will be closed first before the new file is opened (even if the 'new' file is the same as the previous one). Only one file can be open for printing to at a time.
<b>Note</b>: If the &lt;file_path&gt; is not NULL but is also not openable for writing in append mode, then an information notice will be printed to the script console but the program will continue and no file printing will be done until you call print_file again with a valid, usable &lt;file_path&gt;.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">print_graphics</span>
print_graphics &lt;varval&gt;
This sets whether printing of text and graphics characters will be done in graphics mode or not. Graphics mode means that a special square font will be used for all text and UTF8 graphics characters and that the space between lines will be zero. The square font ensures all characters have an aspect ratio of 1:1 and the lack of space between lines means that all drawing or plots using those characters will also have an aspect ratio of 1:1. This avoids the vertical elongation of characters and plots made in 'normal' mode (with the standard monospace font and line spacing). However, in normal mode, multiline text will be easier to read.
&lt;varval&gt; - a binary choice varval. If the value of &lt;varval&gt; evaluates to a non-zero numerical value or to TRUE or ON then all printing from this point on will be done in graphics mode. Otherwise printing from this point on will be done in normal mode.
<b>Note</b>: The default mode when the the pardclient starts up is to have graphics mode off. Likewise graphics mode will automatically switch off at the end of a script (whether the script ends normally or is prematurely terminated by clicking the interrupt button on the GUI).
<b>Note</b>: When using PCS in CLI mode, turning ON graphics mode with this command will disable the cursor character and disable echoing of text input to the terminal. It has no effect on font in CLI mode. Turning OFF graphics mode will restore the terminal cursor and echoing. 


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">print_size</span>
print_size &lt;point_size&gt;
This sets the font size of any subsequent print_value prints to the script console. This is a latching command - the colour you set here remains active until either you change it with another print_size command or the script terminates. At the start of each script run, the default font size for print_value output will always be 12.
&lt;point_size&gt; - an integer varval &gt;= 1 and &lt;= 72.
<b>Note</b>: This feature has no effect when using PCS in CLI mode.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">print_value</span>
print_value &lt;varval&gt; [format_number]
This causes the current value of &lt;varval&gt; to be printed to the script console and/or to a file. Where it prints to is dictated by the arguments to the command print_file, which see. The default behaviour is to print to the script console only.
&lt;varval&gt; - a varval of any type.
[format_number] - a varval which must evaluate to an integer from 0 to 7 (inclusive) and determines the print format. For details see the notes below.
<b>Note</b>: The [format_number] conveys the on/off status of following three binary flags: debug, newline  and  overwrite.  These flags have the following meaning:
<i>debug</i>:  When on this causes not only the value but also other information about the varval (such as it's name and data type) to be printed.
<i>newline</i>: When on this ensures that the printed string ends in a newline character.
<i>overwrite</i>: This first deletes the last line on the script console and then prints the current message where that line used to be. For this to function as an overwrite printer you must also have the newline flag set to on otherwise the print start position for the current text to be printed will jump up one line each time till it eventually erases all the text that came before it.
These are private flags in the interpreter, they are not variables that the script writer can access by name but they control their on/off status by the value of the single integer they supply for the [format_number] argument. This value, when converted to a 3-bit binary number, sets the on/off (i.e. 1/0) states of the three flags in the order [debug][newline][overwrite] like this:
    [format_number] = 0 i.e. binary 000 = all 3 flags are off
    [format_number] = 1 i.e. binary 001 = overwrite on and the other two off
    [format_number] = 2 i.e. binary 010 = newline on and the other two off
    [format_number] = 3 i.e. binary 011 = newline and overwrite on (only)
    [format_number] = 4 i.e. binary 100 = debug on and the other two are off
    [format_number] = 5 i.e. binary 101 = debug and overwrite are on (only)
    [format_number] = 6 i.e. binary 110 = debug and newline on (only)
    [format_number] = 7 i.e. binary 111 = all three flags on
<b>Note</b>: If a print_value command is issued without the optional [format_number] then the behaviour defaults to the last [format_number] issued in a previous print_value command. If there was no previous use of the [format_number] option then the default behaviour is equivalent to [format_number] = 6. 
<b>Note</b>: The [format_number] value will reset itself to 6 before and after every script run.
<b>Note</b>: As noted above, for a single line to overprint in situ you need to have both  the overwrite and newline flags on because the overwrite flag tells the console printer to delete all text from the end of the current text buffer up to the first newline character it encounters before printing the current text. If the current text has no newline in it then the delete operation will always go up a line till it finds the newline character of the line above. This 'delete the line above' feature may be useful in cases where you print several lines - e.g. three lines, one for a red value, one for a green value and one for a blue value (like variance for example) and then you want to delete all those three lines before printing the next set of three lines. In that case just print an empty string with no newline and with the overwrite flag enabled 3 times before starting to print your new set of three lines.
<b>Note</b>: The [format_number] value will remain at whatever you last set it to, even when using the print_value command after that without using the optional [format_number] argument.
<b>Note</b>: When &lt;varval&gt; is a string variable, the literal contents of the string stored in the variable is printed even if that string can be cast to a numerical value (so "1.200" will be printed as 1.200 and not 1.2). However if &lt;varval&gt; is a literal constant that can be expressed as a numerical value then it will be converted to an actual numerical value and not treated as a string (so 1.200 will be printed as 1.2). This is in keeping with the general rule that when a programmer hard codes a number into a program they expect it to be treated as a number not a string. This only needs clarify in PCS because PCS does not use delimiters to define strings so writing `print_value 1.200` requires some rule to decide whether the argument is to be interpreted as a string or a number - it is interpreted as number.
  

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_cdf_use</span>
prv_cdf_use &lt;ON|OFF|image_file&gt; 
Whether or not to apply subtraction of a custom preview colour dark field image. This is not the same as the dark field image used for the full resolution image dark field subtraction. 
&lt;ON|OFF|image_file&gt; - a string varval. Supply either the (case sensitive) strings ON or OFF or the file name (optionally to include the full path) of an image to be used as the colour dark field image. File names and paths must not contain spaces. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: Acceptable formats for the image file are: PPM (P6) and BMP (24 bpp). No other options are allowed. For a file name to be acceptable it must have a .ppm or .bmp extension (case is not important here).
<b>Note</b>: The ON/OFF actions will only work if you have loaded a custom colour dark field image before setting this to ON or OFF. Submitting a file name will cause pardcap to assess the file and set 'pending' flag to 1 if it is successful. The ON/OFF status will be whatever it happens to be at the pardcap end and will not change just by virtue of submitting an image file. So you need to call this function twice the very first time you use it in a script.  The first call is to submit your file. Then, if the file was accepted OK, call it again with either ON or OFF.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_fc_posn</span>
prv_fc_posn &lt;y&gt;
This sets the row (&lt;y&gt;) pixel coordinate of the top left corner of the focus assist bar(s) as displayed in the preview image.
&lt;y&gt; - an integer in the range 20 to 428 inclusive.
<b>Note</b>: The range above will be the same regardless of what you have set the preview size to be with the command prv_size. This is because the position coordinate you supply here will automatically be scaled down at the pardcap end if the preview image is set to be smaller than full VGA.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_fc_show</span>
prv_fc_show &lt;show_mode&gt;
Whether to overlay the focus assist bar(s) on the preview and, if so, which bars to show.
&lt;show_mode_int&gt; - an integer determining what to show. The options are:
0 = invisible
1 = all three RGB channels (only applies if previewing in colour)
2 = red channel or mono Y
3 = green channel (only applies if previewing in colour)
4 = blue channel  (only applies if previewing in colour)


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_hgm_posn</span>
prv_hgm_posn &lt;x&gt; &lt;y&gt;
The column (&lt;x&gt;) and row (&lt;y&gt;) pixel coordinates of the top left corner of the histogram displayed in the preview.
&lt;x&gt; - an integer in the range 2 to 382 inclusive. 
&lt;y&gt; - an integer in the range 2 to 222 inclusive.
<b>Note</b>: The ranges above will be the same regardless of what you have set the preview size to be with the command prv_size. This is because the position coordinates you supply here will automatically be scaled down at the pardcap end if the preview image is set to be smaller than full VGA.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_hgm_satlim_l</span>
prv_hgm_satlim_l &lt;R/Y&gt; &lt;G&gt; &lt;B&gt;
Sets the preview image lower histogram saturation limits for the red (or mono), green and blue channels.
&lt;R/Y&gt; - an integer from 0 to 254 inclusive
&lt;G&gt; - an integer from 0 to 254 inclusive
&lt;B&gt; - is an integer from 0 to 254 inclusive
<b>Note</b>: Any pixels with a value less than the lower saturation limit contributes 1 to the total count of lower saturated pixels in each colour channel.
<b>Note</b>: If, when setting these values, the corresponding upper saturation limit is found to be equal to or lower than the value been set, then that upper saturation limit will be set to the lower limit + 1. No error message will be issued in such cases so it is up to the user to ensure the upper saturation limits are set according to the rule that the upper saturation limit values must be greater than the lower saturation limit values if they want their chosen upper saturation limit values to be set and not overruled.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_hgm_satlim_u</span>
prv_hgm_satlim_u &lt;R/Y&gt; &lt;G&gt; &lt;B&gt;
Set the preview image upper histogram saturation limits for the red (or mono), green and
blue channels.
&lt;R/Y&gt; - an integer from 1 to 255 inclusive
&lt;G&gt; - an integer from 1 to 255 inclusive
&lt;B&gt; - is an integer from 1 to 255 inclusive
<b>Note</b>: Any pixels with a value greater than the upper saturation limit contributes 1 to the total count of upper saturated pixels in each colour channel.
<b>Note</b>: If, when setting these values, the corresponding lower saturation limit is found to be equal to or higher than the value been set, then that upper saturation limit requested here will be overridden and set to the lower saturation limit + 1. If this override occurs in any 1 or more of the colour channels then an error message will be issued back to the client of the type 'Failed to apply a setting due to out of range'. To avoid this error the user should set saturation limits for both upper and lower in adjacent calls to prv_hgm_satlim_l (first - because this will never generate an error as long as the values supplied are in the specified range) followed by a call to prv_hgm_satlim_u ensuring that the values you supply are indeed greater than the values you supplied to the prior call to prv_hgm_satlim_l. 


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_hgm_scales</span>
prv_hgm_scales &lt;R/Y&gt; &lt;G&gt; &lt;B&gt;
Sets the preview image histogram manual scaling factors to be used (only) when hgm_nofit is set to ON using the prv_toggle command (which see).
&lt;R/Y&gt; - a float &gt;= 0.0
&lt;G&gt; - is a float &gt;= 0.0
&lt;B&gt; - is a float &gt;= 0.0


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_lut</span>
prv_lut &lt;LUT_number&gt;
Sets the preview image look-up-table (LUT) for display.
&lt;LUT_number&gt; is an integer between 0 to 5 inclusive and defines the LUT according to:
0 = Linear
1 = Inverted
2 = Logarithmic (base 10)
3 = Exponential (base 10)
4 = Square root
5 = Square


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_mask_use</span>
prv_mask_use &lt;ON|OFF|image_file&gt; 
Whether or not to apply a custom preview mask to the calculation of preview stats. This is not the same as whether or not to display that mask over the preview image - for that see the command prv_toggle.
&lt;ON|OFF|image_file&gt; - a string varval. Supply either the (case sensitive) strings ON or OFF or the file name (optionally to include the full path) of an image to be used as the mask image. File names and paths must not contain spaces. This argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: Acceptable formats for the image file are: PGM (p5), PPM (P6) and BMP (8 bpp or 24 bpp). No other options are allowed. If you supply a colour format image then the 'intensity' component of an HSI transform is calculated for each pixel as (R+G+B)/3 and this is thresholded to be an 'on' pixel if it is &gt;127 and and 'off' pixel otherwise. The same threshold is used to binarise grey scale images. For a file name to be acceptable it must have a .pgm, .ppm or .bmp extension (case is not important here).
<b>Note</b>: The ON/OFF actions will only work if you have loaded a mask image before setting this to ON or OFF. Submitting a file name will cause pardcap to assess the file and set 'pending' flag to 1 if it is successful. The ON/OFF status will be whatever it happens to be at the pardcap end and will not change just by virtue of submitting a file. So you need to call this function twice the very first time you use it in a script. The first call is to submit your file. Then, if the file was accepted OK, call it again with either ON or OFF.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_m_bias</span>
prv_m_bias &lt;number_of_grey_levels&gt;
This sets the number of grey levels to add (or subtract) from the preview image after all frames have been integrated (see prv_m_integral). This only applies when previewing in monochrome mode.
&lt;number_of_grey_levels&gt; - an integer between -(PREVINTMAX-1)*255 and 512.
<b>Note</b>: At the time of writing, PREVINTMAX = 20 which makes the lower limit for &lt;number_of_grey_levels&gt; = -4845.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_mcor_eject</span>
prv_mcor_eject
This ejects any loaded custom preview mono dark and flat field correction images.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_mdf_set</span>
prv_mdf_set &lt;image_file&gt; 
This loads the supplied dark field corrections image and sets it as active for use with monochrome previewing.
&lt;image_file&gt; -  a string varval which evaluates to the name (optionally to include the full path) of the image to use. This must not contain white space. This string argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: The image must be of raw doubles mono format with its name ending in _y.dou (case is not important here) and be of the same dimensions as the preview image.
<b>Note</b>: The only way to deactivate its use is to perform a prv_mcor_eject command, which see.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_mff_set</span>
prv_mff_set &lt;image_file&gt; 
This loads the supplied flat field corrections file and sets it as active for use with monochrome previewing.
&lt;image_file&gt; -  a string varval which evaluates to the name (optionally to include the full path) of the image to use. This must not contain white space. This string argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: The image must be of raw doubles mono format with its name ending in _y.dou (case is not important here) and be of the same dimensions as the preview image.
<b>Note</b>: The only way to deactivate its use is to perform a prv_mcor_eject command, which see.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_m_integral</span>
prv_m_integral &lt;number_of_frames&gt;
This sets the number of frames to integrate when previewing in monochrome mode.
&lt;number_of_frames&gt; - an integer between 1 and PREVINTMAX.
<b>Note</b>: At the time of writing, PREVINTMAX = 20


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_size</span>
prv_size &lt;size_number&gt;
This sets the size of the preview image as displayed in the VGA-sized preview area of pardclient.
&lt;size_number&gt; - an integer between 0 and 2 and determines the size of the preview image as follows:
0 = 640x480 (VGA - the whole size of the preview area)
1 = 320x240 (QVGA)
2 = 160x120  (QQVGA)
<b>Note</b>: For preview sizes that are smaller than full VGA, the position of where that preview image is displayed within the full VGA-sized display area of pardclient can be set using the command preview_tile.
<b>Note</b>: The size of the preview set here does not affect the coordinate system used with the preview overlay positioning commands prv_fc_posn and prv_hgm_posn. Those commands will always use coordinates based on the full VGA size. The coordinates will automatically be scaled down at the pardcap end if the preview image is set to be smaller than full VGA.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">prv_toggle</span>
prv_toggle &lt;option&gt; &lt;ON|OFF&gt;
This sets one of the preview-related options that only accept a binary choice varval argument (and nothing else such as a file name). These options correspond to individual check boxes in the pardcap GUI.
&lt;option&gt; - a string which must be one of the following:
  mono
  invert
  flip_h
  flip_v
  laplacian
  premedian
  postmedian
  dctpostlut
  fc_dct
  fc_mal
  fc_vmul
  mask_show
  hgm_show
  hgm_cum_r
  hgm_cum_g
  hgm_cum_b
  hgm_nofit
  hgm_restrictvar
 Their meanings are as follows:
  mono  Previewing in monochrome mode
  invert  Invert the preview colours
  flip_h  Flip the preview horizontally
  flip_v  Flip the preview vertically
  laplacian  Display an absolute Laplacian preview.
  premedian  Do a 3x3 median filter before Laplacian calc.s
  postmedian  Do a 3x3 median filter after Laplacian calc.s
  dctpostlut  If using DCT, do the calculation after applying the LUT
  fc_dct  Use medium frequency DCT as a focus assist measure
  fc_mal  Use mean absolute Laplacian as a focus assist measure
  fc_vmul  Multiply the focus measure by the variance.
  mask_show  Show the preview mask
  hgm_show  Overlay the histogram on the preview image
  hgm_cum_r  Use a cumulative histogram for the red (or mono) channel
  hgm_cum_g  Use a cumulative histogram for the green channel
  hgm_cum_b  Use a cumulative histogram for the blue channel
  hgm_nofit  Prevents automatic scaling of each colour channel data in the histogram so you can get a sense of the absolute relationships between the colour channel data.
  hgm_restrictvar If this is ON the mean and variance calculations will only use pixels whose values are greater than the lower saturation limit and less than the upper saturation limit.
&lt;ON|OFF&gt; - a binary choice varval
<b>Notes</b>:
1. The options laplacian, premedian and postmedian only have any effect when previewing in monochrome mode.
2. The 'fc_...' (focusser statistic) options are multiplicative cumulative in effect. If none of these are ON then the variance alone will be used as the default focus statistic. If fc_dct alone is ON then the mean mid-frequency discrete cosine transform power (DCT statistic) will replace variance as the focus statistic. If fc_mal alone is ON then the mean absolute Laplacian (MAL statistic) will replace variance as the focus statistic. If <i>both</i> fc_dct <i>and</i> fc_mal are ON then the focus statistic will be the product DCTxMAL. If fc_vmul is ON then the focus statistic will be whatever it would otherwise have been multiplied by the variance (but fc_vmul status has no effect if both  fc_dct and fc_mal are OFF - so it won't result in variance x variance being used). The 'focus statistic' is the value stored in the system variable Ps_Focus_R, Ps_Focus_G and Ps_Focus_B whenever these are retrieved from the server with the command get_preview_stats.
3. The fc_dct, when applied, will calculate the DCT statistic on the preview image (optionally under any preview mask if this is used). This calculation may be done before or after the display LUT transforms are applied (according to your current setting for dctpostlut). When in monochrome mode if the preview has been transformed into a Laplacian image then the DCT will be calculated on that Laplacian image ONLY if dctpostlut is ON and, in that case the DCT will be calculated also after any pre- and/or post-Laplacian median filter is applied. If dctpostlut is OFF then the DCT will be calculated before the Laplacian image is formed but after any pre-Laplacian median filtering is performed. For colour previews the median filter options are never implemented at any stage and the Laplacian image is never formed (only a Laplacian statistic is calculated when requested - by toggling fc_mal to ON - but the preview image itself is never transformed into a Laplacian image) so the only options for DCT in colour preview mode is whether to calculate it pre or post application of the LUT transforms. In all cases, monochrome or colour, the other stats for the preview image (including histograms and variance) are calculated before the application of the LUTs. In monochrome mode (only) these stats are calculated after any median filters are applied and after the image is transformed into a Laplacian image (if that is selected by the user via the Laplacian option).
<b><i>Scientific Warning</i></b>: The pixel values in a the mean absolute Laplacian image may have a  significantly different distribution to the original pixel values of the image from which they were calculated. Thus, the llim and ulim set for the original image will likely be "wrong" for a Laplacian image. For this reason be sure you set appropriate limits when using that option with a Laplacian-transformed preview.
4. mask_show will have no effect unless you previously loaded a preview mask image with the prv_mask_use command, which see.
5. For best visual effect, the hgm_nofit option should be used with appropriate manual scale factors. See the full pardcap manual for details. See also prv_hgm_scales.
6. There are many other preview options not covered by this prv_toggle command because those options require something more or other than a binary ON/OFF argument. For example the prv_fc_show command requires an integer argument from 0 to 4 and not just ON/OFF. That is why prv_fc_show is a separate command and we don't have an 'fc_show' option for prv_toggle.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">randpcg</span>
randpcg &lt;init?&gt; &lt;receptacle&gt; [pos?]
This provides a pseudorandom number (PRN) generator for floats and integers.
&lt;init?&gt; - a binary choice varval. If this evaluates to TRUE or non-zero then the PRN sequence is re-initialised prior to providing the pseudorandom number, otherwise it is not.
&lt;receptacle&gt; - a user variable of type int or float (constants, string variables and system variables are not allowed) into which the PRN result will be deposited.
&lt;pos?&gt; - an optional binary choice varval. If this evaluates to TRUE or non-zero then the value deposited in an integer receptacle will always be 0 or positive. Otherwise (or if this argument is not supplied) then the integer value may be positive or negative. This argument has no effect on floating point pseudorandom numbers.
<b>Note: </b>Whether the PRN returned is a float or an int is determined by the data type the supplied &lt;receptacle&gt; variable. 
<b>Note</b>: For an integer the returned PRN will be in the range INT32_MIN to INT32_MAX (-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647) inclusive if [pos?] is 0 or absent. If [pos?] is present and evaluates to TRUE (or non-0) then the range will be 0 to INT32_MAX. This positive range restriction is performed by simply discarding the sign bit in the (otherwise signed) PRN integer and not by use of the integer abs() function.
<b>Note</b>: For a float the returned PRN will be in the range [0.0,1.0). Note that the upper bound is open - it will never return exactly 1.0. A float PRN is made by direct synthesis of an  IEEE 754 double precision floating point number from a string of 52 pseudorandom value bits and not by the using float maths function (except for a final subtraction) in order to preserve cross-platform independence of the result.  
<b>Note</b>: The PRN generator used in PCS is a 32 bit O'Neill 'Permuted congruential generator' (PCG) algorithm using a hard-coded 64-bit initial state thus:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// Global, internal state of the interpreter's RNG</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>static uint64_t pcg_state = 0x853c49e6748fea9bULL;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>static uint64_t const pcg_inc = 0xda3e39cb94b95bdbULL; // Always fixed and odd</tt> </span>
The PRN sequence is initialised with this state at the start of every script run. It is re-initialised to the same pcg_state whenever you supply a TRUE or non-0 value for the &lt;init?&gt; argument. This ensures that you can generate identical PRN sequences across different runs of the same script and also that a PRN sequence generated by a script running on one OS will be identical to the PRN obtained while running the same script on a different OS.
<b>Note</b>: Use of this algorithm has the advantage of supplying PRN sequences that are platform independent, with a long 64-bit repeat period for every bit in the sequence for any given PRN and that lack Marsaglioid hyperplane periodicity patterns.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">return</span>
return
This closes a function definition code block - that is, a block which is opened with the command def_func (which see). You must have strictly one - and only one - return paired to each def_func.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">save_coords</span>
save_coords &lt;ON|OFF&gt;
This allows you to determine whether each captured image is accompanied by a text file containing the coordinates of all 3 stage axes the time that image was taken. This is a latch setting.
&lt;ON|OFF&gt; - a binary choice varval.
<b>Note</b>: The coordinate information will be saved in a plain text file with the same name as the image but with the image file name extension replaced with '_coords.txt'.
<b>Note</b>: The coordinates will always be in atomic step units. It is up to you to make separate arrangements to record any scale factors if you need to convert these to calibrated units at any time.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">save_doubles</span>
save_doubles &lt;OFF|ON|FITS&gt; 
This sets the option of whether to save a main image in a 64 bpp doubles format. 
&lt;OFF|ON|FITS&gt; - a case-sensitive string that means the following:
OFF  = don't use the 'save as doubles' pardcap option (this is the default at first startup of a pardcap server)
ON   = save as raw doubles (.dou and .qih file pair). One such pair for a mono image or 3 such pairs for RGB images, one or each of the RGB channels).
FITS = As for ON but instead of the raw format (.dou and .qih pairs) it saves the doubles pixel values in one (mono) or 3 (RGB) FITS files.
<b>Note</b>: This command sets the save format modifier options 'save as raw doubles' or 'save as FITS' and so helps determine the save format of the image data coming back from a pardcap server according to the rules described in the section on save_format command (which, see).
<b>Note</b>: The .qih file is a plain text external header file. The .dou file contains the actual pixel data.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">save_format</span>
save_format &lt;format&gt;
This allows you to set the file format of an image saved to disk by the grab_image command but the format you select here is not the only determining factor of the type of image will ultimately get saved. The actual format and extension of the saved image will also depend on the option you selected with the save_doubles command and also whether you have opted to do multiframe averaging with the frames_to_average command. The rules of how these options combine to determine the actual file format of the saved image will be detailed below. The actual file format dictated by those rules will determine the file name ending that will be appended to the root file name you supply via the command save_root) and the frame number you supply with the command frame_number to form the full file name for the saved image. 
&lt;format&gt; - must be a string constant (not a variable) with one of the following (case sensitive): yuv, pgm, bm8, int, ppm, bmp, png, jpg
<b>Note</b>: The meanings of the format options are as follows:
yuv - raw binary array of unsigned shorts (16bpp) in YUYV format.
pgm - 8bpp p5 greyscale image in pgm format
bm8 - 8bpp greyscale bmp image
int - the intensity component of an HSI transform of a 24 bpp colour image (saved as raw doubles)
ppm - 24bpp colour p6 ppm image
bmp - 24bpp colour bmp image
png - 24bpp colour png image
jpg - 24bpp colour jpeg image
<b>Note</b>: See also save_doubles and jpeg_quality
<b>Note</b>: The pardcap server will take your above choice and, depending on the following factors, the server will send back image data in one of several formats to be written to disc. The additional factors are: Whether pardcap is using the MJPEG stream of the camera or the raw YUYV stream (at the time of writing, only raw YUYV image streams are used when pardcap is in server mode), your choice of whether to do multi-frame averaging or not via the frames_to_average command and your choice of whether to 'save as doubles' or 'save as FITS' as set by the save_doubles command. The format of the image data sent back to the pardclient in the blob from pardcap is the 'write as' format and that may be the same as the format option you supplied here (the 'save as' format) or might differ. It is the 'write as' format that actually determines what type of image is saved to disc after a successful grab_image command. The rules on how the 'write as' format is determined as described below.
If the 'save as' format going out to the server was int then the 'write as' format coming back will be dou (raw doubles for single channel images) and the image will be saved with the _i.dou file name ending. All other cases of 'write as' format dou will be saved with the _y.dou file name ending. Note that dou is used for monochrome images as opposed to do3 which is the format specifier for 3-channel (colour) images saved as raw doubles or FITS. Note also that the dou and do3 formats are not available as &lt;format&gt; options to send to the pardcap server via this save_format command. Any attempt to use them in this way will result in an error.
If the 'save as' format going out to the server was yuv then the 'write as' format coming back from the server will be yuv because multiframe averaging is not permitted for YUYV format captures and the 'save as raw doubles' and 'save as FITS' options are ignored for raw YUYV format image captures and no corrections are applied to the image (masking, flat field / dark field). If the camera was streaming in V4L2_PIX_FMT_MJPEG format we would not have got this far because a request to do image capture in raw YUYV format would have been be rejected at the pardcap end as a forbidden task and an error would have resulted. Files saved in YUYV format will have the name ending '_yuyv.raw' appended to the root.
If the 'save as' format going out to the server was jpg then the 'write as' format coming back could be do3 or rgb (used by the server to denote it is sending over a 3-channel colour image) or jpg depending on certain options as follows:
do3 with 3 raw doubles images in the blob sent back by pardcap: This occurs if the 'save as raw doubles' or 'save as FITS' flags were set (using the PCS command save_doubles (which, see)) AND (if averaging was done or the camera was <b><i>not</i></b> streaming in V4L2_PIX_FMT_MJPEG format).
rgb with an RGB image in the blob sent back by pardcap: This occurs if averaging was done or the camera was not streaming in V4L2_PIX_FMT_MJPEG AND neither the 'save as raw doubles' nor 'save as FITS' options were selected. In these cases the RGB image in the blob will be saved as a jpeg using the interpreter's function raw_to_jpeg with a name having a .jpg extension.
jpg with a jpeg image in the blob: This occurs regardless of the value of the 'save as raw doubles' and 'save as FITS' options. It occurs if averaging was <b><i>not</i></b> done AND the camera <b><i>was</i></b> streaming in V4L2_PIX_FMT_MJPEG format. In these cases the jpeg data in the blob can be directly written to file with a .jpg extension.
Apart from the rules for the case where 'save as' format was int (see above), all cases where 'write as' format is dou (regardless of what the outgoing 'save as' format was) will be saved with a _y.dou or _y.fit as a FITS file if the 'save as FITS' option is selected (the 'save as FITS' value trumps the 'save as raw doubles' value if both are selected)
All cases where the 'write as' format is do3, the file will be saved in 3 files with their names ending in _r.dou, _g.dou and _b.dou or as 3 FITS files with similar endings but having the .fit extension if the  'save as FITS' option is selected (the 'save as FITS' value trumps the 'save as raw doubles' value if both are selected).
The above rules in prose may seem a bit hard to decipher so here is a distilled table summary:


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Requested      </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Image Type</span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Actual File Extension(s)                          </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Notes / Dependencies                    </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Format         </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">          </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">                                                  </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">                                        </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">(&lt;format&gt;)     </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">          </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">                                                  </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">                                        </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ yuv             │ Raw 16bpp  │ _yuyv.raw                                          │ Direct capture; ignores                  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ Binary     │                                                    │ averaging/save_doubles/image             │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ corrections.                             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ int             │ HSI        │ _i.dou or _i.fit                                   │ Depending on  save_doubles               │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ Intensity  │                                                    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ pgm / bm8       │ 8bpp       │ _y.pgm / _y.bmp (or _y.dou or _y.fit)              │ Returns dou or .fit if save_doubles opti │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ Greyscale  │                                                    │ ons are set.                             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ ppm / bmp / png │ 24bpp      │ _rgb.ppm / _rgb.bmp / _rgb.png (or _r/g/b.dou or   │ Returns do3 or .fit if save_doubles opti │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ Colour     │ _r/g/b.fit)                                        │ ons are set.                             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ jpg             │ 24bpp JPEG │ .jpg (or _r/g/b.dou or _r/g/b.fit)                 │ Direct save if MJPEG; re-encoded if      │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ RGB/Averaged and then save type depends  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ on  save_doubles.                        │ </tt>





<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">save_path</span>
save_path &lt;image_full_save_path&gt;
This sets the absolute path name (the directory) for saving images and any files associated with them.
&lt;image_full_save_path&gt; - a string varval specifying a directory path. It must not contain white space and must not include the file name - only the directory path. This string argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: Any argument longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: The &lt;image_full_save_path&gt; must contain at least one directory separator (/ or \ depending on your OS) to be valid. The interpreter will automatically use the last separator found in your path to join the directory to the file name. You do not need to end the path with a slash; if you do, the resulting file path will contain a double slash (e.g., path//root_0001.bmp).". While this double slash may be handled correctly by your OS, it is cleaner and better practice to avoid the use of a terminal slash in case some OS or applications have difficulties with double slashes in path names.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">save_resolution</span>
save_resolution &lt;width&gt; &lt;height&gt;
This allows you to specify the dimensions of any images saved to disk using the grab_image or xyzs_scan commands (which see).
&lt;width&gt; - the image width in pixels supplied as a varval of type int  &gt;= 1. 
&lt;height&gt; - the image height in pixels supplied as a varval of type int &gt;= 1.
<b>Note</b>: The &lt;width&gt; &lt;height&gt; combination must be supported by the currently attached camera in order for this command to be valid.
<b>Note</b>: Changing the resolution will invalidate any correction images that may have previously been loaded (such as a corrections mask, a flat field image or a dark field image).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">save_root</span>
save_root &lt;image_name_root&gt;
This allows you to input the string used to construct the common part of file names generated when you grab an image with grab_image.
&lt;image_name_root&gt; is a string varval specifying a string without white space which will be used to construct the file name of the saved image and any associated file (like an external header file for saving in raw doubles format). This string argument may be up to SCNFNAME_MAX bytes long (usually 4095 for POSIX or 259 bytes for older MS Windows systems).
<b>Note</b>: If the argument is longer than SCNFNAME_MAX bytes it will be truncated to that limit when read by the interpreter so longer file or path names will likely result in an error when the interpreter attempts to read them.
<b>Note</b>: This may include a path component if you want to store the image in a particular location but the path must not contain any white space. If you do this, avoid the use of a slash at the start of the &lt;image_name_root&gt; (that will be handled for you automatically by the interpreter).
<b>Note</b>: The full file name of the saved image will be a combination of the &lt;image_name_root&gt; string, an integer number that increments with each image captured (and can be set or reset using the frame_number command, which see) and an extension chosen automatically depending on the format of the image to be saved (see the save_format command for details). Associated files (like external header files) will have the same basic name but with a modified extension appropriate to their type.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">select_camera</span>
select_camera &lt;number&gt;
Selects the currently active pardcap server for all camera commands including preview functions.
&lt;number&gt; - an integer from 1 (the default at startup) upwards to the maximum number of cameras (pardcap servers) connected to the pardserver.
<b>Note</b>: Any camera or preview command issued in the script only applies to the currently selected camera and will not take effect in any other camera unless you select that camera and then issue the command.
<b>Note</b>: The number of cameras you can have is limited only by your computing resources and from a pure code limit point of view it is capped at just over 2 billion (actually 2147483647, the number of positive values a 32 bit int can store). Because cameras are connected via TCP/IP, the cameras don't all need to be running on the same PC. 
<b>Note</b>: When the pardclient first starts a login session the camera list is freed and a call to get_cameras is made which re-populates it. The current camera is then set to 1.
<b>Note</b>: You can see what is the current camera number as well as how many cameras you have available by looking at the 'Camera Settings' tab on the pardclient GUI. This will display a list of the settings for the current camera with a note that this is 'Camera # of #' where the first number is the current camera number and the second is the total number of cameras available.
<b>Note</b>: Calling select_camera from a script does not automatically update the GUI list of available camera settings displayed in the Camera Settings tab but calling select_camera from the GUI 'Manual' tab does. If you want the GUI updated from within a script then make a call to get_camsettings and get_resolutions after you call select_camera.
<b>Note</b>: If you have been doing manual operations on cameras from the GUI Manual tab and then load or run a script in the Script tab, several settings may change for your current selection of parameters for the camera because when a script is checked (this happens automatically upon loading a script file and also just prior to running it) then the interpreter runs its internal function nullify_correction_images_and_masks() and also does a resize_blob(). Thus, if you return to manual GUI ops after any script work, be prepared to set your camera settings all over again.
<b>Note</b>: When it comes to correction images (flat field, dark field and corrections mask), these are all individual to each camera. You will need to set and load each such image for each camera. This is also true for preview correction images.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">set_condexp</span>
set_condexp &lt;varval1&gt; &lt;condition&gt; &lt;varval2&gt;
This causes the script to set the value of the system variable Condexp to 1 or 0 depending on the result of a conditional expression (i.e. a comparison between two values specified by the arguments). Condexp may then be used as a value argument in other commands (including those commands that act on conditional expressions such as do_if, goto, call_func and loop_while).
&lt;varval1&gt;  - a varval of any type. 
&lt;condition&gt; - a varval of type string which evaluates to a symbol defining the comparison to be made between &lt;varval1&gt; and &lt;varval2&gt;. The symbol must be one of these:
 ==, &gt;=, &gt;, &lt;=, &lt;, !=, &amp;&amp;, ||  
and these have the same meanings as the same logical comparator symbols in the C programming language (but bear in mind these operators act on the values resulting from any casting using the PCS casting rules rather than the values as literally typed in the PCS source). 
&lt;varval2&gt;  - a varval of any type. 

<b>Note</b>: The comparison aspect of a set_condexp command is symbolically equivalent to conditional expressions in the C programming language e.g. (varval1 &gt; varval2) or (varval1 &lt;= varval2) or (varval1 == varval2), etc. 
<b>Note</b>: If the right hand value &lt;varval2&gt; is of a different data type to the left hand value &lt;varval1&gt; then the interpreter will attempt to cast the right hand value into the same data type as the left hand value prior to the comparison (this will not affect the intrinsic value of &lt;varval2&gt;, it is just a temporary cast for the purpose of making the comparison).
Note: When &lt;varval1&gt; is a string, the comparison is a string comparison, and &lt;varval2&gt; is cast to a string by the rule given under def_var. So with `def_var string a 5.0`, `set_condexp a == 5.0` is true but `set_condexp a == 5` is false, because “5.0” and “5” are different strings. To compare the numeric values of two strings, put one of them into an int or float variable first and compare that variable, which then decides the type of the comparison.
<b>Note</b>: Only == and != are allowed between arguments of type string.
<b>Note</b>: If you use the Condexp itself as your argument for &lt;varval1&gt; or &lt;varval2&gt; then the current value of Condexp will be used (either 0 or 1 as was set by the last call to set_condexp or 0 if this is the first time set_condexp is being called in a script). Accordingly the command:
set_condexp Condexp == Condexp
will always be true and will result in the value of Condexp being set to value 1. Similarly the command
set_condexp Condexp != Condexp
will always be false and will result in the value of Condexp being set to value 0. In this way the script writer can manipulate the value of Condexp at will.
<b>Note</b>: Because the comparator condition parameter is processed via the language's string variable evaluation engine, the operator symbol does not need to be a hardcoded constant. It may be supplied directly by a dynamic string variable, allowing for advanced runtime meta-programming and dynamic dispatch jump-table logic, for example:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Delimiter-free first-class string logic assignment</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var string logical_gate &amp;&amp;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Script dynamically adapts its tracking criteria mid-session</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>do_if emergency_override_active</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>   </tt><tt>set_str logical_gate ||</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>enddo</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># The interpreter resolves the string contents dynamically at runtime</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp axis1_aligned logical_gate axis2_aligned</tt> </span>

<b>Note</b>: If you wish to combine multiple individual evaluation states into a single logical truth result, it can be handled natively in a single 3-argument expression by evaluating two pre-existing Boolean flags or numeric variables using the &amp;&amp; and || operators, for example:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># Native Inline Compound Checking</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp base_not_there || shld_not_there</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>loop_while Condexp</tt> </span>

<b>Note</b>: Alternatively, combining multiple complex conditional expressions can be effected step-by-step by using explicit intermediate variable allocations and basic arithmetic accumulation, as shown in the following configuration examples:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int exp1 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int exp2 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int exp3 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int i1 5</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int i2 -2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int i3 8</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp i1 &lt; i2   </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var exp1 Condexp </tt><tt> </tt><tt># This stores the result of the first conditional</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                      </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> expression into exp1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp i3 &gt; i2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_var exp2 Condexp </tt><tt> </tt><tt># This stores the result of the second conditional</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                      </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> expression into exp2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>add_var exp3 exp1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>add_var exp3 exp2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># With the above setup here are some possibilities for compound</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#</tt><tt> logical expressions:</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp1 &gt; exp2     # If (  exp1  AND !exp2 )  </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> (note that '!' denotes NOT</tt><tt> in these comments </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt># but you cannot use it like that in PCS</tt><tt>)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp1 &lt; exp2     # If ( !exp1  AND  exp2 ) </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                           </tt><tt> </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp1 == exp2   </tt><tt> </tt><tt># If (( exp1  AND  exp2 ) OR ( !exp1 AND !exp2 ))</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                        </tt><tt>  </tt><tt>  </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0 </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt>#</tt><tt> </tt><tt>i.e. (exp1  XNOR  exp2)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp1 != exp2    # If (  exp1  XOR  exp2 ) </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                           </tt><tt> </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp3 == 1  </tt><tt>     </tt><tt># If (  exp1  XOR  exp2 )  </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp3 &gt; 0   </tt><tt>     </tt><tt># If (  exp1   OR  exp2 )  </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp3 == 2  </tt><tt>     </tt><tt># If (  exp1  AND  exp2 )  </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp3 == 0 </tt><tt>     </tt><tt> # If ( !exp1  AND !exp2 )  </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0 </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt>#</tt><tt>i.e. (exp1 N</tt><tt>OR</tt><tt> exp2)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>set_condexp exp3 &lt; 2      </tt><tt> </tt><tt> # If !(exp1 AND exp2)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt># Condexp will equal 1 otherwise 0 </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>                            </tt><tt># i.e. (exp1 NAND exp2)</tt> </span>
More complex logical expressions can be made using more variables to combine more pairs of set_condexp command results.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">set_previewpos</span>
set_previewpos &lt;pos&gt; &lt;inc&gt;
This causes the script to set the value of the system variables PreviewPos and PreviewInc to the values specified in the arguments. These determine what value is returned whenever you read the system variable PreviewVal.
&lt;pos&gt;  - a varval that evaluates to an integer in the range 0 - (PreviewImg_rgb_size-1). 
&lt;inc&gt; - a varval  that evaluates to an integer in the range 1 to 3.
<b>Note</b>: The value of  PreviewImg_rgb_size is VGA*3 i.e. 3*640*480 = 921600.
<b>Note</b>: These 3 system variables (PreviewPos, PreviewInc and PreviewVal) allow the programmer to read the pixel values of whatever is currently stored in the main VGA preview bitmap array, called PreviewImg in the source code. The system works like this:
Whenever PreviewVal is evaluated the following integer value is returned by this logic:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>PreviewVal</tt><tt> = (int32_t) PreviewImg[</tt><tt>PreviewPos];</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>PreviewPos +=  PreviewInc</tt><tt>;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>if(PreviewPos &gt;=  PreviewImg_rgb_size)  PreviewPos -=  PreviewImg_rgb_size</tt><tt>;</tt> </span>
Thus each time PreviewVal is evaluated the value of PreviewPos is incremented by PreviewInc and rolled over to stay within the allowed range.
<b>Note</b>: At the start of each script run, PreviewPos defaults to 0 and PreviewInc defaults to 1. PreviewVal is evaluated afresh each time it is used so there it has no standard default or initial value.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">set_str</span>
set_str &lt;STR_VAR&gt; [[str1] [[str2] ...]]
This allows you to set a user-defined variable of string type to a string of up to 24 words. If no [str] arguments are provided the string variable is set to an empty string. No space will be printed to the end of the list of argument strings.
&lt;STR_VAR&gt; - the string variable to be modified
[[str1] [[str2] ...]] - a list of between 0 and 24 string constants separated by white space. These will replace the existing contents of &lt;STR_VAR&gt; 
<b>Note</b>: Up to 24 string arguments may be used in one command. The limitations on what arguments are permitted are as follows:
Each string argument in the list of words cannot contain white space and each argument must have no more than MAX_ARGLEN bytes (see Note and Caution below).
Each string argument cannot contain the hash character (#) anywhere in it - as soon as a hash character is encountered the word will be truncated to that point and any characters or words after the hash will be ignored. You can add hash characters to a string if you need to by using the add_var command with the Hash system variable or the cat_char command with an argument of value 35.
Only one plain space character (ASCII value 32) will be printed between each string argument - multiple spaces are ignored. You can add more spaces to a string if you need to using the separate add_var command with the Space system variable or the cat_char command with an argument of value 32.
If any of the string arguments are the names of user-defined or system variables they will be added verbatim without conversion to their current value. Use the set_var or add_var commands if you need that functionality.
Standard formatting / control / escape characters like '\n' will be printed verbatim and will not have their intended special effect.
Recall that PCS does not use delimiters of any kind to define strings or characters to be inserted into strings. If you use single or double quotes as either the whole or part of an argument then those quotes will become part of the string.
<b>Note</b>: Each [str] argument will be appended to the &lt;STR_VAR&gt; separated by a single space (ASCII character 32) regardless of whatever type or number of white space character(s) was used to separate arguments in the command. No space will be added between the initial value of &lt;STR_VAR&gt; and the first [str] argument. No space will be printed to the end of the list of argument strings. 
<b>Note</b>: Each [str] argument will be treated as a string constant and not a variable (of any data type). If a variable name is included in these arguments it will be treated just as a string and will not be evaluated (so the variable's name will be concatenated 'as is'). If you want to add the value contained in some variable to the end of a string then use the add_var command instead (which see).
<b>Note</b>: The interpreter has a limit on the maximum size of any argument. This is set by the MAX_ARGLEN value in the interpreter source code which, at the time of writing is 64 bytes. It is important to note that bytes do not necessarily equate to characters. While standard ASCII characters are 1 byte each, UTF-8 Unicode characters (such as box-drawing symbols or emojis) typically consume 3 or 4 bytes each. For example, the ═ symbol uses 3 bytes; therefore, a single argument can contain a maximum of 21 of these characters before exceeding the 64-byte limit.
<b>Caution</b>: Do not exceed MAX_ARGLEN bytes per argument. If a string exceeds this limit, the interpreter will truncate it at the byte level. This may result in <b>malformed UTF-8 sequences</b> for Unicode characters, likely causing the string to fail to render. For ASCII text, the string will simply be truncated.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">set_tracker</span>
set_tracker &lt;tracker_name&gt; &lt;tracker_setting&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
This assigns a value to a parameter or other setting in an extant tracker.
&lt;tracker_name&gt; - a string varval defining the name of the tracker to be customised. This is case sensitive and must pertain to an extant user-defined tracker.
&lt;tracker_setting&gt; - an integer varval that must be one of the constants #defined in stdpard.pch (listed below) that define a valid setting. A number that is not in the range 1 to 39 is a script error and terminates the script.
&lt;varval&gt; - a varval that contains the value you wish to set the setting to. It must be of the type given in the tables below: an integer for the integer settings (it must be &gt;= 0 because none of them accepts a negative number) and a float for the float settings.
<b>Note</b>: The tracking sub-system is designed to fail without terminating the script (except for catastrophic memory failures and invalid arguments). If the value of &lt;varval&gt; is a valid integer or float but is outside the allowed range for that setting, or you set a setting that belongs to a different tracker type (the COM, KLT and GHT settings below only apply to trackers of that type), the command makes no change and instead sets the status flag of your tracker to TRKS_BAD_SETTING (value 6). TRKS_BAD_SETTING is replaced the next time a setting is accepted, or by a successful init_tracker or track. You can also clear it yourself with TKSET_STATUS. Settings that are general but have no effect for a particular tracker type (for example TKSET_MERGEALPHA on a hog tracker) are accepted and stored without any effect.
<b>Note</b>: get_tracker can read back only the last known position (set by TKSET_ROW and TKSET_COL) and the occlusion counter (TKSET_OCCLCOUNT). The other settings cannot be read back, so keep your own record of the values you set.
General settings (all tracker types unless stated):


  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Setting        </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Value     </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Default                                           </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">Meaning                                 </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">(number)       </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">          </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">                                                  </span> │ <span weight="bold" foreground="#1a5fb4">                                        </span> │ </tt>
  <tt> ├─────────────────┼────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKSET_TEMPDIM   │ integer,   │ as defined                                         │ Change the template window size. If the  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ (1)             │ &gt;= 16, and │                                                    │ size is different, the tracker becomes   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ obeying    │                                                    │ un-initialised (TRKS_UNSEEDED) and must  │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ the def_tr │                                                    │ be initialised again. The change is      │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ acker      │                                                    │ rejected if it would break the           │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ rules for  │                                                    │ def_tracker rules (for example the hog   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ this type  │                                                    │ multiple-of-&lt;cell_dim&gt; rule).            │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKSET_SEARCHDIM │ integer,   │ as defined                                         │ Change the search area size. The tracker │ </tt>
  <tt> │ (2)             │ obeying    │                                                    │ stays initialised. (Ignored by klt.)     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ the def_tr │                                                    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ acker      │                                                    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ rules for  │                                                    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ this type  │                                                    │                                          │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKSET_COLCHAN   │ integer 1  │ set by init_tracker                                │ Change the colour channel. If this is a  │ </tt>
  <tt> │ (3)             │ - 4        │                                                    │ different channel the tracker becomes    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ (CCHAN_    │                                                    │ un-initialised because its target was    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │ constants) │                                                    │ recorded in the old channel.             │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKSET_SEARCHSIG │ float &gt; 0  │ a quarter of the square of the difference between  │ zmncc and ght only: width of the         │ </tt>
  <tt> │ (4)             │            │ the search and template sizes                      │ preference for positions near the last   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ known position. A candidate position     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ that is a distance d (pixels, in each    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ direction separately) from the last      │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ position has its score multiplied by     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ exp(-d^2 / value). Smaller values make   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ the tracker stick closer to where it     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ was; very large values remove the        │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ preference. If you never set it, the     │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ default follows any later change of      │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ search or template size. Accepted but    │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ unused by hog, com and klt.              │ </tt>
  <tt> │ TKSET_MERGEALPH │ float 0 -  │ 0.02                                               │ zmncc only: how quickly the template     │ </tt>
  <tt> │ A (5)           │ 1          │                                                    │ adapts to the target's current           │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ appearance. After each confident track   │ </tt>
  <tt> │                 │            │                                                    │ (confidence not below the occlusion      │ </tt>
