<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">set_var</span>
set_var &lt;varname&gt; &lt;varval&gt; [charpos]
This assigns a value to a user-defined variable. This is symbolically equivalent to 'varname = varval'. The [charpos] option allows additional operations on string variables.
&lt;varname&gt; - a character string constant representing the name of the variable whose value is to be set.
&lt;varval&gt; - a varval and is the value that is to be assigned to the variable specified in &lt;varname&gt;.
[charpos] - a integer varval specifying the position in a string to operate on (starting from 0) or a negative number if you want the string length to be evaluated. See notes for details.
Note: When &lt;varname&gt; is a string, &lt;varval&gt; is cast to a string by the rule given under def_var: a constant written in the script is used exactly as written, and a numeric variable is written out as a number.
Note: The [charpos] optional argument has no effect unless &lt;varname&gt; is a string and &lt;varval&gt; is either a string variable or a constant written in the script (a constant is a string for this purpose, so `set_var s 12345 2` gives “3”). It is ignored when &lt;varval&gt; is an int or float variable.
<b>Note</b>: When &lt;varname&gt; and &lt;varval&gt; are string data then, if [charpos] is supplied and:
[charpos] is &gt;= 0 : The the set operation will only pertain to the single character in the the &lt;varval&gt; string that is the [charpos]th character in that string (the first character being at position 0) unless the value of [charpos] is &gt;= the number of characters in the string of &lt;varval&gt; in which case no operation is performed (&lt;varname&gt; is left unaltered) but this will not result in an error - the script will silently continue.
[charpos] is &lt; 0 : The string value of the variable &lt;varname&gt; will be assigned a numerical integer value (in ASCII form, as a string) which is the length of the string of &lt;varval&gt; as returned by the C function strlen.
<b>Note</b>: Thus the optional [charpos] argument allows you to extract a specific single character from the string of &lt;varval&gt; or return the length of that string. It serves no function for for other data types.
<b>Note</b>: The assignment equals (=) operation can also be done as an inline operation as part of the math_var command (which see).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">skip_frames</span>
skip_frames &lt;skip&gt;
This causes the camera frame grabber to activate and discard a certain number of frames. This allows any frames stored in the grabber buffers to be expunged. This clearing of the buffer queue might be useful to ensure a subsequent grab command begins at the most recent thing stationed in front of the camera or to ensure that any change of camera setting (like brightness, gain, colour balance, etc.) is fully enacted in the next image you capture (otherwise if you simply take the next image in the buffer it will be the one that was put there before your setting change was made).
&lt;skip&gt; - a varval of type int &gt;= 0. It tells the frame grabber how many frames to grab and discard.
<b>Note</b>: This is a 'one-off' operation that will happen immediately when you issue the command. This will not change the pardcap internal global variable 'skiplim' which is set using the command frames_to_skip (which see).
<b>Note</b>: At the time of writing pardcap uses a buffer queue of 2, so if you set &lt;skip&gt; to 3 this should ensure the buffers are purged and the next image in the buffer queue is up-to-date with any settings or image currently on the camera sensor.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">sleep</span>
sleep &lt;value&gt;
This pauses execution of the script for the specified amount of time in seconds.
&lt;value&gt; - a varval of type float and &gt;= 0.0
<b>Note</b>: Fractions of a second are respected - there is no need to use whole numbers.
<b>Note</b>: This may be useful to allow any temporary fluctuation in illumination or wobble in the mechanical axes (e.g. due to sudden deceleration), or other process to stabilise before going on to do other things (such as capture an image sequence). It may also be used if you want to pause while PARDUS communicates with another computer (such as an external image processing computer).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">stats_array</span>
stats_array &lt;array_in&gt; &lt;stats_func&gt; &lt;threshold&gt; &lt;arguments as per &lt;stats_func&gt;&gt;
This calculates various statistical measures on the input array. Some of these measures are for 1D or 2D arrays while, for other measures, dimensionality is not important.  The index of the input array is respected.
&lt;array_in&gt; - The name of the input array variable whose values are to be analysed. This must be of type int or float. String arrays are not allowed.
&lt;stats_func&gt; - This is a string varval specifying the range of statistics to be calculated. This must be one of these options: basic, spatial, distro, texture.
&lt;threshold&gt; - A numerical varval specifying the value, below which all array cell values in the input array will be ignored (excluded from the processing). For the case of float type input arrays this threshold is applied always. For the case of integer input arrays this threshold is only applied if it is &gt;0.
The meaning of the subsequent arguments depends on your choice for &lt;stats_func&gt; as follows:
For basic the remaining arguments are: &lt;len&gt; &lt;rec_mean&gt; &lt;rec_integral&gt; &lt;rec_min&gt; &lt;rec_max&gt; &lt;rec_range&gt; &lt;rec_var&gt; &lt;rec_SD&gt; &lt;rec_energy&gt; &lt;rec_sparsity&gt;
For spatial the remaining arguments are: &lt;height&gt; &lt;width/rec_com_c&gt; &lt;rec_com_r&gt; &lt;rec_bb_r&gt; &lt;rec_bb_c&gt; &lt;rec_bb_ht&gt; &lt;rec_bb_wd&gt; &lt;rec_orient&gt; &lt;rec_eccent&gt;
For distro the remaining arguments are: &lt;len&gt; &lt;rec_mdn&gt; &lt;rect_lquart&gt; &lt;rec_uquart&gt; &lt;rec_iqr&gt; &lt;ptile&gt; &lt;rec_mode&gt; &lt;rec_peak&gt; &lt;rec_entropy&gt;
For texture the remaining arguments are: &lt;height&gt; &lt;width&gt; &lt;tv&gt; &lt;gradenergy&gt; 
<b>Note</b>:  The index of the input array is respected - processing only occurs from that index onwards.
<b>Note</b>: Arrays of integer type are given some special treatment to make the processing faster. This special treatment is as follows: Threshold values &lt;=0 are treated as meaning 'do not apply any thresholding' and this therefore prevents you from applying a negative threshold. This limitation does not apply to float arrays where any threshold value is applied - positive or negative. Another special treatment for integer input is that those stats which require a histogram to be built, the histogram is made on 256 bins assuming the input pixel value range is 0 to 255 and any pixel value outside that range are excluded from the distribution calculations. These special circumstances exist so you can use the integer route for fast processing of video frames. If you want the full range of the inputs beyond this 8-bit span to be processed without these 8-bit assumptions then use a float array as input instead of integer.
<b>Note</b>: The output variables may be of any type - all statistics are produced in double precision floating point maths but the PCS auto-casting rules will be applied if any of your receptor variables are of int or string type.
<b>Note</b>: You must supply at least as many arguments as you need. If you supply the maximum number (14) any unused arguments will be ignored. If you supply too few, the script will terminate due to a run-time error or it will not get past the script check phase.
<b>Note</b>: The size arguments, &lt;len&gt; &lt;height&gt; &lt;width&gt;, must all be integer varvals which may be conveyed as a constant or a variable of any data type. The integer value they convey must be &gt;=1. These specify the total number of array elements to process (&lt;len&gt; on its own or &lt;height&gt; x &lt;width&gt;). If that total number is greater than the available cells of the input array from its supplied index to the end of the array then a script-terminating error will occur.
<b>Note</b>: For all &lt;stats_func&gt; except texture the &lt;width&gt; argument may, alternatively be used as a receptor. In those cases you must supply the value as a variable - not a constant. If the &lt;stats_func&gt; value may ever be anything other than texture in your script supplying a constant for this argument will result in a script-terminating error.
<b>Note</b>: All the other argument may only be user-defined variables so they can receive the result they are intended for. All results are calculated in doubles but the PCS autocasting rules will be applied if you supply a receptor variable of int or string type. The initial values in all these variables does not matter because it will get overwritten with the result with the sole exception of &lt;ptile&gt; which must convey a valid number. If that number is in the closed range 0.0 - 100.0 then that is the percentile that will be written into that variable after processing, replacing its original value. If its original value is non-numerical this will result in a script-terminating error. If its value is outside the closed range described above then the returned value will be the 95th percentile.
<b>Note</b>: The meaning of each receptor variable is described below. For all modes, any pixel with a value strictly less than &lt;threshold&gt; is completely ignored by the processing loops. The remaining pixels that meet or exceed &lt;threshold&gt; are defined as the <b><i>active data set</i></b>. If no pixels meet this requirement, output values gracefully default to zero (or 1.0 for sparsity) to prevent division-by-zero errors:
<b><i>For </i></b><b><i>basic</i></b><b><i> stats</i></b>:
&lt;rec_mean&gt; - The average intensity value of the image. Calculated as the sum of all elements in the active data set divided by the number of active elements. Impact of Threshold: Excluding dark background noise or negative anomalies will cleanly increase this value to reflect only your target highlights.
&lt;rec_integral&gt; - The total accumulated sum of all pixel values in the active data set. Useful for overall light exposure measurements.
&lt;rec_min&gt; - The lowest pixel intensity value discovered within the active data set. Impact of Threshold: This value will always be greater than or equal to your chosen &lt;threshold&gt;.
&lt;rec_max&gt; - The absolute peak pixel intensity value found within the active data set.
&lt;rec_range&gt; - The difference between maximum and minimum intensity (max - min). Measures the total contrast envelope of your active pixels.
&lt;rec_var&gt; - The statistical population variance. Measures how widely the pixel values fluctuate around the calculated mean.
&lt;rec_SD&gt; - The standard deviation (square root of variance). Expresses image contrast variations in the exact same units as your raw pixels (e.g. 0–255).
&lt;rec_energy&gt; - The Root Mean Square (RMS) Amplitude. Measures the total signal power or global noise energy across the processed canvas block.
&lt;rec_sparsity&gt; - The ratio of zero-value pixels to the total number of items in the active data set. Impact of Threshold: Setting &lt;threshold&gt; greater than 0 filters out all physical zeroes, automatically locking this output to 0.0.
<b><i>For </i></b><b><i>spatial</i></b><b><i> stats</i></b>:
&lt;rec_com_r&gt; - The row position of the calculated global Center of Mass ('Mass' = pixel value). Impact of Threshold: Isolates an object's physical coordinates by ignoring asymmetric background noise.
&lt;width/rec_com_c&gt; - [In-Place Dual Parameter]. This must hold the integer width of the input array when submitted. When the results are calculated its value will be over-written with the column position of the calculated global Center of Mass ('Mass' = pixel value). Impact of Threshold: Isolates an object's physical coordinates by ignoring asymmetric background noise.
&lt;rec_bb_r&gt; - The bounding box row offset. Marks the top-most row index that contains at least one active pixel.
&lt;rec_bb_c&gt; - The bounding box column offset. Marks the left-most column index that contains at least one active pixel.
&lt;rec_bb_ht&gt; - The total height of the bounding box enclosure spanning your active pixels.
&lt;rec_bb_wd&gt; - The total width of the bounding box enclosure spanning your active pixels.
&lt;rec_orient&gt; - The dominant tilt angle of light distribution across the canvas (in radians from -p/2 to +p/2), calculated using central moments. Impact of Threshold: Allows you to find the orientation of a specific shape or object while completely ignoring surrounding background artefacts.
&lt;rec_eccent&gt; - The shape eccentricity index (elongation factor) between 0.0 and 1.0. A value of 0.0 means a perfectly round circle of light, while values approaching 1.0 describe a stretched line.
<b><i>For </i></b><b><i>distro</i></b><b><i> stats</i></b>:
&lt;rec_mdn&gt; - The median (50th percentile). The exact middle intensity value when your active data set is sorted. Unlike the mean, it is highly resistant to outlier spikes like hot-pixel camera artefacts.
&lt;rect_lquart&gt; - The lower quartile (25th percentile). 25% of your active pixels fall at or below this intensity value.
&lt;rec_uquart&gt; - The upper quartile (75th percentile). 75% of your active pixels fall at or below this intensity value.
&lt;rec_iqr&gt; - The Interquartile Range (&lt;rec_uquart&gt; - &lt;rect_lquart&gt;). Measures the spread of the middle 50% of your data, providing a robust descriptor of uniform mid-tone contrast.
&lt;ptile&gt; - [In-Place Dual Parameter] Input a floating-point target percentile value (0.0 to 100.0) into this variable. The command reads your configuration, executes, and overwrites this exact variable with the corresponding pixel intensity value.
&lt;rec_mode&gt; - The intensity value that occurs most frequently across the active data set.
&lt;rec_peak&gt; - The maximum count/frequency of your mode. Tells you exactly how many active pixels share that dominant intensity value.
&lt;rec_entropy&gt; - The information-theory randomness density. High entropy indicates a highly detailed texture or busy noise pattern, while low values indicate flat, uniform regions of colour. Impact of Threshold: Restricting the data set to a specific highlight window will drop entropy to focus purely on the target's internal texture density.
<b><i>For </i></b><b><i>texture</i></b><b><i> stats</i></b>:
&lt;tv&gt; - Total Variation. Sums the absolute derivative differences between every pixel and its neighbours across rows and columns. Measures raw canvas sharpness.
&lt;gradenergy&gt; - Gradient Energy. Sums the squared variations of local pixel transitions. This value spikes significantly in highly focused, sharp-edged imagery, making it an excellent metric for camera autofocus tuning routines. Impact of Threshold: Filters out uniform background gradient shifts so your sharpness metric is calculated purely from your prioritized regions of interest.



<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">sub_var</span>
sub_var &lt;varname&gt; &lt;varval&gt;
This subtracts a value from a user-defined variable. 
&lt;varname&gt; - The name of the variable whose value you want to change
&lt;varval&gt; - The value (or variable containing the value) you want to subtract from &lt;varname&gt;.
<b>Note</b>: This is symbolically equivalent to &lt;varname&gt; -= &lt;varval&gt; in C.
<b>Note</b>: Subtraction can also be done inline using math_var (which see).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">tc_threshold</span>
tc_threshold &lt;lower&gt; &lt;upper&gt;
This sets lower and upper tissue content thresholds. See notes below for more information.
&lt;lower&gt; - a varval of type float. It must not be greater than &lt;upper&gt;
&lt;upper&gt; - a varval of type float. It must not be less than &lt;lower&gt; (unless &lt;upper&gt; is negative - which is permitted but which also disables the use of this upper limit value as described below).
    
<b>Note</b>: Tissue content thresholding is an optional test to determine whether a captured image is discarded or saved. Even if an image is discarded due to not passing the tissue content threshold test you may still request a coordinates file by setting the save_coords value to ON (see save_coords).
<b>Note</b>: If the &lt;lower&gt; value supplied here as negative then tissue content thresholding will not be used at all and any image capture request (if the capture succeeds without error) will result in an image to be saved. If only the &lt;upper&gt; value supplied here is negative (when the &lt;lower&gt; is non-negative) then tissue content thresholding will be done but without an upper limit imposed in the thresholding process.
<b>Note</b>: Tissue content threshold tests are applied to the number returned from the tissue content analysis (TCA) algorithm applied to the Y-component of a preview image taken just prior to a full-size frame image capture event (see the command get_preview_tc for details of that TCA algorithm). If the threshold test is passed then the full image capture goes ahead. If the test fails then the full image capture does not go ahead. 
<b>Note</b>: Unless you call  get_preview_tc OFF on, at least once in any given login session to a pardcap server after the pardcap server first starts up, the reference values  MALref and VARref will not be set. Their default values when a pardcap server first starts are -1.0 (for each of them). If VARref is negative it means the tc reference values have not yet been set and so tc measurements (let alone tc thresholding) will not be possible and so will not be applied regardless of what values you set with this tc_threshold command.
<b>Note</b>: The thresholding tests are implemented in the pardcap C source code according to the pseudocode logic shown below (tcval is the result of the TCA algorithm applied to the preview image):
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>if (&lt;lower&gt; &lt; 0.0) go_ahead_with_full_image_capture</tt><tt>()</tt><tt>;</tt><tt> // No thresholding</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>else {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>int score = 0;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>int passmark = 1;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>grab</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>a</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>preview</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>image</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>and</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>calculate</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>tcval</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>from</tt><tt>_</tt><tt>it</tt><tt>();</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>if (tcval &gt;= &lt;lower&gt;) score++;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>if (&lt;upper&gt; &gt;= 0.0) {</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>passmark++;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>    </tt><tt>if (tcval &lt;= &lt;upper&gt;) score++;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>}</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28">  </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>  </tt><tt>if (score == passmark) go_ahead_with_full_image_capture</tt><tt>()</tt><tt>;</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>}</tt> </span>


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">tmc_chconf_parse</span>
tmc_chconf_parse &lt;chconf_info&gt; &lt;receptor&gt;
Extracts a piece of config information from the current value of the locally stored TMC stepper motor driver chop conf register (CHOPCONF).
&lt;chconf_info&gt; - an integer determining what piece of config information to get.
&lt;receptor&gt; - a variable which will receive the information.
<b>Note</b>: The received information will be an integer.
<b>Note</b>: This is a purely local command with no traffic to the server or Arduino.
<b>Note</b>: The value of the local copy of CHOPCONF can be updated with the separate act_get command using the INFO_TMCCHCONF option.
<b>Note</b>: This parsing command uses a custom PARDUS C function and does not use the TMCStepper library code. It thereby bypasses the commonly known bug in that library for parsing the microstep denominator (TMC_CI_MRESMSDEN) value.
<b>Note</b>: The currently acceptable &lt;chconf_info&gt; options are defined in parddefs.h as:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// TMC2209 chopconf info item</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_TOFFLEVEL 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_HYSTSTART 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_HYST__END 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_CPBLANKTM 4</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_VSENSITIV 5</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_MRESMSDEN 6</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_INTERPOLN 7</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_DOUBLEDGE 8</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_DISSHRTTG 9</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_CI_DISSHRTTL 10</tt> </span>
<b>Note</b>: Information returned will conform to the following ranges:
For TMC_CI_TOFFLEVEL the result will be a number between 0 and 15.
For TMC_CI_HYSTSTART the result will be a number between 1 and 8.
For TMC_CI_HYST__END the result will be a number between -3 and 12.
For TMC_CI_CPBLANKTM the result will be a number between 0 and 3 with the following meaning:
0=16 clocks
1=24 clocks
2=36 clocks
3=54 clocks
For TMC_CI_MRESMSDEN the result will be a number between 1 and 256.
For all other options the result will be 1 (for true) or 0 (for false).
For more details on these data items see the TMC2209 data sheet.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">tmc_status_parse</span>
tmc_status_parse &lt;status_info&gt; &lt;receptor&gt;
Extracts a piece of status information from the current value of the locally stored TMC stepper motor driver status register (DRV_STATUS).
&lt;status_info&gt; - an integer determining what piece of status information to get.
&lt;receptor&gt; - a variable which will receive the information.
<b>Note</b>: The received information will be an integer.
<b>Note</b>: This is a purely local command with no traffic to the server or Arduino.
<b>Note</b>: The value of the local copy of DRV_STATUS can be updated with the separate act_get command using the INFO_TMCSTATUS option
Note: The currently acceptable &lt;status_info&gt; options are defined in parddefs.h as:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>// TMC2209 status info item</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_OVER_TEMP 1</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_PREO_TEMP 2</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_SHORTGNDA 3</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_SHORTGNDB 4</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_SHORTSUPA 5</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_SHORTSUPB 6</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_OPENLOADA 7</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_OPENLOADB 8</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_CS_ACTUAL 9</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_TEMP_THRS 10</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_STNDSTILL 11</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#define TMC_SI_SPRDCYCLE 12</tt> </span>
<b>Note</b>: Information returned will conform to the following ranges:
For TMC_SI_CS_ACTUAL the result will be a number between 0 and 31.
For TMC_SI_TEMP_THRS the result will be a number between 0 and 15.
For all other options the result will be 1 (for true) or 0 (for false).
For more details on these data items see the TMC2209 data sheet.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">track</span>
track &lt;tracker_name&gt; &lt;receptor_r&gt; &lt;receptor_c&gt; &lt;receptor_score&gt;
This causes a tracker to assess the current position of the target it is tracking and return its best estimate of the current position of the target and its confidence measure of target acquisition. Use it once for each new image placed in the preview buffer.
&lt;tracker_name&gt; - a string varval defining the name of the tracker to be activated. This is case sensitive and must pertain to an extant user-defined tracker.
&lt;receptor_r&gt; - a user-defined variable to receive the top of the template window area of the tracker's current estimate of the position of the target. The variable must be capable of storing an integer number.
&lt;receptor_c&gt; - a user-defined variable to receive the extreme left column of the template window area of the tracker's current estimate of the position of the target. The variable must be capable of storing an integer number.
&lt;receptor_score&gt; - a user-defined variable to receive the match strength / confidence value. The variable must be capable of storing a float number.
<b>Note</b>: Row and column coordinates are provided in the reference frame of the preview image's VGA field (even if smaller or non 4:3 preview images are being used).
<b>Note</b>: What you get back:
Normal tracking: the position of the best match and a confidence at or above the occlusion threshold. The status flag is TRKS_OK.
Occluded or poor match: the confidence is below the occlusion threshold. The best position found is still returned, the occlusion counter increases by one and the status flag is TRKS_OCCLUDED. (zmncc does not adapt its template on such a frame. klt: if too few points agree on the motion, the target is declared lost: the box is held at its previous position, which is what is returned, with confidence 0.)
No search possible because the tracker is not initialised (never initialised, initialisation failed, or a setting change discarded it): &lt;receptor_score&gt; is set to -1.0, &lt;receptor_r&gt; and &lt;receptor_c&gt; are left exactly as they were, and the status flag is TRKS_UNSEEDED.
No search possible because the search area could not be placed in the frame: the same, with the status flag TRKS_BAD_WINDOW. This should never happen with a 640 x 480 preview image.
<b>Note</b>: A &lt;receptor_score&gt; of -1.0 means that no search was made and that your row and column receptor variables have not been changed. Every position that track does write is a valid template position (row between 0 and 480-&lt;template_dim&gt;, column between 0 and 640-&lt;template_dim&gt;), so it can be passed straight to init_tracker, match_tracker or TKSET_ROW and TKSET_COL without checking. A score of 0 or a low score, by contrast, means that a search was made and the target was not found well (TRKS_OCCLUDED): the position returned is then only the best guess.
<b>Note</b>: The returned position becomes the tracker's last known position and the centre of the next search. The confidence has these meanings:
zmncc: the correlation between the (adapting) template and the best window. 1 = identical pattern, near 0 = no resemblance.
hog: the overlap of the edge-direction histograms of the template and the best window. 1 = identical.
com: the fraction of the bright (or dark) "mass" in the whole search area that lies inside the final window. It is low if there is a second blob in the search area, or none.
ght: the number of votes for the winning position (smoothed, see TKSET_GHT_SMOOTH) divided by the number of edge pixels in the template, limited to 1.
klt: how many of the box points moved together as the target and how many survived, scaled down when fewer than 8 points agree; 0 if fewer than TKSET_KLT_MINPTS agree (track lost).
<b>Note</b>: Speed of the target. For zmncc, hog, com and ght, the target must stay inside the search area: it can move at most about (&lt;search_dim&gt; - &lt;template_dim&gt;) / 2 pixels between two track commands. A klt tracker copes with much larger movements because it covers the whole frame. If a target is lost, use match_tracker to look for it, then redirect the tracker with set_tracker (TKSET_ROW, TKSET_COL) or init_tracker.
<b>Note</b>: All tracking activity is based on the VGA preview buffer - even if that buffer is displaying multiple images or images that are not of VGA dimensions (i.e. non 4:3 aspect ratio). For non-4:3 aspect ratio preview images, the grey zones of padding (used to pad the preview image to fit in the 4:3 display zone) will be used as if they were image pixels for all tracking purposes.
<b>Note</b>: The tracking sub-system is designed to fail without terminating the script (except for catastrophic memory failures or invalid arguments). Failures are reported by setting the tracker's status code, which you can read with get_tracker.
Example - tracking a target through a series of images with a zmncc tracker:
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>#include stdpard.pch</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int</tt><tt> </tt><tt> row 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int</tt><tt> </tt><tt> col 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var float score 0.0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_var int</tt><tt> </tt><tt> status 0</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># (load the first image into the preview buffer, then)</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>def_tracker zmncc t1 64 128 16</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>init_tracker t1 244 267 CCHAN_R</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt># for each later image: load it into the preview buffer, then</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>track t1 row col score</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>get_tracker t1 TKGET_ERRSTATUS status</tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt> </tt> </span>
    <span font="monospace" background="#f0f0f0" foreground="#c01c28"> <tt>undef_tracker t1</tt> </span>



<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">undef_actuator</span>
undef_actuator &lt;act_id&gt;
This undefines (frees) an actuator definition. This clears all data associated with an actuator such that it cannot be used in any way after it is undefined. You may re-define it in order to use it (using def_actuator) but it will then be created in the completely nascent state (unhomed, step count invalid, requiring all pins to be re-defined, etc.).
&lt;act_id&gt; - This is a string that uniquely identifies the actuator. It may be one of 2 things:
The name of an extant actuator
The word CurrAct
In both cases these are case sensitive and may be supplied either as a string constant or in a string variable.
Internally, the command uses the values of the system variables CurrActIdx (actuator index) and CurrActType (actuator type value) to identify the actuator to undefine. If you supply CurrAct as &lt;act_id&gt; then it simply uses whatever values those two system variables happen to have at the time. If you supply the name of an actuator then it attempts to find the index and type of that actuator and will set CurrActIdx and CurrActType to those values before using them. 
The actuator of the specified type and index must exist on the server for this command to succeed, otherwise an error will occur at run time. The script check procedure will not fail if the specific actuator does not exist because it can't predict what actuators will come into existence upon a run-time call to get_actuators so it is up to the script writer to ensure these will exist at run time to avoid error. To find out if an actuator exists, do a get_actuators command to refresh the local list of extant actuators from the server's data, then do either an act_getname or act_getid command (which see).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">undef_tracker</span>
undef_tracker &lt;tracker_name&gt;
This destroys a user-defined tracker and deletes any information it contained. Its name becomes available for reuse (to define a new tracker) and any later tracker command that names it (apart from def_tracker) will cause an error.
&lt;tracker_name&gt; - a string varval defining the name of the tracker to be destroyed. This is case sensitive and must pertain to an extant user-defined tracker.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">undef_var</span>
undef_var &lt;varname&gt;
This destroys a user-defined variable (including an array) and deletes any information it contained.
&lt;varname&gt; - a string constant defining the name of the variable or array to be destroyed. This is case sensitive and must pertain to a extant user-defined variable or array (not a system variable).
<b>Note</b>: This command works for arrays of size &gt; 1 as well as single variables. When operating on an array only supply the base name and do not use dereferencing brackets. This is the only occasion, other than with def_var, where an array of size &gt; 1 can (and must) be used without dereferencing brackets.
<b>Note</b>: This allows you to use system resources (in particular, RAM) more efficiently by giving variables temporally limited scope. Bear in mind, however, that for the duration in which a variable exists it will always have global scope. For mote details about variable scope rules see p.39 . For example you can create a set of temporary variables to act as 'arguments' to pass values to a function that expects those variables and then undefine those variables at the end of the function just before the function returns. Alternatively you can define a set of variables at the beginning of a function to act as temporary working variables and then undefine them just before you return from the function. However, unlike in other languages, these temporary variables are not 'automatic' or limited in global scope. You can also define a large array for reading in data and then undefine that array when it is no longer needed. It will be entirely up to the PCS programmer to establish and stick to a valid protocol for defining, using and destroying those variables.
<b>Note</b>: The script checker part of the interpreter imposes certain restrictions on the use of variables and their existence. For details see the dedicated section on that topic on page 39 .
<b>Note</b>: In general, problems may occur if you use def_var, def_array or undef_var inside loops or blocks of code that are conditionally accessed (by do_if, else or any block that is the target of a conditional jump etc.). Such usage is not forbidden and may well pass the syntax checking phase (where conditions are not evaluated and jumps or loops are not enacted) but may results in errors or unforeseen behaviour during run time. Sometimes such placement may be desirable but it is left to the programmer to determine when this is appropriate according to their intended logic. For example, it is almost never a good idea to use these commands inside a loop_while loop but it might be acceptable if you need to read large amounts of data into an array from a file and the size of that array might need to change on each iteration of the loop. In such cases using def_array for dynamic allocation of the array to the appropriate size followed, after reading and using the data, by undef_var before the loop repeats might be a resource efficient way to code this and would be perfectly safe provided the loop can never be exited by a non-local jump that comes between def_array and undef_var.
<b>Note</b>: By using the def_array -&gt; undef_var -&gt; def_array sequence you can effect dynamic re-sizing of an array at run time. See the notes in the description of def_array for more detail.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">update_gui_coords</span>
update_gui_coords &lt;server_sync?&gt;
This updates the client side values and display on the GUI controls of the current XYZ stage coordinates and calibration information.
&lt;server_sync?&gt; - is a binary choice varval. If it evaluates to non-zero then the command queries the server to get the latest values of the stage XYZ coordinates, calibration factors, calibration units name and currently used unit type (as set by act_unit) and updates the local client side copies of these variables. If this evaluates to zero then the GUI coordinate values are updated with whatever coordinates are currently held on the client.
<b>Note</b>: The reason that making a syncing call to the server is optional is that most 'normal' motion commands that wait for a PARDUS movement report (PMR) will already have the very latest coordinates held locally in the client once the motion has ended and so there is no need to query the server again, only to update the GUI widgets with those values using the pardclient function update_axis_positions_gui(); (which this command will ultimately call regardless of the value of &lt;server_sync?&gt; ). However, there are occasions when these coordinate values might change on the server and Arduino without the latest values coming over to the client (such as after an act_set command using the INFO_STEPCOUNT info item) in which case using a &lt;server_sync?&gt; value of TRUE (or similar) will allow full syncing to what is current on the server.
<b>Note</b>: Using act_get with an info item of INFO_STEPCOUNT or INFO_XYZCOORDS will only update script variables with the latest coordinate values but will not change the client local PMRep globals. Only using this update_gui_coords command will do that.
<b>Note</b>: This command will not work unless all 3 stage axis stepper motors are defined (X, Y and Z).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">verbosity</span>
verbosity &lt;level&gt;
This sets how much output to show in the server console, script console and log file.
&lt;level&gt; is a varval of type int and can be:
0  = only show and log error messages (plus camera setup/quit notices)
1  = as for 0 plus show (but don't log) the results of print_value commands
2  = as for 1 plus log the results of print_value commands (and show check/run status lines in CLI mode)
3  = show and log everything.
<b>Note</b>: Having a verbosity less than 3 can be useful, esp. for live preview and other rapid loops.
<b>Note</b>: The default value is 1 at program start (or a value read from a config file). Verbosity is reset to this default value before and after every script run.
<b>Note</b>: In pardclient some messages are printed to the script console during loading and checking of files regardless of the default or current verbosity to give visual feedback to the user that the file was read and checked or processed OK.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">xyzs_af_period</span>
xyzs_af_period &lt;period&gt;
This command sets the period of performing an autofocus attempt during an XY-scan performed by the command xyzs_scan (which see).
&lt;period&gt; - a varval of type int that must evaluate to &gt;= 0. It is the period (in terms of number of scan moves) at which autofocus is triggered during an XY scan.
<b>Note</b>: If &lt;period&gt; is 0 then no autofocus will be performed throughout the whole scan. Otherwise an autofocus procedure will be attempted every &lt;period&gt; moves the scan takes. Use a value of 1 to perform autofocus at every step. If tissue content thresholding is on, scan positions will only be counted towards this period if there is tissue detected in the field, so the actual period will only relate to the number of tissue-containing positions in the scan.
Note: If &lt;period&gt; is &gt;= 1 then the pre-requisite commands for the PCS command autofocus to function must have been called at some point before calling xyzs_scan. See autofocus for details.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">xyzs_finish</span>
xyzs_finish &lt;coord_x&gt; &lt;coord_y&gt;
Set the end-point coordinates of an XY scan performed by the command xyzs_scan (which see).
&lt;coord_x&gt; - a varval of type float and must be non-negative (&gt;= 0.0). It is the X coordinate for the end of the scan.
&lt;coord_y&gt; - a varval of type float and must be non-negative (&gt;= 0.0). It is the Y coordinate for the end of the scan.
<b>Note</b>: All coordinates are expected to be supplied in either calibrated units (if you set act_unit to &gt; 0 for steppers) or in atomic steps (1/maxmsdenom microsteps). Positions are measured from 0 (= the homed position) and no negative values are allowed. Because a stepper motor can only travel in steps of a minimum of 1/msdenom microsteps, this means that whatever values you supply here for distances and positions will be rounded to the nearest <i>onestep</i> value (see p.97). For example, when using atomic steps, if you set &lt;coord_x&gt; to 15 when maxmsdenom in 256 and msdenom is 8 (giving a onestep value of 256/8 = 32) then this will result in an effective &lt;coord_x&gt; of 0 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (15.0 / 32.0)) = 0. However if you set &lt;coord_x&gt; = 16 then the effective value will be 1 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (16.0 / 32.0)) = 1.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">xyzs_process</span>
xyzs_process &lt;progname&gt; [&lt;arg1&gt; ...]
Spawn an external non-blocking command during an XY scan. This is similar to execute_daemon but is run at every step of an automated XY scan run by the command xyzs_scan. You must issue this command at least once before issuing xyzs_scan but it you do not want any processes to run on the scanned images you may use NULL (case sensitive) as your &lt;progrname&gt; argument.
&lt;programe&gt; - a string which is the name of the executable file of the command to pass to the command processor. Alternatively supply NULL if you do not want any process to run on your scanned images.
[&lt;arg1&gt; ...] - an optional list of arguments for the program to be executed. If any of the arguments to be passed is the name of the most recently saved image then this must be specified within the argument list as '[img]' (without the quote marks) and the PARDUS script interpreter will replace that with the name of the last scanned image (this will be the full path name). If tissue content thresholding is active and no image was captured due to lack of tissue content then the [img] symbol will be replaced with NoTissue so your external program should check for this specific string (if it is expecting an image file name) to decide what to do.
<b>Note</b>: For more details on the rules see execute_daemon.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">xyzs_scan</span>
xyzs_scan
Begin an XY scan of moves in a snake pattern from a start position to an end position with various options pre-set using other commands. This has no arguments but for this command to work without an error certain pre-requisite commands must have been successfully issued before calling it. These commands are listed below but see their individual entries in the manual for detail:
All the commands required for grab_image to work (see grab_image for the list).
xyzs_start - sets the (x,y,z) start position of the scan.
xyzs_finish - sets the (x,y) end position of the scan.
xyzs_process - sets what external process to run on each image of the scan.
xyzs_stepsize - sets the width and height and depth of each move in the scan pattern.
xyzs_af_period - sets how often to perform autofocus during the scan. If this is set to anything more than 0 then you must also have called the pre-conditional commands required for autofocus to work (see the section on autofocus for the list).
<b>Note</b>: For a scan to work a pardcap server must be connected with a valid camera available and all three stage axes (X, Y and Z) must be extant, mature and homed.
<b>Note</b>: A text file will be opened at the start of the scan to record scan parameters and also to record the positions of each image (stage coordinates) and whether they had tissue or not (if tissue thresholding is used). The name of this file will be constructed from your image capture path and root file name settings and have the name ending in "_xys.xys". This text file output must succeed for the scan to complete successfully - any file IO errors will terminate the scan. This file is also used to load a scan into the GUI for the pardclient scan view feature.
<b>Note</b>: At the end of a scan, the preview image will be attempted to be saved to disc with a file name beginning with your image capture path and root file name settings and have the name ending in "_preview.ppm" (it is saved in p6 ppm format).
<b>Note</b>: During a scan run in the headless pcs-cli CLI mode, each tile output will be stitched into  the file described above. So it will be available and updated atomically after each tile as the scan progresses, not only at the end of the scan, and in this way you can monitor the progress of the scan (an external viewer or daemon can inspect it safely mid-scan).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">xyzs_semino_z</span>
xyzs_semino_z &lt;slices&gt;
Set the number of focus plane slices above and below the central focus plane in a scan performed by the command xyzs_scan (which see).
&lt;slices&gt; - a varval of type int and must be non-negative (&gt;= 0). It is the number of slices to be captured at each XY position of the scan both below and above the central Z position (hence 'semi').
<b>Note</b>: For a scan involving Z slices (i.e. if &lt;slices&gt; is &gt; 0) then you must also have previously called the command af_setup successfully even if you have no intention of using autofocus in your scan. The reason for this is that a call to af_setup ensures that the Z motor motion is well characterised and, in particular, the client ensures it knows which direction is 'up' and which is 'down' because these are needed to perform Z stacks.
<b>Note</b>: At each XY scan position, if xyzs_semino_z is &gt; 0, the Z motor starts at the current Z position (z0) and first moves down by (&lt;slices&gt; * &lt;size_z&gt;) distance units (where &lt;size_z&gt; is the Z step size supplied in xyzs_stepsize; if this starting position is &lt; 0.0, the scan aborts with an out-of-bounds error). It then performs &lt;slices&gt; iterations of moving to the slice position and capturing an image, stepping upward by &lt;size_z&gt; each time until it reaches z0. After verifying it has returned to z0, it captures the central z0 image (which is also used to render the XY scan preview tile and execute any background daemon command). It then steps upward by &lt;size_z&gt; and performs another &lt;slices&gt; iterations of moving to the slice position and capturing an image above z0, before returning the Z stage to z0 and continuing the XY scan. Thus, at each XY position, a symmetric Z-stack of (2 * &lt;slices&gt;) + 1 images is acquired (&lt;slices&gt; below z0, 1 at z0, and &lt;slices&gt; above z0).


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">xyzs_start</span>
xyzs_start &lt;coord_x&gt; &lt;coord_y&gt; &lt;coord_z&gt;
Set the start point coordinates for an XY scan performed by the command xyzs_scan (which see).
&lt;coord_x&gt; - a varval of type float and must be non-negative (&gt;= 0.0). It is the X coordinate for the end of the scan.
&lt;coord_y&gt; - a varval of type float and must be non-negative (&gt;= 0.0). It is the Y coordinate for the end of the scan.
&lt;coord_z&gt; - a varval of type float and must be non-negative (&gt;= 0.0). It is the Z coordinate for the end of the scan.
<b>Note</b>: All coordinates are expected to be supplied in either calibrated units (if you set act_unit to &gt; 0 for steppers) or in atomic steps (1/maxmsdenom microsteps). Positions are measured from 0 (= the homed position) and no negative values are allowed. Because a stepper motor can only travel in steps of a minimum of 1/msdenom microsteps, this means that whatever values you supply here for distances and positions will be rounded to the nearest <i>onestep</i> value (see p.97). For example, when using atomic steps, if you set &lt;coord_x&gt; to 15 when maxmsdenom in 256 and msdenom is 8 (giving a onestep value of 256/8 = 32) then this will result in an effective &lt;coord_x&gt; of 0 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (15.0 / 32.0)) = 0. However if you set &lt;coord_x&gt; = 16 then the effective value will be 1 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (16.0 / 32.0)) = 1.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">xyzs_stepsize</span>
xyzs_stepsize &lt;size_x&gt; &lt;size_y&gt; &lt;size_z&gt;
Set the width and height for each move in an XY scan performed by the command xyzs_scan (which see).
&lt;size_x&gt; - a varval of type float and must be non-negative (&gt;= 1.0e-3). It is the search step distance for each X motion in the scan.
&lt;size_y&gt; - a varval of type float and must be non-negative (&gt;= 1.0e-3). It is the search step distance for each Y motion in the scan.
&lt;size_z&gt; - a varval of type float and must be non-negative (&gt;= 1.0e-3). It is the search step distance for each Z motion in the scan.
<b>Note</b>: The term 'step' in xyzs_stepsize refers to each step in the scan pattern, not the microstep size of the motors. If you set the &lt;size_x&gt; and &lt;size_y&gt; values so that the stage moves exactly one camera frame width in X and one camera frame height in Y for each scan move then your XY scan images will not overlap. If you set them to smaller values then your scan images will have overlap at the boundaries of each image (which may be useful for 'stitching' the images together into a larger image after the scan is complete.
<b>Note</b>: The value you set for all of these arguments must be &gt;= 1.0e-3 to satisfy the syntax checker even if you do not intend to scan along a particular axis. For example, the value you supply for &lt;size_z&gt; will never be used if you are not doing any Z slices above and below the current Z position (see the command xyzs_semino_z).
<b>Note</b>: All step size distances are expected to be supplied in either calibrated units (if you set act_unit to &gt;0 for steppers) or in atomic steps (1/maxmsdenom microsteps). Positions are measured from 0 (= the homed position) and no negative values are allowed. Because a stepper motor can only travel in steps of a minimum of 1/msdenom microsteps, this means that whatever values you supply here for distances and positions will be rounded to the nearest <i>onestep</i> value (see p.97). For example, when using atomic steps, if you set &lt;size_x&gt; to 15 when maxmsdenom in 256 and msdenom is 8 (giving a onestep value of 256/8 = 32) then this will result in an effective &lt;size_x&gt; of 0 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (15.0 / 32.0)) = 0. However if you set &lt;size_x&gt; = 16 then the effective value will be 1 because (uint32_t)(0.5 + (16.0 / 32.0)) = 1.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">yuyv_bias</span>
yuyv_bias &lt;value&gt;
This sets the value of the bias constant used in the YUYV-to-RGB conversion that occurs for colour images captured with a camera streaming raw YUYV data.
&lt;value&gt; is a varval of type float. 
<b>Note</b>: There is no restriction on the value. By default the value is 0.0.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">yuyv_gain</span>
yuyv_gain &lt;value&gt;
This sets the value of the gain coefficient used in the YUYV-to-RGB conversion that occurs for colour images captured with a camera streaming raw YUYV data.
&lt;value&gt; is a varval of type float. 
<b>Note</b>: There is no restriction on the value. By default the value is 1.0.


 


 





